window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["database-with-python"]=[{"id":"database-with-python-f0001","card":"q-database-with-python-001-why-python-apps-use-a-database","sec":"database-with-python-s002","q":"Why would a Python application use a database instead of keeping data in variables or lists?","a":"<p>Variables live in memory and disappear when the process ends. A database persists data between runs and user sessions. It also gives structure (schemas, types, constraints), efficient queries over large datasets through a query language such as SQL, and safe concurrent access by many users.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0002","card":"q-database-with-python-001-why-python-apps-use-a-database","sec":"database-with-python-s002","q":"Why use a database instead of plain JSON or CSV files for application data?","a":"<p>Files work for small, single-user data. A database adds indexed lookups instead of scanning the whole file, transactions so a crash cannot leave half-written data, constraints (unique, foreign key, not null), and concurrent readers and writers without corrupting the file. Files are fine for config, exports and tiny datasets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0003","card":"q-database-with-python-001-why-python-apps-use-a-database","sec":"database-with-python-s002","q":"What pain point does an ORM such as SQLAlchemy remove for a Python developer who writes raw SQL strings?","a":"<p>Hand-written SQL strings are not checked until runtime, are easy to break with typos or injection, and tie the code to one database dialect. An ORM maps tables to Python classes and builds the SQL for you, so you work with objects, get one API across databases, and bind parameters safely. The trade-off is that it can hide the SQL and add overhead.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0004","card":"q-database-with-python-002-relational-databases","sec":"database-with-python-s003","q":"What is a primary key, and why does every table row need a unique identifier?","a":"<p>A primary key is a column (or set of columns) whose value uniquely identifies each row and is never NULL. Other tables use it to refer to the row, and the database uses it to find and update exactly one record. Without it you cannot reliably update, delete or link a single row.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0005","card":"q-database-with-python-002-relational-databases","sec":"database-with-python-s003","q":"What is a foreign key, and what does the database enforce with it?","a":"<p>A foreign key is a column that references the primary key of another table. The database enforces referential integrity: you cannot insert a child row that points to a missing parent, and you cannot delete a parent that still has children unless a rule such as <code>ON DELETE CASCADE</code> says what to do.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0006","card":"q-database-with-python-002-relational-databases","sec":"database-with-python-s003","q":"When would you pick a relational database over a NoSQL one?","a":"<p>Pick relational when the data has clear structure and relationships, you need joins, strong consistency and multi-row transactions (ACID), or ad hoc queries across entities. Examples are orders, payments and inventory. Pick NoSQL when the schema varies a lot, the scale needs horizontal partitioning, or access is mostly by key.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0007","card":"q-database-with-python-002-relational-databases","sec":"database-with-python-s003","q":"In SQL, why is <code>SELECT Name FROM Students</code> usually better than <code>SELECT * FROM Students</code> in application code?","a":"<p>Naming columns returns only the data you need, which cuts network and memory use and lets the database use covering indexes. It also keeps the result shape stable when someone adds a column to the table, whereas <code>SELECT *</code> can break code that unpacks rows by position.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0008","card":"q-database-with-python-002-relational-databases","sec":"database-with-python-s003","q":"How do you model a many-to-many relationship, such as books and authors, in a relational database?","a":"<p>Use a junction (association) table with two foreign keys, one to each side, for example <code>book_id</code> and <code>author_id</code>. Each row is one pairing. This lets a book have several authors and an author have several books without repeating data in either table.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0009","card":"q-database-with-python-003-non-relational-nosql-databases","sec":"database-with-python-s004","q":"What are the main types of NoSQL databases, and what is each good for?","a":"<p>Document (MongoDB, DynamoDB): JSON-like records with varying fields, good for flexible entities. Key-value (Redis, DynamoDB): lookup by key, good for caches and sessions. Wide-column (Cassandra): huge write-heavy data spread over many nodes. Graph (Neo4j): nodes and edges, good for highly connected data such as social networks or fraud rings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0010","card":"q-database-with-python-003-non-relational-nosql-databases","sec":"database-with-python-s004","q":"What does this data show about how a document database differs from a relational table?","a":"<p>Each document carries its own fields, so one record can have <code>cell</code> and <code>email</code> while another does not. There is no fixed schema forcing every row to have the same columns and no NULL padding. The cost is that the application, not the database, must handle missing or inconsistent fields.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{ &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Susie Campbell&quot;, &quot;cell&quot;: &quot;123-456-7890&quot;, &quot;email&quot;: &quot;susie@example.com&quot;, &quot;birthday&quot;: &quot;2000-01-01&quot; },\n{ &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Mark Watson&quot;, &quot;birthday&quot;: &quot;1999-05-20&quot; }</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0011","card":"q-database-with-python-003-non-relational-nosql-databases","sec":"database-with-python-s004","q":"What does \"NoSQL\" stand for, and does it mean these databases have no transactions or no query language?","a":"<p>It is read as \"not only SQL\". Many NoSQL stores have their own query APIs, and some offer transactions. For example, MongoDB 4.0 and later supports multi-document ACID transactions, and DynamoDB has transactional APIs. The difference is that many NoSQL stores trade strict consistency or joins for scale and flexibility.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0012","card":"q-database-with-python-003-non-relational-nosql-databases","sec":"database-with-python-s004","q":"What are the trade-offs of choosing a NoSQL database for a new application?","a":"<p>Gains: flexible schema, easy horizontal scaling, fast key-based access. Costs: usually no joins, so related data is duplicated or joined in application code; weaker or more limited transactions; and consistency rules you must understand (for example eventual consistency). Choose it for a clear access pattern, not by default.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0013","card":"q-database-with-python-003-non-relational-nosql-databases","sec":"database-with-python-s004","q":"When is a graph database a better fit than a relational database?","a":"<p>When the main questions are about relationships and paths, such as friends of friends, recommendations, or fraud rings. A relational database needs many self-joins for these, which get slow with depth. A graph database stores relationships directly and walks them efficiently.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0014","card":"q-database-with-python-004-the-python-database-api","sec":"database-with-python-s005","q":"What is the Python Database API (PEP 249), and why does it matter?","a":"<p>It is a specification that database modules (<code>sqlite3</code>, <code>psycopg2</code>, <code>mysql-connector</code>) follow, with the same <code>connect</code>, connection and cursor interface. Code written for one driver is easy to port to another, and a developer who knows one module can use the rest. Parameter placeholder style (<code>?</code> or <code>%s</code>) still differs, so it is portable but not identical.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0015","card":"q-database-with-python-004-the-python-database-api","sec":"database-with-python-s005","q":"In the Python Database API, what is the difference between a connection and a cursor?","a":"<p>The connection represents the session with the database and owns the transaction: <code>commit()</code>, <code>rollback()</code> and <code>close()</code>. The cursor is created from the connection and is used to run statements (<code>execute</code>) and read results (<code>fetchone</code>, <code>fetchmany</code>, <code>fetchall</code>). One connection can have several cursors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0016","card":"q-database-with-python-004-the-python-database-api","sec":"database-with-python-s005","q":"When do you use <code>fetchone()</code>, <code>fetchmany(n)</code> and <code>fetchall()</code>, and what is the risk of <code>fetchall()</code>?","a":"<p><code>fetchone()</code> returns the next row or <code>None</code>, <code>fetchmany(n)</code> returns up to n rows, and <code>fetchall()</code> returns every remaining row as a list. <code>fetchall()</code> loads the whole result into memory, so on a large table use <code>fetchmany</code> in a loop or iterate over the cursor.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0017","card":"q-database-with-python-004-the-python-database-api","sec":"database-with-python-s005","q":"In the Python Database API, why do you need <code>conn.commit()</code> after an <code>INSERT</code>, and what happens if you skip it?","a":"<p>DB-API drivers normally open a transaction implicitly. Changes are visible only to that connection until <code>commit()</code>. If you close the connection without committing, the changes are rolled back and lost. Reads do not need a commit. <code>rollback()</code> discards pending changes after an error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0018","card":"q-database-with-python-004-the-python-database-api","sec":"database-with-python-s005","q":"What does this code print, and what is the role of each step?","a":"<p>It prints a list of tuples such as <code>[(1, 'Alice', 25)]</code>. <code>connect</code> opens (or creates) the database file, <code>cursor</code> runs statements, <code>CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS</code> makes the script safe to rerun, <code>?</code> placeholders bind values safely, <code>commit</code> saves the insert, and <code>close</code> releases the file. Running the script twice inserts Alice twice, because nothing prevents duplicates.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import sqlite3\nconn = sqlite3.connect(&#x27;example.db&#x27;)\ncursor = conn.cursor()\ncursor.execute(&#x27;&#x27;&#x27;CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS users\n                 (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT, age INTEGER)&#x27;&#x27;&#x27;)\ncursor.execute(&quot;INSERT INTO users (name, age) VALUES (?, ?)&quot;, (&quot;Alice&quot;, 25))\nconn.commit()\ncursor.execute(&quot;SELECT * FROM users&quot;)\nprint(cursor.fetchall())\nconn.close()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0019","card":"q-database-with-python-004-the-python-database-api","sec":"database-with-python-s005","q":"In Python's <code>sqlite3</code>, does <code>with conn:</code> close the connection at the end of the block?","a":"<p>No. The connection context manager only manages the transaction: it commits if the block succeeds and rolls back if it raises. You still have to call <code>conn.close()</code>, or wrap the connection in <code>contextlib.closing</code>. <code>psycopg2</code> behaves the same way: <code>with conn:</code> commits or rolls back but leaves the connection open (in <code>psycopg</code> 3 the <code>with</code> block does close the connection).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0020","card":"q-database-with-python-004-the-python-database-api","sec":"database-with-python-s005","q":"Why does the DB-API have a <code>rollback()</code> method, and when do you call it?","a":"<p>A transaction is all-or-nothing. If one statement in a multi-step change fails, <code>rollback()</code> returns the database to the state at the start of the transaction so you do not keep half the changes. Call it in the <code>except</code> branch, then re-raise or report the error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0021","card":"q-database-with-python-005-quick-tour-sqlite3-and-sqlalchemy-side-b","sec":"database-with-python-s006","q":"What is the difference between using <code>sqlite3</code> directly and using SQLAlchemy with <code>create_engine('sqlite:///students.db')</code>?","a":"<p><code>sqlite3</code> is the standard-library driver: you write SQL strings and manage the connection and cursor. SQLAlchemy sits on top of a driver, builds SQL from Python objects, pools connections, and lets you change database by changing the URL. Use <code>sqlite3</code> for small scripts, SQLAlchemy when you want portability, models or a larger app.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0022","card":"q-database-with-python-005-quick-tour-sqlite3-and-sqlalchemy-side-b","sec":"database-with-python-s006","q":"In this SQLAlchemy snippet, what does <code>create_engine</code> with <code>echo=True</code> do, and when would you turn it off?","a":"<p><code>echo=True</code> logs every SQL statement SQLAlchemy sends, which is useful for learning and debugging. Turn it off in production, because logging all SQL is noisy, slows the app, and can write sensitive values into logs.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>engine = create_engine(&#x27;sqlite:///students.db&#x27;, echo=True)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0023","card":"q-database-with-python-005-quick-tour-sqlite3-and-sqlalchemy-side-b","sec":"database-with-python-s006","q":"In SQLAlchemy, what does <code>Base.metadata.create_all(engine)</code> do, and what does it not do?","a":"<p>It issues <code>CREATE TABLE</code> for every mapped table that does not already exist. It does not alter an existing table, so adding a column to a model later does nothing to a table that is already there. For schema changes use a migration tool such as Alembic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0024","card":"q-database-with-python-005-quick-tour-sqlite3-and-sqlalchemy-side-b","sec":"database-with-python-s006","q":"In SQLAlchemy, what is the role of <code>sessionmaker(bind=engine)</code> compared with creating <code>Session(engine)</code> directly?","a":"<p><code>sessionmaker</code> is a factory: you configure it once with the engine and options, then call it to get a new <code>Session</code> wherever you need one. Creating <code>Session(engine)</code> directly does the same for one session. The factory avoids repeating configuration across the app.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0025","card":"q-database-with-python-005-quick-tour-sqlite3-and-sqlalchemy-side-b","sec":"database-with-python-s006","q":"In a SQLAlchemy model, what is the difference between <code>Column(String, nullable=False)</code> and a plain <code>Column(String)</code>?","a":"<p><code>nullable=False</code> adds <code>NOT NULL</code> to the column definition, so the database rejects rows where the value is missing. A plain <code>Column(String)</code> allows NULL. The constraint is enforced by the database, so it holds for every client and not only for your Python code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0026","card":"q-database-with-python-005-quick-tour-sqlite3-and-sqlalchemy-side-b","sec":"database-with-python-s006","q":"In SQLAlchemy, how do the URLs <code>sqlite:///students.db</code> and <code>sqlite:////tmp/students.db</code> differ?","a":"<p>The pattern is <code>dialect:///path</code>. Three slashes then a relative path means a file relative to the working directory. Four slashes means an absolute path, because the path itself starts with a slash. <code>sqlite://</code> with no path (or <code>:memory:</code>) gives an in-memory database.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0027","card":"q-database-with-python-005-quick-tour-sqlite3-and-sqlalchemy-side-b","sec":"database-with-python-s006","q":"In SQLAlchemy 1.4 and 2.0, where should <code>declarative_base</code> be imported from?","a":"<p>From <code>sqlalchemy.orm</code> (<code>from sqlalchemy.orm import declarative_base</code>). The old location <code>sqlalchemy.ext.declarative</code> still works in 1.4 but is deprecated in 2.0. In 2.0 style you can also subclass <code>DeclarativeBase</code> and use <code>Mapped</code> and <code>mapped_column</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0028","card":"q-database-with-python-006-what-sqlite-is","sec":"database-with-python-s007","q":"What does it mean that SQLite is \"serverless\", and what are the consequences?","a":"<p>There is no separate database server process. The SQLite library runs inside your application and reads and writes a single file on disk. Consequences: zero setup, no network latency, easy to ship and back up, but no user management or network access, and concurrency is limited.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0029","card":"q-database-with-python-006-what-sqlite-is","sec":"database-with-python-s007","q":"When is SQLite a good choice, and when should you move to MySQL or PostgreSQL?","a":"<p>SQLite fits prototypes, tests, local tools, mobile and desktop apps, and low-write websites. Move to a client-server database when many clients write at the same time, when you need network access from several machines, fine-grained user permissions, or replication and high availability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0030","card":"q-database-with-python-006-what-sqlite-is","sec":"database-with-python-s007","q":"What is the concurrency limit of SQLite?","a":"<p>SQLite allows many readers but only one writer at a time, because writes lock the database file. Write-ahead logging (WAL mode) lets readers continue while one writer works. With heavy concurrent writes, writers queue or fail with \"database is locked\".</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0031","card":"q-database-with-python-006-what-sqlite-is","sec":"database-with-python-s007","q":"Is SQLite ACID compliant, and what does that give you?","a":"<p>Yes. Transactions are atomic (all or nothing), consistent (constraints hold), isolated, and durable (survive a crash after commit). A crash during a write leaves the database in the state before the transaction instead of a half-written one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0032","card":"q-database-with-python-006-what-sqlite-is","sec":"database-with-python-s007","q":"SQLite has no dedicated date or time column type. How do you store dates?","a":"<p>SQLite stores values as NULL, INTEGER, REAL, TEXT or BLOB. Store dates as ISO 8601 text (<code>2024-05-01</code>), as a Unix timestamp integer, or as a Julian day number, and use SQLite date functions to work with them. Keep one format throughout so comparisons and sorting work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0033","card":"q-database-with-python-006-what-sqlite-is","sec":"database-with-python-s007","q":"What is <code>sqlite3.connect(':memory:')</code> used for?","a":"<p>It creates a database that lives only in RAM and disappears when the connection closes. It is useful for fast unit tests and throw-away calculations, because nothing is written to disk.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0034","card":"q-database-with-python-006-what-sqlite-is","sec":"database-with-python-s007","q":"Why is it a good practice to use the same database engine in development and production?","a":"<p>Databases differ in SQL dialect, types, constraint behavior and locking. Code that works on SQLite can fail or behave differently on PostgreSQL (for example type strictness and concurrent writes). Using the same engine locally and in production, even through a container, catches these differences before release.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0035","card":"q-database-with-python-007-create-a-sqlite-database-and-table","sec":"database-with-python-s008","q":"In Python's <code>sqlite3</code>, what does <code>sqlite3.connect(\"movies.db\")</code> do if the file does not exist?","a":"<p>It creates an empty database file with that name in the current working directory. A relative path depends on where the script is run from, so use an absolute path or a path built from the script's location if the file must be in a fixed place.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0036","card":"q-database-with-python-007-create-a-sqlite-database-and-table","sec":"database-with-python-s008","q":"Why does the table creation use <code>CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS</code>, and what happens without it?","a":"<p><code>IF NOT EXISTS</code> makes the statement idempotent, so rerunning the script does nothing if the table is there. Without it, the second run raises <code>sqlite3.OperationalError: table Movies already exists</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>cursor.execute(&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS Movies (\n        title TEXT,\n        director TEXT,\n        year INT\n    )\n&quot;&quot;&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0037","card":"q-database-with-python-007-create-a-sqlite-database-and-table","sec":"database-with-python-s008","q":"What are the design weaknesses of a table that stores the director's name as text in every movie row?","a":"<p>The name is repeated for every movie, so a typo or a rename must be fixed in many rows, and a movie with several directors does not fit. Normalize it: a <code>directors</code> table plus a foreign key (or a junction table for many-to-many). Add a primary key to the movies table as well.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0038","card":"q-database-with-python-008-insert-and-read-one-record","sec":"database-with-python-s009","q":"What is the problem with writing <code>INSERT INTO Movies VALUES ('Taxi Driver', 'Martin Scorsese', 1976)</code> without a column list?","a":"<p>The values are matched to columns by position, so adding or reordering a column later breaks the statement or puts data in the wrong column. Name the columns (<code>INSERT INTO Movies (title, director, year) VALUES (...)</code>). Also bind values with placeholders instead of writing literals into the SQL string.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0039","card":"q-database-with-python-008-insert-and-read-one-record","sec":"database-with-python-s009","q":"A script that inserts a row on every run now has duplicate rows after the second run. How do you prevent that?","a":"<p>Add a constraint so the database refuses duplicates, for example a <code>UNIQUE</code> or primary key, then use <code>INSERT OR IGNORE</code> (SQLite) or catch <code>sqlite3.IntegrityError</code>. Alternatively check with a <code>SELECT</code> first, though a unique constraint is safer because it also protects against concurrent inserts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0040","card":"q-database-with-python-009-insert-many-records-with-executemany","sec":"database-with-python-s010","q":"Why use <code>cursor.executemany</code> instead of calling <code>execute</code> in a loop?","a":"<p>One call runs the same parameterized statement for every parameter set. It is shorter, avoids repeated Python overhead, and runs inside one transaction, which is much faster in SQLite than committing each row. It works for <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code> and <code>DELETE</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0041","card":"q-database-with-python-009-insert-many-records-with-executemany","sec":"database-with-python-s010","q":"What does this call do, and what must each element of the list look like?","a":"<p>It inserts one row per element of the list (here two rows). Each element must be a sequence (such as a tuple) with exactly three values to fill the three <code>?</code> placeholders in order. A tuple of the wrong length raises <code>sqlite3.ProgrammingError</code> about the number of bindings.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>films = [(&quot;Heat&quot;, &quot;Michael Mann&quot;, 1995), (&quot;Alien&quot;, &quot;Ridley Scott&quot;, 1979)]\ncursor.executemany(&quot;INSERT INTO Movies VALUES (?, ?, ?)&quot;, films)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0042","card":"q-database-with-python-009-insert-many-records-with-executemany","sec":"database-with-python-s010","q":"In <code>sqlite3</code>, why does <code>cursor.fetchone()</code> return <code>None</code> right after <code>fetchall()</code> on the same cursor?","a":"<p>The cursor keeps a read position in the current result. <code>fetchall()</code> consumes every row, so nothing is left. Running another <code>execute</code> produces a new result and resets the position.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0043","card":"q-database-with-python-009-insert-many-records-with-executemany","sec":"database-with-python-s010","q":"Does <code>executemany</code> accept only lists?","a":"<p>No. It accepts any iterable of parameter sets, such as a generator, so you can stream rows from a file without building a list in memory.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0044","card":"q-database-with-python-010-filter-with-a-parameterized-query","sec":"database-with-python-s011","q":"What is SQL injection, and how do parameterized queries prevent it?","a":"<p>Injection happens when user input is concatenated into the SQL text, so input like <code>1985; DROP TABLE movies</code> or <code>' OR '1'='1</code> changes the statement. With placeholders, the SQL text and the values are sent separately, and the driver binds each value as data, never as SQL. Building the statement with an f-string is unsafe for the same reason, and it also breaks on quotes in the data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0045","card":"q-database-with-python-010-filter-with-a-parameterized-query","sec":"database-with-python-s011","q":"In <code>sqlite3</code>, why is the parameter written as <code>(1985,)</code> with a trailing comma?","a":"<p>The driver expects a sequence of parameters. <code>(1985)</code> is just the integer 1985 in parentheses, not a tuple, and <code>execute</code> would fail. A one-element tuple needs the trailing comma, or use a list <code>[1985]</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>release_year = (1985,)\ncursor.execute(&quot;SELECT * FROM movies WHERE year = ?&quot;, release_year)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0046","card":"q-database-with-python-010-filter-with-a-parameterized-query","sec":"database-with-python-s011","q":"In <code>sqlite3</code>, how do named placeholders work and why use them?","a":"<p>Write <code>:year</code> in the SQL and pass a dictionary: <code>cursor.execute(\"SELECT * FROM movies WHERE year = :year\", {\"year\": 1985})</code>. They are clearer for statements with many parameters and let the same value be used twice.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0047","card":"q-database-with-python-010-filter-with-a-parameterized-query","sec":"database-with-python-s011","q":"Which placeholder style do <code>sqlite3</code>, <code>psycopg2</code> and <code>mysql-connector</code> use?","a":"<p><code>sqlite3</code> uses <code>?</code> (qmark) and also named <code>:name</code>. <code>psycopg2</code> and <code>mysql-connector</code> use <code>%s</code> (format) for positional values, and <code>psycopg2</code> also supports <code>%(name)s</code>. The style is exposed as the module attribute <code>paramstyle</code>. Use <code>%s</code> for every type in those two drivers, not only strings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0048","card":"q-database-with-python-011-users-challenge-three-ways-to-build-the","sec":"database-with-python-s012","q":"In SQLite, what is the difference between <code>INTEGER PRIMARY KEY</code> and <code>INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT</code>?","a":"<p>An <code>INTEGER PRIMARY KEY</code> column already becomes an alias for the row id and auto-assigns the next number. <code>AUTOINCREMENT</code> adds a guarantee that ids are never reused, even after deleting the highest row, at the cost of extra bookkeeping. Most tables do not need it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0049","card":"q-database-with-python-011-users-challenge-three-ways-to-build-the","sec":"database-with-python-s012","q":"In SQLAlchemy Core, what does this bulk insert do, and why is it better than a loop of single inserts?","a":"<p>Passing a list of dictionaries as the second argument makes SQLAlchemy run the insert as an <code>executemany</code>, one parameterized statement for all rows. That is shorter and faster than one <code>execute</code> per row. The statement is built with the expression language instead of an SQL string.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>with engine.connect() as conn:\n    conn.execute(insert(users_table), users)\n    result = conn.execute(select(users_table))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0050","card":"q-database-with-python-011-users-challenge-three-ways-to-build-the","sec":"database-with-python-s012","q":"In SQLAlchemy 2.0, why might rows inserted inside <code>with engine.connect() as conn:</code> not be saved?","a":"<p>In 2.0 a connection works in \"commit as you go\" mode. Nothing is committed unless you call <code>conn.commit()</code>, and leaving the block without a commit rolls the transaction back. To commit automatically, use <code>with engine.begin() as conn:</code> instead. In 1.x, autocommit behavior hid this.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0051","card":"q-database-with-python-011-users-challenge-three-ways-to-build-the","sec":"database-with-python-s012","q":"In SQLAlchemy, what does <code>conn.execute(users_table.insert().values(**user))</code> mean when <code>user</code> is a dictionary?","a":"<p><code>**user</code> unpacks the dictionary into keyword arguments, so each key names a column and each value is the value to insert. It builds an <code>INSERT</code> for that table with bound parameters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0052","card":"q-database-with-python-012-what-sqlalchemy-is","sec":"database-with-python-s013","q":"What is the difference between SQLAlchemy Core and the SQLAlchemy ORM?","a":"<p>Core is the SQL toolkit: engine, connection, tables, and the SQL expression language, working with rows and statements. The ORM is built on Core and maps Python classes to tables, tracks object changes in a session, and writes the SQL when you commit. Core is table-centric, the ORM is object-centric.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0053","card":"q-database-with-python-012-what-sqlalchemy-is","sec":"database-with-python-s013","q":"What is the unit of work pattern in the SQLAlchemy ORM?","a":"<p>The session records which objects were added, changed or deleted, and sends all of those changes to the database in the right order when you flush or commit. This keeps the objects and the rows consistent and lets one commit save a whole group of changes together.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0054","card":"q-database-with-python-012-what-sqlalchemy-is","sec":"database-with-python-s013","q":"What are the advantages and drawbacks of using an ORM instead of raw SQL?","a":"<p>Advantages: less repetitive code, SQL injection protection through bound parameters, database portability, and objects that fit the application model. Drawbacks: the SQL is hidden, so queries can be inefficient (for example N+1 loading), complex queries can be awkward, and there is a learning curve. Use raw SQL or Core for heavy reporting queries.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0055","card":"q-database-with-python-012-what-sqlalchemy-is","sec":"database-with-python-s013","q":"How does SQLAlchemy differ from the Django ORM?","a":"<p>SQLAlchemy is a standalone library that works with any framework, follows the data mapper pattern (models are plain classes mapped to tables), and exposes Core for explicit SQL. The Django ORM is part of Django, is closer to the active record pattern (models carry their own save and query methods), and is simpler but less flexible outside Django.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0056","card":"q-database-with-python-012-what-sqlalchemy-is","sec":"database-with-python-s013","q":"What changed in SQLAlchemy 2.0 compared with 1.x?","a":"<p>2.0 unifies Core and ORM around <code>select()</code> and <code>session.execute</code> or <code>session.scalars</code>, removes implicit autocommit in favor of explicit <code>commit()</code>, requires <code>text()</code> for plain SQL strings, and adds typed declarative models with <code>Mapped</code> and <code>mapped_column</code>. The legacy <code>session.query()</code> API still works but is considered legacy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0057","card":"q-database-with-python-013-engine-connection-and-running-a-query","sec":"database-with-python-s014","q":"In SQLAlchemy, what is the engine, and is it the same as a DB-API connection?","a":"<p>The engine is the entry point. It holds the database URL, the dialect and a connection pool. It is not a DB-API connection. <code>engine.connect()</code> borrows a pooled DB-API connection wrapped in a SQLAlchemy <code>Connection</code>. Create one engine per database for the whole application and reuse it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0058","card":"q-database-with-python-013-engine-connection-and-running-a-query","sec":"database-with-python-s014","q":"In SQLAlchemy, does <code>create_engine(...)</code> open a connection to the database?","a":"<p>No. The engine connects lazily, when the first connection is requested. A wrong host or password therefore shows up at the first query, not at <code>create_engine</code>. To check early, run a trivial statement such as <code>SELECT 1</code> at startup.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0059","card":"q-database-with-python-013-engine-connection-and-running-a-query","sec":"database-with-python-s014","q":"In SQLAlchemy 1.4 and 2.0, why does <code>conn.execute(\"SELECT * FROM Movies\")</code> fail, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>Plain strings are no longer accepted as executable. In 2.0 it raises an error (<code>ObjectNotExecutableError</code>). Wrap the SQL: <code>conn.execute(sqlalchemy.text(\"SELECT * FROM Movies\"))</code>. <code>text()</code> also supports bound parameters such as <code>:year</code>, which keeps the query safe from injection.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>with engine.connect() as conn:\n    query = &quot;SELECT * FROM Movies&quot;\n    result = conn.execute(query)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0060","card":"q-database-with-python-013-engine-connection-and-running-a-query","sec":"database-with-python-s014","q":"In SQLAlchemy, what does the engine's connection pool do, and why is it useful?","a":"<p>It keeps open database connections and hands them out for reuse, so each request does not pay the cost of a new connection (TCP, authentication). <code>connect()</code> borrows a connection and closing it returns it to the pool. Pool settings such as <code>pool_size</code> and <code>max_overflow</code> limit the number of connections.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0061","card":"q-database-with-python-013-engine-connection-and-running-a-query","sec":"database-with-python-s014","q":"In SQLAlchemy 2.0, what happens at the end of a <code>with engine.connect() as conn:</code> block when you only ran a <code>SELECT</code> and never called commit?","a":"<p>The connection returns to the pool and SQLAlchemy rolls back the transaction it began. With <code>echo=True</code> you see <code>BEGIN</code> and then <code>ROLLBACK</code>. For a read-only block this is harmless, but for writes it means the changes are lost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0062","card":"q-database-with-python-013-engine-connection-and-running-a-query","sec":"database-with-python-s014","q":"In SQLAlchemy 2.0, how do <code>engine.begin()</code> and <code>engine.connect()</code> differ for a write?","a":"<p><code>engine.begin()</code> opens a connection and a transaction, commits when the block ends without error, and rolls back on an exception. With <code>engine.connect()</code> you must call <code>conn.commit()</code> yourself, otherwise the changes are rolled back when the block ends. Use <code>begin()</code> for write blocks that are all-or-nothing.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>with engine.begin() as conn:\n    conn.execute(stmt)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0063","card":"q-database-with-python-014-expression-language-metadata-and-table","sec":"database-with-python-s015","q":"In SQLAlchemy Core, what are <code>MetaData</code> and <code>Table</code> for?","a":"<p><code>Table</code> describes one table: name, columns, types and constraints. <code>MetaData</code> is the registry that collects the Table objects, like a dictionary of table name to Table. Together they let SQLAlchemy build SQL, create tables with <code>metadata.create_all(engine)</code>, and know the schema.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0064","card":"q-database-with-python-014-expression-language-metadata-and-table","sec":"database-with-python-s015","q":"Why build queries with <code>select(table)</code> instead of writing SQL strings?","a":"<p>Statements are Python objects, so typos in column names are caught early, values are bound as parameters automatically, and the same code compiles to the right SQL for each database. They are also easy to compose conditionally, for example adding <code>.where()</code> only if a filter is set.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0065","card":"q-database-with-python-014-expression-language-metadata-and-table","sec":"database-with-python-s015","q":"When would you use <code>sqlalchemy.text()</code> instead of the expression language?","a":"<p>When you need a SQL feature the expression language does not express well, such as a database-specific function or a hand-tuned query, or when you have existing SQL. Still pass values as bound parameters (<code>text(\"... WHERE year = :year\")</code>) and not through string formatting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0066","card":"q-database-with-python-014-expression-language-metadata-and-table","sec":"database-with-python-s015","q":"In SQLAlchemy Core, what does <code>Table('sales', metadata, autoload_with=engine)</code> do, and what is the trade-off?","a":"<p>It reflects the existing table from the database, so you do not declare the columns by hand. It is quick for an existing schema. The trade-off is that the schema is read at runtime, so there is no static definition for tools, type checking or code review, and it costs a query at startup.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0067","card":"q-database-with-python-015-core-vs-orm-which-to-use","sec":"database-with-python-s016","q":"How do you choose between SQLAlchemy Core and the ORM?","a":"<p>Use the ORM when the application is object-oriented and works with entities and relationships (web apps, business logic). Use Core when you are comfortable with SQL concepts and want table-level control, such as bulk loads, reports and data pipelines. They can be mixed in one project because the ORM is built on Core.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0068","card":"q-database-with-python-015-core-vs-orm-which-to-use","sec":"database-with-python-s016","q":"Does SQLAlchemy let you switch from SQLite to PostgreSQL by changing only the URL?","a":"<p>Mostly. The URL selects the dialect and driver, so the same Core and ORM code runs on another database. In practice you must still check dialect differences: column types (for example <code>String</code> length on MySQL), raw SQL in <code>text()</code>, transaction and locking behavior, and database-specific features.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0069","card":"q-database-with-python-015-core-vs-orm-which-to-use","sec":"database-with-python-s016","q":"When is the ORM a poor choice?","a":"<p>For large bulk loads, analytics queries with many joins and aggregates, or code where you need exact control of the SQL. Loading objects one by one is slow, and lazy relationships can cause the N+1 query problem. Core, bulk-insert APIs, or raw SQL are better there.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0070","card":"q-database-with-python-016-what-mysql-is","sec":"database-with-python-s017","q":"What does the client-server model mean for MySQL, compared with SQLite?","a":"<p>The database runs as a separate server process. Clients (your Python app) connect over the network, send SQL, and receive results. This allows many clients to read and write at once, user accounts and permissions, remote access and replication. The costs are installation, configuration, and a connection for each client.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0071","card":"q-database-with-python-016-what-mysql-is","sec":"database-with-python-s017","q":"What is the difference between SQL and MySQL?","a":"<p>SQL is the query language. MySQL is a database management system that implements a dialect of it. Other systems (PostgreSQL, SQL Server, Oracle, SQLite) implement SQL with their own differences, so some syntax and functions are not portable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0072","card":"q-database-with-python-016-what-mysql-is","sec":"database-with-python-s017","q":"MySQL does not support <code>FULL OUTER JOIN</code>. How do you get the same result?","a":"<p>Combine a left join and a right join with <code>UNION</code>: <code>SELECT ... FROM a LEFT JOIN b ON ... UNION SELECT ... FROM a RIGHT JOIN b ON ...</code>. <code>UNION</code> removes the duplicate rows that appear in both joins. This is a common MySQL interview question.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0073","card":"q-database-with-python-016-what-mysql-is","sec":"database-with-python-s017","q":"Which Python options exist to talk to MySQL, and how do you choose?","a":"<p>Drivers: <code>mysql-connector-python</code> (Oracle's pure-Python driver), <code>PyMySQL</code> (pure Python) and <code>mysqlclient</code> (C extension, faster). All follow the DB-API. SQLAlchemy sits on top of one of them. Pick a driver for small scripts and SQLAlchemy when you want an ORM or portability, and keep one approach per project for consistency. <code>mysql-connector-python</code> is the official driver (pure Python with an optional C extension). <code>PyMySQL</code> installs anywhere. <code>mysqlclient</code> is a C extension around libmysqlclient, usually fastest and the Django default, but it needs system libraries and compiler headers to build.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0074","card":"q-database-with-python-016-what-mysql-is","sec":"database-with-python-s017","q":"What are the main differences between MySQL and PostgreSQL?","a":"<p>PostgreSQL follows the SQL standard more closely, has richer types (JSONB, arrays, custom types), extensions (PostGIS, pgvector), and strong support for complex queries. MySQL is simpler to set up, widely hosted, and fast for read-heavy web workloads, with mature replication. For new projects with complex data or analytics, many teams prefer PostgreSQL.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0075","card":"q-database-with-python-017-install-and-create-a-mysql-database","sec":"database-with-python-s018","q":"Why add the MySQL <code>bin</code> directory to <code>PATH</code>?","a":"<p><code>PATH</code> is the list of directories the shell searches for commands. Adding the folder that holds <code>mysql</code> lets you run <code>mysql</code> from any directory instead of typing the full path. Put the line in the shell's startup file (<code>.zshrc</code>, <code>.bashrc</code>) so it persists, and reload the file with <code>source</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>export PATH=$PATH:/usr/local/mysql/bin</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0076","card":"q-database-with-python-017-install-and-create-a-mysql-database","sec":"database-with-python-s018","q":"What does <code>mysql -u root -p</code> do, and why is it better than typing the password on the command line?","a":"<p>It connects as user <code>root</code> and the <code>-p</code> flag prompts for the password. A password written on the command line (<code>-pSecret</code>) is visible in the shell history and in the process list. Prompting keeps it out of both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0077","card":"q-database-with-python-017-install-and-create-a-mysql-database","sec":"database-with-python-s018","q":"Why should your Python application not connect to MySQL as <code>root</code>?","a":"<p><code>root</code> has full control over every database. If the app has a bug or is compromised, the attacker gets everything. Create a dedicated user with only the privileges the app needs on its own database (<code>GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON mydb.* TO 'app'@'localhost'</code>), following least privilege.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0078","card":"q-database-with-python-017-install-and-create-a-mysql-database","sec":"database-with-python-s018","q":"In MySQL, what do <code>CREATE DATABASE projects;</code> and <code>USE projects;</code> do?","a":"<p><code>CREATE DATABASE</code> makes a new database (a namespace for tables). <code>USE</code> selects it as the default for the session, so later statements such as <code>CREATE TABLE</code> or <code>SELECT</code> apply to it without a prefix. You can also name the database in the connection settings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0079","card":"q-database-with-python-018-create-tables-with-a-foreign-key","sec":"database-with-python-s019","q":"In MySQL, what happens if you insert a <code>tasks</code> row whose <code>project_id</code> does not exist in <code>projects</code>?","a":"<p>The insert fails with a foreign key constraint error, because the database enforces that every task points to an existing project. Insert parent rows first, then children.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>CREATE TABLE tasks (\n    task_id INT AUTO_INCREMENT,\n    project_id INT NOT NULL,\n    description VARCHAR(100),\n    PRIMARY KEY (task_id),\n    FOREIGN KEY (project_id) REFERENCES projects(project_id)\n);</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0080","card":"q-database-with-python-018-create-tables-with-a-foreign-key","sec":"database-with-python-s019","q":"What does <code>ON DELETE CASCADE</code> do on a foreign key, and what are the alternatives?","a":"<p><code>CASCADE</code> deletes the child rows automatically when the parent row is deleted. <code>RESTRICT</code> (the default behavior) blocks deleting a parent that has children. <code>SET NULL</code> keeps the children and sets the foreign key to NULL (the column must allow it). Choose cascade only when children have no meaning without the parent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0081","card":"q-database-with-python-018-create-tables-with-a-foreign-key","sec":"database-with-python-s019","q":"In what order do you delete rows when a foreign key has no <code>ON DELETE CASCADE</code>?","a":"<p>Children first, then the parent. Deleting the parent first fails because child rows still reference it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0082","card":"q-database-with-python-018-create-tables-with-a-foreign-key","sec":"database-with-python-s019","q":"In MySQL, which storage engine is needed for foreign keys to be enforced?","a":"<p>InnoDB, the default engine in current MySQL. Older engines such as MyISAM accept the <code>FOREIGN KEY</code> syntax but ignore it, so the constraint is not enforced. InnoDB also provides transactions and row-level locking.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0083","card":"q-database-with-python-018-create-tables-with-a-foreign-key","sec":"database-with-python-s019","q":"How does the auto-increment syntax differ between MySQL and SQLite?","a":"<p>MySQL uses <code>AUTO_INCREMENT</code> on an integer column that is a key. SQLite uses <code>AUTOINCREMENT</code> after <code>INTEGER PRIMARY KEY</code> (and works without it). PostgreSQL uses <code>SERIAL</code> or identity columns (<code>... AS IDENTITY</code>). This is a common portability trap in DDL.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0084","card":"q-database-with-python-019-insert-and-verify-data-in-the-mysql-shel","sec":"database-with-python-s020","q":"In MySQL, a <code>tasks</code> table has a foreign key <code>project_id</code> to <code>projects(id)</code>. In what order do you insert rows, and what error appears if you get it wrong?","a":"<p>Insert the parent row first (the project), then the child rows (tasks) that reference it. Inserting a task whose <code>project_id</code> does not exist fails with error 1452 (cannot add or update a child row: a foreign key constraint fails). Deleting works in the opposite order: children first, or use <code>ON DELETE CASCADE</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0085","card":"q-database-with-python-019-insert-and-verify-data-in-the-mysql-shel","sec":"database-with-python-s020","q":"How can you insert several rows into a MySQL table in one statement, and why is that better than separate inserts?","a":"<p>Give several value lists: <code>INSERT INTO tasks (project_id, description) VALUES (1, 'a'), (1, 'b'), (2, 'c');</code>. It needs one round trip and one parse instead of one per row, so it is faster, and it succeeds or fails as a single statement.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0086","card":"q-database-with-python-020-connect-python-to-mysql-with-mysql-conne","sec":"database-with-python-s021","q":"Why is <code>mysql-connector</code> often imported with an alias, and what does it change in the code?","a":"<p><code>import mysql.connector as mysql</code> only gives the module a shorter name, so calls read <code>mysql.connect(...)</code> and <code>mysql.Error</code> instead of <code>mysql.connector.connect(...)</code>. It changes no behavior. Keep one alias across the project so readers know which library it is, and do not confuse it with other packages that have <code>mysql</code> in their name.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import mysql.connector as mysql\n\nconn = mysql.connect(host=&quot;localhost&quot;, user=&quot;app&quot;, password=&quot;...&quot;, database=&quot;shop&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0087","card":"q-database-with-python-020-connect-python-to-mysql-with-mysql-conne","sec":"database-with-python-s021","q":"A Python helper that wraps <code>mysql-connector</code>'s <code>connect</code> returns <code>None</code> when it catches <code>mysql.Error</code>. What must the caller do, and what is a better design?","a":"<p>The caller must check for <code>None</code> before using the connection. Otherwise the next line fails with <code>AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'cursor'</code>, which hides the real error. A better design is to log the error and re-raise it (or raise a clear custom exception), so the failure shows up where it happened.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0088","card":"q-database-with-python-020-connect-python-to-mysql-with-mysql-conne","sec":"database-with-python-s021","q":"In <code>mysql-connector</code>, why is <code>connect</code> wrapped in <code>try/except mysql.Error</code>?","a":"<p>Connecting can fail for reasons outside the code: the server is down, the host is wrong, or the credentials are rejected. Catching <code>mysql.Error</code> lets the app report the problem or retry instead of crashing with a traceback. The caller must then handle the <code>None</code> return. In real code, log the error and avoid printing secrets.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>try:\n    return mysql.connect(\n        host=&quot;localhost&quot;,\n        user=&quot;root&quot;,\n        password=&quot;password&quot;,  # replace with your MySQL root password\n        database=db_name\n    )\nexcept mysql.Error as e:\n    print(f&quot;Error connecting to MySQL: {e}&quot;)\n    return None</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0089","card":"q-database-with-python-020-connect-python-to-mysql-with-mysql-conne","sec":"database-with-python-s021","q":"In <code>mysql-connector</code>, you run an <code>INSERT</code> and the row is missing after the script ends. What is the likely cause?","a":"<p>The transaction was never committed. <code>mysql-connector</code> does not autocommit by default, so you must call <code>connection.commit()</code> (or set <code>autocommit=True</code>). Closing the connection without a commit rolls the insert back.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0090","card":"q-database-with-python-020-connect-python-to-mysql-with-mysql-conne","sec":"database-with-python-s021","q":"In <code>mysql-connector</code>, how do you get rows as dictionaries instead of tuples?","a":"<p>Create the cursor with <code>connection.cursor(dictionary=True)</code>. Each row from <code>fetchone()</code> or <code>fetchall()</code> is then a dict keyed by column name, which is easier to read than indexing by position.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0091","card":"q-database-with-python-020-connect-python-to-mysql-with-mysql-conne","sec":"database-with-python-s021","q":"Why close the cursor and the connection in <code>mysql-connector</code> code, and how do you make sure it happens on errors?","a":"<p>Open connections hold server memory and slots (the server has a maximum connection count), and unclosed ones can lock rows. Use <code>try/finally</code> so <code>cursor.close()</code> and <code>connection.close()</code> always run, or use a connection pool.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0092","card":"q-database-with-python-021-keep-credentials-out-of-code","sec":"database-with-python-s022","q":"Why should database passwords not be written directly in Python source code?","a":"<p>Source code is committed to version control, shared and copied, so the password leaks to everyone with access, and it stays in the git history even after you delete it. Different environments also need different credentials. Read them from the environment at runtime.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0093","card":"q-database-with-python-021-keep-credentials-out-of-code","sec":"database-with-python-s022","q":"What does this code do, and why is it a better pattern?","a":"<p><code>load_dotenv()</code> reads key-value pairs from a local <code>.env</code> file into the process environment, and <code>os.getenv</code> reads one value. The password lives outside the code, can differ per machine, and the <code>.env</code> file is kept out of git (listed in <code>.gitignore</code>). <code>os.getenv</code> returns <code>None</code> when the variable is missing, so check for it.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from dotenv import load_dotenv\nimport os\nload_dotenv()\npassword = os.getenv(&quot;MYSQL_PASSWORD&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0094","card":"q-database-with-python-021-keep-credentials-out-of-code","sec":"database-with-python-s022","q":"A database password was accidentally committed to a git repository. What do you do?","a":"<p>Treat it as compromised: rotate (change) the password immediately, because removing it from later commits does not remove it from history. Then move it to an environment variable or secrets manager, and consider scanning the history and rewriting it only as a secondary step.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0095","card":"q-database-with-python-021-keep-credentials-out-of-code","sec":"database-with-python-s022","q":"A password contains characters such as <code>@</code> or <code>/</code>. What goes wrong when you put it in a SQLAlchemy URL, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>The URL parser treats those characters as separators, so the connection string breaks or points to the wrong host. Percent-encode the password (<code>urllib.parse.quote_plus</code>), or build the URL with <code>sqlalchemy.engine.URL.create(...)</code> which takes the parts separately.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0096","card":"q-database-with-python-022-encapsulate-database-operations-in-funct","sec":"database-with-python-s023","q":"Why wrap database operations in functions such as <code>add_project</code>?","a":"<p>It separates business logic from data access. Callers do not repeat SQL, the SQL lives in one place where it can be tested and changed, and parameter binding is done consistently. This is a simple form of the repository or data-access-layer pattern.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0097","card":"q-database-with-python-022-encapsulate-database-operations-in-funct","sec":"database-with-python-s023","q":"In <code>mysql-connector</code>, what does <code>cursor.lastrowid</code> give you, and why is it used here?","a":"<p><code>lastrowid</code> is the auto-increment id assigned to the last inserted row. It lets the code insert child rows (tasks) that reference the new project without running another <code>SELECT</code>. It is per cursor, so it is safe with concurrent clients, unlike querying <code>MAX(id)</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>project_sql = &quot;INSERT INTO projects (title, description) VALUES (%s, %s)&quot;\ncursor.execute(project_sql, (project_data[&#x27;title&#x27;], project_data[&#x27;description&#x27;]))\nproject_id = cursor.lastrowid  # id of the project just inserted</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0098","card":"q-database-with-python-022-encapsulate-database-operations-in-funct","sec":"database-with-python-s023","q":"In a Python function that inserts a project and its tasks with <code>mysql-connector</code>, why should it not call <code>commit()</code> itself and leave that to the caller?","a":"<p>The caller decides the transaction boundary. If the project and all its tasks should be saved together or not at all, the caller commits once after all functions succeed, and rolls back on any error. Committing inside each function would save partial work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0099","card":"q-database-with-python-022-encapsulate-database-operations-in-funct","sec":"database-with-python-s023","q":"Why is <code>%s</code> used as the placeholder in <code>mysql-connector</code>, and does it depend on the value's type?","a":"<p><code>%s</code> is the driver's placeholder style (format paramstyle). It is used for every type (strings, ints, dates), and the driver escapes and converts the value. It is not Python string formatting, so never build the SQL with <code>%</code> or f-strings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0100","card":"q-database-with-python-023-design-trade-offs-when-encapsulating","sec":"database-with-python-s024","q":"What are the options for handling the connection in data-access functions, and what does each cost?","a":"<p>Pass the connection or cursor in: simple and lets the caller control the transaction, but couples functions to the driver. Open and close a connection inside each function: no idle connections, but extra latency per call and no shared transaction. Use a connection pool: reuses connections and removes most of that cost. Commit inside functions: easy to use but cannot group operations atomically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0101","card":"q-database-with-python-023-design-trade-offs-when-encapsulating","sec":"database-with-python-s024","q":"When does hand-written data-access code become a reason to adopt an ORM?","a":"<p>When you keep adding functions for each table, relationships, filters and schema changes, you are rebuilding an ORM by hand. At that point an ORM such as SQLAlchemy gives mapping, relationship loading, sessions and migrations tested by many users. For a few simple tables, plain functions are enough.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0102","card":"q-database-with-python-023-design-trade-offs-when-encapsulating","sec":"database-with-python-s024","q":"What is the downside of tying data-access functions to the table columns?","a":"<p>A schema change (adding or removing a column) forces edits in every function that builds SQL or unpacks rows. Mitigate with a single data layer module, named columns instead of positions, tests against a real database, and migrations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0103","card":"q-database-with-python-024-sqlalchemy-engine-for-mysql","sec":"database-with-python-s025","q":"What are the parts of this SQLAlchemy URL?","a":"<p><code>mysql</code> is the dialect (the database type), <code>mysqlconnector</code> is the driver that talks to it, then <code>user:password@host:port/database</code>. 3306 is the default MySQL port. The pattern is <code>dialect+driver://user:password@host:port/database</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>engine = create_engine(\n    &quot;mysql+mysqlconnector://root:password@localhost:3306/projects&quot;,\n    echo=True\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0104","card":"q-database-with-python-024-sqlalchemy-engine-for-mysql","sec":"database-with-python-s025","q":"Does SQLAlchemy create the MySQL database named in the URL?","a":"<p>No. The database must already exist. SQLAlchemy can create tables inside it (<code>create_all</code>), but creating the database itself is done with <code>CREATE DATABASE</code>, usually by an administrator or a migration tool.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0105","card":"q-database-with-python-024-sqlalchemy-engine-for-mysql","sec":"database-with-python-s025","q":"In SQLAlchemy with MySQL, why might connections fail with \"MySQL server has gone away\" after the app has been idle?","a":"<p>The server closes idle connections after <code>wait_timeout</code> (8 hours by default), but the pool still holds the dead connection. Set <code>pool_recycle</code> to less than the server timeout so connections are replaced, or use <code>pool_pre_ping=True</code> so the pool tests a connection before using it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0106","card":"q-database-with-python-024-sqlalchemy-engine-for-mysql","sec":"database-with-python-s025","q":"Why does SQLAlchemy need a driver such as <code>mysqlconnector</code> or <code>pymysql</code> in addition to the <code>mysql</code> dialect?","a":"<p>SQLAlchemy builds SQL and manages the pool, but the actual network protocol is spoken by a DB-API driver. The dialect says which SQL flavor to produce, and the driver is the library that sends it. You must install the driver package separately.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0107","card":"q-database-with-python-025-orm-model-for-the-projects-table","sec":"database-with-python-s026","q":"In the SQLAlchemy ORM, what does a declarative base class do?","a":"<p>Models inherit from it. It collects each model's table definition into a shared <code>MetaData</code> and maps the class to the table named in <code>__tablename__</code>. Because every model shares the same metadata, <code>Base.metadata.create_all(engine)</code> can create all tables.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0108","card":"q-database-with-python-025-orm-model-for-the-projects-table","sec":"database-with-python-s026","q":"In SQLAlchemy, how does <code>registry().generate_base()</code> differ from <code>declarative_base()</code>?","a":"<p>Both return a declarative base class. <code>declarative_base()</code> is the older shortcut that creates a registry for you. <code>registry().generate_base()</code> creates the base from a registry you control, useful when you want to share or customize the registry. In SQLAlchemy 2.0 style, subclass <code>DeclarativeBase</code> instead.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0109","card":"q-database-with-python-025-orm-model-for-the-projects-table","sec":"database-with-python-s026","q":"In SQLAlchemy 2.0 style, how would you write a model with typed attributes?","a":"<p>The type annotations on <code>Mapped[...]</code> describe the Python type and nullability, and <code>mapped_column</code> configures the column. This works with type checkers. It is the recommended style from SQLAlchemy 2.0, while <code>Column(...)</code> attributes still work.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from sqlalchemy.orm import DeclarativeBase, Mapped, mapped_column\n\nclass Base(DeclarativeBase):\n    pass\n\nclass Project(Base):\n    __tablename__ = &quot;projects&quot;\n    project_id: Mapped[int] = mapped_column(primary_key=True)\n    title: Mapped[str | None] = mapped_column()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0110","card":"q-database-with-python-025-orm-model-for-the-projects-table","sec":"database-with-python-s026","q":"In SQLAlchemy with MySQL, why must you write <code>String(255)</code> rather than <code>String</code> for a column?","a":"<p>MySQL's <code>VARCHAR</code> requires a length. If you call <code>create_all</code> with a plain <code>String</code>, SQLAlchemy raises an error such as \"VARCHAR requires a length on dialect mysql\". SQLite and PostgreSQL accept it without a length. The length in the model should match the table.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0111","card":"q-database-with-python-025-orm-model-for-the-projects-table","sec":"database-with-python-s026","q":"What is the purpose of defining <code>__repr__</code> on a model class?","a":"<p>It controls how an object prints in logs, the REPL and tracebacks, so <code>print(project)</code> shows <code>&lt;Project(title='...')&gt;</code> instead of a memory address. It speeds up debugging. Avoid putting sensitive fields in it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0112","card":"q-database-with-python-026-foreign-key-and-relationship-in-the-orm","sec":"database-with-python-s027","q":"In the SQLAlchemy ORM, what is the difference between <code>ForeignKey(\"projects.project_id\")</code> and <code>relationship(\"Project\")</code>?","a":"<p><code>ForeignKey</code> is the database-level constraint: it names a table column and creates the foreign key in the schema. <code>relationship</code> is Python-level: it names a model class and lets you navigate objects (<code>task.project.title</code>). It uses the foreign key to know how to join, but it adds no column.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class Task(Base):\n    __tablename__ = &quot;tasks&quot;\n    task_id = Column(Integer, primary_key=True, autoincrement=True)\n    project_id = Column(Integer, ForeignKey(&quot;projects.project_id&quot;))\n    description = Column(String(100))\n    project = relationship(&quot;Project&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0113","card":"q-database-with-python-026-foreign-key-and-relationship-in-the-orm","sec":"database-with-python-s027","q":"In the SQLAlchemy ORM, what does <code>back_populates</code> do on a relationship?","a":"<p>It links two relationships to each other so the relationship is bidirectional: <code>project.tasks</code> and <code>task.project</code>. When you append a task to <code>project.tasks</code>, SQLAlchemy sets <code>task.project</code> in memory too. Both sides must name each other.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0114","card":"q-database-with-python-026-foreign-key-and-relationship-in-the-orm","sec":"database-with-python-s027","q":"In SQLAlchemy, what is the N+1 query problem with relationships, and how do you avoid it?","a":"<p>Loading N parents and then touching a lazy relationship on each triggers one extra query per parent, N+1 in total. Fix it with eager loading options: <code>selectinload</code> (one extra query for all children) or <code>joinedload</code> (a join). Use <code>echo=True</code> to spot repeated queries.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0115","card":"q-database-with-python-026-foreign-key-and-relationship-in-the-orm","sec":"database-with-python-s027","q":"How does SQLAlchemy represent a one-to-many and a many-to-many relationship?","a":"<p>One-to-many: the \"many\" side has a foreign key to the \"one\" side, and <code>relationship</code> on the one side returns a list. Many-to-many: an association table with two foreign keys, linked with <code>relationship(secondary=...)</code>, or an association class when the link has its own fields.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0116","card":"q-database-with-python-027-sessions-flush-and-commit","sec":"database-with-python-s028","q":"In the SQLAlchemy ORM, what is the difference between <code>session.flush()</code> and <code>session.commit()</code>?","a":"<p><code>flush()</code> sends the pending SQL to the database inside the current transaction, so auto-assigned values like primary keys become available, but the transaction stays open and can still be rolled back. <code>commit()</code> flushes if needed and then makes the transaction permanent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0117","card":"q-database-with-python-027-sessions-flush-and-commit","sec":"database-with-python-s028","q":"Why does the code call <code>session.flush()</code> before creating the <code>Task</code> objects?","a":"<p>The <code>project_id</code> is assigned by the database on insert. Before the flush it is <code>None</code>, so the tasks would be created with no parent id and the foreign key insert would fail. Flush sends the <code>INSERT</code> and fills in the id. Alternatively, assign the relationship (<code>task.project = project</code>) and let the unit of work order the inserts.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>session.add(organize_closet_project)\nsession.flush()  # generates project_id\ntasks = [\n    Task(project_id=organize_closet_project.project_id, description=&quot;Sort clothes&quot;),\n    Task(project_id=organize_closet_project.project_id, description=&quot;Donate unused items&quot;)\n]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0118","card":"q-database-with-python-027-sessions-flush-and-commit","sec":"database-with-python-s028","q":"In SQLAlchemy, why is the <code>try/except</code> in this snippet misleading?","a":"<p><code>with Session(engine) as session:</code> closes the session when the block exits, so the <code>session</code> object used in <code>except</code> is already closed. Without a <code>commit()</code>, closing discards the changes, so the explicit rollback is not needed. Put the <code>try/except</code> inside the <code>with</code> block, or use <code>with Session(engine) as session, session.begin():</code> which commits on success and rolls back on error.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>try:\n    with Session(engine) as session:\n        # ... insert operations ...\n        session.commit()\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(f&quot;Error: {e}&quot;)\n    session.rollback()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0119","card":"q-database-with-python-027-sessions-flush-and-commit","sec":"database-with-python-s028","q":"What is the benefit of using <code>with Session(engine) as session:</code> over creating a session and closing it manually?","a":"<p>The context manager closes the session even if an exception occurs, which returns its connection to the pool. An unclosed session can keep a transaction open, hold locks and starve the pool.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0120","card":"q-database-with-python-027-sessions-flush-and-commit","sec":"database-with-python-s028","q":"What does <code>session.bulk_save_objects</code> give up compared with <code>session.add_all</code>, and what is the modern alternative?","a":"<p>It is faster for many objects because it skips parts of the unit of work: no relationship cascades, limited event handling, and objects are not tracked afterward. In SQLAlchemy 2.0 it is a legacy API. Use <code>session.add_all</code> for normal work, and for bulk speed use <code>session.execute(insert(Task), [dicts])</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0121","card":"q-database-with-python-027-sessions-flush-and-commit","sec":"database-with-python-s028","q":"In SQLAlchemy, what is autoflush?","a":"<p>By default a session flushes pending changes before running a query, so the query sees objects you just added in the same transaction. It can be disabled with <code>autoflush=False</code> or temporarily with <code>session.no_autoflush</code> when you want to avoid that.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0122","card":"q-database-with-python-027-sessions-flush-and-commit","sec":"database-with-python-s028","q":"Why is it a problem to leave a SQLAlchemy session or transaction open for a long time?","a":"<p>An open transaction holds a pooled connection and can hold row or table locks, which blocks other writers and can cause lock timeouts. Keep sessions short: open per request or unit of work, commit or roll back, then close.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0123","card":"q-database-with-python-028-query-with-the-orm","sec":"database-with-python-s029","q":"In the SQLAlchemy ORM, what is the difference between <code>session.scalars(stmt).first()</code>, <code>.all()</code> and <code>.one()</code>?","a":"<p><code>.first()</code> returns the first object or <code>None</code>. <code>.all()</code> returns a list of all objects. <code>.one()</code> returns exactly one object and raises <code>NoResultFound</code> if there are none or <code>MultipleResultsFound</code> if there are several. <code>.one_or_none()</code> returns the object or <code>None</code> and raises if there are several.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0124","card":"q-database-with-python-028-query-with-the-orm","sec":"database-with-python-s029","q":"In the SQLAlchemy ORM, what is the difference between <code>session.scalars(stmt)</code> and <code>session.execute(stmt)</code> for <code>select(Project)</code>?","a":"<p><code>execute</code> returns <code>Row</code> objects, so each ORM object comes wrapped in a one-element row. <code>scalars</code> returns the first column of each row, which here is the <code>Project</code> object itself. Use <code>scalars</code> when selecting a single entity, and <code>execute</code> for several columns or aggregates.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0125","card":"q-database-with-python-028-query-with-the-orm","sec":"database-with-python-s029","q":"What does this ORM query do, and how is it different from the legacy <code>session.query(Project).filter(...)</code> form?","a":"<p>It builds a <code>SELECT ... WHERE title = :param</code> statement with a bound parameter and returns the first matching <code>Project</code> or <code>None</code>. This is the 2.0 style, which uses <code>select()</code> for both Core and ORM. <code>session.query()</code> is the legacy 1.x API: it still works but is not recommended for new code.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stmt = select(Project).where(Project.title == &quot;Organize Closet&quot;)\norganize_closet_project = session.scalars(stmt).first()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0126","card":"q-database-with-python-028-query-with-the-orm","sec":"database-with-python-s029","q":"In the SQLAlchemy ORM, how do you fetch one row by primary key, and why prefer it for that case?","a":"<p>Use <code>session.get(Project, 1)</code> (SQLAlchemy 1.4+). It returns the object or <code>None</code>, and it first checks the session's identity map, so a repeat lookup in the same session can avoid a database query.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0127","card":"q-database-with-python-028-query-with-the-orm","sec":"database-with-python-s029","q":"How do you avoid loading a huge result set into memory with the ORM?","a":"<p>Add <code>.limit()</code> and <code>.offset()</code> or keyset pagination (<code>WHERE id &gt; last_id ORDER BY id LIMIT n</code>), or stream results with <code>yield_per</code> instead of <code>.all()</code>. Select only the columns you need when you do not need full objects.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0128","card":"q-database-with-python-029-red30-challenge-database-and-sales-table","sec":"database-with-python-s030","q":"What do the two numbers in <code>DECIMAL(10, 2)</code> mean?","a":"<p>The first is precision, the total number of digits, and the second is scale, the digits after the decimal point. <code>DECIMAL(10, 2)</code> stores up to 8 digits before the point and 2 after, for example 12345678.90.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>price DECIMAL(10, 2),\ndiscount DECIMAL(5, 2),\norder_total DECIMAL(10, 2)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0129","card":"q-database-with-python-029-red30-challenge-database-and-sales-table","sec":"database-with-python-s030","q":"Why use <code>DECIMAL</code> instead of <code>FLOAT</code> or <code>DOUBLE</code> for money?","a":"<p>Floating-point types store binary approximations, so values like 0.1 cannot be represented exactly and sums drift (for example 0.1 + 0.2 is not exactly 0.3). <code>DECIMAL</code> stores exact decimal values and gives exact arithmetic, which accounting needs. The cost is somewhat slower arithmetic. The same rule applies in PostgreSQL (<code>NUMERIC</code>) and in SQLAlchemy: map money with <code>Numeric(10, 2)</code>, not <code>Float</code>, so rows come back as Python <code>Decimal</code>. Storing integer cents is another safe option.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0130","card":"q-database-with-python-030-red30-challenge-insert-and-query-with-th","sec":"database-with-python-s031","q":"In the SQLAlchemy ORM, how do you find the largest <code>order_total</code> in two different ways, and when does each apply?","a":"<p><code>select(func.max(...))</code> runs <code>SELECT MAX(order_total)</code> and returns just the value, which is best when you only need the number. Ordering descending and taking the first row (add <code>.limit(1)</code>) returns the whole record, which you need to know who placed the order. With ties, <code>max</code> returns one value while the ordering returns several rows.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stmt = select(func.max(Sale.order_total))\nprint(f&quot;Highest Order Total: {session.execute(stmt).scalar()}&quot;)\nstmt = select(Sale).order_by(Sale.order_total.desc())</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0131","card":"q-database-with-python-030-red30-challenge-insert-and-query-with-th","sec":"database-with-python-s031","q":"In SQLAlchemy, what does <code>session.execute(stmt).scalar()</code> return?","a":"<p>The first column of the first row of the result, or <code>None</code> if there are no rows. It is a convenient way to read a single value such as an aggregate. <code>scalar_one()</code> is stricter and raises if the result is not exactly one row.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0132","card":"q-database-with-python-030-red30-challenge-insert-and-query-with-th","sec":"database-with-python-s031","q":"In SQLAlchemy, what is the difference between <code>session.add()</code> and <code>session.add_all()</code>?","a":"<p><code>add</code> queues one object and <code>add_all</code> queues an iterable of objects. Both only stage the objects in the session. The inserts are sent on flush or commit, usually as batched statements.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0133","card":"q-database-with-python-031-red30-challenge-reusable-query-and-plain","sec":"database-with-python-s032","q":"In MySQL, what is wrong with this query used to find the highest order and its customer?","a":"<p><code>GROUP BY cust_name</code> returns the maximum for each customer, one row per customer, and <code>fetchone()</code> takes whichever row comes first, not the largest overall. Use <code>SELECT cust_name, order_total FROM sales ORDER BY order_total DESC LIMIT 1</code>, or a subquery comparing to <code>MAX(order_total)</code> to include ties.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>cursor.execute(&quot;SELECT MAX(order_total), cust_name FROM sales GROUP BY cust_name&quot;)\nresult = cursor.fetchone()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0134","card":"q-database-with-python-031-red30-challenge-reusable-query-and-plain","sec":"database-with-python-s032","q":"Why is it better to write a query as a function that takes the session as a parameter?","a":"<p>The function can be reused and tested with any session, such as one bound to a test database. The caller controls the session and transaction scope, and the function hides the query details. Passing dependencies in instead of creating them inside is dependency injection.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def get_max_order(session):\n    stmt = select(func.max(Sale.order_total))\n    return session.execute(stmt).scalar()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0135","card":"q-database-with-python-032-what-postgresql-is","sec":"database-with-python-s033","q":"What does it mean that PostgreSQL is an object-relational database?","a":"<p>Besides tables, rows and columns, it supports features from object-oriented design: table inheritance, user-defined types and operators, and function overloading. In practice it also means rich built-in types such as arrays, JSONB and ranges.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0136","card":"q-database-with-python-032-what-postgresql-is","sec":"database-with-python-s033","q":"Why is PostgreSQL called extensible, and what is an example?","a":"<p>You can add data types, functions, index types and whole features through extensions, without changing the core. Examples are PostGIS for geospatial data and <code>pgvector</code> for storing and searching embeddings, which is used in AI retrieval applications.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0137","card":"q-database-with-python-032-what-postgresql-is","sec":"database-with-python-s033","q":"Why use a connection pooler such as PgBouncer with PostgreSQL?","a":"<p>PostgreSQL starts a separate backend process for each client connection, which costs memory and startup time. A pooler keeps a small number of real server connections and multiplexes many client connections over them, so thousands of app connections do not overload the database.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0138","card":"q-database-with-python-032-what-postgresql-is","sec":"database-with-python-s033","q":"Which Python driver is the standard way to talk to PostgreSQL, and what is its relation to the DB-API?","a":"<p><code>psycopg2</code> is the most widely used driver, and <code>psycopg</code> (version 3) is its successor. Both follow the DB-API (connection and cursor, <code>%s</code> placeholders) just like <code>sqlite3</code> and <code>mysql-connector</code>, so the code shape is the same. SQLAlchemy can use either one underneath.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0139","card":"q-database-with-python-033-install-postgres-and-create-a-database","sec":"database-with-python-s034","q":"What do the <code>psql</code> commands <code>\\l</code>, <code>\\c red30</code>, <code>\\dt</code> and <code>\\d table</code> do?","a":"<p><code>\\l</code> lists databases, <code>\\c name</code> connects to a database, <code>\\dt</code> lists the tables in the current schema, and <code>\\d table</code> describes a table's columns and indexes. They are <code>psql</code> meta-commands, handled by the client and not SQL, so they do not end with a semicolon and cannot be run from Python through a cursor.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0140","card":"q-database-with-python-033-install-postgres-and-create-a-database","sec":"database-with-python-s034","q":"Why is there no <code>USE database</code> in PostgreSQL, and what do you do instead?","a":"<p>A PostgreSQL connection is bound to one database, and you cannot switch it with SQL. In <code>psql</code> use <code>\\c dbname</code>, which reconnects. In Python, set <code>dbname</code> in <code>psycopg2.connect(...)</code>. Cross-database queries need a foreign data wrapper or another connection.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0141","card":"q-database-with-python-033-install-postgres-and-create-a-database","sec":"database-with-python-s034","q":"Which port and default administrative user does PostgreSQL use?","a":"<p>Port 5432 by default, and the initial superuser is usually <code>postgres</code> (on some installs, the OS user that installed it). As with MySQL, create a limited user for the application instead of using the superuser.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0142","card":"q-database-with-python-034-create-a-table-with-psycopg2","sec":"database-with-python-s035","q":"In <code>psycopg2</code>, when does a transaction start, and why do you need <code>conn.commit()</code> after <code>CREATE TABLE</code>?","a":"<p><code>psycopg2</code> opens a transaction automatically on the first statement and keeps it open until <code>commit()</code> or <code>rollback()</code>. PostgreSQL even makes DDL transactional (unlike MySQL, where most DDL commits implicitly), so an uncommitted <code>CREATE TABLE</code> is rolled back when the connection closes.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>cursor.execute(&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    CREATE TABLE sales (\n        id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n        product VARCHAR(100),\n        order_total FLOAT\n    );\n&quot;&quot;&quot;)\nconn.commit()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0143","card":"q-database-with-python-034-create-a-table-with-psycopg2","sec":"database-with-python-s035","q":"What does <code>SERIAL</code> do in PostgreSQL, and what is the modern alternative?","a":"<p><code>SERIAL</code> creates an integer column backed by a sequence that supplies the next value as a default, giving auto-increment behavior. Since PostgreSQL 10 the standard identity column (<code>... AS IDENTITY</code>) is recommended for new tables.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0144","card":"q-database-with-python-034-create-a-table-with-psycopg2","sec":"database-with-python-s035","q":"What is the difference between the <code>psycopg2</code> and <code>psycopg2-binary</code> packages?","a":"<p><code>psycopg2</code> builds from source and needs compiler tools and the PostgreSQL client headers. <code>psycopg2-binary</code> ships precompiled wheels, so it installs easily and is fine for development and tests. The maintainers advise building <code>psycopg2</code> from source for production, because the bundled libraries can conflict with the system ones.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0145","card":"q-database-with-python-035-insert-data-with-executemany","sec":"database-with-python-s036","q":"In <code>psycopg2</code>, is <code>executemany</code> faster than looping with <code>execute</code>?","a":"<p>Not much. <code>psycopg2</code> implements <code>executemany</code> as a loop of separate statements, so it still makes one round trip per row. For real speed use <code>psycopg2.extras.execute_values</code> or <code>execute_batch</code>, which send many rows per statement. (<code>psycopg</code> 3 improved <code>executemany</code> with pipeline mode.)</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0146","card":"q-database-with-python-035-insert-data-with-executemany","sec":"database-with-python-s036","q":"Why does this <code>psycopg2</code> call use <code>%s</code> placeholders, and what is the role of each argument?","a":"<p><code>%s</code> marks where the driver binds a value (psycopg2's format paramstyle, used for every type). The second argument is a list of tuples, one tuple per row. Binding keeps data separate from SQL, which prevents injection and handles quoting and types.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>cursor.executemany(\n    &quot;INSERT INTO sales (product, order_total) VALUES (%s, %s)&quot;,\n    sales\n)\nconn.commit()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0147","card":"q-database-with-python-035-insert-data-with-executemany","sec":"database-with-python-s036","q":"Which database-access approaches work the same way across MySQL and PostgreSQL in Python?","a":"<p>The DB-API pattern (connect, cursor, execute with placeholders, commit, close), SQLAlchemy Core and ORM, and the database shells. Only the driver, the URL or connect arguments, and some SQL syntax differ. That is why code written to the DB-API is easy to port.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0148","card":"q-database-with-python-036-insert-dynamic-data-with-named-placehold","sec":"database-with-python-s037","q":"In <code>psycopg2</code>, how do named placeholders work, and when are they better than positional ones?","a":"<p><code>%(name)s</code> is replaced with the value under that key in the dictionary <code>sale_data</code>. With many columns it is clearer and less error-prone than matching a long positional tuple, because order does not matter and a missing key raises <code>KeyError</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>query = &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    INSERT INTO sales (order_number, product_number, quantity, price, discount, order_total)\n    VALUES (%(order_number)s, %(product_number)s, %(quantity)s, %(price)s, %(discount)s, %(order_total)s)\n&quot;&quot;&quot;\ncursor.execute(query, sale_data)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0149","card":"q-database-with-python-036-insert-dynamic-data-with-named-placehold","sec":"database-with-python-s037","q":"In <code>psycopg2</code>, why might an insert helper take the <code>cursor</code> as a parameter and leave <code>commit()</code> and <code>close()</code> to the caller?","a":"<p>The caller owns the connection and the transaction. It can run several helpers in one transaction, commit once if all succeed and roll back on any error. The helper is also easy to test with a test cursor. The cost is that every caller must remember to commit, and forgetting it silently loses the inserts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0150","card":"q-database-with-python-036-insert-dynamic-data-with-named-placehold","sec":"database-with-python-s037","q":"A function reads <code>quantity = int(input(...))</code> and <code>price = float(input(...))</code> and then inserts them. Why convert, and what else should it do?","a":"<p><code>input()</code> returns strings, so convert to the right numeric types before computing and inserting, and handle <code>ValueError</code> for bad input. Also validate ranges (no negative quantity), and keep the calculation of the total in its own function so it can be tested. For money, prefer <code>Decimal</code> over <code>float</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0151","card":"q-database-with-python-036-insert-dynamic-data-with-named-placehold","sec":"database-with-python-s037","q":"How do you safely use a dynamic table or column name with <code>psycopg2</code>?","a":"<p>Use the <code>psycopg2.sql</code> module: <code>sql.SQL(\"SELECT * FROM {}\").format(sql.Identifier(table_name))</code> quotes the identifier correctly. Values still go through <code>%s</code> parameters. Identifiers cannot be bound as parameters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0152","card":"q-database-with-python-037-sqlalchemy-core-with-postgres-crud","sec":"database-with-python-s038","q":"In SQLAlchemy Core, how do you run the four CRUD operations on a table object <code>sales_table</code>?","a":"<p>Read: <code>select(sales_table)</code>. Create: <code>sales_table.insert().values(...)</code>. Update: <code>sales_table.update().where(...).values(...)</code>. Delete: <code>sales_table.delete().where(...)</code>. Each is run with <code>connection.execute(...)</code>, and in SQLAlchemy 2.0 the transaction must be committed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0153","card":"q-database-with-python-037-sqlalchemy-core-with-postgres-crud","sec":"database-with-python-s038","q":"What is the danger of <code>sales_table.update().values(quantity=2)</code> or <code>sales_table.delete()</code> without a <code>.where(...)</code>?","a":"<p>Both apply to every row in the table, so you overwrite or delete all data. Always add a <code>where</code> clause, and check <code>result.rowcount</code> after the statement for the number of rows affected. Use a transaction so you can roll back if the count is wrong.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0154","card":"q-database-with-python-037-sqlalchemy-core-with-postgres-crud","sec":"database-with-python-s038","q":"In SQLAlchemy Core, how do you access a column of a table object in a <code>where</code> clause?","a":"<p>Through the table's <code>c</code> collection: <code>sales_table.c.order_number == '1105910'</code>. The comparison builds an SQL expression, not a Python boolean, and the value is bound as a parameter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0155","card":"q-database-with-python-037-sqlalchemy-core-with-postgres-crud","sec":"database-with-python-s038","q":"In SQLAlchemy 2.0 with PostgreSQL, how do you get the new row's id back from an insert without a second query?","a":"<p>Use <code>RETURNING</code>: <code>connection.execute(sales_table.insert().values(...).returning(sales_table.c.id))</code>. PostgreSQL supports <code>RETURNING</code>, so the id comes back in the same round trip. For a single insert, <code>result.inserted_primary_key</code> also gives the key.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0156","card":"q-database-with-python-037-sqlalchemy-core-with-postgres-crud","sec":"database-with-python-s038","q":"What does the URL <code>postgresql+psycopg2://user:password@localhost/red30</code> select?","a":"<p>The <code>postgresql</code> dialect with the <code>psycopg2</code> driver, user and password, host <code>localhost</code> (default port 5432) and database <code>red30</code>. To use <code>psycopg</code> 3 the driver part is <code>postgresql+psycopg</code>. The older name <code>postgres://</code> is not accepted by SQLAlchemy 1.4 and later.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0157","card":"q-database-with-python-038-sqlalchemy-orm-with-postgres-crud-with-a","sec":"database-with-python-s039","q":"In the SQLAlchemy ORM, what does <code>automap_base</code> do, and when would you use it instead of writing model classes by hand?","a":"<p><code>automap_base</code> inspects an existing database and builds mapped classes (and relationships from foreign keys) at runtime. Use it for existing databases, quick scripts, admin tools and data checks. For an application you own, hand-written models are better: they give IDE completion and type checking, make the schema visible in code, and do not change silently when someone alters a table. Tables without a primary key are not mapped as classes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0158","card":"q-database-with-python-038-sqlalchemy-orm-with-postgres-crud-with-a","sec":"database-with-python-s039","q":"In this SQLAlchemy automap code, what does <code>Base.prepare</code> do, and what changed about <code>reflect=True</code> in newer versions?","a":"<p><code>prepare</code> reads the table definitions from the database through the engine and creates a mapped class for each table, available under <code>Base.classes</code> by table name. In SQLAlchemy 1.4 and later the <code>reflect=True</code> form is deprecated. Use <code>Base.prepare(autoload_with=engine)</code> instead.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>Base = automap_base()\nBase.prepare(engine, reflect=True)\nSales = Base.classes.sales</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0159","card":"q-database-with-python-038-sqlalchemy-orm-with-postgres-crud-with-a","sec":"database-with-python-s039","q":"In the SQLAlchemy ORM, why does this code update the row with one <code>commit()</code> and no explicit <code>UPDATE</code> statement?","a":"<p>The session tracks every object it loaded or added. Assigning attributes marks the object as modified. At flush time (part of <code>commit</code>) the session compares the object with its loaded state and emits a single <code>UPDATE</code> containing both changed columns. If nothing changed, no statement is sent.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>new_sale.quantity = 2\nnew_sale.order_total = 39\nsession.commit()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0160","card":"q-database-with-python-038-sqlalchemy-orm-with-postgres-crud-with-a","sec":"database-with-python-s039","q":"In the SQLAlchemy ORM, what happens to the attributes of <code>new_sale</code> after <code>session.commit()</code>, and what does the next attribute access cost?","a":"<p>With the default <code>expire_on_commit=True</code>, <code>commit()</code> expires all loaded attributes. The next access, for example <code>new_sale.quantity</code>, issues a <code>SELECT</code> to reload the row. That keeps the object consistent with the database but costs a query. Set <code>expire_on_commit=False</code> when you need to read objects after the session closes, accepting that they may be stale.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>session.add(new_sale)\nsession.commit()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0161","card":"q-database-with-python-038-sqlalchemy-orm-with-postgres-crud-with-a","sec":"database-with-python-s039","q":"In the SQLAlchemy ORM, what is the difference between <code>filter_by(order_number='1105910')</code> and <code>filter(Sales.order_number == '1105910')</code>?","a":"<p>Both produce the same <code>WHERE</code> clause. <code>filter_by</code> takes keyword arguments and only supports equality on the main entity of the query. <code>filter</code> takes full SQL expressions, so it supports <code>&gt;</code>, <code>like</code>, <code>in_</code>, <code>or_</code>, and columns of joined entities.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0162","card":"q-database-with-python-038-sqlalchemy-orm-with-postgres-crud-with-a","sec":"database-with-python-s039","q":"In the SQLAlchemy ORM, what SQL does <code>session.query(Sales).order_by(Sales.order_total).first()</code> send, and why is it cheaper than loading all rows?","a":"<p>It sends <code>SELECT ... FROM sales ORDER BY order_total LIMIT 1</code>. The database returns one row, and an index on <code>order_total</code> can answer it without sorting the whole table. Calling <code>.all()</code> and taking the first item in Python would transfer and build every row.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0163","card":"q-database-with-python-038-sqlalchemy-orm-with-postgres-crud-with-a","sec":"database-with-python-s039","q":"In the SQLAlchemy ORM, when should you change rows with a bulk <code>update()</code> statement instead of loading objects and setting attributes?","a":"<p>Use a bulk <code>update(...).where(...)</code> when you change many rows by a rule, for example raise all prices by 5 percent. It runs one statement in the database and loads nothing. Load objects when you need per-object logic, validation, events or relationship handling. A bulk update bypasses the session's tracking, so objects already loaded can be stale.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0164","card":"q-database-with-python-039-library-challenge-tables-and-models","sec":"database-with-python-s040","q":"In a many-to-many junction table such as <code>bookauthors</code>, should the primary key be a separate id column or the pair of foreign keys?","a":"<p>Both work. A composite primary key <code>(author_id, book_id)</code> is the natural choice: it also stops the same pair from being stored twice and needs no extra column. A surrogate id is simpler for ORMs and for referencing a pairing from other tables, but then you must add a <code>UNIQUE (author_id, book_id)</code> constraint yourself or duplicates can appear.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0165","card":"q-database-with-python-039-library-challenge-tables-and-models","sec":"database-with-python-s040","q":"In SQLAlchemy, why must model classes be defined (or imported) before <code>Base.metadata.create_all(engine)</code> runs?","a":"<p><code>create_all</code> only knows tables that are registered in <code>Base.metadata</code>, and a table is registered when its model class is defined. If a module with models was never imported, its tables are silently skipped. <code>create_all</code> also sorts tables by foreign key dependencies, so parents are created before children.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0166","card":"q-database-with-python-039-library-challenge-tables-and-models","sec":"database-with-python-s040","q":"How do you prevent the same author and book pair from being inserted twice in a SQLAlchemy junction model?","a":"<p>Add a unique constraint in the model: <code>__table_args__ = (UniqueConstraint('author_id', 'book_id'),)</code>. The database then rejects a duplicate pair with an integrity error, which holds even when two processes insert at the same time. Checking in Python first is not enough.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class BookAuthor(Base):\n    __tablename__ = &#x27;bookauthors&#x27;\n    bookauthor_id = Column(Integer, primary_key=True)\n    author_id = Column(Integer, ForeignKey(&#x27;authors.author_id&#x27;))\n    book_id = Column(Integer, ForeignKey(&#x27;books.book_id&#x27;))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0167","card":"q-database-with-python-039-library-challenge-tables-and-models","sec":"database-with-python-s040","q":"In PostgreSQL, does a <code>ForeignKey</code> column get an index automatically, and why does it matter for a junction table?","a":"<p>No. PostgreSQL indexes primary keys and unique constraints, not foreign key columns. Without an index on <code>book_id</code>, a join from books to authors, or a delete of a parent row (which must check children), scans the junction table. Add <code>index=True</code> on frequently joined foreign key columns. The composite unique index on <code>(author_id, book_id)</code> already helps lookups by author.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0168","card":"q-database-with-python-039-library-challenge-tables-and-models","sec":"database-with-python-s040","q":"In SQLAlchemy, what is the difference between a many-to-many <code>relationship(secondary=...)</code> and a mapped association class like <code>BookAuthor</code>?","a":"<p><code>secondary</code> points to a plain link table and lets you use <code>author.books</code> directly, with no class for the link rows. It is simplest when the link table has only the two foreign keys. An association class is a full model, so use it when the link carries its own data, such as a role, an order or a date added, or when you need to query link rows themselves.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0169","card":"q-database-with-python-039-library-challenge-tables-and-models","sec":"database-with-python-s040","q":"What is a weakness of these foreign key columns in a junction model, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>Both columns allow NULL, so a link row can exist without an author or a book. Add <code>nullable=False</code> (or make them part of a composite primary key, which implies NOT NULL). Also consider <code>ondelete='CASCADE'</code> so deleting a book removes its links.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>author_id = Column(Integer, ForeignKey(&#x27;authors.author_id&#x27;))\nbook_id = Column(Integer, ForeignKey(&#x27;books.book_id&#x27;))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0170","card":"q-database-with-python-039-library-challenge-tables-and-models","sec":"database-with-python-s040","q":"In SQLAlchemy with PostgreSQL, does <code>Column(String)</code> without a length work, and how does that differ from MySQL?","a":"<p>Yes. PostgreSQL accepts <code>VARCHAR</code> without a length (and <code>TEXT</code>), so <code>Column(String)</code> creates a column with no limit. MySQL requires a length for <code>VARCHAR</code>, so the same model fails on MySQL unless you write <code>String(255)</code>. Choose an explicit length when you want the database to enforce a limit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0171","card":"q-database-with-python-040-library-challenge-add-a-book-in-one-tran","sec":"database-with-python-s041","q":"Why should adding a book, a new author and the link row happen in one transaction with a single <code>commit()</code>?","a":"<p>The three inserts form one logical change. If the link insert fails after the book was committed, you keep a book without an author. One transaction makes the change atomic: either all rows are saved or none. Use <code>flush()</code> in between to get database-assigned ids without committing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0172","card":"q-database-with-python-040-library-challenge-add-a-book-in-one-tran","sec":"database-with-python-s041","q":"In the SQLAlchemy ORM, how can you create the link row without calling <code>flush()</code> to get the ids first?","a":"<p>Set the relationships instead of the ids: <code>BookAuthor(author=author, book=new_book)</code>. The unit of work knows that the link row depends on both parents, inserts the author and book first, and fills in the foreign keys itself. Using ids requires a <code>flush()</code> because the ids do not exist until the parents are inserted.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pairing = BookAuthor(author_id=author.author_id, book_id=new_book.book_id)\nsession.add(pairing)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0173","card":"q-database-with-python-040-library-challenge-add-a-book-in-one-tran","sec":"database-with-python-s041","q":"In this add-a-book logic, two users add the same book at the same moment. What can go wrong, and how do you make it safe?","a":"<p>This is a check-then-insert race. Both requests see no book and both insert. The reliable fix is a unique constraint in the database, then catch the integrity error and treat it as \"already exists\". In PostgreSQL you can also use <code>INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING</code>. The Python check is only a friendly shortcut.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>existing_book = session.query(Book).filter_by(title=title).first()\nif existing_book:\n    print(f&quot;Book &#x27;{title}&#x27; already exists.&quot;)\n    return</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0174","card":"q-database-with-python-040-library-challenge-add-a-book-in-one-tran","sec":"database-with-python-s041","q":"In a library schema, why is looking up an author by first name and last name a weak way to decide whether the author already exists?","a":"<p>Names are not unique: two different people can share a name, and the same person can be spelled differently. The lookup would merge different authors or create duplicates. Use a stable identifier such as an external author id, or let the user pick from matching authors. Keep the name only as display data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0175","card":"q-database-with-python-040-library-challenge-add-a-book-in-one-tran","sec":"database-with-python-s041","q":"Where should the rule \"page count must be a positive number\" be enforced in a SQLAlchemy and PostgreSQL application?","a":"<p>In both places. Validate in Python for fast, friendly errors (<code>int(input())</code> raises <code>ValueError</code> on text). Add a <code>CheckConstraint('number_of_pages &gt; 0')</code> in the model so the database rejects bad data from any client, script or future service. The database is the last line of defence.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0176","card":"q-database-with-python-040-library-challenge-add-a-book-in-one-tran","sec":"database-with-python-s041","q":"In SQLAlchemy 1.4 and 2.0, how can you make a session commit on success and roll back on an exception automatically?","a":"<p><code>session.begin()</code> used as a context manager starts a transaction, commits when the block ends normally, and rolls back if an exception escapes. The outer <code>with Session(engine)</code> closes the session. This removes the need for manual <code>commit()</code>, <code>rollback()</code> and <code>close()</code> calls.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>with Session(engine) as session:\n    with session.begin():\n        session.add(Book(title=&quot;Example&quot;, number_of_pages=100))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0177","card":"q-database-with-python-041-mysql-database-management","sec":"database-with-python-s042","q":"What happens when you drop a database?","a":"<p>The data is lost for good, so back it up first.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0178","card":"q-database-with-python-041-mysql-database-management","sec":"database-with-python-s042","q":"In MySQL, what is the difference between <code>CREATE DATABASE</code> and <code>CREATE SCHEMA</code>?","a":"<p>None. In MySQL, <code>SCHEMA</code> is a synonym for <code>DATABASE</code>. This differs from PostgreSQL and SQL Server, where a schema is a namespace inside a database.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0179","card":"q-database-with-python-041-mysql-database-management","sec":"database-with-python-s042","q":"In MySQL, what does <code>SHOW CREATE DATABASE my_database;</code> show, and why is it useful?","a":"<p>It prints the <code>CREATE DATABASE</code> statement that would recreate the database, including its default character set and collation. Use it to check or copy those settings before creating a matching database or when text sorting or emoji storage behaves unexpectedly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0180","card":"q-database-with-python-041-mysql-database-management","sec":"database-with-python-s042","q":"Which character set should a new MySQL database use for text with emoji and many languages, and what changed in MySQL 8.0?","a":"<p>Use <code>utf8mb4</code>. MySQL's older <code>utf8</code> is an alias for <code>utf8mb3</code>, which stores at most 3 bytes per character and cannot hold emoji. Since MySQL 8.0 the server default is <code>utf8mb4</code> (with the collation <code>utf8mb4_0900_ai_ci</code>); in 5.7 the default was <code>latin1</code>. Set it on the database, tables and the client connection (<code>charset='utf8mb4'</code>), otherwise inserting an emoji fails.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0181","card":"q-database-with-python-042-mysql-table-management","sec":"database-with-python-s043","q":"Which constraints should you know?","a":"<p>PRIMARY KEY, FOREIGN KEY, UNIQUE and NOT NULL, plus the CASCADE options on foreign keys.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0182","card":"q-database-with-python-042-mysql-table-management","sec":"database-with-python-s043","q":"In MySQL, what is the risk of <code>ALTER TABLE employees MODIFY COLUMN email VARCHAR(150);</code>?","a":"<p><code>MODIFY</code> replaces the whole column definition. If the column was <code>NOT NULL</code> or had a default, and you only write the type, those attributes are lost. Always restate the full definition, for example <code>VARCHAR(150) NOT NULL</code>. <code>CHANGE</code> is similar but also renames the column. Also check that data fits when you shrink a column.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0183","card":"q-database-with-python-042-mysql-table-management","sec":"database-with-python-s043","q":"In a MySQL table, what does <code>email VARCHAR(100) UNIQUE</code> create besides a rule?","a":"<p>A unique index on the column. The index enforces uniqueness (a second row with the same email fails with a duplicate key error) and also speeds up lookups by email. A unique column allows several NULLs in MySQL.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>email VARCHAR(100) UNIQUE,</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0184","card":"q-database-with-python-042-mysql-table-management","sec":"database-with-python-s043","q":"You add a <code>NOT NULL</code> column to a table that already contains rows in MySQL. What must you provide?","a":"<p>A default value, for example <code>ALTER TABLE employees ADD status VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL DEFAULT 'active';</code>. Without a default, existing rows get the implicit default of the type (an empty string or 0) in non-strict mode, or the statement fails in strict mode. Backfill in stages for large tables.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0185","card":"q-database-with-python-042-mysql-table-management","sec":"database-with-python-s043","q":"In MySQL, what should you check before running <code>ALTER TABLE</code> on a very large production table?","a":"<p>Whether it locks the table and how long it takes. In MySQL 8.0, many changes can run online with <code>ALGORITHM=INPLACE</code>, and adding a column at the end can use <code>ALGORITHM=INSTANT</code> (8.0.12 and later), which changes only metadata. Other changes rebuild the table. Specify <code>ALGORITHM</code> and <code>LOCK=NONE</code> so the statement fails instead of silently blocking writes, or use a tool such as gh-ost or pt-online-schema-change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0186","card":"q-database-with-python-042-mysql-table-management","sec":"database-with-python-s043","q":"In MySQL, can <code>TRUNCATE TABLE</code> be rolled back, and why does it matter?","a":"<p>No. In MySQL <code>TRUNCATE</code> is DDL: it commits implicitly, so a later <code>ROLLBACK</code> cannot restore the rows. It also resets <code>AUTO_INCREMENT</code>, and it fails on a table referenced by a foreign key. <code>DELETE</code> without <code>WHERE</code> is slower but runs inside a transaction and can be rolled back.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0187","card":"q-database-with-python-042-mysql-table-management","sec":"database-with-python-s043","q":"In MySQL, what must be true for this <code>ALTER TABLE</code> to succeed?","a":"<p>Adding the constraint requires an InnoDB table, matching data types and signedness on both columns, and an index on the referenced column (usually its primary key). It also fails if existing rows already point to missing departments, so clean the data first.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ALTER TABLE employees ADD CONSTRAINT fk_dept FOREIGN KEY (department_id) REFERENCES departments(department_id);</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0188","card":"q-database-with-python-042-mysql-table-management","sec":"database-with-python-s043","q":"In MySQL, when would you choose <code>VARCHAR(n)</code> over <code>TEXT</code>?","a":"<p>Use <code>VARCHAR(n)</code> for short, bounded values such as names and emails: it states the limit, can be fully indexed and is stored with the row. <code>TEXT</code> is for long, unbounded content. <code>TEXT</code> columns need a prefix length to be indexed and are stored off-page when large.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0189","card":"q-database-with-python-043-mysql-data-manipulation-dml","sec":"database-with-python-s044","q":"DELETE vs TRUNCATE?","a":"<p>DELETE removes specific rows (with a WHERE clause). TRUNCATE empties the whole table and resets it. Always use WHERE when you update or delete.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0190","card":"q-database-with-python-043-mysql-data-manipulation-dml","sec":"database-with-python-s044","q":"You ran an <code>UPDATE</code> without a <code>WHERE</code> clause on a production MySQL table. What do you do?","a":"<p>Stop further writes if you can, then restore the rows. If it was inside a transaction that is still open, run <code>ROLLBACK</code>. Otherwise recover from a backup, or from the binary log with point-in-time recovery, or from a replica that has not applied it yet. Prevent it with <code>sql_safe_updates</code> and by running changes in a transaction you can check first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0191","card":"q-database-with-python-043-mysql-data-manipulation-dml","sec":"database-with-python-s044","q":"In MySQL, what does the <code>sql_safe_updates</code> setting do?","a":"<p>With <code>SET sql_safe_updates = 1</code>, the server rejects an <code>UPDATE</code> or <code>DELETE</code> that has no <code>WHERE</code> clause using a key column and no <code>LIMIT</code>. It protects against full-table changes typed by mistake in an interactive session. It is a safety net, not a replacement for care.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0192","card":"q-database-with-python-043-mysql-data-manipulation-dml","sec":"database-with-python-s044","q":"In MySQL, why is <code>UPDATE employees SET salary = salary + 5000 WHERE id = 1;</code> better than reading the salary in Python and writing the new value back?","a":"<p>The database does the read and write as one statement, so it is atomic for that row and no other session can change the value in between. The read-modify-write pattern in application code can lose updates when two requests run together. It also saves a round trip.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0193","card":"q-database-with-python-043-mysql-data-manipulation-dml","sec":"database-with-python-s044","q":"In MySQL, how do you insert a row if it does not exist and update it if it does?","a":"<p>Use <code>INSERT ... ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE</code>, which relies on a primary or unique key: <code>INSERT INTO employees (id, salary) VALUES (1, 52000) ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE salary = VALUES(salary);</code>. <code>INSERT IGNORE</code> silently skips the duplicate. <code>REPLACE</code> deletes the old row and inserts a new one, which can break foreign key references and changes auto-increment values, so it is usually the wrong choice. In MySQL 8.0.20 and later, <code>VALUES(salary)</code> is deprecated; use a row alias: <code>INSERT ... VALUES (1, 52000) AS new ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE salary = new.salary</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0194","card":"q-database-with-python-044-mysql-select-queries","sec":"database-with-python-s045","q":"In SQL, what is the difference between <code>WHERE</code> and <code>HAVING</code>?","a":"<p><code>WHERE</code> filters individual rows before grouping and cannot use aggregate functions. <code>HAVING</code> filters groups after <code>GROUP BY</code> and can use aggregates such as <code>COUNT(*) &gt; 2</code>. Put row conditions in <code>WHERE</code> so fewer rows are grouped.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0195","card":"q-database-with-python-044-mysql-select-queries","sec":"database-with-python-s045","q":"In what logical order does SQL evaluate the parts of a <code>SELECT</code> statement?","a":"<p><code>FROM</code> and joins, <code>WHERE</code>, <code>GROUP BY</code>, <code>HAVING</code>, <code>SELECT</code> (including aliases and window functions), <code>DISTINCT</code>, <code>ORDER BY</code>, then <code>LIMIT</code>. This is why a <code>WHERE</code> clause cannot use a column alias defined in <code>SELECT</code>, while <code>ORDER BY</code> can.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0196","card":"q-database-with-python-044-mysql-select-queries","sec":"database-with-python-s045","q":"In MySQL, why does this query work, and would it work in PostgreSQL?","a":"<p>MySQL allows a <code>SELECT</code> alias in <code>HAVING</code> as an extension. Standard SQL and PostgreSQL do not, so there you write <code>HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 2</code>. The portable form works in both.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>SELECT department_id, COUNT(*) AS emp_count\nFROM employees\nGROUP BY department_id\nHAVING emp_count &gt; 2;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0197","card":"q-database-with-python-044-mysql-select-queries","sec":"database-with-python-s045","q":"In SQL, what is the difference between <code>COUNT(*)</code>, <code>COUNT(column)</code> and <code>COUNT(DISTINCT column)</code>?","a":"<p><code>COUNT(*)</code> counts rows. <code>COUNT(column)</code> counts rows where the column is not NULL. <code>COUNT(DISTINCT column)</code> counts the different non-NULL values. Other aggregates such as <code>AVG</code> also ignore NULL values.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0198","card":"q-database-with-python-044-mysql-select-queries","sec":"database-with-python-s045","q":"In MySQL, why is <code>SELECT * FROM employees LIMIT 5</code> not a reliable way to get the same five rows each time?","a":"<p>Without <code>ORDER BY</code>, the order of rows is not defined. The server returns whatever it finds first, which can change with the plan or after data changes. Always add <code>ORDER BY</code> with a unique tiebreaker, such as the primary key, when you use <code>LIMIT</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0199","card":"q-database-with-python-044-mysql-select-queries","sec":"database-with-python-s045","q":"What is the problem with paging by <code>LIMIT 20 OFFSET 100000</code>, and what is the alternative?","a":"<p>The database still reads and discards the first 100000 rows, so deep pages get slower. Use keyset (seek) pagination: remember the last seen key and query <code>WHERE id &gt; :last_id ORDER BY id LIMIT 20</code>. It uses the index and has constant cost, but it cannot jump to an arbitrary page number.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0200","card":"q-database-with-python-044-mysql-select-queries","sec":"database-with-python-s045","q":"In MySQL 5.7 and later, what does <code>ONLY_FULL_GROUP_BY</code> do?","a":"<p>It makes the server reject a query that selects a column that is neither in <code>GROUP BY</code> nor inside an aggregate, because the value would be ambiguous. It is part of the default <code>sql_mode</code> from MySQL 5.7. Older versions silently returned an arbitrary row's value.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0201","card":"q-database-with-python-045-mysql-joins-subqueries-union-and-case","sec":"database-with-python-s046","q":"INNER vs OUTER join?","a":"<p>INNER keeps only rows that match in both tables. LEFT and RIGHT outer joins also keep unmatched rows from one side. Be ready to write queries with several joins, subqueries and aggregations.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0202","card":"q-database-with-python-045-mysql-joins-subqueries-union-and-case","sec":"database-with-python-s046","q":"In SQL, how do you list employees who have no department using a <code>LEFT JOIN</code>?","a":"<p>Add <code>WHERE d.department_id IS NULL</code> to this query. The left join keeps every employee, and unmatched rows have NULL in all department columns. Filtering for NULL on a column of the right table keeps only the unmatched rows. This pattern is called an anti-join. <code>NOT EXISTS</code> is an equivalent form.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>SELECT e.first_name, e.last_name, d.department_name\nFROM employees e\nLEFT JOIN departments d ON e.department_id = d.department_id;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0203","card":"q-database-with-python-045-mysql-joins-subqueries-union-and-case","sec":"database-with-python-s046","q":"In a <code>LEFT JOIN</code>, what changes when you move a condition on the right table from <code>ON</code> to <code>WHERE</code>?","a":"<p>A condition in <code>ON</code> only decides which right rows match, so unmatched left rows are still returned with NULLs. The same condition in <code>WHERE</code> runs after the join and removes the NULL rows, which turns the left join into an inner join. Put right-table filters in <code>ON</code> when you want to keep all left rows.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0204","card":"q-database-with-python-045-mysql-joins-subqueries-union-and-case","sec":"database-with-python-s046","q":"In SQL, what is the difference between <code>UNION</code> and <code>UNION ALL</code>, and which should you prefer?","a":"<p><code>UNION</code> removes duplicate rows, which requires a sort or hash step. <code>UNION ALL</code> keeps every row and is faster. Prefer <code>UNION ALL</code> when duplicates cannot occur or do not matter. Both need the same number of columns with compatible types.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0205","card":"q-database-with-python-045-mysql-joins-subqueries-union-and-case","sec":"database-with-python-s046","q":"When would you write a join instead of a subquery, and the other way round?","a":"<p>Joins are usually clearer and the optimizer handles them well when you need columns from both tables. A subquery fits when you only need a filter or a single value, such as <code>salary &gt; (SELECT AVG(salary) ...)</code>. A correlated subquery runs once per outer row, so rewrite it as a join or window function when it is slow. Modern MySQL and PostgreSQL often transform both into the same plan.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0206","card":"q-database-with-python-045-mysql-joins-subqueries-union-and-case","sec":"database-with-python-s046","q":"In SQL, why can <code>NOT IN (subquery)</code> return no rows when the subquery contains a NULL, and what do you use instead?","a":"<p><code>x NOT IN (1, NULL)</code> evaluates to unknown for every value, because comparing with NULL is unknown, so no row passes the filter. Use <code>NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM ... WHERE ...)</code>, which handles NULLs correctly, or make sure the subquery excludes NULLs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0207","card":"q-database-with-python-045-mysql-joins-subqueries-union-and-case","sec":"database-with-python-s046","q":"In SQL, how do you use <code>CASE</code> inside an aggregate to count rows by category in a single query?","a":"<p>Use conditional aggregation: <code>SELECT SUM(CASE WHEN salary &gt; 60000 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS high, SUM(CASE WHEN salary &lt;= 60000 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS other FROM employees;</code>. This pivots categories into columns in one table scan, instead of several queries. <code>CASE</code> also works in <code>ORDER BY</code> and <code>UPDATE</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0208","card":"q-database-with-python-045-mysql-joins-subqueries-union-and-case","sec":"database-with-python-s046","q":"In SQL, how do you list each employee with the name of their manager when both are rows of the same <code>employees</code> table?","a":"<p>Use a self join with two aliases: <code>SELECT e.first_name AS employee, m.first_name AS manager FROM employees e LEFT JOIN employees m ON e.manager_id = m.id;</code>. Use <code>LEFT JOIN</code> so top-level employees without a manager still appear.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0209","card":"q-database-with-python-045-mysql-joins-subqueries-union-and-case","sec":"database-with-python-s046","q":"In this MySQL query, is the subquery run once per employee?","a":"<p>No. The subquery does not refer to the outer query, so it is uncorrelated and its result is computed once and reused for the comparison. A correlated subquery, which refers to a column of the outer row, is conceptually evaluated per row.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>SELECT first_name, last_name\nFROM employees\nWHERE salary &gt; (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM employees);</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0210","card":"q-database-with-python-046-mysql-indexing","sec":"database-with-python-s047","q":"When do you add an index and what does it cost?","a":"<p>Index columns that are queried often. The cost is slower INSERT and UPDATE operations.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0211","card":"q-database-with-python-046-mysql-indexing","sec":"database-with-python-s047","q":"In MySQL, which of <code>WHERE first_name = 'Ann'</code>, <code>WHERE last_name = 'Lee'</code> and <code>WHERE first_name = 'Ann' AND last_name = 'Lee'</code> can use this composite index, and why?","a":"<p>Queries that filter on <code>first_name</code>, or on <code>first_name</code> and <code>last_name</code>, can use it. A query that filters only on <code>last_name</code> generally cannot, because of the leftmost prefix rule: the index is sorted by <code>first_name</code> first. Put the column used by the most queries, and usually the more selective one, first.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>CREATE INDEX idx_name ON employees(first_name, last_name);</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0212","card":"q-database-with-python-046-mysql-indexing","sec":"database-with-python-s047","q":"In MySQL, why can <code>WHERE YEAR(hire_date) = 2023</code> ignore an index on <code>hire_date</code>, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>Applying a function to the column means the stored index values no longer match the expression, so the server scans the table. Rewrite as a range: <code>WHERE hire_date &gt;= '2023-01-01' AND hire_date &lt; '2024-01-01'</code>. In MySQL 8.0.13 and later you can instead create a functional index on the expression. A leading wildcard, as in <code>LIKE '%smith'</code>, has the same problem.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0213","card":"q-database-with-python-046-mysql-indexing","sec":"database-with-python-s047","q":"In InnoDB, what is a clustered index, and how does it affect secondary indexes?","a":"<p>InnoDB stores the table rows inside the primary key B-tree, so the primary key is the clustered index. Each secondary index entry holds the indexed value plus the primary key value, so a lookup through a secondary index finds the key and then reads the row through the primary key. A long primary key therefore makes every secondary index larger.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0214","card":"q-database-with-python-046-mysql-indexing","sec":"database-with-python-s047","q":"What is a covering index, and why is it fast?","a":"<p>A covering index contains all the columns a query needs, so the database answers from the index alone and never reads the table rows. In MySQL, <code>EXPLAIN</code> shows <code>Using index</code> in <code>Extra</code>. For example, an index on <code>(department_id, salary)</code> covers <code>SELECT salary FROM employees WHERE department_id = 3</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0215","card":"q-database-with-python-046-mysql-indexing","sec":"database-with-python-s047","q":"Why is an index on a column with only a few distinct values, such as a yes/no flag, often not useful?","a":"<p>A low-selectivity index matches a large share of the rows. Following the index for each match is slower than reading the table sequentially, so the optimizer ignores it. Indexes pay off when they narrow the result to a small fraction of the table. Combine the flag with a selective column in a composite index.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0216","card":"q-database-with-python-046-mysql-indexing","sec":"database-with-python-s047","q":"How does a B-tree index make a lookup fast?","a":"<p>A B-tree keeps keys sorted in a balanced tree with a high fan-out, so a lookup reads only a few pages (logarithmic in the table size) instead of scanning every row. Sorted order also supports range queries, <code>ORDER BY</code> and prefix matches such as <code>LIKE 'abc%'</code>. It does not help with <code>LIKE '%abc'</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0217","card":"q-database-with-python-047-mysql-users-and-privileges","sec":"database-with-python-s048","q":"How do you secure access?","a":"<p>Know the privilege levels (global, database, table) and grant the minimum needed.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0218","card":"q-database-with-python-047-mysql-users-and-privileges","sec":"database-with-python-s048","q":"In MySQL, what does the <code>@'localhost'</code> part of <code>'new_user'@'localhost'</code> mean?","a":"<p>A MySQL account is the pair of a user name and a host the user may connect from. <code>'new_user'@'localhost'</code> and <code>'new_user'@'10.0.0.%'</code> are two different accounts with their own passwords and privileges. Use <code>%</code> to allow any host, which is rarely a good idea in production.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>CREATE USER &#x27;new_user&#x27;@&#x27;localhost&#x27; IDENTIFIED BY &#x27;password&#x27;;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0219","card":"q-database-with-python-047-mysql-users-and-privileges","sec":"database-with-python-s048","q":"In MySQL 8.0, can <code>GRANT</code> create a user that does not exist?","a":"<p>No. Before 8.0, <code>GRANT ... IDENTIFIED BY</code> could create the account implicitly. In 8.0 you must run <code>CREATE USER</code> first, then <code>GRANT</code>. A GRANT for a missing account fails.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0220","card":"q-database-with-python-047-mysql-users-and-privileges","sec":"database-with-python-s048","q":"In MySQL, do you need <code>FLUSH PRIVILEGES</code> after <code>GRANT</code>, <code>REVOKE</code> or <code>ALTER USER</code>?","a":"<p>No. Those account statements update the in-memory privilege tables immediately. <code>FLUSH PRIVILEGES</code> is only needed when you change the <code>mysql.*</code> grant tables directly with <code>INSERT</code> or <code>UPDATE</code>, which you should avoid.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0221","card":"q-database-with-python-047-mysql-users-and-privileges","sec":"database-with-python-s048","q":"What privileges should the account used by a web application have, compared with the account used for schema migrations?","a":"<p>The application account gets only data privileges, usually <code>SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE</code> on its own database. The migration account also gets <code>CREATE</code>, <code>ALTER</code>, <code>INDEX</code> and <code>DROP</code> and is used only during deployments. If the application account is compromised or hit by SQL injection, it cannot drop tables or read other databases.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0222","card":"q-database-with-python-047-mysql-users-and-privileges","sec":"database-with-python-s048","q":"In MySQL 8.0, what are roles, and why use them?","a":"<p>A role is a named set of privileges that you grant to users: <code>CREATE ROLE app_read; GRANT SELECT ON shop.* TO app_read; GRANT app_read TO 'alice'@'localhost';</code>. Roles avoid repeating long grant lists per user and make changes one step. Users must have the role activated (default roles) to use it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0223","card":"q-database-with-python-047-mysql-users-and-privileges","sec":"database-with-python-s048","q":"In MySQL 8.0, which default authentication plugin is used, and what problem can it cause for older clients?","a":"<p>MySQL 8.0 uses <code>caching_sha2_password</code> by default (5.7 used <code>mysql_native_password</code>). Older drivers and clients that do not support it fail with an authentication error. Fix by upgrading the driver, or create the user with <code>IDENTIFIED WITH mysql_native_password BY '...'</code> when you cannot.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0224","card":"q-database-with-python-048-mysql-transactions","sec":"database-with-python-s049","q":"What are the ACID properties and when do you need transactions?","a":"<p>Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability. Use transactions for work that must succeed or fail together, such as financial operations.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0225","card":"q-database-with-python-048-mysql-transactions","sec":"database-with-python-s049","q":"In MySQL, what does <code>autocommit</code> do, and why does <code>START TRANSACTION</code> matter?","a":"<p>By default <code>autocommit</code> is on, so every statement is its own transaction and commits immediately. <code>START TRANSACTION</code> (or <code>BEGIN</code>) groups statements until <code>COMMIT</code> or <code>ROLLBACK</code>, so they succeed or fail together. With autocommit on and no explicit transaction, you cannot undo a statement that has run.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0226","card":"q-database-with-python-048-mysql-transactions","sec":"database-with-python-s049","q":"In MySQL, what does this code do?","a":"<p><code>SAVEPOINT</code> marks a point inside the current transaction. <code>ROLLBACK TO savepoint1</code> undoes the work done after that point but keeps the transaction open and the earlier work intact. Use it to retry or skip one step of a long transaction without abandoning everything.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>SAVEPOINT savepoint1;\nROLLBACK TO savepoint1;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0227","card":"q-database-with-python-048-mysql-transactions","sec":"database-with-python-s049","q":"What is the default isolation level of InnoDB, and which problems does each level allow?","a":"<p>InnoDB defaults to <code>REPEATABLE READ</code>. <code>READ UNCOMMITTED</code> allows dirty reads (seeing uncommitted data). <code>READ COMMITTED</code> prevents dirty reads but allows non-repeatable reads (the same row read twice differs). <code>REPEATABLE READ</code> prevents both, and InnoDB's consistent snapshot and gap locks also limit phantom rows (new rows appearing in a repeated range query). <code>SERIALIZABLE</code> prevents all of them at the cost of more locking.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0228","card":"q-database-with-python-048-mysql-transactions","sec":"database-with-python-s049","q":"In MySQL InnoDB, what does <code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</code> do, and when do you use it?","a":"<p>It reads rows and locks them until the transaction ends, so other transactions cannot update them or lock them. Use it for read-then-write logic such as reserving stock or checking a balance before changing it. Keep the transaction short and lock rows in a consistent order to avoid deadlocks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0229","card":"q-database-with-python-048-mysql-transactions","sec":"database-with-python-s049","q":"In MySQL, why do you not wrap a <code>CREATE TABLE</code> in a transaction to roll it back?","a":"<p>DDL statements such as <code>CREATE</code>, <code>ALTER</code>, <code>DROP</code> and <code>TRUNCATE</code> cause an implicit commit in MySQL. The current transaction is committed first, and the DDL cannot be rolled back. Keep schema changes outside the transaction that protects your data changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0230","card":"q-database-with-python-049-mysql-views","sec":"database-with-python-s050","q":"Why use views?","a":"<p>They abstract complex queries and add a security layer. A limitation is that they are not always updatable.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0231","card":"q-database-with-python-049-mysql-views","sec":"database-with-python-s050","q":"In MySQL, when can you <code>INSERT</code> or <code>UPDATE</code> through a view?","a":"<p>Only when the view maps directly to rows of one underlying table. Views with <code>GROUP BY</code>, aggregates, <code>DISTINCT</code>, <code>UNION</code> or some joins are not updatable. Add <code>WITH CHECK OPTION</code> so changes through the view must still satisfy the view's <code>WHERE</code> condition. Otherwise you can update a row so it disappears from the view.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0232","card":"q-database-with-python-049-mysql-views","sec":"database-with-python-s050","q":"Does MySQL support materialized views, and what do you do if you need one?","a":"<p>No. A MySQL view stores only its query and runs it each time. To cache an expensive result, create a summary table and refresh it with a scheduled event or a job, or maintain it with triggers. PostgreSQL has real materialized views.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0233","card":"q-database-with-python-049-mysql-views","sec":"database-with-python-s050","q":"Does creating a view make a slow query faster?","a":"<p>No. A normal view is a saved query. MySQL either merges its definition into the outer query or builds a temporary table, and the cost is the cost of the underlying query. Speed comes from indexes on the base tables. Views help with readability and access control.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0234","card":"q-database-with-python-049-mysql-views","sec":"database-with-python-s050","q":"In MySQL, what do <code>SQL SECURITY DEFINER</code> and <code>SQL SECURITY INVOKER</code> mean for a view?","a":"<p>With <code>DEFINER</code> (the default), the view runs with the privileges of the account that created it, so a user can read through the view without having rights on the base table. With <code>INVOKER</code> it runs with the caller's privileges. Definer views are how you expose a safe subset of columns to restricted users.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0235","card":"q-database-with-python-050-mysql-stored-procedures-and-functions","sec":"database-with-python-s051","q":"Procedure vs function?","a":"<p>Use stored procedures for complex logic and security. Use functions for reusable calculations you can call inside a query.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0236","card":"q-database-with-python-050-mysql-stored-procedures-and-functions","sec":"database-with-python-s051","q":"In the <code>mysql</code> client, why do stored procedure scripts use <code>DELIMITER //</code>?","a":"<p>The client ends a statement at <code>;</code>. The procedure body contains <code>;</code> characters, so the client would cut it into pieces. <code>DELIMITER //</code> temporarily changes the terminator so the whole <code>CREATE PROCEDURE</code> is sent as one statement, and <code>DELIMITER ;</code> restores it. It is a client command, not SQL, so drivers do not need it.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>DELIMITER //\nCREATE PROCEDURE GetHighSalaryEmployees()\nBEGIN\n    SELECT * FROM employees WHERE salary &gt; 60000;\nEND //\nDELIMITER ;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0237","card":"q-database-with-python-050-mysql-stored-procedures-and-functions","sec":"database-with-python-s051","q":"In a MySQL function, what does the <code>DETERMINISTIC</code> keyword declare, and what if it is wrong?","a":"<p>It declares that the same inputs always give the same result. The optimizer and binary logging rely on it. When binary logging is on, creating a function without <code>DETERMINISTIC</code>, <code>NO SQL</code> or <code>READS SQL DATA</code> can fail with an error. If you declare it but the function returns different results (for example it uses <code>NOW()</code>), replicas and caching can behave incorrectly.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>CREATE FUNCTION CalculateBonus(salary DECIMAL(10,2))\nRETURNS DECIMAL(10,2)\nDETERMINISTIC</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0238","card":"q-database-with-python-050-mysql-stored-procedures-and-functions","sec":"database-with-python-s051","q":"In Python with <code>mysql-connector</code>, how do you call a stored procedure?","a":"<p>Use <code>cursor.callproc('GetHighSalaryEmployees', args)</code> and read results with <code>cursor.stored_results()</code>, or run <code>cursor.execute(\"CALL GetHighSalaryEmployees()\")</code>. Passing arguments as a sequence keeps them parameterized, which avoids SQL injection.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0239","card":"q-database-with-python-050-mysql-stored-procedures-and-functions","sec":"database-with-python-s051","q":"What are the trade-offs of putting business logic in stored procedures instead of application code?","a":"<p>Benefits: fewer network round trips, one place for rules shared by many applications, and permission control (users can run a procedure without table access). Drawbacks: harder to version, test and debug, tied to one database vendor, uses database CPU that is hard to scale, and logic is split between two places. Many teams keep procedures for data-heavy batch work only.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0240","card":"q-database-with-python-050-mysql-stored-procedures-and-functions","sec":"database-with-python-s051","q":"In a MySQL stored procedure, what do <code>IN</code>, <code>OUT</code> and <code>INOUT</code> parameters mean?","a":"<p><code>IN</code> passes a value into the procedure (the default). <code>OUT</code> returns a value to the caller through a session variable, such as <code>CALL p(@result)</code>. <code>INOUT</code> passes a value in and lets the procedure change it for the caller. Functions have only input parameters and return one value.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0241","card":"q-database-with-python-050-mysql-stored-procedures-and-functions","sec":"database-with-python-s051","q":"Why can a MySQL stored function not run <code>COMMIT</code>, while a stored procedure can?","a":"<p>A function is called inside a statement, such as a <code>SELECT</code>, and a statement must not commit the transaction it is part of. MySQL forbids explicit and implicit commits inside stored functions and triggers. A procedure is called with <code>CALL</code> on its own, so it can control transactions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0242","card":"q-database-with-python-051-mysql-triggers","sec":"database-with-python-s052","q":"What are triggers for and what do they cost?","a":"<p>Logging and enforcing rules. They add performance overhead.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0243","card":"q-database-with-python-051-mysql-triggers","sec":"database-with-python-s052","q":"In MySQL, what is the difference between <code>BEFORE</code> and <code>AFTER</code> triggers, and which one do you pick for validation?","a":"<p>A <code>BEFORE</code> trigger runs before the row is written and can change <code>NEW</code> values or reject the row with <code>SIGNAL</code>, so it fits validation and defaults. An <code>AFTER</code> trigger runs once the row is stored and suits auditing and updating other tables. In MySQL an <code>AFTER</code> trigger cannot change the row being written.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0244","card":"q-database-with-python-051-mysql-triggers","sec":"database-with-python-s052","q":"In a MySQL trigger, what are <code>NEW</code> and <code>OLD</code>, and which are available for each event?","a":"<p><code>NEW</code> holds the row's new values and <code>OLD</code> the values before the change. <code>INSERT</code> triggers have only <code>NEW</code>, <code>DELETE</code> triggers have only <code>OLD</code>, and <code>UPDATE</code> triggers have both. In a <code>BEFORE</code> trigger you can assign to <code>NEW</code> columns.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>INSERT INTO audit_log (action, employee_id, action_date)\nVALUES (&#x27;INSERT&#x27;, NEW.id, NOW());</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0245","card":"q-database-with-python-051-mysql-triggers","sec":"database-with-python-s052","q":"In MySQL, why does a trigger definition include <code>FOR EACH ROW</code>?","a":"<p>MySQL supports only row-level triggers: the body runs once for every affected row. A statement that changes 10000 rows runs the trigger 10000 times, so heavy logic in a trigger slows bulk operations. PostgreSQL also offers statement-level triggers that run once per statement.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0246","card":"q-database-with-python-051-mysql-triggers","sec":"database-with-python-s052","q":"What are the common problems with database triggers?","a":"<p>The logic is hidden from application developers, so behavior is surprising and hard to debug. Triggers add cost to every write, can cascade into other triggers, complicate bulk loads and migrations, and are hard to test. An error inside a trigger fails the original statement. In MySQL, cascaded foreign key actions such as <code>ON DELETE CASCADE</code> do not fire triggers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0247","card":"q-database-with-python-051-mysql-triggers","sec":"database-with-python-s052","q":"What alternatives exist to a trigger for keeping an audit trail of changes?","a":"<p>Write the audit record in the application layer or a data-access function, use change data capture from the binary log or WAL (for example Debezium), or use built-in features such as temporal tables or an audit plugin. Triggers remain the simplest option when many different clients write to the same tables and the audit must not be bypassed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0248","card":"q-database-with-python-052-mysql-performance-tuning-commands","sec":"database-with-python-s053","q":"How do you optimize MySQL performance?","a":"<p>Indexing strategy, query optimization (avoid <code>SELECT *</code>), and caching (the InnoDB buffer pool).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0249","card":"q-database-with-python-052-mysql-performance-tuning-commands","sec":"database-with-python-s053","q":"In MySQL, which columns of <code>EXPLAIN</code> output do you look at first, and what is a bad sign?","a":"<p><code>type</code> (access method), <code>key</code> (index used), <code>rows</code> (estimated rows examined) and <code>Extra</code>. A <code>type</code> of <code>ALL</code> means a full table scan, <code>key</code> of NULL means no index was used, and <code>Using filesort</code> or <code>Using temporary</code> in <code>Extra</code> mean extra sorting or temporary tables. Good values are <code>const</code>, <code>eq_ref</code>, <code>ref</code> and <code>range</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0250","card":"q-database-with-python-052-mysql-performance-tuning-commands","sec":"database-with-python-s053","q":"What is the difference between <code>EXPLAIN</code> and <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> in MySQL?","a":"<p><code>EXPLAIN</code> shows the plan and row estimates without running the query. <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code>, available from MySQL 8.0.18, runs the query and reports actual time and row counts per step. Comparing estimates with actual rows exposes bad statistics. Because it executes the statement, do not use it carelessly on a write.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0251","card":"q-database-with-python-052-mysql-performance-tuning-commands","sec":"database-with-python-s053","q":"In MySQL, what is the difference between <code>ANALYZE TABLE</code> and <code>OPTIMIZE TABLE</code>?","a":"<p><code>ANALYZE TABLE</code> refreshes the index statistics the optimizer uses to choose plans. It is cheap. <code>OPTIMIZE TABLE</code> rebuilds the table and its indexes to reclaim space and defragment, which locks or copies a large table. Run <code>ANALYZE</code> after large data changes, and <code>OPTIMIZE</code> only after big deletes.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ANALYZE TABLE employees;\nOPTIMIZE TABLE employees;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0252","card":"q-database-with-python-052-mysql-performance-tuning-commands","sec":"database-with-python-s053","q":"In MySQL, how do you find and stop a query that is running too long?","a":"<p>Run <code>SHOW FULL PROCESSLIST</code> (or query <code>performance_schema.threads</code>) to see running statements, their time and state, then stop the offender with <code>KILL QUERY &lt;id&gt;</code> (the statement only) or <code>KILL &lt;id&gt;</code> (the connection). Then fix the cause, for example a missing index, rather than only killing it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0253","card":"q-database-with-python-052-mysql-performance-tuning-commands","sec":"database-with-python-s053","q":"A MySQL query that was fast is now slow. What steps do you take?","a":"<p>Run <code>EXPLAIN</code> to see the plan, and check whether the right index is used. Check that statistics are current (<code>ANALYZE TABLE</code>), look in the slow query log for similar statements, check for lock waits and long transactions, and compare data volume. Then fix with an index, a query rewrite, or config such as the buffer pool size. Measure before and after.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0254","card":"q-database-with-python-053-mysql-backup-restore-and-server-variable","sec":"database-with-python-s054","q":"Logical vs physical backups?","a":"<p><code>mysqldump</code> is a logical backup. A physical backup copies the data files. Also know point-in-time recovery. Key variables: <code>innodb_buffer_pool_size</code>, <code>max_connections</code>, <code>query_cache_size</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0255","card":"q-database-with-python-053-mysql-backup-restore-and-server-variable","sec":"database-with-python-s054","q":"In MySQL, why do you pass <code>--single-transaction</code> to <code>mysqldump</code> for InnoDB tables?","a":"<p>It runs the dump inside one consistent snapshot transaction, so you get a consistent backup without locking tables, and writes continue during the dump. Without it, <code>mysqldump</code> can lock tables or produce data from different moments. It works for InnoDB only, not for MyISAM.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>mysqldump -u root -p my_database &gt; backup.sql</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0256","card":"q-database-with-python-053-mysql-backup-restore-and-server-variable","sec":"database-with-python-s054","q":"In the shell, what do <code>&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;</code> do in the <code>mysqldump</code> and <code>mysql</code> commands?","a":"<p>These are shell redirections. <code>&gt;</code> writes the dump output to the file <code>backup.sql</code>. <code>&lt;</code> feeds the file into the <code>mysql</code> client as input, which runs the SQL statements and recreates the data. The target database must already exist for the restore.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>mysqldump -u root -p my_database &gt; backup.sql\nmysql -u root -p my_database &lt; backup.sql</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0257","card":"q-database-with-python-053-mysql-backup-restore-and-server-variable","sec":"database-with-python-s054","q":"How does point-in-time recovery work for MySQL?","a":"<p>Restore the last full backup, then replay the binary log from the position of that backup up to just before the bad event, using <code>mysqlbinlog --stop-datetime=... | mysql</code>. It requires binary logging to be enabled before the incident. It lets you recover from a mistaken <code>DELETE</code> made after the last backup.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0258","card":"q-database-with-python-053-mysql-backup-restore-and-server-variable","sec":"database-with-python-s054","q":"In MySQL, what is the difference between <code>SET SESSION</code> and <code>SET GLOBAL</code>, and what happens at restart?","a":"<p><code>SET SESSION</code> changes the value for the current connection only. <code>SET GLOBAL</code> changes the default for new connections and does not affect existing ones. Neither survives a server restart. In MySQL 8.0 use <code>SET PERSIST</code> to save the value, or edit <code>my.cnf</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0259","card":"q-database-with-python-053-mysql-backup-restore-and-server-variable","sec":"database-with-python-s054","q":"What effect does <code>sql_mode = 'STRICT_TRANS_TABLES'</code> have on MySQL inserts?","a":"<p>In strict mode, invalid data such as a too-long string or an out-of-range number makes the statement fail with an error. Without it, MySQL truncates or adjusts the value and only raises a warning, which hides bugs and silently corrupts data. Keep strict mode on for applications.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0260","card":"q-database-with-python-053-mysql-backup-restore-and-server-variable","sec":"database-with-python-s054","q":"Why is raising <code>max_connections</code> usually not the right fix for \"Too many connections\"?","a":"<p>Each connection uses memory and a thread, so a large value can exhaust server memory and slow everything. Find why connections are not released: leaks, no pooling or slow queries. Use a connection pool in the application, set sensible pool limits, and only then raise <code>max_connections</code> with enough memory.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0261","card":"q-database-with-python-053-mysql-backup-restore-and-server-variable","sec":"database-with-python-s054","q":"What is a good starting size for <code>innodb_buffer_pool_size</code>, and why does it matter?","a":"<p>On a server dedicated to MySQL, commonly 50 to 75 percent of RAM. The buffer pool caches data and index pages in memory, so a larger pool means fewer disk reads. Too large causes swapping and starves the operating system. Check the hit ratio and the data size.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0262","card":"q-database-with-python-053-mysql-backup-restore-and-server-variable","sec":"database-with-python-s054","q":"Why must you test restoring a MySQL backup regularly?","a":"<p>A backup that has never been restored is unproven. Files can be incomplete, truncated, or miss routines, users or events. Restore into a test server on a schedule, check row counts, and measure how long it takes so you know your real recovery time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0263","card":"q-database-with-python-054-mysql-partitioning-events-and-full-text","sec":"database-with-python-s055","q":"What partitioning types exist and when is full-text search better than a normal index?","a":"<p>RANGE, LIST and HASH. Full-text indexes serve word search over text, which a regular index cannot do well.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0264","card":"q-database-with-python-054-mysql-partitioning-events-and-full-text","sec":"database-with-python-s055","q":"In MySQL, what is partition pruning, and what is the practical benefit of partitioning a large table?","a":"<p>With pruning the optimizer reads only the partitions that can match the <code>WHERE</code> condition, for example only the 2021 partition for a date range in 2021. Partitions also make maintenance cheap: dropping an old partition removes a year of data instantly, where <code>DELETE</code> would be slow and bloat logs. Partitioning does not speed up queries that do not filter on the partition key.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0265","card":"q-database-with-python-054-mysql-partitioning-events-and-full-text","sec":"database-with-python-s055","q":"What are the main limitations of partitioned InnoDB tables in MySQL?","a":"<p>Every unique key, including the primary key, must contain all columns used in the partitioning expression. Foreign keys are not supported on partitioned InnoDB tables. The number of partitions is limited, and queries without the partition key scan all partitions. Choose the partition key to match the most common filter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0266","card":"q-database-with-python-054-mysql-partitioning-events-and-full-text","sec":"database-with-python-s055","q":"In MySQL, what happens if you insert a row for 2023 into this partitioned table?","a":"<p>The insert fails with \"Table has no partition for value 2023\". Range partitions need a partition for every possible value. Add a catch-all <code>PARTITION pmax VALUES LESS THAN MAXVALUE</code> and split it later with <code>REORGANIZE PARTITION</code>, or create new partitions ahead of time.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>PARTITION BY RANGE (YEAR(sale_date)) (\n    PARTITION p0 VALUES LESS THAN (2020),\n    PARTITION p1 VALUES LESS THAN (2021),\n    PARTITION p2 VALUES LESS THAN (2022)\n);</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0267","card":"q-database-with-python-054-mysql-partitioning-events-and-full-text","sec":"database-with-python-s055","q":"In MySQL, what must be true for an event created with <code>CREATE EVENT</code> to run?","a":"<p>The event scheduler must be on (<code>event_scheduler = ON</code>), the event must be enabled, and the account needs the <code>EVENT</code> privilege. If the scheduler is off, events are stored but never run. Set it in the server configuration so it survives a restart.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>SET GLOBAL event_scheduler = ON;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0268","card":"q-database-with-python-054-mysql-partitioning-events-and-full-text","sec":"database-with-python-s055","q":"When would you use a MySQL event instead of a cron job, and what is the trade-off?","a":"<p>An event runs inside the database, needs no external host and works with the database's own privileges, which suits small housekeeping such as purging old rows. A cron job or workflow scheduler is better for logging, alerting, retries, dependencies and work outside the database. Events are invisible to application code and not part of normal monitoring.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0269","card":"q-database-with-python-054-mysql-partitioning-events-and-full-text","sec":"database-with-python-s055","q":"In MySQL, how does <code>MATCH ... AGAINST</code> full-text search differ from <code>LIKE '%word%'</code>?","a":"<p><code>MATCH ... AGAINST</code> uses a full-text index, so it does not scan every row, and it returns results ranked by relevance. <code>LIKE '%word%'</code> cannot use a normal index because of the leading wildcard. Full-text search ignores very short words and common stopwords: the InnoDB minimum word length (<code>innodb_ft_min_token_size</code>) is 3 by default. It is not a replacement for a search engine such as Elasticsearch when you need typo tolerance or complex ranking.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>SELECT * FROM employees\nWHERE MATCH(first_name, last_name) AGAINST(&#x27;John Doe&#x27;);</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0270","card":"q-database-with-python-055-mysql-common-interview-queries","sec":"database-with-python-s056","q":"How do you normalize a table?","a":"<p>Break a denormalized table into 1NF, 2NF and 3NF. <strong>Q. How do you handle deadlocks?</strong> Detect them with <code>SHOW ENGINE INNODB STATUS</code> and prevent them with a consistent locking order.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0271","card":"q-database-with-python-055-mysql-common-interview-queries","sec":"database-with-python-s056","q":"In MySQL, why does this query return the second highest salary, and what does it return when there is only one distinct salary?","a":"<p>The subquery finds the highest salary. The outer query takes the maximum of all smaller salaries, which is the second highest distinct value. Ties at the top do not matter. With only one distinct salary the outer <code>MAX</code> has no rows and returns NULL, not an error.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>SELECT MAX(salary)\nFROM employees\nWHERE salary &lt; (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees);</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0272","card":"q-database-with-python-055-mysql-common-interview-queries","sec":"database-with-python-s056","q":"In MySQL 8.0, how do you get the Nth highest salary in general?","a":"<p>Use a window function: <code>SELECT DISTINCT salary FROM (SELECT salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS r FROM employees) t WHERE r = N;</code>. <code>DENSE_RANK</code> handles ties without gaps. Before 8.0, use <code>ORDER BY salary DESC LIMIT 1 OFFSET N-1</code> on distinct salaries, or a correlated subquery.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0273","card":"q-database-with-python-055-mysql-common-interview-queries","sec":"database-with-python-s056","q":"In MySQL, how do you delete duplicate rows by email and keep one of each?","a":"<p>This query finds the duplicates. To delete, keep the row with the smallest id: <code>DELETE e1 FROM employees e1 JOIN employees e2 ON e1.email = e2.email AND e1.id &gt; e2.id;</code>. To prevent them, add a <code>UNIQUE</code> constraint on <code>email</code> after cleaning the data.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>SELECT email, COUNT(*)\nFROM employees\nGROUP BY email\nHAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0274","card":"q-database-with-python-055-mysql-common-interview-queries","sec":"database-with-python-s056","q":"In SQL, how do you find employees who earn more than their manager?","a":"<p>Join the table to itself: <code>SELECT e.first_name FROM employees e JOIN employees m ON e.manager_id = m.id WHERE e.salary &gt; m.salary;</code>. Each employee row is matched to its manager row, and the filter compares the two salaries.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0275","card":"q-database-with-python-055-mysql-common-interview-queries","sec":"database-with-python-s056","q":"In SQL, how do you find departments that have no employees?","a":"<p>Use <code>LEFT JOIN departments d ... employees e ... WHERE e.id IS NULL</code>, or <code>NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM employees e WHERE e.department_id = d.department_id)</code>. Avoid <code>NOT IN</code> on a column that can contain NULL.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0276","card":"q-database-with-python-056-mysql-8-0-features-cte-and-window-functi","sec":"database-with-python-s057","q":"In SQL, what is a CTE (<code>WITH</code> clause), and why use it instead of a nested subquery?","a":"<p>A CTE gives a name to a query result that you can use in the main statement. It makes long queries readable, lets you reuse the same result several times, and is required for recursive queries. It does not automatically make a query faster: depending on the version, the database either merges it into the outer query or computes it once and stores it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0277","card":"q-database-with-python-056-mysql-8-0-features-cte-and-window-functi","sec":"database-with-python-s057","q":"Which features did MySQL 8.0 add that earlier versions lacked for analytical queries?","a":"<p>Common table expressions (including recursive ones) and window functions such as <code>RANK()</code> and <code>ROW_NUMBER()</code>. In MySQL 5.7 and earlier you needed derived tables, self joins or user variables to rank rows or compute running totals.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0278","card":"q-database-with-python-056-mysql-8-0-features-cte-and-window-functi","sec":"database-with-python-s057","q":"What is the difference between a window function and <code>GROUP BY</code>?","a":"<p><code>GROUP BY</code> collapses rows into one row per group. A window function computes a value over a group of related rows but keeps every input row, so you can show each row's salary next to the department rank or average. For example, <code>SUM(salary) OVER (PARTITION BY department_id)</code> puts the department total on every employee row, which is useful for percentage-of-total, while <code>GROUP BY</code> returns one row per department.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0279","card":"q-database-with-python-056-mysql-8-0-features-cte-and-window-functi","sec":"database-with-python-s057","q":"In a window function <code>RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY department_id ORDER BY salary DESC)</code>, what do <code>PARTITION BY</code> and <code>ORDER BY</code> do?","a":"<p><code>PARTITION BY</code> splits the rows into groups and restarts the ranking in each department. <code>ORDER BY</code> inside <code>OVER</code> defines the order used for ranking, here highest salary first. Without <code>PARTITION BY</code> the whole result is one group.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>SELECT first_name, salary,\n       RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY department_id ORDER BY salary DESC) AS salary_rank\nFROM employees;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0280","card":"q-database-with-python-056-mysql-8-0-features-cte-and-window-functi","sec":"database-with-python-s057","q":"Why does this MySQL query fail, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>Window functions are evaluated after <code>WHERE</code>, so <code>WHERE</code> cannot refer to their results or aliases. Wrap the query in a subquery or CTE and filter in the outer query: <code>WITH ranked AS (SELECT first_name, RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS r FROM employees) SELECT * FROM ranked WHERE r &lt;= 3;</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>SELECT first_name, RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS r\nFROM employees\nWHERE r &lt;= 3;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0281","card":"q-database-with-python-057-mysql-storage-engines","sec":"database-with-python-s058","q":"InnoDB vs MyISAM?","a":"<p>InnoDB is transactional with row-level locking. MyISAM is non-transactional with table-level locking.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0282","card":"q-database-with-python-057-mysql-storage-engines","sec":"database-with-python-s058","q":"Why is InnoDB the default storage engine in MySQL, and since which version?","a":"<p>Since MySQL 5.5 InnoDB is the default. It provides ACID transactions, row-level locking, foreign keys, MVCC (readers do not block writers) and crash recovery using a redo log. These are needed by almost every application. MyISAM lacks all of them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0283","card":"q-database-with-python-057-mysql-storage-engines","sec":"database-with-python-s058","q":"Besides InnoDB and MyISAM, which MySQL storage engines exist and what are they for?","a":"<p><code>MEMORY</code> keeps tables in RAM (fast, lost on restart, for temporary lookup data), <code>ARCHIVE</code> stores compressed insert-only data, <code>CSV</code> stores data in text files, and <code>BLACKHOLE</code> accepts writes and discards them (used in replication setups). For normal application data, use InnoDB.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0284","card":"q-database-with-python-057-mysql-storage-engines","sec":"database-with-python-s058","q":"What happens when you run <code>ALTER TABLE my_table ENGINE = MyISAM;</code> on a large table?","a":"<p>MySQL rebuilds the whole table in the new engine, which takes time and can block access. You lose transactions, row-level locking, foreign keys and crash safety. Converting to InnoDB is the common direction, and it should be tested on a copy first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0285","card":"q-database-with-python-057-mysql-storage-engines","sec":"database-with-python-s058","q":"In InnoDB, what does MVCC mean for readers and writers?","a":"<p>Multi-version concurrency control keeps older versions of changed rows in the undo log. A reader sees a consistent snapshot as of its transaction start, so plain <code>SELECT</code> statements do not wait for writers and writers do not wait for readers. Only writers touching the same row block each other.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0286","card":"q-database-with-python-058-mysql-replication-and-high-availability","sec":"database-with-python-s059","q":"How does replication help?","a":"<p>A primary-replica setup scales reads (read/write splitting). Be ready to discuss replication lag.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0287","card":"q-database-with-python-058-mysql-replication-and-high-availability","sec":"database-with-python-s059","q":"In MySQL replication, what do <code>SOURCE_LOG_FILE</code> and <code>SOURCE_LOG_POS</code> tell the replica?","a":"<p>They tell the replica where in the source's binary log to start reading: which log file and which byte position. The replica fetches events from there and applies them. With GTID-based replication you use <code>SOURCE_AUTO_POSITION=1</code> and the server finds the position itself. The <code>CHANGE REPLICATION SOURCE TO</code> statement exists from MySQL 8.0.23 and <code>START REPLICA</code> from 8.0.22; older versions use different keywords for the same commands.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>CHANGE REPLICATION SOURCE TO\n    SOURCE_HOST=&#x27;source_ip&#x27;,\n    SOURCE_USER=&#x27;repl_user&#x27;,\n    SOURCE_PASSWORD=&#x27;password&#x27;,\n    SOURCE_LOG_FILE=&#x27;mysql-bin.000001&#x27;,\n    SOURCE_LOG_POS=123;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0288","card":"q-database-with-python-058-mysql-replication-and-high-availability","sec":"database-with-python-s059","q":"What causes replication lag in MySQL, and how do you reduce it?","a":"<p>Common causes: a single-threaded applier on the replica, long transactions on the source, heavy write load, slow disks or network, and large batch statements. Reduce it with parallel replication (<code>replica_parallel_workers</code>, 8.0.26 and later), smaller transactions, faster hardware on replicas and avoiding bulk changes in one transaction. Monitor the lag value (<code>Seconds_Behind_Source</code>) in <code>SHOW REPLICA STATUS</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0289","card":"q-database-with-python-058-mysql-replication-and-high-availability","sec":"database-with-python-s059","q":"Your application writes a row and immediately reads it from a replica but does not see it. Why, and what do you do?","a":"<p>Replication is asynchronous by default, so the replica may not have applied the write yet (read-after-write inconsistency). Send reads that must see recent writes to the primary, or use sticky routing for a short period after a write, or wait for the replica to reach a position or GTID. Accept stale reads only where they are harmless.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0290","card":"q-database-with-python-058-mysql-replication-and-high-availability","sec":"database-with-python-s059","q":"What is the difference between asynchronous, semi-synchronous and group replication in MySQL?","a":"<p>Asynchronous: the source commits without waiting for replicas, which is fastest but can lose recent commits on failover. Semi-synchronous: the source waits until at least one replica has received (not necessarily applied) the event. Group Replication and InnoDB Cluster: a group of servers agree on each transaction through a consensus protocol and provide automatic failover, with higher write latency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0291","card":"q-database-with-python-058-mysql-replication-and-high-availability","sec":"database-with-python-s059","q":"Does a MySQL replica replace backups?","a":"<p>No. Replication copies every change, including mistakes: a <code>DROP TABLE</code> or bad <code>DELETE</code> on the source is replayed on the replica. Backups with point-in-time recovery are still required. A delayed replica can reduce the damage window but is not a backup.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0292","card":"q-database-with-python-058-mysql-replication-and-high-availability","sec":"database-with-python-s059","q":"What are the binary log formats in MySQL, and which is the safest for replication?","a":"<p><code>STATEMENT</code> logs the SQL text, <code>ROW</code> logs the changed row values and <code>MIXED</code> switches between them. <code>ROW</code> is the default since MySQL 5.7.7 and the safest, because non-deterministic statements (<code>UUID()</code>, <code>NOW()</code>, unordered <code>LIMIT</code>) produce the same data on the replica. It writes more log data for big updates.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0293","card":"q-database-with-python-059-mysql-recursive-ctes-and-advanced-window","sec":"database-with-python-s060","q":"When do you use a recursive CTE?","a":"<p>For tree-like data such as employee-manager relationships. Learn <code>RANK()</code>, <code>DENSE_RANK()</code>, <code>ROW_NUMBER()</code> and aggregate window functions.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0294","card":"q-database-with-python-059-mysql-recursive-ctes-and-advanced-window","sec":"database-with-python-s060","q":"In a recursive CTE, what are the anchor member and the recursive member, and when does the recursion stop?","a":"<p>The first <code>SELECT</code> (the anchor) returns the starting rows, here the top-level managers. The second <code>SELECT</code> (the recursive member) joins the table to the rows produced in the previous round, and repeats until a round returns no new rows. Each round adds the next level of the hierarchy.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>WITH RECURSIVE emp_hierarchy AS (\n    SELECT id, first_name, manager_id\n    FROM employees\n    WHERE manager_id IS NULL\n    UNION ALL\n    SELECT e.id, e.first_name, e.manager_id\n    FROM employees e\n    INNER JOIN emp_hierarchy eh ON e.manager_id = eh.id\n)\nSELECT * FROM emp_hierarchy;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0295","card":"q-database-with-python-059-mysql-recursive-ctes-and-advanced-window","sec":"database-with-python-s060","q":"In MySQL, what limits a recursive CTE that loops forever, for example because of a cycle in the data?","a":"<p>The <code>cte_max_recursion_depth</code> system variable, which defaults to 1000 in MySQL 8.0. When a recursion goes deeper, the query fails with an error. Fix the data or add a depth counter column and a condition such as <code>WHERE depth &lt; 10</code>. Do not simply raise the limit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0296","card":"q-database-with-python-059-mysql-recursive-ctes-and-advanced-window","sec":"database-with-python-s060","q":"What is the difference between <code>ROW_NUMBER()</code>, <code>RANK()</code> and <code>DENSE_RANK()</code> when two rows tie?","a":"<p>With salaries 100, 90, 90, 80: <code>ROW_NUMBER</code> gives 1, 2, 3, 4 (ties get different numbers, order undefined). <code>RANK</code> gives 1, 2, 2, 4 (ties share a rank and the next rank is skipped). <code>DENSE_RANK</code> gives 1, 2, 2, 3 (no gaps).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0297","card":"q-database-with-python-059-mysql-recursive-ctes-and-advanced-window","sec":"database-with-python-s060","q":"How do you get the top 3 earners in each department in MySQL 8.0?","a":"<p>Rank the rows in a subquery or CTE, then filter: <code>WITH r AS (SELECT first_name, department_id, salary, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY department_id ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rn FROM employees) SELECT * FROM r WHERE rn &lt;= 3;</code>. The filter has to be outside because window results cannot be used in <code>WHERE</code>. Use <code>DENSE_RANK</code> if ties should all be included.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0298","card":"q-database-with-python-059-mysql-recursive-ctes-and-advanced-window","sec":"database-with-python-s060","q":"How do you calculate a running total with a window function?","a":"<p>Add <code>ORDER BY</code> inside <code>OVER</code>: <code>SUM(amount) OVER (ORDER BY sale_date)</code>. Without a frame clause, the default frame runs from the first row to the current row (and includes rows that tie on the order key). Use <code>ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW</code> for strict row-by-row behavior.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0299","card":"q-database-with-python-059-mysql-recursive-ctes-and-advanced-window","sec":"database-with-python-s060","q":"What do <code>LAG()</code> and <code>LEAD()</code> do, and when are they used?","a":"<p>They return a value from a previous (<code>LAG</code>) or following (<code>LEAD</code>) row in the window order, without a self join. Use them to compare a row with the prior one: month-over-month change, time between events, or detecting gaps.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0300","card":"q-database-with-python-060-mysql-large-datasets-stored-procedure-er","sec":"database-with-python-s061","q":"How do you manage very large tables?","a":"<p>Delete in batches, and use partitioning or archiving. <strong>Q. How do you harden MySQL?</strong> Remove anonymous users, disable remote root login, use SSL, and handle errors in stored procedures so production failures roll back cleanly.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0301","card":"q-database-with-python-060-mysql-large-datasets-stored-procedure-er","sec":"database-with-python-s061","q":"In MySQL, why delete old rows in batches of 1000 instead of one huge <code>DELETE</code>?","a":"<p>A single huge delete holds locks for a long time, builds a very large undo log and transaction, can fill the disk, and makes replicas lag. Small batches, repeated until zero rows are affected (with a short pause in between), keep locks short and each commit small. An index on the filter column avoids scanning the table for every batch.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>DELETE FROM employees\nWHERE hire_date &lt; &#x27;2020-01-01&#x27;\nLIMIT 1000;   -- batch delete</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0302","card":"q-database-with-python-060-mysql-large-datasets-stored-procedure-er","sec":"database-with-python-s061","q":"What alternatives exist to deleting most rows of a huge table?","a":"<p>Create a new table, copy the rows you keep, and swap with <code>RENAME TABLE</code>. Or partition by date and drop old partitions, or archive old rows to another table or storage first. These avoid the cost of deleting millions of rows one by one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0303","card":"q-database-with-python-060-mysql-large-datasets-stored-procedure-er","sec":"database-with-python-s061","q":"In a MySQL stored procedure, what is the difference between an <code>EXIT</code> handler and a <code>CONTINUE</code> handler?","a":"<p>With <code>EXIT</code>, after the handler runs, execution leaves the enclosing <code>BEGIN ... END</code> block. With <code>CONTINUE</code>, execution carries on with the statement after the one that raised the error. For a transaction you normally want <code>EXIT</code>, with a <code>ROLLBACK</code> in the handler.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION\nBEGIN\n    ROLLBACK;\n    SELECT &#x27;Error occurred, transaction rolled back&#x27; AS message;\nEND;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0304","card":"q-database-with-python-060-mysql-large-datasets-stored-procedure-er","sec":"database-with-python-s061","q":"In a MySQL stored procedure, why is it better to re-raise the error with <code>RESIGNAL</code> than to return a message with <code>SELECT</code>?","a":"<p>Returning a message makes the call look successful to the client, so the application cannot tell it failed. <code>RESIGNAL</code> (or <code>SIGNAL</code>) raises the error again after the rollback, so the driver throws an exception and the caller can handle it, log it and retry.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0305","card":"q-database-with-python-060-mysql-large-datasets-stored-procedure-er","sec":"database-with-python-s061","q":"How do you limit network exposure of a MySQL server?","a":"<p>Set <code>bind-address</code> to a private interface (or localhost) instead of all interfaces, put the server in a private subnet behind a firewall or security group that only allows the application hosts on port 3306, and use accounts limited to specific hosts. Require encrypted connections with <code>require_secure_transport</code> or <code>REQUIRE SSL</code> on accounts. A database should never be open to the internet.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0306","card":"q-database-with-python-061-mysql-json-profiling-and-scaling","sec":"database-with-python-s062","q":"When do you use JSON columns?","a":"<p>For flexible schemas. Use profiling and the slow query log to find slow queries. <strong>Q. How does MySQL scale out?</strong> MySQL Cluster (NDB) or sharding, which has challenges such as data distribution. There is no single command for it.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0307","card":"q-database-with-python-061-mysql-json-profiling-and-scaling","sec":"database-with-python-s062","q":"In MySQL, what is the difference between <code>-&gt;</code> and <code>-&gt;&gt;</code> on a JSON column?","a":"<p><code>-&gt;</code> extracts a JSON value, so a string stays quoted (<code>\"John\"</code>). <code>-&gt;&gt;</code> extracts it and unquotes it, so you get the text <code>John</code>. Use <code>-&gt;&gt;</code> when comparing with normal strings or returning plain text. The path form is <code>'$.name'</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>SELECT details-&gt;&gt;&#x27;name&#x27; AS name FROM users;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0308","card":"q-database-with-python-061-mysql-json-profiling-and-scaling","sec":"database-with-python-s062","q":"In MySQL, how do you index a value inside a JSON column?","a":"<p>JSON itself cannot be indexed as a whole. Create a virtual column from the JSON path and index that, or in MySQL 8.0.13 and later create a functional index on the expression, for example <code>CREATE INDEX idx_name ON users ((CAST(details-&gt;&gt;'$.name' AS CHAR(50))));</code>. Without it, filters on JSON fields scan the table.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0309","card":"q-database-with-python-061-mysql-json-profiling-and-scaling","sec":"database-with-python-s062","q":"What are the drawbacks of storing data in a JSON column instead of normal columns?","a":"<p>You lose type checks, foreign keys and simple constraints, queries are harder to write and slower without a special index, and the schema becomes implicit in application code. Use JSON for sparse, varying attributes you rarely filter on. Keep the fields you query, join or constrain in real columns.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0310","card":"q-database-with-python-061-mysql-json-profiling-and-scaling","sec":"database-with-python-s062","q":"In MySQL, how do you find slow queries in production, and is <code>SET profiling</code> the recommended tool?","a":"<p>Turn on the slow query log (<code>slow_query_log = 1</code>, with <code>long_query_time</code>, default 10 seconds, set lower, for example 1) and review it with <code>pt-query-digest</code> or the Performance Schema. <code>SET profiling</code> and <code>SHOW PROFILES</code> still work but are deprecated since MySQL 5.6.7 in favor of the Performance Schema and <code>sys</code> schema.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0311","card":"q-database-with-python-061-mysql-json-profiling-and-scaling","sec":"database-with-python-s062","q":"How do you choose a shard key when you scale MySQL out by sharding?","a":"<p>Choose a key that spreads data and load evenly and that most queries include, such as a tenant id or user id, so a query touches one shard. A bad key creates hot shards or forces cross-shard queries. Cross-shard joins, global unique ids, distributed transactions and rebalancing are the hard parts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0312","card":"q-database-with-python-061-mysql-json-profiling-and-scaling","sec":"database-with-python-s062","q":"In what order do you usually scale a MySQL database before sharding it?","a":"<p>First fix queries and indexes, then add caching (for example Redis), scale up the server, add read replicas for read-heavy load, and archive old data. Sharding adds a lot of complexity, so it comes last, when a single primary cannot handle the write volume or data size.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0313","card":"q-database-with-python-062-postgresql-databases-and-schemas","sec":"database-with-python-s063","q":"Why use schemas?","a":"<p>They organize objects and help multi-tenant applications. Know the database creation options (ENCODING, TEMPLATE) and connection management.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0314","card":"q-database-with-python-062-postgresql-databases-and-schemas","sec":"database-with-python-s063","q":"In PostgreSQL, how does a schema differ from a database, and which can you query across in one statement?","a":"<p>A database is a separate unit: a connection is made to one database, and a plain query cannot read tables from another database (you need <code>dblink</code> or a foreign data wrapper). A schema is a namespace inside a database, so one query can join <code>sales.orders</code> with <code>public.customers</code>. Use schemas to group objects and databases to isolate applications.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0315","card":"q-database-with-python-062-postgresql-databases-and-schemas","sec":"database-with-python-s063","q":"In PostgreSQL, what does <code>SET search_path TO my_schema, public;</code> do?","a":"<p>It sets the list of schemas PostgreSQL searches, in order, when a table name is written without a schema. Unqualified <code>employees</code> resolves to <code>my_schema.employees</code> first, then <code>public.employees</code>. It is per session unless set on a role or database. Fully qualified names are safer in code, because a writable schema earlier in the path can shadow a trusted object.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0316","card":"q-database-with-python-062-postgresql-databases-and-schemas","sec":"database-with-python-s063","q":"In PostgreSQL, what is the difference between <code>DROP SCHEMA my_schema</code> and <code>DROP SCHEMA my_schema CASCADE</code>?","a":"<p>Plain <code>DROP SCHEMA</code> (same as <code>RESTRICT</code>) fails if the schema still contains objects. <code>CASCADE</code> drops the schema together with every table, view, function and other object inside it, and objects that depend on them elsewhere. It is destructive, so check what is inside first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0317","card":"q-database-with-python-062-postgresql-databases-and-schemas","sec":"database-with-python-s063","q":"What are the options for multi-tenant data in PostgreSQL: database per tenant, schema per tenant, or shared tables?","a":"<p>Database per tenant: strongest isolation, easy per-tenant backup, but many connections and hard cross-tenant reporting. Schema per tenant: good isolation with one connection pool, but thousands of schemas make migrations and catalog size painful. Shared tables with a <code>tenant_id</code> column: scales best and is simplest to run, but you must enforce isolation yourself, for example with row-level security.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0318","card":"q-database-with-python-062-postgresql-databases-and-schemas","sec":"database-with-python-s063","q":"In PostgreSQL, what are <code>template1</code> and <code>template0</code>, and when do you use <code>TEMPLATE template0</code>?","a":"<p><code>CREATE DATABASE</code> copies a template database. By default it copies <code>template1</code>, so anything installed in <code>template1</code> (extensions, objects) appears in every new database. <code>template0</code> is a clean, unmodified copy. Use <code>TEMPLATE template0</code> when you need a different <code>ENCODING</code> or locale than <code>template1</code> has, or a database without local additions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0319","card":"q-database-with-python-062-postgresql-databases-and-schemas","sec":"database-with-python-s063","q":"What changed about the <code>public</code> schema in PostgreSQL 15?","a":"<p>Before PostgreSQL 15 every user could create objects in the <code>public</code> schema by default. From PostgreSQL 15, the <code>CREATE</code> privilege on <code>public</code> is no longer granted to all users; only the database owner has it. Applications that relied on any role creating tables there must now be granted <code>CREATE</code>, or use their own schema.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0320","card":"q-database-with-python-063-postgresql-tables","sec":"database-with-python-s064","q":"In PostgreSQL, is there a performance difference between <code>VARCHAR(n)</code>, <code>VARCHAR</code> and <code>TEXT</code>?","a":"<p>No. They are stored the same way, and the length limit on <code>VARCHAR(n)</code> only adds a check. Use <code>TEXT</code> when there is no real business limit and <code>VARCHAR(n)</code> when the limit is part of the rule. This differs from some other databases where the types have different storage and index limits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0321","card":"q-database-with-python-063-postgresql-tables","sec":"database-with-python-s064","q":"In PostgreSQL, why might <code>ALTER TABLE employees ALTER COLUMN email TYPE VARCHAR(150);</code> need a <code>USING</code> clause, and what does it cost?","a":"<p>When the new type cannot be converted implicitly, such as <code>TEXT</code> to <code>INTEGER</code>, PostgreSQL needs <code>USING column::integer</code> to define the conversion. Many type changes rewrite the whole table while holding an exclusive lock. Increasing the length of a <code>VARCHAR</code> does not rewrite the table in current versions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0322","card":"q-database-with-python-063-postgresql-tables","sec":"database-with-python-s064","q":"In PostgreSQL 11 and later, what makes <code>ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN ... DEFAULT ...</code> fast on a large table?","a":"<p>From PostgreSQL 11, adding a column with a constant default no longer rewrites the table. The default is stored in the catalog and applied when old rows are read. Before version 11, it rewrote every row and held a lock for the whole time. Volatile defaults such as <code>random()</code> still need a rewrite.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0323","card":"q-database-with-python-063-postgresql-tables","sec":"database-with-python-s064","q":"In PostgreSQL, what does <code>DROP TABLE employees CASCADE</code> remove besides the table?","a":"<p>It also drops objects that depend on the table, such as views and foreign key constraints on other tables that reference it. It does not delete the rows of those other tables. Without <code>CASCADE</code>, the drop fails if something depends on the table, which is the safer default.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0324","card":"q-database-with-python-063-postgresql-tables","sec":"database-with-python-s064","q":"In PostgreSQL, what is a <code>CHECK</code> constraint, and why use it when the application already validates input?","a":"<p>A <code>CHECK</code> constraint rejects rows that break a condition, for example <code>CHECK (salary &gt;= 0)</code>. Application validation can be bypassed by other services, scripts and manual fixes, so the database enforces the rule for every writer. It cannot reference other tables.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0325","card":"q-database-with-python-063-postgresql-tables","sec":"database-with-python-s064","q":"How do <code>TRUNCATE</code> and <code>DELETE</code> differ in PostgreSQL regarding transactions?","a":"<p>Unlike MySQL, <code>TRUNCATE</code> in PostgreSQL is transactional, so you can <code>ROLLBACK</code> it. It takes an exclusive lock, is much faster than <code>DELETE</code> on a big table, and with <code>RESTART IDENTITY</code> resets sequences. It needs <code>CASCADE</code> to empty tables that are referenced by foreign keys.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0326","card":"q-database-with-python-063-postgresql-tables","sec":"database-with-python-s064","q":"How can you inspect table columns from SQL instead of using <code>\\d employees</code> in <code>psql</code>?","a":"<p>Query the standard <code>information_schema.columns</code> view, for example <code>SELECT column_name, data_type FROM information_schema.columns WHERE table_name = 'employees';</code>, or the PostgreSQL catalogs such as <code>pg_catalog.pg_attribute</code>. <code>\\d</code> is a <code>psql</code> client command, so it does not work through a Python driver.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0327","card":"q-database-with-python-064-postgresql-dml-and-queries","sec":"database-with-python-s065","q":"In PostgreSQL, how do you insert a row, or update it if it already exists?","a":"<p>Use an upsert: <code>INSERT INTO employees (id, salary) VALUES (1, 52000) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET salary = EXCLUDED.salary;</code>. <code>EXCLUDED</code> refers to the row that failed to insert. <code>ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING</code> skips the duplicate. Available since PostgreSQL 9.5, and it needs a unique index or constraint on the conflict target.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0328","card":"q-database-with-python-064-postgresql-dml-and-queries","sec":"database-with-python-s065","q":"In PostgreSQL, what does the <code>RETURNING</code> clause do, and which statements support it?","a":"<p>It returns column values of the affected rows from the same statement. <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code> and <code>DELETE</code> all support it, for example <code>DELETE FROM employees WHERE id = 1 RETURNING first_name;</code>. It avoids a second query to read assigned ids or to see what was removed, and it is atomic with the change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0329","card":"q-database-with-python-064-postgresql-dml-and-queries","sec":"database-with-python-s065","q":"In PostgreSQL, how do NULL values sort with <code>ORDER BY salary DESC</code>, and how do you control it?","a":"<p>In PostgreSQL, NULL is treated as larger than any value, so it comes first in <code>DESC</code> order and last in <code>ASC</code> order. MySQL does the opposite. Use <code>ORDER BY salary DESC NULLS LAST</code> to place them explicitly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0330","card":"q-database-with-python-064-postgresql-dml-and-queries","sec":"database-with-python-s065","q":"Are string comparisons case-sensitive in PostgreSQL, and how do you search case-insensitively?","a":"<p>Yes, <code>WHERE name = 'john'</code> does not match <code>John</code>. Use <code>ILIKE</code>, <code>LOWER(name) = 'john'</code> (ideally with an expression index), or the <code>citext</code> extension. MySQL default collations are case-insensitive, so queries ported from MySQL can return fewer rows.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0331","card":"q-database-with-python-064-postgresql-dml-and-queries","sec":"database-with-python-s065","q":"In PostgreSQL, how do you get the highest paid employee of each department with <code>DISTINCT ON</code>?","a":"<p><code>SELECT DISTINCT ON (department_id) department_id, first_name, salary FROM employees ORDER BY department_id, salary DESC;</code>. <code>DISTINCT ON</code> keeps the first row for each department according to the <code>ORDER BY</code>, so the salary order decides which row is kept. It is PostgreSQL-specific and shorter than a window function with <code>ROW_NUMBER()</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0332","card":"q-database-with-python-064-postgresql-dml-and-queries","sec":"database-with-python-s065","q":"In PostgreSQL, how do you update a table using values from another table?","a":"<p>Use <code>UPDATE ... FROM</code>: <code>UPDATE employees e SET salary = e.salary * 1.1 FROM departments d WHERE e.department_id = d.department_id AND d.department_name = 'Sales';</code>. The <code>FROM</code> table is joined to the target through the <code>WHERE</code> condition. MySQL uses <code>UPDATE employees e JOIN departments d ON ... SET ...</code> instead.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0333","card":"q-database-with-python-065-postgresql-indexes","sec":"database-with-python-s066","q":"B-tree vs GIN?","a":"<p>B-tree is the default for equality and range lookups. GIN suits JSONB and full-text data. Both affect performance.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0334","card":"q-database-with-python-065-postgresql-indexes","sec":"database-with-python-s066","q":"In PostgreSQL, why use <code>GIN</code> for a <code>JSONB</code> column, and which kind of query does it speed up?","a":"<p>A GIN index is an inverted index: it maps each key or value inside the document to the rows that contain it. It speeds up containment and existence operators such as <code>details @&gt; '{\"city\": \"Paris\"}'</code> and <code>details ? 'email'</code>. It does not help with <code>details-&gt;&gt;'name' = 'John'</code>; that needs a B-tree expression index on <code>(details-&gt;&gt;'name')</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>CREATE INDEX idx_details ON users USING GIN(details);   -- JSONB</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0335","card":"q-database-with-python-065-postgresql-indexes","sec":"database-with-python-s066","q":"Which index types does PostgreSQL offer, and when would you pick each?","a":"<p>B-tree (default): equality, range and sorting. Hash: equality only, rarely needed. GIN: arrays, JSONB, full-text. GiST: geometry, ranges and nearest-neighbor search. SP-GiST: partitioned search structures such as tries. BRIN: very large tables where values correlate with physical order, such as append-only timestamps, with a tiny index.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0336","card":"q-database-with-python-065-postgresql-indexes","sec":"database-with-python-s066","q":"In PostgreSQL, why use <code>CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY</code> on a live table?","a":"<p>A normal <code>CREATE INDEX</code> blocks writes to the table while it builds. <code>CONCURRENTLY</code> allows inserts, updates and deletes during the build, at the cost of taking longer and doing two table scans. It cannot run inside a transaction block, and if it fails it leaves an invalid index that you must drop.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0337","card":"q-database-with-python-065-postgresql-indexes","sec":"database-with-python-s066","q":"In PostgreSQL, what is an index-only scan, and what does it need?","a":"<p>The query is answered from the index alone, without reading the table. All needed columns must be in the index, and the visibility map must show the table pages as all-visible, which <code>VACUUM</code> maintains. A table with many recent changes may still read the heap. Since PostgreSQL 11, <code>CREATE INDEX ... INCLUDE (col)</code> adds non-key columns to make covering indexes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0338","card":"q-database-with-python-065-postgresql-indexes","sec":"database-with-python-s066","q":"How do you find unused indexes in PostgreSQL, and why remove them?","a":"<p>Check <code>pg_stat_user_indexes.idx_scan</code>: indexes with a count of 0 over a long period (and that do not enforce a constraint) are candidates. Every index slows <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code> and <code>DELETE</code>, uses disk and memory, and adds vacuum work. Check replicas too, since the statistics are per server.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0339","card":"q-database-with-python-065-postgresql-indexes","sec":"database-with-python-s066","q":"In PostgreSQL, why might the planner ignore an index you created?","a":"<p>The table may be small or the condition may match a large fraction of rows, so a sequential scan is cheaper. Statistics may be stale (run <code>ANALYZE</code>). The query may apply a function or a cast to the column, or the types differ. For <code>LIKE 'abc%'</code> on a non-C locale you need the <code>text_pattern_ops</code> operator class. Use <code>EXPLAIN</code> to see the real plan.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0340","card":"q-database-with-python-066-postgresql-roles-and-privileges","sec":"database-with-python-s067","q":"In PostgreSQL, what is the difference between a user and a role?","a":"<p>There is no difference in the engine: users and groups are both roles. <code>CREATE USER</code> is the same as <code>CREATE ROLE ... LOGIN</code>. A role without <code>LOGIN</code> is used as a group that you grant to other roles, and privileges can be inherited from it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0341","card":"q-database-with-python-066-postgresql-roles-and-privileges","sec":"database-with-python-s067","q":"In PostgreSQL, why does <code>SELECT</code> on a table fail with \"permission denied for schema\" even after <code>GRANT SELECT ON employees TO new_user</code>?","a":"<p>Table privileges are not enough. The role also needs <code>USAGE</code> on the schema that contains the table: <code>GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA public TO new_user;</code>. Without it, the schema cannot be looked into.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0342","card":"q-database-with-python-066-postgresql-roles-and-privileges","sec":"database-with-python-s067","q":"In PostgreSQL, why do new tables not become readable for a role after <code>GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public</code>?","a":"<p>That statement only applies to tables that exist at that moment. For tables created later, set <code>ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES IN SCHEMA public GRANT SELECT ON TABLES TO new_user;</code>, which applies to objects created by the role that ran it (or the role named with <code>FOR ROLE</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0343","card":"q-database-with-python-066-postgresql-roles-and-privileges","sec":"database-with-python-s067","q":"What is <code>pg_hba.conf</code> in PostgreSQL, and how does it relate to roles and privileges?","a":"<p>It is the host-based authentication file that decides which users may connect, to which databases, from which addresses, and with which method (such as <code>scram-sha-256</code> or <code>cert</code>). It controls who may connect, while <code>GRANT</code> controls what they can do afterward. A correct role with a wrong <code>pg_hba.conf</code> rule still cannot connect.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0344","card":"q-database-with-python-066-postgresql-roles-and-privileges","sec":"database-with-python-s067","q":"How are PostgreSQL passwords stored, and what changed in version 14?","a":"<p>The server stores a hash, not the password. Up to PostgreSQL 13 the default <code>password_encryption</code> was <code>md5</code>; from PostgreSQL 14 it is <code>scram-sha-256</code>, which is stronger. Old md5 hashes keep working until the password is reset, and the client driver must support SCRAM.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0345","card":"q-database-with-python-066-postgresql-roles-and-privileges","sec":"database-with-python-s067","q":"What is row-level security in PostgreSQL, and when is it useful?","a":"<p>With <code>ALTER TABLE ... ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY</code> and <code>CREATE POLICY</code>, the database filters which rows each role can see or change, for example <code>USING (tenant_id = current_setting('app.tenant_id')::int)</code>. It suits multi-tenant tables where isolation must hold even if application code forgets a filter. Table owners bypass it unless it is forced, and superusers always bypass it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0346","card":"q-database-with-python-066-postgresql-roles-and-privileges","sec":"database-with-python-s067","q":"What happens when you try to <code>DROP ROLE</code> for a role that owns objects in PostgreSQL?","a":"<p>The drop fails because the role still owns tables or has privileges. First reassign its objects with <code>REASSIGN OWNED BY old_role TO new_role;</code>, then run <code>DROP OWNED BY old_role;</code> to remove its remaining privileges, and then <code>DROP ROLE</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0347","card":"q-database-with-python-067-postgresql-transactions-and-isolation","sec":"database-with-python-s068","q":"What are the isolation levels?","a":"<p>Compare READ COMMITTED, REPEATABLE READ and SERIALIZABLE and their trade-offs, and explain ACID.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0348","card":"q-database-with-python-067-postgresql-transactions-and-isolation","sec":"database-with-python-s068","q":"In PostgreSQL, does this code run the transaction at the <code>SERIALIZABLE</code> level, and if not, how do you fix it?","a":"<p>No. <code>SET TRANSACTION</code> only applies to the current transaction block, so outside one it has no effect and PostgreSQL raises a warning. Start the transaction first: <code>BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;</code>, or run <code>SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;</code> after <code>BEGIN</code>. To change the default for a session, use <code>SET SESSION CHARACTERISTICS AS TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL ...</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;\nBEGIN;\nSELECT * FROM employees WHERE salary &gt; 60000;\nCOMMIT;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0349","card":"q-database-with-python-067-postgresql-transactions-and-isolation","sec":"database-with-python-s068","q":"What is the default isolation level in PostgreSQL, and how does it treat <code>READ UNCOMMITTED</code>?","a":"<p>The default is <code>READ COMMITTED</code>: each statement sees the data committed before it started. PostgreSQL accepts <code>READ UNCOMMITTED</code> but treats it as <code>READ COMMITTED</code>, so dirty reads never happen.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0350","card":"q-database-with-python-067-postgresql-transactions-and-isolation","sec":"database-with-python-s068","q":"In PostgreSQL, how does <code>REPEATABLE READ</code> differ from the SQL standard, and what must the application do?","a":"<p>PostgreSQL implements it with a snapshot taken at the first statement, so it prevents dirty reads, non-repeatable reads and phantom reads. If two concurrent transactions update the same row, the later one fails with a serialization error (SQLSTATE <code>40001</code>). Code using <code>REPEATABLE READ</code> or <code>SERIALIZABLE</code> must retry the whole transaction on that error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0351","card":"q-database-with-python-067-postgresql-transactions-and-isolation","sec":"database-with-python-s068","q":"How does MVCC work in PostgreSQL, and why do readers not block writers?","a":"<p>An <code>UPDATE</code> does not overwrite a row. It creates a new row version, and each version has transaction ids (<code>xmin</code> and <code>xmax</code>) that define who can see it. A transaction reads the versions visible in its snapshot, so readers never wait for writers. Old versions pile up until <code>VACUUM</code> removes them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0352","card":"q-database-with-python-067-postgresql-transactions-and-isolation","sec":"database-with-python-s068","q":"In PostgreSQL, you run a statement that fails inside a transaction, and every later statement returns \"current transaction is aborted\". What do you do?","a":"<p>PostgreSQL marks the whole transaction as failed after an error, and it only accepts <code>ROLLBACK</code>. Roll back and retry. To continue after an expected error inside the same transaction, wrap the risky statement in <code>SAVEPOINT</code> and <code>ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT</code>. In <code>psycopg2</code>, call <code>conn.rollback()</code> after an exception.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0353","card":"q-database-with-python-067-postgresql-transactions-and-isolation","sec":"database-with-python-s068","q":"How do you let several workers take jobs from a PostgreSQL table without picking the same row?","a":"<p>Use <code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED LIMIT 1</code> inside a transaction. The first worker locks a row, and other workers skip locked rows and take the next one, so no one blocks and no job is taken twice. It turns a table into a simple queue.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0354","card":"q-database-with-python-068-postgresql-views-and-materialized-views","sec":"database-with-python-s069","q":"View vs materialized view?","a":"<p>A view is computed dynamically on each query. A materialized view stores (caches) the result until you refresh it.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0355","card":"q-database-with-python-068-postgresql-views-and-materialized-views","sec":"database-with-python-s069","q":"In PostgreSQL, why use <code>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY</code>, and what does it require?","a":"<p>A plain refresh locks the materialized view against reads while it rebuilds. <code>CONCURRENTLY</code> rebuilds in the background and keeps the old data readable. It requires at least one unique index on the materialized view and takes longer than a normal refresh.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0356","card":"q-database-with-python-068-postgresql-views-and-materialized-views","sec":"database-with-python-s069","q":"In PostgreSQL, what is the difference between <code>WITH DATA</code> and <code>WITH NO DATA</code> when you create a materialized view?","a":"<p><code>WITH DATA</code> (the default) runs the query and fills the view immediately. <code>WITH NO DATA</code> creates only the definition, and reading the view fails until the first <code>REFRESH</code>. Use <code>NO DATA</code> to create objects quickly in a migration and populate later.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0357","card":"q-database-with-python-068-postgresql-views-and-materialized-views","sec":"database-with-python-s069","q":"How do you keep a PostgreSQL materialized view reasonably fresh?","a":"<p>PostgreSQL does not refresh it automatically. Schedule <code>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW</code> with <code>cron</code> or the <code>pg_cron</code> extension, or trigger a refresh from the application after a batch load. The choice depends on how stale the data may be. If you need real-time results, use a normal view or maintain a summary table with triggers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0358","card":"q-database-with-python-068-postgresql-views-and-materialized-views","sec":"database-with-python-s069","q":"Can you create an index on a PostgreSQL view or a materialized view?","a":"<p>You can index a materialized view, because it stores rows, and this is a main reason to use one. You cannot index a normal view, since it stores no data; the query uses indexes on the base tables.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0359","card":"q-database-with-python-068-postgresql-views-and-materialized-views","sec":"database-with-python-s069","q":"Can you insert into a PostgreSQL view?","a":"<p>Simple views over a single table, without aggregates, <code>DISTINCT</code>, or set operations, are automatically updatable since PostgreSQL 9.3. For more complex views, define <code>INSTEAD OF</code> triggers. A materialized view can never be updated directly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0360","card":"q-database-with-python-069-postgresql-functions-procedures-and-trig","sec":"database-with-python-s070","q":"In PostgreSQL, what is the difference between a function and a procedure, and how do you call each?","a":"<p>A function returns a value and is called inside a query, such as <code>SELECT calculate_bonus(salary) FROM employees</code>. A procedure is called with <code>CALL update_salary(1, 500)</code> and returns no value (except <code>OUT</code> parameters). Procedures, added in PostgreSQL 11, can run <code>COMMIT</code> and <code>ROLLBACK</code> inside the body, which functions cannot.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0361","card":"q-database-with-python-069-postgresql-functions-procedures-and-trig","sec":"database-with-python-s070","q":"In PostgreSQL function definitions, why is the body wrapped in <code>$$ ... $$</code>?","a":"<p>The body is passed as a string. Dollar quoting lets you write single quotes and semicolons inside it without escaping them. You can use a named tag such as <code>$body$</code> when the body itself contains <code>$$</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>RETURNS NUMERIC AS $$\nBEGIN\n    RETURN salary * 0.1;\nEND;\n$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0362","card":"q-database-with-python-069-postgresql-functions-procedures-and-trig","sec":"database-with-python-s070","q":"In a PostgreSQL trigger function, why does it <code>RETURN NEW</code>, and what happens if it returns <code>NULL</code>?","a":"<p>For a row-level <code>BEFORE</code> trigger, the returned row is the one that gets written, so <code>RETURN NEW</code> lets the change go ahead (possibly modified), and <code>RETURN NULL</code> silently cancels it for that row. For an <code>AFTER</code> row trigger the return value is ignored, but returning <code>NEW</code> is the convention.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>INSERT INTO audit_log (action, employee_id) VALUES (&#x27;INSERT&#x27;, NEW.id);\nRETURN NEW;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0363","card":"q-database-with-python-069-postgresql-functions-procedures-and-trig","sec":"database-with-python-s070","q":"How is defining a trigger in PostgreSQL different from MySQL?","a":"<p>PostgreSQL separates the logic from the binding: you create a trigger function that returns <code>trigger</code>, then <code>CREATE TRIGGER ... EXECUTE FUNCTION fn()</code> attaches it. One function can serve many tables. MySQL puts the body directly inside <code>CREATE TRIGGER</code>. PostgreSQL also supports statement-level triggers (<code>FOR EACH STATEMENT</code>) and <code>INSTEAD OF</code> triggers on views.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0364","card":"q-database-with-python-069-postgresql-functions-procedures-and-trig","sec":"database-with-python-s070","q":"In PostgreSQL 11 and later, which keyword attaches a trigger function: <code>EXECUTE FUNCTION</code> or <code>EXECUTE PROCEDURE</code>?","a":"<p><code>EXECUTE FUNCTION</code>. Before PostgreSQL 11, the syntax was <code>EXECUTE PROCEDURE</code>, because procedures did not exist yet. The old keyword is still accepted for compatibility, but new code should use <code>EXECUTE FUNCTION</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0365","card":"q-database-with-python-069-postgresql-functions-procedures-and-trig","sec":"database-with-python-s070","q":"In PostgreSQL, what do the function volatility labels <code>IMMUTABLE</code>, <code>STABLE</code> and <code>VOLATILE</code> mean, and why do they matter?","a":"<p><code>IMMUTABLE</code>: same inputs always give the same result (can be used in an index expression). <code>STABLE</code>: the same result within one statement, but may change between statements, such as functions that read tables. <code>VOLATILE</code> (the default): the result can change on every call, such as <code>random()</code>. The planner uses the label to cache, inline and use indexes, so a wrong label gives wrong results.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0366","card":"q-database-with-python-069-postgresql-functions-procedures-and-trig","sec":"database-with-python-s070","q":"Why might you write a PostgreSQL function in <code>LANGUAGE sql</code> instead of <code>plpgsql</code>?","a":"<p>A simple <code>sql</code> function that is one query can be inlined by the planner, so there is no call overhead and the optimizer sees through it. <code>plpgsql</code> is needed for variables, loops, conditionals and exception handling. Choose the simplest language that does the job.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0367","card":"q-database-with-python-070-postgresql-performance-backup-and-jsonb","sec":"database-with-python-s071","q":"In PostgreSQL, what is the difference between <code>EXPLAIN</code> and <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code>, and what is the danger of the second?","a":"<p><code>EXPLAIN</code> shows the planned strategy with estimated costs and rows. <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> actually executes the statement and adds real timings and row counts. Because it executes, running it on an <code>UPDATE</code> or <code>DELETE</code> changes data. Wrap it in <code>BEGIN; ... ROLLBACK;</code> to measure without keeping the change.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE SELECT * FROM employees WHERE salary &gt; 50000;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0368","card":"q-database-with-python-070-postgresql-performance-backup-and-jsonb","sec":"database-with-python-s071","q":"When reading a PostgreSQL query plan, what do you look for?","a":"<p>Scan types (a <code>Seq Scan</code> on a big table with a selective filter suggests a missing index), large gaps between estimated and actual rows (stale statistics), expensive sorts or hashes that spill to disk, nested loops over large inputs, and the node with the highest actual time. Fix the cause: index, statistics, query rewrite or memory settings such as <code>work_mem</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0369","card":"q-database-with-python-070-postgresql-performance-backup-and-jsonb","sec":"database-with-python-s071","q":"Why does PostgreSQL need <code>VACUUM</code>?","a":"<p>Because of MVCC, <code>UPDATE</code> and <code>DELETE</code> leave dead row versions in the table. <code>VACUUM</code> marks that space reusable, updates the visibility map and prevents transaction id wraparound. Without it, tables and indexes bloat and queries slow down. Autovacuum runs it in the background, and busy tables may need a more aggressive setting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0370","card":"q-database-with-python-070-postgresql-performance-backup-and-jsonb","sec":"database-with-python-s071","q":"What is the difference between <code>VACUUM</code>, <code>VACUUM FULL</code> and <code>ANALYZE</code>?","a":"<p><code>VACUUM</code> frees dead space for reuse without blocking normal reads and writes, but usually does not shrink the file. <code>VACUUM FULL</code> rewrites the table to return space to the operating system and takes an exclusive lock, so avoid it on a live system. <code>ANALYZE</code> updates planner statistics. <code>VACUUM ANALYZE</code> does a plain vacuum and an analyze in one command.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>VACUUM ANALYZE employees;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0371","card":"q-database-with-python-070-postgresql-performance-backup-and-jsonb","sec":"database-with-python-s071","q":"In PostgreSQL, what is the difference between <code>JSON</code> and <code>JSONB</code>?","a":"<p><code>JSON</code> stores the exact input text, keeping whitespace, key order and duplicate keys, and parses it on every access. <code>JSONB</code> stores a decomposed binary form: slightly slower to write, but faster to query, it removes duplicate keys, does not keep key order, and supports GIN indexes. Use <code>JSONB</code> unless you must keep the original text.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0372","card":"q-database-with-python-070-postgresql-performance-backup-and-jsonb","sec":"database-with-python-s071","q":"In PostgreSQL, what does the <code>@&gt;</code> operator do on a <code>JSONB</code> column?","a":"<p>It tests containment: <code>details @&gt; '{\"name\": \"John\"}'</code> is true when the left document contains the right one. It is the operator a GIN index on the column accelerates. <code>-&gt;</code> returns a JSON value, <code>-&gt;&gt;</code> returns text.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0373","card":"q-database-with-python-070-postgresql-performance-backup-and-jsonb","sec":"database-with-python-s071","q":"In PostgreSQL, what is the difference between <code>pg_dump</code> formats, and how do you restore each?","a":"<p>The plain format here is a SQL script that you restore with <code>psql</code>. The custom format (<code>pg_dump -Fc</code>) is compressed and lets <code>pg_restore</code> restore selected tables and in parallel (<code>-j</code>). <code>pg_dump</code> takes a consistent snapshot without blocking writers, but it is a logical backup of one database; use <code>pg_dumpall</code> for roles and global objects.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pg_dump -U postgres my_database &gt; backup.sql\npsql -U postgres my_database &lt; backup.sql</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0374","card":"q-database-with-python-070-postgresql-performance-backup-and-jsonb","sec":"database-with-python-s071","q":"How do you achieve point-in-time recovery in PostgreSQL?","a":"<p>Take a physical base backup with <code>pg_basebackup</code> and keep archiving the write-ahead log (WAL) segments. To recover, restore the base backup and replay the WAL up to a chosen timestamp or transaction (<code>recovery_target_time</code>). A <code>pg_dump</code> alone cannot do this, because it captures only one moment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0375","card":"q-database-with-python-071-postgresql-recursive-ctes-replication-an","sec":"database-with-python-s072","q":"Streaming vs logical replication?","a":"<p>Be able to explain both. Popular extensions include PostGIS and UUID-OSSP.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0376","card":"q-database-with-python-071-postgresql-recursive-ctes-replication-an","sec":"database-with-python-s072","q":"In PostgreSQL logical replication, what do <code>CREATE PUBLICATION</code> and <code>CREATE SUBSCRIPTION</code> do?","a":"<p>The publisher creates a publication, which names the tables whose row changes are sent. The subscriber creates a subscription with a connection string, which copies the initial data and then applies the changes continuously. It works per table, can cross major versions, and lets the subscriber have other tables and indexes.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>CREATE PUBLICATION my_pub FOR TABLE employees;\nCREATE SUBSCRIPTION my_sub\nCONNECTION &#x27;host=subscriber_ip dbname=my_database user=rep_user password=password&#x27;\nPUBLICATION my_pub;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0377","card":"q-database-with-python-071-postgresql-recursive-ctes-replication-an","sec":"database-with-python-s072","q":"What must be set up before PostgreSQL logical replication works?","a":"<p>The publisher needs <code>wal_level = logical</code>, a role with the <code>REPLICATION</code> privilege and access allowed in <code>pg_hba.conf</code>. The tables must already exist on the subscriber with matching columns, because the schema is not replicated. Tables need a primary key (or a replica identity) for updates and deletes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0378","card":"q-database-with-python-071-postgresql-recursive-ctes-replication-an","sec":"database-with-python-s072","q":"What does logical replication in PostgreSQL not replicate?","a":"<p>DDL statements (schema changes), sequence values and large objects. You must apply schema changes on both sides yourself, usually subscriber first for added columns. Physical streaming replication copies everything because it ships WAL byte for byte.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0379","card":"q-database-with-python-071-postgresql-recursive-ctes-replication-an","sec":"database-with-python-s072","q":"What is a replication slot in PostgreSQL, and what is its risk?","a":"<p>A replication slot makes the primary keep the WAL that a replica or subscriber has not yet consumed, so the consumer can reconnect without losing changes. If the consumer is down for a long time, WAL piles up and can fill the disk of the primary. Monitor <code>pg_replication_slots</code> and drop unused slots.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0380","card":"q-database-with-python-071-postgresql-recursive-ctes-replication-an","sec":"database-with-python-s072","q":"What does <code>CREATE EXTENSION postgis;</code> do in PostgreSQL, and what must be present first?","a":"<p>It loads an extension's objects (types, functions, operators) into the current database, so extensions are per database. The extension files must already be installed on the server host, and creating many extensions needs privileges. <code>\\dx</code> lists installed ones. Common examples: <code>pg_stat_statements</code>, <code>pgcrypto</code>, <code>postgis</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0381","card":"q-database-with-python-071-postgresql-recursive-ctes-replication-an","sec":"database-with-python-s072","q":"In PostgreSQL, do you still need the <code>uuid-ossp</code> extension to create UUIDs?","a":"<p>Not in PostgreSQL 13 and later: <code>gen_random_uuid()</code> is built in. In earlier versions it needed the <code>pgcrypto</code> extension, and <code>uuid-ossp</code> provides <code>uuid_generate_v4()</code> and other UUID versions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0382","card":"q-database-with-python-071-postgresql-recursive-ctes-replication-an","sec":"database-with-python-s072","q":"In PostgreSQL 14 and later, how do you stop a recursive CTE from looping forever on cyclic data?","a":"<p>Add the <code>CYCLE</code> clause, for example <code>CYCLE id SET is_cycle USING path</code>, which marks rows that revisit an id and stops following them. Before version 14, keep an array of visited ids in the CTE and test <code>NOT id = ANY(path)</code>. Unlike MySQL, PostgreSQL has no recursion depth limit variable, so a cycle with <code>UNION ALL</code> runs until memory or disk runs out.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0383","card":"q-database-with-python-072-postgresql-interview-queries","sec":"database-with-python-s073","q":"PostgreSQL vs MySQL talking points?","a":"<p>JSONB, window functions and extensibility. Also be ready to explain MVCC (Multiversion Concurrency Control), handle NULLs, and optimize slow queries. For DBA roles stress replication, vacuuming and configuration tuning. For developer roles stress complex queries and JSONB. Practise 20 to 30 SQL problems on LeetCode or HackerRank, and test commands on a local instance (Docker works).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0384","card":"q-database-with-python-072-postgresql-interview-queries","sec":"database-with-python-s073","q":"In PostgreSQL, what is the pitfall of this second-highest-salary query when the <code>salary</code> column contains NULL values?","a":"<p>In PostgreSQL, NULL sorts first in <code>DESC</code> order, so a NULL can take the first position and the query returns the highest real salary instead of the second one. Filter with <code>WHERE salary IS NOT NULL</code> or use <code>ORDER BY salary DESC NULLS LAST</code>. If there are fewer than two distinct salaries, the query returns no row.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>SELECT DISTINCT salary\nFROM employees\nORDER BY salary DESC\nOFFSET 1 LIMIT 1;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0385","card":"q-database-with-python-072-postgresql-interview-queries","sec":"database-with-python-s073","q":"In PostgreSQL, why do two employees with the same salary show the same cumulative salary in this query?","a":"<p>With <code>ORDER BY</code> and no frame, the default frame is <code>RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW</code>, and rows that tie on the order key count as peers, so they are all added at once. For a strict running total per row, add <code>ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW</code> or order by a unique column.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>SELECT first_name, salary,\n       SUM(salary) OVER (PARTITION BY department_id ORDER BY salary) AS cumulative_salary\nFROM employees;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0386","card":"q-database-with-python-072-postgresql-interview-queries","sec":"database-with-python-s073","q":"Why does <code>WHERE salary = NULL</code> return no rows in PostgreSQL, and what do you use?","a":"<p>Any comparison with NULL gives unknown, which a <code>WHERE</code> clause treats as false. Use <code>IS NULL</code> and <code>IS NOT NULL</code>. For a null-safe comparison, use <code>IS DISTINCT FROM</code> (treats two NULLs as equal). <code>COALESCE(salary, 0)</code> replaces NULL with a default.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0387","card":"q-database-with-python-072-postgresql-interview-queries","sec":"database-with-python-s073","q":"In PostgreSQL, how do you get the percentage of the department payroll that each employee's salary represents?","a":"<p>Divide by a window sum: <code>SELECT first_name, salary, 100.0 * salary / SUM(salary) OVER (PARTITION BY department_id) AS pct FROM employees;</code>. The window function keeps every row and adds the department total, so no self join is needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0388","card":"q-database-with-python-073-postgresql-partial-and-expression-indexe","sec":"database-with-python-s074","q":"When do these help?","a":"<p>Partial indexes cover only the rows you filter on. Expression indexes support queries such as case-insensitive search.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0389","card":"q-database-with-python-073-postgresql-partial-and-expression-indexe","sec":"database-with-python-s074","q":"In PostgreSQL, when can the planner use this partial index?","a":"<p>Only when the query's <code>WHERE</code> clause implies the index condition. <code>WHERE salary &gt; 70000</code> can use it, <code>WHERE salary &gt; 50000</code> cannot, because it asks for rows that are not in the index. A partial index is smaller and cheaper to maintain, so it fits queries that target a small, stable subset.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>CREATE INDEX idx_high_salary ON employees(salary) WHERE salary &gt; 60000;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0390","card":"q-database-with-python-073-postgresql-partial-and-expression-indexe","sec":"database-with-python-s074","q":"How do you enforce \"unique email among active users\" in PostgreSQL when deleted users keep their rows?","a":"<p>Use a partial unique index: <code>CREATE UNIQUE INDEX uq_active_email ON users(email) WHERE deleted_at IS NULL;</code>. Only active rows are checked for uniqueness, so a deleted user's email can be registered again.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0391","card":"q-database-with-python-073-postgresql-partial-and-expression-indexe","sec":"database-with-python-s074","q":"In PostgreSQL, what must a query look like to use this expression index?","a":"<p>It must use the same expression: <code>WHERE LOWER(email) = 'a@example.com'</code>. A query on <code>email = ...</code> or <code>UPPER(email)</code> cannot use it. The function must be <code>IMMUTABLE</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>CREATE INDEX idx_lower_email ON employees(LOWER(email));</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0392","card":"q-database-with-python-073-postgresql-partial-and-expression-indexe","sec":"database-with-python-s074","q":"What other ways exist in PostgreSQL to compare text without regard to case?","a":"<p>Use the <code>citext</code> extension type, a case-insensitive (nondeterministic) collation in PostgreSQL 12 and later, or an expression index on <code>LOWER(column)</code>. The expression index is simple and portable. <code>citext</code> keeps queries natural, but it is an extension.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0393","card":"q-database-with-python-073-postgresql-partial-and-expression-indexe","sec":"database-with-python-s074","q":"Does MySQL have partial or expression indexes?","a":"<p>MySQL has no partial indexes. It supports functional (expression) indexes from version 8.0.13. A common workaround is an index on a stored or virtual column that holds the expression.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0394","card":"q-database-with-python-074-postgresql-partitioning","sec":"database-with-python-s075","q":"In PostgreSQL 10 and later, what is declarative partitioning, and what did people do before?","a":"<p>You declare <code>PARTITION BY RANGE/LIST/HASH (column)</code> on the parent table, and PostgreSQL routes inserts to the right child and prunes partitions in queries. Before version 10, you used table inheritance with <code>CHECK</code> constraints and trigger functions to route rows, which was manual and slow. <code>HASH</code> partitioning came in PostgreSQL 11.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0395","card":"q-database-with-python-074-postgresql-partitioning","sec":"database-with-python-s075","q":"In PostgreSQL, what happens when you insert a row whose date has no matching partition, and how do you handle it?","a":"<p>The insert fails with \"no partition of relation found for row\". Create the partitions before data arrives, usually with a scheduled job, or add a <code>DEFAULT</code> partition (PostgreSQL 11 and later) to catch other rows. A default partition has to be checked when you add a new partition.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>CREATE TABLE sales_2023 PARTITION OF sales\nFOR VALUES FROM (&#x27;2023-01-01&#x27;) TO (&#x27;2024-01-01&#x27;);</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0396","card":"q-database-with-python-074-postgresql-partitioning","sec":"database-with-python-s075","q":"In PostgreSQL, why does a primary key on a partitioned table fail unless it includes the partition column?","a":"<p>A unique index exists separately in each partition, so PostgreSQL can only guarantee global uniqueness if the partition key is part of the key; then equal values land in the same partition. Use a composite key such as <code>(id, sale_date)</code>. This limit also exists in MySQL.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0397","card":"q-database-with-python-074-postgresql-partitioning","sec":"database-with-python-s075","q":"How do you remove old data from a partitioned PostgreSQL table, and why is it better than <code>DELETE</code>?","a":"<p>Detach and drop the partition: <code>ALTER TABLE sales DETACH PARTITION sales_2023;</code> then <code>DROP TABLE sales_2023;</code>. It is a metadata operation that removes a year of rows instantly, with no dead rows to vacuum. A <code>DELETE</code> of the same rows would be slow, write a lot of WAL and bloat the table.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0398","card":"q-database-with-python-074-postgresql-partitioning","sec":"database-with-python-s075","q":"When should you not partition a PostgreSQL table?","a":"<p>When the table is small enough for an index to work well, or when queries rarely filter on the partition key, so every partition is scanned. Many partitions increase planning time, and partitioning adds operational work. Partition for pruning, data lifecycle (dropping old data) or maintenance on very large tables. It is not a substitute for good indexes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0399","card":"q-database-with-python-074-postgresql-partitioning","sec":"database-with-python-s075","q":"How do partitioning and sharding differ?","a":"<p>Partitioning splits one table into pieces inside a single database server, so the table is still managed by one machine. Sharding spreads data across several servers, each holding part of it, and needs routing and cross-shard handling in the application or a layer such as Citus. Partitioning helps manage size; sharding adds capacity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0400","card":"q-database-with-python-074-postgresql-partitioning","sec":"database-with-python-s075","q":"How can you check that a PostgreSQL query benefits from partition pruning?","a":"<p>Run <code>EXPLAIN</code> and check that only the matching partitions appear in the plan, for example <code>sales_2023</code> and no others. If all partitions are scanned, the filter probably does not use the partition key directly, or applies a function to it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0401","card":"q-database-with-python-075-postgresql-error-handling-and-full-text","sec":"database-with-python-s076","q":"Full-text search vs LIKE?","a":"<p>Full-text search with a GIN index is faster and understands words, while <code>LIKE</code> scans text.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0402","card":"q-database-with-python-075-postgresql-error-handling-and-full-text","sec":"database-with-python-s076","q":"In PostgreSQL PL/pgSQL, what happens to the <code>INSERT</code> when this function hits an error and the <code>EXCEPTION</code> block runs?","a":"<p>A <code>BEGIN ... EXCEPTION</code> block runs as a subtransaction. When an error is caught, every change made inside that block is rolled back, so the failed <code>INSERT</code> leaves nothing behind. The rest of the outer transaction stays valid. The cost is extra overhead for each block that has an <code>EXCEPTION</code> clause, so avoid it in hot loops.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION safe_insert_employee(p_first_name VARCHAR, p_salary NUMERIC)\nRETURNS TEXT AS $$\nBEGIN\n    INSERT INTO employees (first_name, salary) VALUES (p_first_name, p_salary);\n    RETURN &#x27;Insert successful&#x27;;\nEXCEPTION\n    WHEN OTHERS THEN\n        RETURN &#x27;Error: &#x27; || SQLERRM;\nEND;\n$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0403","card":"q-database-with-python-075-postgresql-error-handling-and-full-text","sec":"database-with-python-s076","q":"In PostgreSQL PL/pgSQL, why is <code>WHEN OTHERS THEN RETURN 'Error: ' || SQLERRM</code> risky as the only error handling in a function?","a":"<p>It catches every error (except query cancel and a few fatal ones), so real bugs, permission problems and constraint violations all turn into a returned string. The caller may treat that string as success. Catch specific conditions such as <code>unique_violation</code>, and re-raise anything you cannot handle with <code>RAISE</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0404","card":"q-database-with-python-075-postgresql-error-handling-and-full-text","sec":"database-with-python-s076","q":"In PostgreSQL PL/pgSQL, what is the difference between <code>SQLERRM</code> and <code>SQLSTATE</code> inside an exception handler?","a":"<p><code>SQLERRM</code> is the human-readable error message. <code>SQLSTATE</code> is the five-character standard error code (for example <code>23505</code> for a unique violation). Code should branch on the condition name or <code>SQLSTATE</code>, never parse the message text, because messages can change and can be translated.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0405","card":"q-database-with-python-075-postgresql-error-handling-and-full-text","sec":"database-with-python-s076","q":"In PL/pgSQL, how do you catch only duplicate-key errors and let every other error propagate?","a":"<p>Name the specific condition in the <code>WHEN</code> clause, as <code>unique_violation</code> does here (SQLSTATE <code>23505</code>). Errors that match no <code>WHEN</code> branch are not caught and abort the surrounding transaction as usual. Use <code>RAISE NOTICE</code> for messages and <code>RAISE EXCEPTION</code> to raise your own error.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>BEGIN\n    INSERT INTO employees (id, first_name) VALUES (1, &#x27;Ann&#x27;);\nEXCEPTION\n    WHEN unique_violation THEN\n        RAISE NOTICE &#x27;already exists&#x27;;\nEND;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0406","card":"q-database-with-python-075-postgresql-error-handling-and-full-text","sec":"database-with-python-s076","q":"In PL/pgSQL, what is the difference between <code>RAISE NOTICE</code> and <code>RAISE EXCEPTION</code>?","a":"<p><code>RAISE NOTICE</code> sends a message to the client or log and execution continues. <code>RAISE EXCEPTION</code> raises an error that aborts the current transaction (or subtransaction) unless an enclosing <code>EXCEPTION</code> block catches it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0407","card":"q-database-with-python-075-postgresql-error-handling-and-full-text","sec":"database-with-python-s076","q":"In PostgreSQL, why does this full-text index use <code>GIN</code>, and what is the trade-off against <code>GiST</code>?","a":"<p>A <code>tsvector</code> holds many lexemes per row, and <code>GIN</code> is an inverted index (lexeme to row list), so lookups are fast. The trade-off is slower writes and a larger index. <code>GiST</code> is smaller and cheaper to update but lossy, so it needs rechecks and is slower to search. Choose <code>GIN</code> for read-heavy search, <code>GiST</code> for write-heavy data.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>CREATE INDEX idx_fts ON employees USING GIN(to_tsvector(&#x27;english&#x27;, first_name || &#x27; &#x27; || last_name));</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0408","card":"q-database-with-python-075-postgresql-error-handling-and-full-text","sec":"database-with-python-s076","q":"In PostgreSQL full-text search, what does <code>to_tsvector('english', text)</code> do to the text?","a":"<p>It parses the text into tokens, lowercases them, removes stop words for the chosen configuration, and reduces words to stems (lexemes), keeping their positions. 'running' and 'runs' both become 'run', which is why full-text search matches word forms where <code>LIKE</code> cannot.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0409","card":"q-database-with-python-075-postgresql-error-handling-and-full-text","sec":"database-with-python-s076","q":"In PostgreSQL, why must this query repeat exactly the same expression that was used to build the index?","a":"<p>An expression index is only used when the query contains the same expression. Also, the two-argument form with an explicit configuration is required for indexing, because the one-argument form depends on the <code>default_text_search_config</code> setting and is not immutable. A different expression or configuration makes the planner scan the table.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>SELECT first_name, last_name\nFROM employees\nWHERE to_tsvector(&#x27;english&#x27;, first_name || &#x27; &#x27; || last_name) @@ to_tsquery(&#x27;John &amp; Doe&#x27;);</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0410","card":"q-database-with-python-075-postgresql-error-handling-and-full-text","sec":"database-with-python-s076","q":"In PostgreSQL full-text search, what does the <code>@@</code> operator do?","a":"<p>It tests whether a <code>tsvector</code> matches a <code>tsquery</code>. The query side can use operators such as <code>&amp;</code> (and), <code>|</code> (or), <code>!</code> (not) and <code>&lt;-&gt;</code> (followed by).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0411","card":"q-database-with-python-075-postgresql-error-handling-and-full-text","sec":"database-with-python-s076","q":"In PostgreSQL, what is the difference between <code>to_tsquery</code>, <code>plainto_tsquery</code> and <code>websearch_to_tsquery</code>?","a":"<p><code>to_tsquery</code> expects tsquery syntax, so <code>to_tsquery('John Doe')</code> fails with a syntax error and you must write <code>'John &amp; Doe'</code>. <code>plainto_tsquery</code> takes plain words and ANDs them. <code>websearch_to_tsquery</code> (PostgreSQL 11+) accepts search-box syntax such as quotes, <code>or</code> and <code>-word</code>, and never raises syntax errors on user input, so it is the safest choice for raw user text.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0412","card":"q-database-with-python-075-postgresql-error-handling-and-full-text","sec":"database-with-python-s076","q":"In PostgreSQL, what is wrong with indexing <code>to_tsvector('english', first_name || ' ' || last_name)</code> when <code>last_name</code> can be NULL?","a":"<p>In SQL, <code>||</code> with NULL returns NULL, so the whole document is NULL and the row never matches a search. Use <code>concat_ws(' ', first_name, last_name)</code> or <code>coalesce</code> on each column, and use the same expression in the query.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0413","card":"q-database-with-python-075-postgresql-error-handling-and-full-text","sec":"database-with-python-s076","q":"In PostgreSQL, how can you avoid recomputing <code>to_tsvector</code> on every query and keep the index simple?","a":"<p>Store the <code>tsvector</code> in its own column that the database keeps up to date, and put the <code>GIN</code> index on that column. In PostgreSQL 12+ use a stored computed column. In older versions use a trigger such as the built-in <code>tsvector_update_trigger</code>. The query then uses <code>search_col @@ query</code> with no function call.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0414","card":"q-database-with-python-075-postgresql-error-handling-and-full-text","sec":"database-with-python-s076","q":"In PostgreSQL, how do you sort full-text search results by relevance?","a":"<p>Compute a score with <code>ts_rank</code> (or <code>ts_rank_cd</code>, which also considers how close the terms are) and order by it descending, for example <code>ORDER BY ts_rank(doc, query) DESC</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0415","card":"q-database-with-python-075-postgresql-error-handling-and-full-text","sec":"database-with-python-s076","q":"In PostgreSQL, when is the <code>pg_trgm</code> extension a better fit than full-text search?","a":"<p>Use <code>pg_trgm</code> with a <code>GIN</code> or <code>GiST</code> index for substring and typo-tolerant matching: it speeds up <code>LIKE '%abc%'</code>, <code>ILIKE</code> and similarity searches on names or codes. Full-text search matches whole words with stemming and is the better choice for natural-language text. They solve different problems.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0416","card":"q-database-with-python-076-postgresql-inheritance-monitoring-and-co","sec":"database-with-python-s077","q":"In PostgreSQL, what does <code>INHERITS</code> do in this table definition, and what do you get when you query <code>staff</code>?","a":"<p><code>contractors</code> gets all the columns of <code>staff</code> plus <code>contract_end</code>. A <code>SELECT</code> from <code>staff</code> also returns rows stored in <code>contractors</code>. Use <code>SELECT * FROM ONLY staff</code> to read only the parent's own rows, and <code>tableoid::regclass</code> to see which table each row came from.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>CREATE TABLE staff (\n    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n    name VARCHAR(100)\n);\nCREATE TABLE contractors (\n    contract_end DATE\n) INHERITS (staff);</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0417","card":"q-database-with-python-076-postgresql-inheritance-monitoring-and-co","sec":"database-with-python-s077","q":"In PostgreSQL, what are the main limitations of table inheritance?","a":"<p>Primary keys, unique constraints, foreign keys and indexes are not inherited, and a unique constraint on the parent does not stop the same value appearing in a child. A foreign key that references the parent does not see child rows. <code>CHECK</code> and <code>NOT NULL</code> constraints are inherited. These gaps are why inheritance is rarely used for modelling today.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0418","card":"q-database-with-python-076-postgresql-inheritance-monitoring-and-co","sec":"database-with-python-s077","q":"In PostgreSQL, why is declarative partitioning preferred over table inheritance for splitting a large table?","a":"<p>Declarative partitioning (PostgreSQL 10+) manages routing of inserts, partition pruning and constraints for you. With inheritance you wrote triggers or rules to route rows and <code>CHECK</code> constraints by hand, and key constraints did not span the hierarchy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0419","card":"q-database-with-python-076-postgresql-inheritance-monitoring-and-co","sec":"database-with-python-s077","q":"In PostgreSQL, what does <code>pg_stat_activity</code> show, and how do you use its <code>state</code> column?","a":"<p>It has one row per server process, with user, database, client address, current query, query start time, wait events and state. <code>active</code> means a query is running. <code>idle</code> means waiting for a command. <code>idle in transaction</code> means a transaction is open with no running statement, which holds locks and blocks vacuum, so it is the state to hunt for when things stall.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>SELECT * FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE state = &#x27;active&#x27;;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0420","card":"q-database-with-python-076-postgresql-inheritance-monitoring-and-co","sec":"database-with-python-s077","q":"In PostgreSQL, what is the difference between <code>pg_cancel_backend(pid)</code> and <code>pg_terminate_backend(pid)</code>?","a":"<p><code>pg_cancel_backend</code> cancels the current query but keeps the session connected. <code>pg_terminate_backend</code> ends the whole session and rolls back its open transaction. Cancel first, and terminate only if the session stays stuck. Both need to be the same role as the target, a member of <code>pg_signal_backend</code>, or a superuser.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0421","card":"q-database-with-python-076-postgresql-inheritance-monitoring-and-co","sec":"database-with-python-s077","q":"In PostgreSQL, what is dangerous about this statement for stopping a runaway query?","a":"<p>The <code>LIKE '%SELECT%'</code> filter matches every session whose query text contains SELECT, including innocent queries and the monitoring query itself. It cancels all of them. Filter on the specific <code>pid</code>, or on <code>state</code>, <code>datname</code>, <code>usename</code> and a long <code>now() - query_start</code>, and exclude your own session with <code>pid &lt;&gt; pg_backend_pid()</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>SELECT pg_cancel_backend(pid) FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE query LIKE &#x27;%SELECT%&#x27;;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0422","card":"q-database-with-python-076-postgresql-inheritance-monitoring-and-co","sec":"database-with-python-s077","q":"In PostgreSQL, how do you find which session is blocking a waiting query?","a":"<p>Call <code>pg_blocking_pids(pid)</code> for the waiting backend, or join <code>pg_stat_activity</code> to <code>pg_locks</code>. The result lists the pids holding the locks. Then inspect those sessions (often <code>idle in transaction</code>) before deciding to cancel or terminate one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0423","card":"q-database-with-python-076-postgresql-inheritance-monitoring-and-co","sec":"database-with-python-s077","q":"In PostgreSQL, what does the <code>work_mem</code> setting control, and why is it dangerous to set it very high?","a":"<p>It is the memory one sort or hash operation can use before spilling to disk. The limit applies per operation, not per query or per connection, so one complex query can use several multiples of it and many connections multiply that again. A high global value can exhaust RAM. Raise it per session or per role (<code>SET LOCAL work_mem</code>) for specific heavy queries.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>SHOW work_mem;\nALTER SYSTEM SET work_mem = &#x27;16MB&#x27;;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0424","card":"q-database-with-python-076-postgresql-inheritance-monitoring-and-co","sec":"database-with-python-s077","q":"In PostgreSQL, what does <code>ALTER SYSTEM SET work_mem = '16MB'</code> change, and what must you do for it to take effect?","a":"<p><code>ALTER SYSTEM</code> writes the setting to <code>postgresql.auto.conf</code>, which overrides <code>postgresql.conf</code>. <code>pg_reload_conf()</code> makes the server re-read configuration, which is enough for settings that can change at runtime, such as <code>work_mem</code>. Settings that need a restart, such as <code>shared_buffers</code>, only apply after the server restarts. Check the <code>context</code> column in <code>pg_settings</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ALTER SYSTEM SET work_mem = &#x27;16MB&#x27;;\nSELECT pg_reload_conf();</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0425","card":"q-database-with-python-076-postgresql-inheritance-monitoring-and-co","sec":"database-with-python-s077","q":"In PostgreSQL, what is <code>shared_buffers</code>, and what is a common starting value?","a":"<p>It is the memory PostgreSQL uses to cache table and index pages. A usual starting point on a dedicated database server is about 25% of RAM, then tune from measurements. The operating system cache covers the rest. Changing it requires a restart.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0426","card":"q-database-with-python-076-postgresql-inheritance-monitoring-and-co","sec":"database-with-python-s077","q":"In PostgreSQL, how do you enable <code>pg_stat_statements</code>, and what does it give you?","a":"<p>Add <code>pg_stat_statements</code> to <code>shared_preload_libraries</code> in the configuration, restart the server, then run <code>CREATE EXTENSION pg_stat_statements;</code> in the database. The view shows per-query call counts, total and mean execution time, and rows. In PostgreSQL 13+ the time columns are <code>total_exec_time</code> and <code>mean_exec_time</code>. Before 13 the column was <code>total_time</code>. Sort by total time to find the queries worth tuning first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0427","card":"q-database-with-python-076-postgresql-inheritance-monitoring-and-co","sec":"database-with-python-s077","q":"In PostgreSQL, how do you see the current value of a setting and whether it needs a restart to change?","a":"<p><code>SHOW name</code> returns the value. <code>SELECT name, setting, unit, context, pending_restart FROM pg_settings WHERE name = 'work_mem'</code> also shows the <code>context</code> (for example <code>user</code>, <code>sighup</code> or <code>postmaster</code>) and whether a restart is pending.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0428","card":"q-database-with-python-077-postgresql-advanced-replication-and-fore","sec":"database-with-python-s078","q":"In PostgreSQL logical replication, after you run <code>ALTER PUBLICATION my_pub ADD TABLE departments;</code> what must you also do on the subscriber?","a":"<p>Create the same table on the subscriber, because logical replication does not copy schema. Then run <code>ALTER SUBSCRIPTION ... REFRESH PUBLICATION</code> so the subscription picks up the new table and copies its initial data. Without the refresh the new table is not replicated.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ALTER PUBLICATION my_pub ADD TABLE departments;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0429","card":"q-database-with-python-077-postgresql-advanced-replication-and-fore","sec":"database-with-python-s078","q":"In PostgreSQL, what does querying <code>pg_stat_subscription</code> tell you?","a":"<p>It shows the state of the subscription's apply and sync workers: process id, the last received and last reported WAL positions, and message timestamps. A missing worker row or a stale position means the subscription is not applying changes and needs checking in the server log.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>SELECT * FROM pg_stat_subscription;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0430","card":"q-database-with-python-077-postgresql-advanced-replication-and-fore","sec":"database-with-python-s078","q":"In PostgreSQL logical replication, what happens when a change from the publisher conflicts with data on the subscriber, such as a duplicate key?","a":"<p>PostgreSQL has no built-in conflict resolution. The apply worker raises an error and keeps retrying the same change, so replication stalls and WAL builds up on the publisher. You fix the data on the subscriber, or in PostgreSQL 15+ skip the failing transaction with <code>ALTER SUBSCRIPTION ... SKIP</code>. PostgreSQL 15+ also has <code>disable_on_error</code> to stop the subscription instead of retrying.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0431","card":"q-database-with-python-077-postgresql-advanced-replication-and-fore","sec":"database-with-python-s078","q":"In PostgreSQL, how do you avoid conflicts when using logical replication?","a":"<p>Treat the subscriber's replicated tables as read-only. Allow writes to a table on one side only, or partition writes so the two sides never touch the same rows. For two-way setups, PostgreSQL 16+ offers the subscription option <code>origin = none</code> so changes that arrived by replication are not sent back and looped.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0432","card":"q-database-with-python-077-postgresql-advanced-replication-and-fore","sec":"database-with-python-s078","q":"In PostgreSQL, what is a foreign data wrapper (FDW), and why use one?","a":"<p>An FDW lets you query an external data source, such as another PostgreSQL database, as if it were a local table. It avoids copying data and lets one query join local and remote tables. The trade-off is network latency on every query and no local copy if the remote is down.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0433","card":"q-database-with-python-077-postgresql-advanced-replication-and-fore","sec":"database-with-python-s078","q":"In PostgreSQL, in what order do you set up <code>postgres_fdw</code>, and what does each statement do?","a":"<ol>\n  <li><code>CREATE EXTENSION</code> installs the wrapper. 2) <code>CREATE SERVER</code> defines where the remote database is. 3) <code>CREATE USER MAPPING</code> says which remote credentials a local role uses. 4) <code>CREATE FOREIGN TABLE</code> maps a remote table to a local name and columns. Each step depends on the one before. <code>IMPORT FOREIGN SCHEMA</code> can create many foreign tables at once.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>CREATE EXTENSION postgres_fdw;\nCREATE SERVER remote_server FOREIGN DATA WRAPPER postgres_fdw\nOPTIONS (host &#x27;remote_host&#x27;, dbname &#x27;remote_db&#x27;, port &#x27;5432&#x27;);\nCREATE USER MAPPING FOR postgres SERVER remote_server\nOPTIONS (user &#x27;remote_user&#x27;, password &#x27;remote_pass&#x27;);\nCREATE FOREIGN TABLE remote_employees (\n    id INTEGER,\n    first_name VARCHAR(50)\n) SERVER remote_server OPTIONS (schema_name &#x27;public&#x27;, table_name &#x27;employees&#x27;);</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0434","card":"q-database-with-python-077-postgresql-advanced-replication-and-fore","sec":"database-with-python-s078","q":"In PostgreSQL <code>postgres_fdw</code>, how can you tell whether a <code>WHERE</code> clause or join runs on the remote server or locally?","a":"<p>Run <code>EXPLAIN VERBOSE</code> and read the <code>Remote SQL</code> line of the Foreign Scan. Conditions, joins and aggregates that can be pushed down are sent to the remote server, and only matching rows cross the network. If they cannot be pushed down (for example a local function), all rows are fetched and filtered locally, which is slow on large tables.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0435","card":"q-database-with-python-077-postgresql-advanced-replication-and-fore","sec":"database-with-python-s078","q":"In PostgreSQL <code>postgres_fdw</code>, how should you handle the remote password stored in a <code>USER MAPPING</code>?","a":"<p>Use a dedicated remote account with minimal privileges, because the password is stored in the system catalog and visible to superusers. Ordinary users see mappings through the <code>pg_user_mappings</code> view with the password hidden. Prefer certificate or other non-password authentication where possible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0436","card":"q-database-with-python-077-postgresql-advanced-replication-and-fore","sec":"database-with-python-s078","q":"In PostgreSQL, when would you choose a foreign data wrapper over logical replication to get remote data?","a":"<p>Choose an FDW for occasional or small reads where fresh data matters and you do not want to maintain a copy. Choose logical replication when you need a local copy for heavy queries, low latency or availability when the remote is down, and you can accept slight lag.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0437","card":"q-database-with-python-077-postgresql-advanced-replication-and-fore","sec":"database-with-python-s078","q":"In PostgreSQL, can one query read tables from two different databases on the same server directly?","a":"<p>No. A connection is bound to one database, and cross-database references are not supported. Use <code>postgres_fdw</code> (or the older <code>dblink</code>) to reach another database, or put the data in separate schemas of one database.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0438","card":"q-database-with-python-077-postgresql-advanced-replication-and-fore","sec":"database-with-python-s078","q":"In PostgreSQL, is a transaction that writes to both a local table and a foreign table atomic across both?","a":"<p>No. <code>postgres_fdw</code> opens a remote transaction and commits it along with the local one, but it does not use two-phase commit. A failure between the two commits can leave one side committed and the other not, so avoid relying on cross-server atomicity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0439","card":"q-database-with-python-078-python-basics-what-it-is-and-how-it-runs","sec":"database-with-python-s079","q":"What is Python, and what are its key features?","a":"<p>A high-level, interpreted language known for simplicity. Features: readable syntax, dynamic typing, extensive libraries, cross-platform, multi-paradigm (procedural, OOP, functional), garbage-collected.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0440","card":"q-database-with-python-078-python-basics-what-it-is-and-how-it-runs","sec":"database-with-python-s079","q":"Python 2 vs Python 3?","a":"<p>Python 2 reached end of life in 2020. In Python 3, <code>print</code> is a function, <code>5/2</code> gives <code>2.5</code> (float division), Unicode is native, <code>range()</code> replaces <code>xrange()</code>, and modern features such as f-strings exist.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0441","card":"q-database-with-python-078-python-basics-what-it-is-and-how-it-runs","sec":"database-with-python-s079","q":"What are PEP 8 guidelines and why do they matter?","a":"<p>Python's style guide: 4-space indentation, 79-character lines, snake_case for functions. It keeps code readable and consistent for collaboration.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0442","card":"q-database-with-python-078-python-basics-what-it-is-and-how-it-runs","sec":"database-with-python-s079","q":"How is Python an interpreted language?","a":"<p>The interpreter runs code line by line, converting it to bytecode that the Python Virtual Machine (PVM) executes. It is not compiled to machine code.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0443","card":"q-database-with-python-078-python-basics-what-it-is-and-how-it-runs","sec":"database-with-python-s079","q":"How does Python handle memory management?","a":"<p>Reference counting (memory is freed when the count hits 0) plus a garbage collector for cyclic references, available through the <code>gc</code> module.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0444","card":"q-database-with-python-078-python-basics-what-it-is-and-how-it-runs","sec":"database-with-python-s079","q":"In CPython, what happens between running <code>python script.py</code> and the code executing?","a":"<p>The source is compiled to bytecode, then the Python Virtual Machine runs the bytecode instruction by instruction. Bytecode for imported modules is cached as <code>.pyc</code> files in <code>__pycache__</code> so later imports skip compiling. Python is called interpreted, but it has this compile step.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0445","card":"q-database-with-python-078-python-basics-what-it-is-and-how-it-runs","sec":"database-with-python-s079","q":"In Python, what is the difference between CPython, PyPy and Jython?","a":"<p>They are implementations of the same language. CPython is the reference one, written in C, with the widest library support. PyPy has a just-in-time compiler and is often faster for long-running pure Python code. Jython runs on the JVM. C extensions such as NumPy are tuned for CPython, so PyPy support can lag.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0446","card":"q-database-with-python-078-python-basics-what-it-is-and-how-it-runs","sec":"database-with-python-s079","q":"In Python, what does dynamic typing mean, and what are its trade-offs?","a":"<p>Types belong to objects, not variables, so <code>x = 1</code> then <code>x = \"a\"</code> is legal and type errors appear at run time. It speeds up writing and gives flexible code. The cost is bugs found late and weaker tooling. Type hints (PEP 484) with a checker such as mypy add static checks without changing runtime behavior.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0447","card":"q-database-with-python-078-python-basics-what-it-is-and-how-it-runs","sec":"database-with-python-s079","q":"In Python 3, what do <code>5 / 2</code> and <code>5 // 2</code> return, and how did Python 2 differ?","a":"<p><code>5 / 2</code> is <code>2.5</code> (true division) and <code>5 // 2</code> is <code>2</code> (floor division). In Python 2, <code>/</code> on two ints did floor division and gave <code>2</code>, a common source of bugs when migrating.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0448","card":"q-database-with-python-078-python-basics-what-it-is-and-how-it-runs","sec":"database-with-python-s079","q":"In Python, how can reference counting alone leak memory, and what does the <code>gc</code> module add?","a":"<p>Each object still has a reference from the other, so their counts never reach 0 and reference counting cannot free them. The cyclic garbage collector in <code>gc</code> finds such unreachable cycles and frees them. It runs automatically, and <code>gc.collect()</code> forces a run.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class Node:\n    pass\n\na = Node()\nb = Node()\na.other = b\nb.other = a\ndel a, b</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0449","card":"q-database-with-python-078-python-basics-what-it-is-and-how-it-runs","sec":"database-with-python-s079","q":"In Python, why is pure Python code slower than C or Java, and what do you do about it?","a":"<p>The interpreter executes bytecode with dynamic type checks, and the GIL limits CPU threading. Profile first, then use better algorithms and data structures, built-ins and libraries written in C (NumPy), multiprocessing for CPU-bound work, or Cython or a rewrite of the hot function.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0450","card":"q-database-with-python-078-python-basics-what-it-is-and-how-it-runs","sec":"database-with-python-s079","q":"In Python, why use a tool like <code>black</code>, <code>flake8</code> or <code>ruff</code> in a team project?","a":"<p>They enforce PEP 8 style automatically and catch simple bugs (unused imports, undefined names). A formatter ends style arguments in reviews, and running the linter in CI keeps the codebase consistent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0451","card":"q-database-with-python-079-python-data-types-and-mutability","sec":"database-with-python-s080","q":"Lists vs tuples vs sets?","a":"<p>Lists <code>[1, 2]</code> are mutable, ordered, and allow duplicates. Tuples <code>(1, 2)</code> are immutable, ordered, and allow duplicates. Sets <code>{1, 2}</code> are mutable, unordered, and have no duplicates.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0452","card":"q-database-with-python-079-python-data-types-and-mutability","sec":"database-with-python-s080","q":"<code>==</code> vs <code>is</code>?","a":"<p><code>==</code> checks value equality. <code>is</code> checks identity (same memory location).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>a = [1, 2]; b = [1, 2]\nprint(a == b)  # True\nprint(a is b)  # False</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0453","card":"q-database-with-python-079-python-data-types-and-mutability","sec":"database-with-python-s080","q":"Mutable vs immutable objects?","a":"<p>Mutable: lists, dicts, sets. Immutable: int, str, tuple.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0454","card":"q-database-with-python-079-python-data-types-and-mutability","sec":"database-with-python-s080","q":"What are Python's built-in data types?","a":"<p>Numeric (<code>int</code>, <code>float</code>, <code>complex</code>), sequence (<code>str</code>, <code>list</code>, <code>tuple</code>), mapping (<code>dict</code>), set (<code>set</code>, <code>frozenset</code>), boolean (<code>bool</code>), and <code>None</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0455","card":"q-database-with-python-079-python-data-types-and-mutability","sec":"database-with-python-s080","q":"<code>range()</code> vs <code>xrange()</code>?","a":"<p>In Python 2, <code>range()</code> returns a list and <code>xrange()</code> an iterator. In Python 3 only <code>range()</code> exists and it behaves like <code>xrange()</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0456","card":"q-database-with-python-079-python-data-types-and-mutability","sec":"database-with-python-s080","q":"In Python, when do you choose a tuple over a list?","a":"<p>Choose a tuple for a fixed group of values that should not change, such as coordinates or a database row. Tuples signal intent, are slightly smaller and faster, and can be used as dict keys or set members when all their items are hashable. A list is for a collection that grows or changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0457","card":"q-database-with-python-079-python-data-types-and-mutability","sec":"database-with-python-s080","q":"In Python, why can a tuple be a dictionary key but a list cannot?","a":"<p>Dict keys must be hashable, meaning the hash never changes during the object's life. A tuple of immutable items is hashable. A list is mutable, so it is unhashable and raises <code>TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'</code>. A tuple that contains a list is also unhashable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0458","card":"q-database-with-python-079-python-data-types-and-mutability","sec":"database-with-python-s080","q":"In Python, what does this code print, and what does it show about mutability?","a":"<p>It prints <code>[1, 2, 3]</code>. <code>b = a</code> copies the reference, not the list, so both names point to one object, and a mutation through one name shows through the other. Use <code>a.copy()</code> or <code>list(a)</code> for an independent (shallow) copy.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>a = [1, 2]\nb = a\nb.append(3)\nprint(a)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0459","card":"q-database-with-python-079-python-data-types-and-mutability","sec":"database-with-python-s080","q":"In Python, why does this raise a <code>TypeError</code> and still change the tuple?","a":"<p><code>t[0] += [4]</code> first extends the list in place, which succeeds, then tries to assign the result back to <code>t[0]</code>, which fails because tuples do not support item assignment. After the error <code>t</code> is <code>([1, 2, 4], 3)</code>. A tuple is immutable only in which objects it holds, not in the state of those objects.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>t = ([1, 2], 3)\nt[0] += [4]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0460","card":"q-database-with-python-079-python-data-types-and-mutability","sec":"database-with-python-s080","q":"In Python, why can sets and dicts check membership in O(1) on average while a list needs O(n)?","a":"<p>Sets and dicts store items in a hash table. The hash of the item points directly to its slot, so lookup does not scan. A list compares against each element in turn. For many membership tests, convert the list to a set first. The items must be hashable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0461","card":"q-database-with-python-079-python-data-types-and-mutability","sec":"database-with-python-s080","q":"In Python, why does <code>==</code> behave differently from <code>is</code> for small integers and <code>None</code>?","a":"<p><code>is</code> compares object identity. CPython caches small integers (about -5 to 256) and has a single <code>None</code> object, so <code>is</code> can look like it works for them, but that is an implementation detail. Use <code>==</code> for values and <code>is</code> only for singletons such as <code>None</code>, as in <code>if x is None</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0462","card":"q-database-with-python-079-python-data-types-and-mutability","sec":"database-with-python-s080","q":"In Python, what does <code>0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3</code> return, and how do you compare floats or handle money?","a":"<p>It returns <code>False</code>, because binary floating point cannot represent 0.1 and 0.2 exactly. Compare with <code>math.isclose(a, b)</code>. For money use <code>decimal.Decimal</code> (built from strings) or integer cents.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0463","card":"q-database-with-python-079-python-data-types-and-mutability","sec":"database-with-python-s080","q":"In Python, how does <code>bool</code> relate to <code>int</code>?","a":"<p><code>bool</code> is a subclass of <code>int</code>, with <code>True == 1</code> and <code>False == 0</code>. So <code>True + True</code> is <code>2</code>, and <code>sum</code> over a list of booleans counts the true values.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0464","card":"q-database-with-python-079-python-data-types-and-mutability","sec":"database-with-python-s080","q":"In Python, what is <code>frozenset</code> and when do you use it?","a":"<p>It is an immutable, hashable set. Use it when a set must be a dict key or an item of another set, or when you want to guarantee it is not changed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0465","card":"q-database-with-python-079-python-data-types-and-mutability","sec":"database-with-python-s080","q":"In Python, in what way are arguments passed to functions, and what does that mean for mutable arguments?","a":"<p>Python passes references to objects (\"pass by object reference\"). A function can mutate a list or dict it receives and the caller sees it, but rebinding the parameter name to a new object does not affect the caller. To avoid surprising callers, copy the argument first or return a new value.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0466","card":"q-database-with-python-080-python-basics-statements-and-arguments","sec":"database-with-python-s081","q":"What does <code>pass</code> do?","a":"<p>It is a no-op placeholder for an empty code block.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def func(): pass</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0467","card":"q-database-with-python-080-python-basics-statements-and-arguments","sec":"database-with-python-s081","q":"How do you swap two variables without a temp variable?","a":"<p>Tuple unpacking.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>a, b = 5, 10; a, b = b, a  # a=10, b=5</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0468","card":"q-database-with-python-080-python-basics-statements-and-arguments","sec":"database-with-python-s081","q":"What is a module and how do you import one?","a":"<p>A file of reusable code.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import math; print(math.sqrt(16))  # 4.0</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0469","card":"q-database-with-python-080-python-basics-statements-and-arguments","sec":"database-with-python-s081","q":"What does <code>if __name__ == \"__main__\":</code> do?","a":"<p>It runs the code only when the script is executed directly, not when imported.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>if __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:\n    print(&quot;Direct run&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0470","card":"q-database-with-python-080-python-basics-statements-and-arguments","sec":"database-with-python-s081","q":"What are <code>*args</code> and `","a":"<p>kwargs<code>?** </code>*args<code>collects variable positional arguments (a tuple).</code>**kwargs` collects variable keyword arguments (a dict).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def func(*args, **kwargs):\n    print(args, kwargs)\nfunc(1, 2, a=3)  # (1, 2) {&#x27;a&#x27;: 3}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0471","card":"q-database-with-python-080-python-basics-statements-and-arguments","sec":"database-with-python-s081","q":"In Python, what is the difference between <code>pass</code>, <code>continue</code> and <code>...</code> (Ellipsis) as placeholders?","a":"<p><code>pass</code> does nothing and is used where a statement is required, such as an empty class or function body. <code>continue</code> skips to the next loop iteration. <code>...</code> is a valid expression often used for stubs and type hints. Only <code>pass</code> is the conventional \"do nothing\" statement.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0472","card":"q-database-with-python-080-python-basics-statements-and-arguments","sec":"database-with-python-s081","q":"In Python, how does <code>a, b = b, a</code> swap two variables without a temporary?","a":"<p>The right side is evaluated first into a tuple of the current values <code>(b, a)</code>, and only then assigned to <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> in order. Because both values were captured before any assignment, nothing is overwritten early.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>a, b = 5, 10\na, b = b, a</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0473","card":"q-database-with-python-080-python-basics-statements-and-arguments","sec":"database-with-python-s081","q":"In Python, what is the order rule for parameters in a function definition with positional, default, <code>*args</code> and <code>**kwargs</code> parameters?","a":"<p>Order is: positional parameters, parameters with defaults, <code>*args</code>, keyword-only parameters, then <code>**kwargs</code>, for example <code>def f(a, b=1, *args, c, **kwargs)</code>. Parameters after <code>*args</code> can only be passed by keyword.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0474","card":"q-database-with-python-080-python-basics-statements-and-arguments","sec":"database-with-python-s081","q":"In Python, what do <code>*</code> and <code>**</code> do at the call site?","a":"<p><code>*args</code> unpacks a sequence into positional arguments and <code>**kwargs</code> unpacks a dict into keyword arguments. The call is <code>add(1, 2, c=3)</code> and prints <code>6</code>. It is used to forward arguments to another function.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def add(a, b, c):\n    return a + b + c\n\nargs = (1, 2)\nkwargs = {&quot;c&quot;: 3}\nprint(add(*args, **kwargs))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0475","card":"q-database-with-python-080-python-basics-statements-and-arguments","sec":"database-with-python-s081","q":"In Python, what does this <code>*args</code>/<code>**kwargs</code> function print, and what type is each?","a":"<p><code>args</code> is a tuple and <code>kwargs</code> is a dict, and the call prints <code>(1, 2) {'a': 3}</code>. Wrapper functions and decorators use this signature to accept anything and pass it through.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def func(*args, **kwargs):\n    print(args, kwargs)\nfunc(1, 2, a=3)  # (1, 2) {&#x27;a&#x27;: 3}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0476","card":"q-database-with-python-080-python-basics-statements-and-arguments","sec":"database-with-python-s081","q":"In Python, why is the <code>if __name__ == \"__main__\":</code> guard important in a module that other code imports?","a":"<p>When a file runs as a script, <code>__name__</code> is <code>\"__main__\"</code>. When imported, it is the module name. The guard keeps start-up code (running servers, tests, prints) from executing on import. It is also required with <code>multiprocessing</code> on platforms that spawn processes.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>if __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:\n    print(&quot;Direct run&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0477","card":"q-database-with-python-080-python-basics-statements-and-arguments","sec":"database-with-python-s081","q":"In Python, what happens the second time a module is imported, and why can circular imports fail?","a":"<p>The first import runs the module and stores it in <code>sys.modules</code>, so later imports reuse that object without re-running. In a circular import, module A is partly initialised when B imports names from it, causing <code>ImportError</code> or missing attributes. Fix by restructuring shared code into a third module or importing inside a function.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0478","card":"q-database-with-python-080-python-basics-statements-and-arguments","sec":"database-with-python-s081","q":"In Python 3.8+, what does <code>/</code> in a function signature mean?","a":"<p>Parameters before <code>/</code> are positional-only: <code>f(1, 2, c=3)</code> works but <code>f(a=1, b=2, c=3)</code> raises <code>TypeError</code>. It lets library authors rename parameters without breaking callers.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def f(a, b, /, c):\n    return a + b + c</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0479","card":"q-database-with-python-081-strings-reverse-slice-palindrome","sec":"database-with-python-s082","q":"How do you reverse a string?","a":"<p>Slice with a step of -1.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>s = &quot;hello&quot;; print(s[::-1])  # &quot;olleh&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0480","card":"q-database-with-python-081-strings-reverse-slice-palindrome","sec":"database-with-python-s082","q":"How does string slicing work?","a":"<p><code>[start:stop:step]</code> extracts a substring.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>s = &quot;Python&quot;; print(s[1:4])  # &quot;yth&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0481","card":"q-database-with-python-081-strings-reverse-slice-palindrome","sec":"database-with-python-s082","q":"How do you check for a palindrome?","a":"<p>Compare the string with its reverse.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def is_palindrome(s): return s.lower() == s[::-1]\nprint(is_palindrome(&quot;radar&quot;))  # True</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0482","card":"q-database-with-python-081-strings-reverse-slice-palindrome","sec":"database-with-python-s082","q":"In Python, how does the negative step in <code>s[::-1]</code> reverse a string?","a":"<p>A slice is <code>[start:stop:step]</code>. With a negative step, the default start is the last character and the default stop is before the first, so it walks backward and builds a new string <code>\"olleh\"</code>. The original string is unchanged. It creates a copy and runs in O(n).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>s = &quot;hello&quot;\nprint(s[::-1])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0483","card":"q-database-with-python-081-strings-reverse-slice-palindrome","sec":"database-with-python-s082","q":"In Python, what do <code>s[-3:]</code> and <code>s[10:]</code> return when <code>s = \"Python\"</code>?","a":"<p><code>s[-3:]</code> is <code>\"hon\"</code> (the last three characters). <code>s[10:]</code> is <code>\"\"</code>: slices clamp to the string bounds and never raise <code>IndexError</code>, unlike indexing <code>s[10]</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0484","card":"q-database-with-python-081-strings-reverse-slice-palindrome","sec":"database-with-python-s082","q":"In Python, why does assigning to <code>s[0]</code> fail, and how do you change a character?","a":"<p>Strings are immutable, so <code>s[0] = \"J\"</code> raises <code>TypeError</code>. Build a new string such as <code>\"J\" + s[1:]</code> or <code>s.replace(...)</code>. To make many edits, convert to a list, edit, and <code>\"\".join(...)</code> it back.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0485","card":"q-database-with-python-081-strings-reverse-slice-palindrome","sec":"database-with-python-s082","q":"In Python, what is wrong with this palindrome check?","a":"<p>Only the left side is lowercased, so <code>\"Radar\"</code> becomes <code>\"radar\"</code> and is compared with <code>\"radaR\"</code>, which returns <code>False</code>. Lowercase both sides (<code>t = s.lower(); t == t[::-1]</code>). Real palindrome tasks also ignore spaces and punctuation, for example by keeping only <code>c.isalnum()</code> characters.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def is_palindrome(s): return s.lower() == s[::-1]\nprint(is_palindrome(&quot;radar&quot;))  # True</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0486","card":"q-database-with-python-081-strings-reverse-slice-palindrome","sec":"database-with-python-s082","q":"In Python, how can you check a palindrome using O(1) extra space?","a":"<p>Use two indexes, one at each end, and move them toward the middle while comparing characters. The slicing version uses O(n) extra memory for the reversed copy. Both are O(n) time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0487","card":"q-database-with-python-081-strings-reverse-slice-palindrome","sec":"database-with-python-s082","q":"In Python, what is the difference between <code>reversed(s)</code> and <code>s[::-1]</code>?","a":"<p><code>s[::-1]</code> returns a new string. <code>reversed(s)</code> returns a lazy iterator, so you need <code>\"\".join(reversed(s))</code> to get a string. The iterator avoids a copy when you only loop over the characters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0488","card":"q-database-with-python-082-strings-join-format-case-and-search","sec":"database-with-python-s083","q":"<code>str.join()</code> vs <code>str.format()</code>?","a":"<p><code>join()</code> concatenates an iterable with a separator. <code>format()</code> inserts values into placeholders.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>print(&quot; &quot;.join([&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;]))  # &quot;a b&quot;\nprint(&quot;{}-{}&quot;.format(1, 2))  # &quot;1-2&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0489","card":"q-database-with-python-082-strings-join-format-case-and-search","sec":"database-with-python-s083","q":"How do you change case?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>s = &quot;Hello&quot;; print(s.lower())  # &quot;hello&quot;; print(s.upper())  # &quot;HELLO&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0490","card":"q-database-with-python-082-strings-join-format-case-and-search","sec":"database-with-python-s083","q":"<code>find()</code> vs <code>index()</code>?","a":"<p><code>find()</code> returns the index or <code>-1</code> if not found. <code>index()</code> returns the index or raises <code>ValueError</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>s = &quot;hello&quot;; print(s.find(&quot;x&quot;))  # -1; print(s.index(&quot;l&quot;))  # 2</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0491","card":"q-database-with-python-082-strings-join-format-case-and-search","sec":"database-with-python-s083","q":"How do you remove leading and trailing whitespace?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>s = &quot;  hi  &quot;; print(s.strip())  # &quot;hi&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0492","card":"q-database-with-python-082-strings-join-format-case-and-search","sec":"database-with-python-s083","q":"How do you count substring occurrences?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>s = &quot;hello hello&quot;; print(s.count(&quot;hello&quot;))  # 2</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0493","card":"q-database-with-python-082-strings-join-format-case-and-search","sec":"database-with-python-s083","q":"What are f-strings?","a":"<p>Concise formatting with <code>f\"...\"</code>. They are faster and cleaner than <code>%</code> or <code>.format()</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name = &quot;Alice&quot;; print(f&quot;Hi, {name}&quot;)  # &quot;Hi, Alice&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0494","card":"q-database-with-python-082-strings-join-format-case-and-search","sec":"database-with-python-s083","q":"How do you split a string into a list?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>s = &quot;hello world&quot;; print(s.split())  # [&quot;hello&quot;, &quot;world&quot;]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0495","card":"q-database-with-python-082-strings-join-format-case-and-search","sec":"database-with-python-s083","q":"In Python, why is building a big string with <code>\"\".join(parts)</code> better than repeated <code>+=</code> in a loop?","a":"<p>Strings are immutable, so each <code>+=</code> can copy the whole string, giving quadratic time in the worst case. <code>join</code> computes the total size once and copies each piece once, so it is linear. CPython sometimes optimises <code>+=</code> but it is not guaranteed on other implementations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0496","card":"q-database-with-python-082-strings-join-format-case-and-search","sec":"database-with-python-s083","q":"In Python, why does <code>\" \".join([1, 2])</code> fail, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p><code>join</code> needs every item to be a <code>str</code>, so it raises <code>TypeError</code> for ints. Convert first: <code>\" \".join(map(str, [1, 2]))</code> or a generator expression with <code>str(x)</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0497","card":"q-database-with-python-082-strings-join-format-case-and-search","sec":"database-with-python-s083","q":"In Python, how do f-strings compare with <code>str.format()</code> and <code>%</code> formatting, and which should you pick?","a":"<p>All three build formatted strings. F-strings (Python 3.6+) evaluate expressions inline, are the most readable and are usually the fastest. <code>format()</code> is useful when the template is stored separately from the values. <code>%</code> is the oldest style. Python 3.8+ also supports <code>f\"{x=}\"</code> for debugging. Never build SQL with f-strings from user input, because that causes SQL injection. Use query parameters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0498","card":"q-database-with-python-082-strings-join-format-case-and-search","sec":"database-with-python-s083","q":"In Python, what is the difference between <code>find()</code>, <code>index()</code> and the <code>in</code> operator for searching a substring?","a":"<p><code>in</code> just returns True or False. <code>find()</code> returns the position or <code>-1</code>, so check the result before using it. <code>index()</code> returns the position or raises <code>ValueError</code>. Use <code>in</code> for a yes/no test and <code>find</code> when you need the position without exceptions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0499","card":"q-database-with-python-082-strings-join-format-case-and-search","sec":"database-with-python-s083","q":"In Python, what is the difference between <code>\"a  b\".split()</code> and <code>\"a  b\".split(\" \")</code>?","a":"<p><code>split()</code> with no argument splits on any run of whitespace and drops empty strings, giving <code>['a', 'b']</code>. <code>split(\" \")</code> splits on every single space, giving <code>['a', '', 'b']</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0500","card":"q-database-with-python-082-strings-join-format-case-and-search","sec":"database-with-python-s083","q":"In Python, what does <code>\"abcba\".strip(\"ab\")</code> return, and what is a common misunderstanding about <code>strip</code>?","a":"<p>It returns <code>\"c\"</code>. The argument is a set of characters to remove from both ends, not a prefix or suffix. To remove an exact prefix or suffix use <code>removeprefix</code> / <code>removesuffix</code> (Python 3.9+).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0501","card":"q-database-with-python-082-strings-join-format-case-and-search","sec":"database-with-python-s083","q":"In Python, what does <code>\"aaaa\".count(\"aa\")</code> return?","a":"<p>It returns <code>2</code>. <code>count</code> counts non-overlapping occurrences, scanning left to right.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0502","card":"q-database-with-python-082-strings-join-format-case-and-search","sec":"database-with-python-s083","q":"In Python, when should you use <code>casefold()</code> instead of <code>lower()</code>?","a":"<p>Use <code>casefold()</code> for case-insensitive comparison of text that may contain non-English characters. It is more aggressive; for example, German <code>\"ß\"</code> becomes <code>\"ss\"</code>. <code>lower()</code> is fine for ASCII and for display.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0503","card":"q-database-with-python-083-lists-duplicates-comprehensions-sorting","sec":"database-with-python-s084","q":"How do you remove duplicates from a list?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>lst = [1, 2, 2]; print(list(set(lst)))  # [1, 2]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0504","card":"q-database-with-python-083-lists-duplicates-comprehensions-sorting","sec":"database-with-python-s084","q":"What is a list comprehension?","a":"<p>A concise way to build a list.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>squares = [x**2 for x in range(5)]; print(squares)  # [0, 1, 4, 9, 16]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0505","card":"q-database-with-python-083-lists-duplicates-comprehensions-sorting","sec":"database-with-python-s084","q":"How do you sort a list of dicts by a key?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>lst = [{&quot;age&quot;: 25}, {&quot;age&quot;: 20}]; print(sorted(lst, key=lambda x: x[&quot;age&quot;]))  # [{&quot;age&quot;: 20}, {&quot;age&quot;: 25}]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0506","card":"q-database-with-python-083-lists-duplicates-comprehensions-sorting","sec":"database-with-python-s084","q":"<code>append()</code> vs <code>extend()</code>?","a":"<p><code>append()</code> adds one item. <code>extend()</code> adds each element of an iterable.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>lst = [1]; lst.append([2, 3]); print(lst)  # [1, [2, 3]]\nlst = [1]; lst.extend([2, 3]); print(lst)  # [1, 2, 3]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0507","card":"q-database-with-python-083-lists-duplicates-comprehensions-sorting","sec":"database-with-python-s084","q":"<code>pop()</code> vs <code>remove()</code>?","a":"<p><code>pop()</code> removes by index and returns the value. <code>remove()</code> removes by value.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>lst = [1, 2]; print(lst.pop(0))  # 1; lst.remove(2); print(lst)  # []</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0508","card":"q-database-with-python-083-lists-duplicates-comprehensions-sorting","sec":"database-with-python-s084","q":"How do you flatten a nested list?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>nested = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]; flat = [x for sub in nested for x in sub]; print(flat)  # [1, 2, 3, 4]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0509","card":"q-database-with-python-083-lists-duplicates-comprehensions-sorting","sec":"database-with-python-s084","q":"In Python, how do you remove duplicates from a list and keep the original order?","a":"<p>Use <code>list(dict.fromkeys(lst))</code>. Dicts keep insertion order (guaranteed in Python 3.7+) and drop repeated keys. <code>list(set(lst))</code> also removes duplicates but loses order, and both need hashable items.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0510","card":"q-database-with-python-083-lists-duplicates-comprehensions-sorting","sec":"database-with-python-s084","q":"In Python, why is a list comprehension usually preferred over a <code>for</code> loop with <code>append</code>?","a":"<p>It is shorter, reads as one transformation, and is typically a little faster because the loop runs inside the interpreter's optimised path. Stop using it when it needs several nested conditions or side effects, where an ordinary loop is clearer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0511","card":"q-database-with-python-083-lists-duplicates-comprehensions-sorting","sec":"database-with-python-s084","q":"In Python, what is the difference between <code>sorted(lst)</code> and <code>lst.sort()</code>?","a":"<p><code>sorted</code> returns a new list and works on any iterable. <code>lst.sort()</code> sorts in place and returns <code>None</code>, a common bug is <code>lst = lst.sort()</code>. Both are stable (equal items keep their order) and use Timsort, which is O(n log n).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0512","card":"q-database-with-python-083-lists-duplicates-comprehensions-sorting","sec":"database-with-python-s084","q":"In Python, how do you sort a list of dicts by two keys, one ascending and one descending?","a":"<p>A tuple key with a negated numeric field: <code>sorted(people, key=lambda p: (-p[\"age\"], p[\"name\"]))</code>. Alternatively sort twice, relying on stability: first by name, then by age with <code>reverse=True</code>. <code>operator.itemgetter</code> is a faster key for plain ascending keys.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>people = [{&quot;name&quot;: &quot;A&quot;, &quot;age&quot;: 30}, {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;B&quot;, &quot;age&quot;: 30}, {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;C&quot;, &quot;age&quot;: 25}]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0513","card":"q-database-with-python-083-lists-duplicates-comprehensions-sorting","sec":"database-with-python-s084","q":"In Python, what is the difference between these two calls?","a":"<p>The first prints <code>[1, [2, 3]]</code> because <code>append</code> adds its argument as one item. The second prints <code>[1, 2, 3]</code> because <code>extend</code> adds each element of the iterable.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>lst = [1]\nlst.append([2, 3])\nprint(lst)\nlst = [1]\nlst.extend([2, 3])\nprint(lst)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0514","card":"q-database-with-python-083-lists-duplicates-comprehensions-sorting","sec":"database-with-python-s084","q":"In Python, why is <code>lst.pop(0)</code> a poor choice for a queue, and what do you use?","a":"<p>Removing the first item shifts every other element, so it is O(n). Use <code>collections.deque</code> with <code>popleft()</code>, which is O(1).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0515","card":"q-database-with-python-083-lists-duplicates-comprehensions-sorting","sec":"database-with-python-s084","q":"In Python, what is the bug in removing items from a list while looping over it?","a":"<p>Removing shifts later items left while the loop index keeps moving, so items are skipped. Here the output is <code>[1, 2, 3]</code>, not <code>[1, 3]</code>. Build a new list with a comprehension (<code>[n for n in nums if n != 2]</code>) or iterate over a copy.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>nums = [1, 2, 2, 3]\nfor n in nums:\n    if n == 2:\n        nums.remove(n)\nprint(nums)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0516","card":"q-database-with-python-083-lists-duplicates-comprehensions-sorting","sec":"database-with-python-s084","q":"In Python, why does <code>grid = [[0] * 3] * 3</code> behave unexpectedly?","a":"<p>The outer <code>*</code> repeats the same inner list three times, so all rows are one object and <code>grid[0][0] = 1</code> changes every row. Build rows separately: <code>[[0] * 3 for _ in range(3)]</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0517","card":"q-database-with-python-083-lists-duplicates-comprehensions-sorting","sec":"database-with-python-s084","q":"In Python, how do you flatten a list of lists, and what is the reading order of a nested comprehension?","a":"<p>The <code>for</code> clauses read in the same order as nested loops: first <code>for sub in nested</code>, then <code>for x in sub</code>. <code>itertools.chain.from_iterable(nested)</code> does the same lazily. These only flatten one level. Arbitrary depth needs recursion.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>nested = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]\nflat = [x for sub in nested for x in sub]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0518","card":"q-database-with-python-084-dictionaries-and-sets","sec":"database-with-python-s085","q":"How do you merge two dictionaries?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>d1 = {&quot;a&quot;: 1}; d2 = {&quot;b&quot;: 2}; print(d1 | d2)  # {&quot;a&quot;: 1, &quot;b&quot;: 2} (Python 3.9+)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0519","card":"q-database-with-python-084-dictionaries-and-sets","sec":"database-with-python-s085","q":"How do you check if a key exists?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>d = {&quot;a&quot;: 1}; print(&quot;a&quot; in d)  # True; print(d.get(&quot;b&quot;, 0))  # 0</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0520","card":"q-database-with-python-084-dictionaries-and-sets","sec":"database-with-python-s085","q":"What is a dict comprehension?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>d = {x: x**2 for x in range(3)}; print(d)  # {0: 0, 1: 1, 2: 4}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0521","card":"q-database-with-python-084-dictionaries-and-sets","sec":"database-with-python-s085","q":"How do set operations work?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>s1 = {1, 2}; s2 = {2, 3}\nprint(s1 | s2)  # {1, 2, 3} (union)\nprint(s1 &amp; s2)  # {2} (intersection)\nprint(s1 - s2)  # {1} (difference)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0522","card":"q-database-with-python-084-dictionaries-and-sets","sec":"database-with-python-s085","q":"In Python, which ways merge two dicts, and what happens to duplicate keys?","a":"<p><code>d1 | d2</code> (Python 3.9+) and <code>{**d1, **d2}</code> (3.5+) create a new dict. <code>d1.update(d2)</code> changes <code>d1</code> in place. In every case the value from the right-hand dict wins for duplicate keys.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0523","card":"q-database-with-python-084-dictionaries-and-sets","sec":"database-with-python-s085","q":"In Python, what is the difference between <code>d.get(k)</code>, <code>d[k]</code>, <code>d.setdefault(k, v)</code> and <code>defaultdict</code>?","a":"<p><code>d[k]</code> raises <code>KeyError</code> if missing. <code>d.get(k, default)</code> returns the default and does not insert. <code>setdefault</code> returns the value and inserts the default if missing. <code>defaultdict(list)</code> creates the default automatically on every missing key access. Use <code>defaultdict</code> for grouping and counting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0524","card":"q-database-with-python-084-dictionaries-and-sets","sec":"database-with-python-s085","q":"In Python, do dicts keep insertion order?","a":"<p>Yes, as a language guarantee from Python 3.7 (it was an implementation detail of CPython 3.6). Iteration returns keys in the order they were first inserted. Updating an existing key does not change its position.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0525","card":"q-database-with-python-084-dictionaries-and-sets","sec":"database-with-python-s085","q":"In Python, how do you invert a dict, and what is the pitfall?","a":"<p><code>{v: k for k, v in d.items()}</code>. If two keys share a value, the later one overwrites the earlier, and values must be hashable. To keep all keys, group them into lists with <code>defaultdict(list)</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0526","card":"q-database-with-python-084-dictionaries-and-sets","sec":"database-with-python-s085","q":"In Python, what do the set operators <code>|</code>, <code>&amp;</code>, <code>-</code> and <code>^</code> return?","a":"<p><code>s1 | s2</code> is the union <code>{1, 2, 3}</code>. <code>s1 &amp; s2</code> is the intersection <code>{2}</code>. <code>s1 - s2</code> is the difference <code>{1}</code>. <code>s1 ^ s2</code> is the symmetric difference <code>{1, 3}</code> (in exactly one of them).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>s1 = {1, 2}\ns2 = {2, 3}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0527","card":"q-database-with-python-084-dictionaries-and-sets","sec":"database-with-python-s085","q":"In Python, why does <code>{[1, 2], [3]}</code> fail, and what do you use instead?","a":"<p>Set members must be hashable and lists are not, so it raises <code>TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'</code>. Use tuples (<code>{(1, 2), (3,)}</code>) or frozensets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0528","card":"q-database-with-python-084-dictionaries-and-sets","sec":"database-with-python-s085","q":"In Python, how do you count how often each item appears in a list?","a":"<p>Use <code>collections.Counter(items)</code>, which is a dict subclass. <code>most_common(n)</code> returns the top counts. A plain loop with <code>d[x] = d.get(x, 0) + 1</code> does the same without an import.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0529","card":"q-database-with-python-084-dictionaries-and-sets","sec":"database-with-python-s085","q":"In Python, how does <code>{}</code> differ from <code>set()</code> for an empty set?","a":"<p><code>{}</code> is an empty dict. An empty set must be written <code>set()</code>, because braces with no items default to a dict literal.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0530","card":"q-database-with-python-085-functions-lambda-map-filter-reduce","sec":"database-with-python-s086","q":"What is a lambda and when do you use it?","a":"<p>An anonymous function for short tasks such as sorting keys.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>f = lambda x: x+1; print(f(2))  # 3</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0531","card":"q-database-with-python-085-functions-lambda-map-filter-reduce","sec":"database-with-python-s086","q":"<code>map()</code> vs <code>filter()</code> vs <code>reduce()</code>?","a":"<p><code>map()</code> transforms items, <code>filter()</code> selects items, <code>reduce()</code> aggregates items.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>print(list(map(lambda x: x*2, [1, 2])))  # [2, 4]\nprint(list(filter(lambda x: x &gt; 1, [1, 2])))  # [2]\nfrom functools import reduce; print(reduce(lambda x, y: x+y, [1, 2]))  # 3</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0532","card":"q-database-with-python-085-functions-lambda-map-filter-reduce","sec":"database-with-python-s086","q":"How do you define default arguments?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def func(x=1): print(x); func()  # 1</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0533","card":"q-database-with-python-085-functions-lambda-map-filter-reduce","sec":"database-with-python-s086","q":"How do you pass a function as an argument?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def call(f): f(); call(lambda: print(&quot;Hi&quot;))  # &quot;Hi&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0534","card":"q-database-with-python-085-functions-lambda-map-filter-reduce","sec":"database-with-python-s086","q":"In Python, what are the limits of a <code>lambda</code>, and when should you write a <code>def</code> instead?","a":"<p>A lambda is a single expression, so it cannot contain statements, assignments or multiple lines, and it has no name in tracebacks. Use it for short inline functions such as a sort <code>key</code>. Use <code>def</code> when it needs a name, a docstring, several steps or reuse.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0535","card":"q-database-with-python-085-functions-lambda-map-filter-reduce","sec":"database-with-python-s086","q":"In Python 3, what do <code>map()</code> and <code>filter()</code> return, and how do you get a list?","a":"<p>Both return lazy iterators (a <code>map</code> or <code>filter</code> object), not lists. Wrap with <code>list(...)</code> to materialise, and note that an iterator can be consumed only once. In Python 2 they returned lists.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0536","card":"q-database-with-python-085-functions-lambda-map-filter-reduce","sec":"database-with-python-s086","q":"In Python, when is a list comprehension better than <code>map</code> with a lambda?","a":"<p>A comprehension like <code>[x * 2 for x in data if x &gt; 0]</code> combines mapping and filtering, avoids the lambda call overhead, and is usually more readable. <code>map</code> is fine when you already have a named function, such as <code>map(str, nums)</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0537","card":"q-database-with-python-085-functions-lambda-map-filter-reduce","sec":"database-with-python-s086","q":"In Python, what is the role of the initial value in <code>functools.reduce</code>?","a":"<p>The optional third argument is the starting accumulator and the result for an empty iterable. Without it, <code>reduce</code> on an empty list raises <code>TypeError</code>. <code>reduce</code> lives in <code>functools</code> since Python 3. For sums, <code>sum()</code> is clearer.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from functools import reduce\nprint(reduce(lambda x, y: x + y, [1, 2]))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0538","card":"q-database-with-python-085-functions-lambda-map-filter-reduce","sec":"database-with-python-s086","q":"In Python, what is the pitfall of using a mutable default argument?","a":"<p>Default values are evaluated once, when the function is defined, so every call without <code>items</code> shares the same list: <code>add_item(1)</code> then <code>add_item(2)</code> returns <code>[1, 2]</code>. Use <code>items=None</code> and create <code>items = []</code> inside the function.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def add_item(item, items=[]):\n    items.append(item)\n    return items</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0539","card":"q-database-with-python-085-functions-lambda-map-filter-reduce","sec":"database-with-python-s086","q":"In Python, what is the late-binding pitfall with lambdas created in a loop?","a":"<p>It prints <code>[2, 2, 2]</code>. The lambdas look up <code>i</code> when called, not when created, and by then <code>i</code> is 2. Bind the value at definition with a default argument: <code>lambda i=i: i</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>funcs = [lambda: i for i in range(3)]\nprint([f() for f in funcs])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0540","card":"q-database-with-python-085-functions-lambda-map-filter-reduce","sec":"database-with-python-s086","q":"In Python, what does it mean that functions are first-class objects, and where is it useful?","a":"<p>A function can be assigned to a variable, passed as an argument, returned from another function and stored in a data structure. This enables callbacks, <code>sorted(key=...)</code>, decorators, dispatch dicts and closures.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0541","card":"q-database-with-python-086-functions-recursion-scope-decorators","sec":"database-with-python-s087","q":"What is recursion?","a":"<p>A function that calls itself, with a base case.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def fact(n): return 1 if n == 0 else n * fact(n-1)\nprint(fact(5))  # 120</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0542","card":"q-database-with-python-086-functions-recursion-scope-decorators","sec":"database-with-python-s087","q":"How does Python resolve variable scope (LEGB)?","a":"<p>Local, Enclosing, Global, Built-in.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>x = &quot;global&quot;; def f(): x = &quot;local&quot;; print(x); f()  # &quot;local&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0543","card":"q-database-with-python-086-functions-recursion-scope-decorators","sec":"database-with-python-s087","q":"What are decorators and how do you create one?","a":"<p>They modify a function using <code>@decorator</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def deco(func):\n    def wrapper(): print(&quot;Before&quot;); func()\n    return wrapper\n@deco\ndef hi(): print(&quot;Hi&quot;)\nhi()  # &quot;Before&quot;, &quot;Hi&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0544","card":"q-database-with-python-086-functions-recursion-scope-decorators","sec":"database-with-python-s087","q":"Why does the <code>global</code> keyword exist?","a":"<p>It lets a function modify a global variable.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>x = 1\ndef f():\n    global x\n    x = 2\n\nf()\nprint(x)  # 2</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0545","card":"q-database-with-python-086-functions-recursion-scope-decorators","sec":"database-with-python-s087","q":"In Python, what happens when recursion goes too deep, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>CPython has a recursion limit (about 1000 frames by default), and exceeding it raises <code>RecursionError</code>. <code>sys.setrecursionlimit</code> raises it but risks crashing the interpreter. Better: rewrite the algorithm as a loop with an explicit stack, or memoise to cut the depth. CPython does not do tail-call optimisation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0546","card":"q-database-with-python-086-functions-recursion-scope-decorators","sec":"database-with-python-s087","q":"In Python, what is the time complexity of the naive recursive Fibonacci, and how do you improve it?","a":"<p>It is exponential, about O(2^n), because it recomputes the same sub-problems. Memoisation with <code>functools.lru_cache</code> makes it O(n) time. An iterative loop is O(n) time and O(1) space.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0547","card":"q-database-with-python-086-functions-recursion-scope-decorators","sec":"database-with-python-s087","q":"In Python, why does this code raise <code>UnboundLocalError</code>, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>An assignment anywhere in a function makes the name local to that function. <code>x += 1</code> reads the local <code>x</code> before it has a value. Declare <code>global x</code> to change the module variable, or pass the value in and return the result, which is cleaner.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>x = 1\ndef f():\n    x += 1\n    return x</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0548","card":"q-database-with-python-086-functions-recursion-scope-decorators","sec":"database-with-python-s087","q":"In Python, what does the <code>nonlocal</code> keyword do, and how does it differ from <code>global</code>?","a":"<p><code>nonlocal</code> lets a nested function rebind a variable of the nearest enclosing function scope. <code>global</code> refers to the module-level scope. Without <code>nonlocal</code>, <code>count += 1</code> would raise <code>UnboundLocalError</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def counter():\n    count = 0\n    def inc():\n        nonlocal count\n        count += 1\n        return count\n    return inc</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0549","card":"q-database-with-python-086-functions-recursion-scope-decorators","sec":"database-with-python-s087","q":"In Python, why is using <code>global</code> variables for state generally discouraged?","a":"<p>Any function can change them, so behavior depends on hidden state, tests influence each other, and threads can race on the value. Pass state as arguments, return results, or keep it in an object or class.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0550","card":"q-database-with-python-086-functions-recursion-scope-decorators","sec":"database-with-python-s087","q":"In Python, what does the <code>@deco</code> syntax do when placed on the line before a function definition?","a":"<p><code>@deco</code> is shorthand for <code>hi = deco(hi)</code>. The name <code>hi</code> is rebound to the <code>wrapper</code> closure that adds behavior before or after calling the original. This is how logging, timing, caching and access checks are added without changing the function body.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def deco(func):\n    def wrapper(): print(&quot;Before&quot;); func()\n    return wrapper\n@deco\ndef hi(): print(&quot;Hi&quot;)\nhi()  # &quot;Before&quot;, &quot;Hi&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0551","card":"q-database-with-python-086-functions-recursion-scope-decorators","sec":"database-with-python-s087","q":"In Python, what is wrong with a decorator whose wrapper is <code>def wrapper(): func()</code>, and how do you make it general?","a":"<p>It accepts no arguments and drops the return value, so it breaks any function with parameters or a result. Write <code>def wrapper(*args, **kwargs): return func(*args, **kwargs)</code>, and add <code>@functools.wraps(func)</code> so the name and docstring survive.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0552","card":"q-database-with-python-086-functions-recursion-scope-decorators","sec":"database-with-python-s087","q":"In Python, why use <code>functools.wraps</code> inside a decorator?","a":"<p>Without it, the decorated function reports the wrapper's <code>__name__</code>, <code>__doc__</code> and signature, which breaks debugging, documentation and tools that read function metadata. <code>@wraps(func)</code> copies those attributes onto the wrapper.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0553","card":"q-database-with-python-086-functions-recursion-scope-decorators","sec":"database-with-python-s087","q":"In Python, how do you write a decorator that takes arguments, such as <code>@retry(3)</code>?","a":"<p>Add one more level: an outer function takes the arguments and returns the actual decorator, which returns the wrapper. <code>@retry(3)</code> calls <code>retry(3)</code> first, then applies the result to the function. That is three nested functions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0554","card":"q-database-with-python-086-functions-recursion-scope-decorators","sec":"database-with-python-s087","q":"In Python, what is a base case in recursion, and what happens without it?","a":"<p>The base case is the condition that stops the recursion without another call, such as <code>n == 0</code> for factorial. Without it, the function calls itself forever until <code>RecursionError</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0555","card":"q-database-with-python-087-copying-objects-and-exception-handling-i","sec":"database-with-python-s088","q":"Shallow vs deep copy?","a":"<p>A shallow copy copies the top level and references nested objects. A deep copy copies every level.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import copy\nlst = [[1]]\ns = copy.copy(lst)\nd = copy.deepcopy(lst)\ns[0][0] = 2\nprint(lst)  # [[2]]\nprint(d)  # [[1]]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0556","card":"q-database-with-python-087-copying-objects-and-exception-handling-i","sec":"database-with-python-s088","q":"How do you handle exceptions in a function?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def div(a, b):\n    try:\n        return a / b\n    except ZeroDivisionError:\n        return &quot;Error&quot;\n\nprint(div(1, 0))  # &quot;Error&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0557","card":"q-database-with-python-087-copying-objects-and-exception-handling-i","sec":"database-with-python-s088","q":"In Python, which of these make a shallow copy, and what does <code>copy.deepcopy</code> add?","a":"<p><code>a</code>, <code>b</code> and <code>c</code> are all shallow copies: new outer list, same inner objects. <code>deepcopy</code> also copies the nested objects recursively and handles cycles, so <code>d</code> is fully independent. Deep copies cost more time and memory, and sharing inner objects is sometimes intended.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import copy\nlst = [[1]]\na = lst.copy()\nb = lst[:]\nc = list(lst)\nd = copy.deepcopy(lst)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0558","card":"q-database-with-python-087-copying-objects-and-exception-handling-i","sec":"database-with-python-s088","q":"In Python, does <code>b = a</code> copy a list?","a":"<p>No. It binds a second name to the same object. Mutations through either name are visible through both. Use <code>copy</code> methods when you need a separate object.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0559","card":"q-database-with-python-087-copying-objects-and-exception-handling-i","sec":"database-with-python-s088","q":"In Python, why is <code>return \"Error\"</code> inside <code>except ZeroDivisionError</code> a weak way to handle errors in a function?","a":"<p>The function now returns a number or a string, so every caller must check the type, and a forgotten check silently propagates a string. Better to let the exception reach a caller who can decide, raise a clearer custom exception, or return <code>None</code> and document it.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def div(a, b):\n    try:\n        return a / b\n    except ZeroDivisionError:\n        return &quot;Error&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0560","card":"q-database-with-python-087-copying-objects-and-exception-handling-i","sec":"database-with-python-s088","q":"In Python, where should you catch an exception: in the low-level function or in a caller?","a":"<p>Catch it where you can do something useful: retry, use a fallback, log with context or show a message. Low-level code that cannot recover should let the exception propagate (optionally wrapping it with <code>raise ... from e</code>). Catching everywhere hides failures.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0561","card":"q-database-with-python-088-oop-basics-classes-inheritance-overridin","sec":"database-with-python-s089","q":"What are the key OOP principles?","a":"<p>Encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism, abstraction. A class is a blueprint and an object is an instance.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class C:\n    pass\n\nobj = C()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0562","card":"q-database-with-python-088-oop-basics-classes-inheritance-overridin","sec":"database-with-python-s089","q":"How does inheritance work?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class A:\n    def x(self):\n        print(&quot;A&quot;)\n\nclass B(A):\n    pass\n\nB().x()  # &quot;A&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0563","card":"q-database-with-python-088-oop-basics-classes-inheritance-overridin","sec":"database-with-python-s089","q":"What is method overriding?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class A:\n    def x(self):\n        print(&quot;A&quot;)\n\nclass B(A):\n    def x(self):\n        print(&quot;B&quot;)\n\nB().x()  # &quot;B&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0564","card":"q-database-with-python-088-oop-basics-classes-inheritance-overridin","sec":"database-with-python-s089","q":"What is the purpose of <code>__init__</code>?","a":"<p>It initializes an instance.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class C:\n    def __init__(self, x):\n        self.x = x\n\nc = C(5)\nprint(c.x)  # 5</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0565","card":"q-database-with-python-088-oop-basics-classes-inheritance-overridin","sec":"database-with-python-s089","q":"In Python, what does <code>super()</code> do, and why use it instead of naming the parent class?","a":"<p><code>super()</code> returns a proxy that calls the next class in the method resolution order. It avoids hard-coding the parent name, and it makes cooperative multiple inheritance work. Without <code>super().__init__()</code> here, <code>self.x</code> would never be set.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class A:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.x = 1\n\nclass B(A):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.y = 2</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0566","card":"q-database-with-python-088-oop-basics-classes-inheritance-overridin","sec":"database-with-python-s089","q":"In Python, what is the method resolution order (MRO), and how is it computed with multiple inheritance?","a":"<p>The MRO is the order in which Python searches base classes for an attribute. It uses the C3 linearisation, which keeps each class before its parents and keeps the order of the base list. View it with <code>ClassName.__mro__</code>. It resolves the diamond problem so a shared base is called once.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0567","card":"q-database-with-python-088-oop-basics-classes-inheritance-overridin","sec":"database-with-python-s089","q":"In Python, why is there no method overloading by argument count, and what do you use instead?","a":"<p>A second <code>def</code> with the same name just replaces the first. Use default arguments, <code>*args</code>, <code>functools.singledispatch</code> for type-based dispatch, or separate methods and <code>@classmethod</code> constructors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0568","card":"q-database-with-python-088-oop-basics-classes-inheritance-overridin","sec":"database-with-python-s089","q":"In Python, what is the difference between <code>__init__</code> and <code>__new__</code>?","a":"<p><code>__new__</code> creates and returns the instance and is rarely overridden (immutable types, singletons). <code>__init__</code> then initialises the already-created instance and must return <code>None</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0569","card":"q-database-with-python-088-oop-basics-classes-inheritance-overridin","sec":"database-with-python-s089","q":"In Python, what is the pitfall of putting a list at class level?","a":"<p><code>items</code> belongs to the class, so all instances share the same list and one basket's items appear in another. Create it per instance in <code>__init__</code> with <code>self.items = []</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class Basket:\n    items = []\n\n    def add(self, x):\n        self.items.append(x)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0570","card":"q-database-with-python-088-oop-basics-classes-inheritance-overridin","sec":"database-with-python-s089","q":"In Python, when should you prefer composition over inheritance?","a":"<p>Use inheritance for a true \"is-a\" relationship. Use composition (\"has-a\") when you only want to reuse behavior or when parent changes would otherwise break the subclass. Composition gives looser coupling and is easier to swap in tests.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0571","card":"q-database-with-python-088-oop-basics-classes-inheritance-overridin","sec":"database-with-python-s089","q":"In Python, what is the difference between overriding and extending a parent method?","a":"<p>Overriding replaces the parent's behavior. Extending overrides but also calls <code>super().method()</code> to keep the parent's behavior and add to it. Constructors almost always extend.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0572","card":"q-database-with-python-089-oop-self-vs-cls-static-vs-class-methods","sec":"database-with-python-s090","q":"<code>self</code> vs <code>cls</code>?","a":"<p><code>self</code> refers to the instance. <code>cls</code> refers to the class.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class C:\n    @classmethod\n    def f(cls):\n        print(cls)\n\nC.f()  # &quot;&lt;class &#x27;__main__.C&#x27;&gt;&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0573","card":"q-database-with-python-089-oop-self-vs-cls-static-vs-class-methods","sec":"database-with-python-s090","q":"Static vs class methods?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class C:\n    @staticmethod\n    def s():\n        print(&quot;Static&quot;)\n\n    @classmethod\n    def c(cls):\n        print(&quot;Class&quot;)\n\nC.s()\nC.c()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0574","card":"q-database-with-python-089-oop-self-vs-cls-static-vs-class-methods","sec":"database-with-python-s090","q":"In Python, when is a <code>@classmethod</code> the right choice, for example for an alternative constructor?","a":"<p>A class method receives the class as <code>cls</code>, so <code>cls(...)</code> builds an instance of whichever class it was called on, including subclasses. This is why alternative constructors use <code>@classmethod</code> rather than a hard-coded class name.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class Date:\n    def __init__(self, y, m, d):\n        self.y, self.m, self.d = y, m, d\n\n    @classmethod\n    def from_string(cls, s):\n        y, m, d = map(int, s.split(&quot;-&quot;))\n        return cls(y, m, d)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0575","card":"q-database-with-python-089-oop-self-vs-cls-static-vs-class-methods","sec":"database-with-python-s090","q":"In Python, when would you use a <code>@staticmethod</code> instead of a module-level function?","a":"<p>A static method needs neither the instance nor the class. It is a plain function placed in the class namespace because it logically belongs to it. If it does not matter for organisation, a module-level function is just as good.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0576","card":"q-database-with-python-089-oop-self-vs-cls-static-vs-class-methods","sec":"database-with-python-s090","q":"In Python, how is <code>self</code> filled in when you call <code>obj.method()</code>?","a":"<p>A function accessed through an instance becomes a bound method: Python passes the instance as the first argument automatically. <code>obj.method()</code> is the same as <code>Class.method(obj)</code>. The name <code>self</code> is only a convention.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0577","card":"q-database-with-python-089-oop-self-vs-cls-static-vs-class-methods","sec":"database-with-python-s090","q":"In Python, what is the difference between an instance method, a class method and a static method in what they can access?","a":"<p>An instance method gets <code>self</code> and can read and change instance and class state. A class method gets <code>cls</code> and can read and change class state but not a particular instance. A static method gets neither and only works with its arguments.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0578","card":"q-database-with-python-090-oop-polymorphism-encapsulation-abstract","sec":"database-with-python-s091","q":"Show polymorphism.","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class Dog:\n    def sound(self):\n        return &quot;Woof&quot;\n\nclass Cat:\n    def sound(self):\n        return &quot;Meow&quot;\n\nfor a in [Dog(), Cat()]:\n    print(a.sound())  # &quot;Woof&quot;, &quot;Meow&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0579","card":"q-database-with-python-090-oop-polymorphism-encapsulation-abstract","sec":"database-with-python-s091","q":"Show encapsulation.","a":"<p>A double underscore makes an attribute private (name-mangled), exposed through a method.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class C:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.__x = 1\n\n    def get(self):\n        return self.__x\n\nc = C()\nprint(c.get())  # 1</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0580","card":"q-database-with-python-090-oop-polymorphism-encapsulation-abstract","sec":"database-with-python-s091","q":"What are abstract base classes (ABCs)?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n\nclass A(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    def m(self):\n        pass\n\nclass B(A):\n    def m(self):\n        print(&quot;B&quot;)\n\nB().m()  # &quot;B&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0581","card":"q-database-with-python-090-oop-polymorphism-encapsulation-abstract","sec":"database-with-python-s091","q":"<code>__str__</code> vs <code>__repr__</code>?","a":"<p><code>__str__</code> is the user-friendly representation. <code>__repr__</code> is the developer and debugging representation.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class C:\n    def __str__(self): return &quot;Hi&quot;\n    def __repr__(self): return &quot;C()&quot;\n\nc = C()\nprint(str(c))  # &quot;Hi&quot;\nprint(repr(c))  # &quot;C()&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0582","card":"q-database-with-python-090-oop-polymorphism-encapsulation-abstract","sec":"database-with-python-s091","q":"In Python, what is duck typing, and how does it relate to polymorphism?","a":"<p>Duck typing means the code only needs an object to have the required methods, not to inherit from a specific class: \"if it quacks like a duck\". Polymorphism in Python therefore works without a shared base class, as with two classes that both define <code>sound()</code>. <code>typing.Protocol</code> (3.8+) describes such an interface for type checkers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0583","card":"q-database-with-python-090-oop-polymorphism-encapsulation-abstract","sec":"database-with-python-s091","q":"In Python, does a double underscore attribute make data truly private?","a":"<p>No. Python renames <code>__x</code> to <code>_C__x</code> (name mangling) to avoid clashes in subclasses, but <code>c._C__x</code> is still reachable. Privacy is by convention: a single underscore means \"internal\", and a <code>@property</code> gives controlled access.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class C:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.__x = 1\n\n    def get(self):\n        return self.__x</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0584","card":"q-database-with-python-090-oop-polymorphism-encapsulation-abstract","sec":"database-with-python-s091","q":"In Python, why use <code>@property</code> instead of a public attribute plus a getter and setter?","a":"<p>A property lets callers use plain attribute syntax (<code>obj.age</code>) while you add validation or computation behind it, and you can start with a simple attribute and add the property later without changing callers. It gives encapsulation without Java-style <code>get</code>/<code>set</code> methods.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0585","card":"q-database-with-python-090-oop-polymorphism-encapsulation-abstract","sec":"database-with-python-s091","q":"In Python, what is the purpose of an abstract base class (<code>ABC</code>) with <code>@abstractmethod</code>?","a":"<p>It defines an interface that subclasses must implement. Instantiating <code>A</code>, or a subclass that did not implement <code>m</code>, raises <code>TypeError</code>. This catches missing methods at object creation instead of during a later call.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n\nclass A(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    def m(self):\n        pass</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0586","card":"q-database-with-python-090-oop-polymorphism-encapsulation-abstract","sec":"database-with-python-s091","q":"In Python, how does an <code>ABC</code> differ from a <code>typing.Protocol</code>?","a":"<p>An <code>ABC</code> uses nominal typing: a class must inherit from it (or be registered). A <code>Protocol</code> uses structural typing: any class with the right methods matches, with no inheritance, and it is checked by static type checkers. Use <code>Protocol</code> for duck-typed interfaces.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0587","card":"q-database-with-python-090-oop-polymorphism-encapsulation-abstract","sec":"database-with-python-s091","q":"In Python, what is the difference between <code>__str__</code> and <code>__repr__</code>, and which one is used when printing a list of objects?","a":"<p><code>str(obj)</code> and <code>print(obj)</code> use <code>__str__</code> for readable output. <code>repr(obj)</code> should be unambiguous and ideally look like code that recreates the object. If <code>__str__</code> is missing Python falls back to <code>__repr__</code>. Containers show their items with <code>__repr__</code>, so <code>print([C()])</code> shows <code>[C()]</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class C:\n    def __str__(self): return &quot;Hi&quot;\n    def __repr__(self): return &quot;C()&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0588","card":"q-database-with-python-090-oop-polymorphism-encapsulation-abstract","sec":"database-with-python-s091","q":"In Python, why does defining <code>__eq__</code> on a class make instances unhashable, and how do you fix that?","a":"<p>When a class defines <code>__eq__</code> without <code>__hash__</code>, Python sets <code>__hash__</code> to <code>None</code>, so instances cannot be dict keys or set members. Define <code>__hash__</code> consistently with <code>__eq__</code> (equal objects must have equal hashes), or use a frozen dataclass.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0589","card":"q-database-with-python-091-data-structures-stack-and-queue","sec":"database-with-python-s092","q":"How do you implement a stack?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class Stack:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.s = []\n\n    def push(self, x):\n        self.s.append(x)\n\n    def pop(self):\n        return self.s.pop() if self.s else None\n\ns = Stack()\ns.push(1)\nprint(s.pop())  # 1</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0590","card":"q-database-with-python-091-data-structures-stack-and-queue","sec":"database-with-python-s092","q":"How do you implement a queue?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from collections import deque\n\nclass Queue:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.q = deque()\n\n    def enqueue(self, x):\n        self.q.append(x)\n\n    def dequeue(self):\n        return self.q.popleft() if self.q else None\n\nq = Queue()\nq.enqueue(1)\nprint(q.dequeue())  # 1</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0591","card":"q-database-with-python-091-data-structures-stack-and-queue","sec":"database-with-python-s092","q":"In Python, how do you implement a stack, and why is a plain list a good choice?","a":"<p>Use <code>list.append</code> to push and <code>list.pop()</code> to pop from the end. Both are amortised O(1). A stack is LIFO and is used for undo, expression parsing, backtracking and DFS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0592","card":"q-database-with-python-091-data-structures-stack-and-queue","sec":"database-with-python-s092","q":"In Python, what is the difference between <code>collections.deque</code> and <code>queue.Queue</code>?","a":"<p><code>deque</code> is a fast general container; its <code>append</code> and <code>popleft</code> are thread-safe but do not block. <code>queue.Queue</code> adds locking, a maximum size and blocking <code>get</code>/<code>put</code> with timeouts, which is what producer-consumer thread code needs. Use <code>asyncio.Queue</code> for coroutines and <code>multiprocessing.Queue</code> across processes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0593","card":"q-database-with-python-091-data-structures-stack-and-queue","sec":"database-with-python-s092","q":"In this Python stack, what is the problem with <code>pop</code> returning <code>None</code> on an empty stack?","a":"<p>If <code>None</code> can be pushed as a value, the caller cannot tell an empty stack from a stored <code>None</code>. Raising an exception such as <code>IndexError</code> on an empty pop (as <code>list.pop</code> does) is clearer and fails early.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class Stack:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.s = []\n\n    def push(self, x):\n        self.s.append(x)\n\n    def pop(self):\n        return self.s.pop() if self.s else None</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0594","card":"q-database-with-python-091-data-structures-stack-and-queue","sec":"database-with-python-s092","q":"In Python, how would you implement a priority queue?","a":"<p>Use <code>heapq</code> on a list of <code>(priority, item)</code> tuples, or <code>queue.PriorityQueue</code> when you need thread safety. Push and pop are O(log n) and the smallest priority comes out first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0595","card":"q-database-with-python-091-data-structures-stack-and-queue","sec":"database-with-python-s092","q":"In Python, how do you check that brackets in a string are balanced?","a":"<p>Scan the string with a stack. Push each opening bracket. On a closing bracket, pop and check that it matches, failing if the stack is empty. At the end the stack must be empty. It is O(n) time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0596","card":"q-database-with-python-092-algorithms-binary-search-linked-lists-ma","sec":"database-with-python-s093","q":"How do you do binary search?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def binary_search(arr, x):\n    l, r = 0, len(arr) - 1\n    while l &lt;= r:\n        m = (l + r) // 2\n        if arr[m] == x:\n            return m\n        elif arr[m] &lt; x:\n            l = m + 1\n        else:\n            r = m - 1\n    return -1\n\nprint(binary_search([1, 3, 5], 3))  # 1</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0597","card":"q-database-with-python-092-algorithms-binary-search-linked-lists-ma","sec":"database-with-python-s093","q":"How do you reverse a linked list?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class Node:\n    def __init__(self, data):\n        self.data = data\n        self.next = None\n\ndef reverse(head):\n    prev, curr = None, head\n    while curr:\n        next_n = curr.next\n        curr.next = prev\n        prev = curr\n        curr = next_n\n    return prev\n\nhead = Node(1)\nhead.next = Node(2)\nhead = reverse(head)\nprint(head.data)  # 2</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0598","card":"q-database-with-python-092-algorithms-binary-search-linked-lists-ma","sec":"database-with-python-s093","q":"How do you detect a cycle in a linked list?","a":"<p>Floyd's method: step a slow and a fast iterator, and a cycle exists if they meet.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def has_cycle(head):\n    slow = fast = head\n    while fast and fast.next:\n        slow = slow.next\n        fast = fast.next.next\n        if slow == fast:\n            return True\n    return False</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0599","card":"q-database-with-python-092-algorithms-binary-search-linked-lists-ma","sec":"database-with-python-s093","q":"How do you find the max without <code>max()</code>?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def find_max(lst):\n    m = lst[0]\n    for x in lst:\n        m = x if x &gt; m else m\n    return m\n\nprint(find_max([1, 5, 3]))  # 5</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0600","card":"q-database-with-python-092-algorithms-binary-search-linked-lists-ma","sec":"database-with-python-s093","q":"In Python, what precondition does binary search need, and why is the loop condition <code>l &lt;= r</code>?","a":"<p>The list must be sorted. Each step halves the range, so it is O(log n). Both ends are inclusive indexes, so a range with one element has <code>l == r</code> and must still be checked. Using <code>&lt;</code> would skip it.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def binary_search(arr, x):\n    l, r = 0, len(arr) - 1\n    while l &lt;= r:\n        m = (l + r) // 2\n        if arr[m] == x:\n            return m\n        elif arr[m] &lt; x:\n            l = m + 1\n        else:\n            r = m - 1\n    return -1</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0601","card":"q-database-with-python-092-algorithms-binary-search-linked-lists-ma","sec":"database-with-python-s093","q":"In Python, what does the binary search code return when the value occurs several times, and how do you find the first occurrence?","a":"<p>It returns whichever matching index the midpoint reaches first, not necessarily the first. For the leftmost match use <code>bisect.bisect_left(arr, x)</code> and check the item at that index, or keep searching left after a match.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0602","card":"q-database-with-python-092-algorithms-binary-search-linked-lists-ma","sec":"database-with-python-s093","q":"In Python, why prefer the <code>bisect</code> module over hand-written binary search?","a":"<p><code>bisect_left</code> and <code>bisect_right</code> are tested, fast (implemented in C) and also give the insert position that keeps a list sorted (<code>insort</code>). Hand-written loops are an interview exercise, with off-by-one risks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0603","card":"q-database-with-python-092-algorithms-binary-search-linked-lists-ma","sec":"database-with-python-s093","q":"In Python, how does a linked list differ from the built-in <code>list</code>, and when is it better?","a":"<p>A <code>list</code> is a dynamic array: O(1) index access, O(n) insert or delete in the middle, good cache locality. A linked list has O(n) access but O(1) insert or delete when you already hold the node. In Python the built-in list or <code>deque</code> is nearly always faster in practice, so linked lists are mostly for learning or special structures.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0604","card":"q-database-with-python-092-algorithms-binary-search-linked-lists-ma","sec":"database-with-python-s093","q":"In Python, why must <code>curr.next</code> be saved before reassigning it in a linked list reversal?","a":"<p>Setting <code>curr.next = prev</code> overwrites the only link to the rest of the list. Saving it in <code>next_n</code> first lets the loop continue. The algorithm is O(n) time and O(1) extra space.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def reverse(head):\n    prev, curr = None, head\n    while curr:\n        next_n = curr.next\n        curr.next = prev\n        prev = curr\n        curr = next_n\n    return prev</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0605","card":"q-database-with-python-092-algorithms-binary-search-linked-lists-ma","sec":"database-with-python-s093","q":"In Python, why do the slow and fast iterators in Floyd's cycle detection meet if the list has a cycle?","a":"<p>Once both are inside the cycle, the fast one gains one node on the slow one each step, so the gap shrinks by one until it is zero. Without a cycle, the fast one reaches <code>None</code>. It uses O(1) space, unlike storing visited nodes in a set (O(n)).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0606","card":"q-database-with-python-092-algorithms-binary-search-linked-lists-ma","sec":"database-with-python-s093","q":"In Python, how do you find where the cycle starts after Floyd's algorithm detects one?","a":"<p>Keep one iterator at the meeting point and move another from the head, both one step at a time. They meet at the first node of the cycle.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0607","card":"q-database-with-python-092-algorithms-binary-search-linked-lists-ma","sec":"database-with-python-s093","q":"In Python, what is wrong with this max function for an empty list?","a":"<p><code>lst[0]</code> raises <code>IndexError</code> on an empty list. Decide the contract: raise a clear <code>ValueError</code>, or return a default. The built-in <code>max(lst, default=None)</code> has a <code>default</code> parameter for this. The loop is O(n) and it can start from index 1.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def find_max(lst):\n    m = lst[0]\n    for x in lst:\n        m = x if x &gt; m else m\n    return m</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0608","card":"q-database-with-python-093-data-structures-hash-table-heap-complexi","sec":"database-with-python-s094","q":"How do you implement a hash table?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class HashTable:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.t = [[] for _ in range(10)]\n\n    def put(self, k, v):\n        i = hash(k) % 10\n        self.t[i].append((k, v))\n\n    def get(self, k):\n        i = hash(k) % 10\n        return next((v for x, v in self.t[i] if x == k), None)\n\nht = HashTable()\nht.put(&quot;a&quot;, 1)\nprint(ht.get(&quot;a&quot;))  # 1</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0609","card":"q-database-with-python-093-data-structures-hash-table-heap-complexi","sec":"database-with-python-s094","q":"How do you use a heap?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import heapq\n\nh = [3, 1, 4]\nheapq.heapify(h)\nprint(heapq.heappop(h))  # 1</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0610","card":"q-database-with-python-093-data-structures-hash-table-heap-complexi","sec":"database-with-python-s094","q":"What is the time complexity of list operations?","a":"<p><code>append</code> O(1), <code>pop()</code> O(1), <code>pop(i)</code> and <code>insert</code> O(n), <code>remove</code> O(n), <code>in</code> O(n).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0611","card":"q-database-with-python-093-data-structures-hash-table-heap-complexi","sec":"database-with-python-s094","q":"BFS vs DFS?","a":"<p>BFS uses a queue and goes level by level, O(V+E). DFS uses a stack or recursion and goes depth first, O(V+E).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0612","card":"q-database-with-python-093-data-structures-hash-table-heap-complexi","sec":"database-with-python-s094","q":"In Python, how does a hash table handle two keys that land in the same slot?","a":"<p>That is a collision. Chaining stores a list per slot, which the simple implementation uses. Open addressing probes for another free slot, which is what CPython's <code>dict</code> does. The average lookup is O(1) while the load factor stays low, and the table resizes as it fills. With many collisions, lookup degrades toward O(n).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0613","card":"q-database-with-python-093-data-structures-hash-table-heap-complexi","sec":"database-with-python-s094","q":"In this simple Python hash table, what happens when you <code>put</code> the same key twice?","a":"<p><code>put</code> always appends, so the key appears twice in its bucket and <code>get</code> finds the first entry, returning the old value. A correct <code>put</code> scans the bucket and replaces the value when the key exists.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def put(self, k, v):\n    i = hash(k) % 10\n    self.t[i].append((k, v))\n\ndef get(self, k):\n    i = hash(k) % 10\n    return next((v for x, v in self.t[i] if x == k), None)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0614","card":"q-database-with-python-093-data-structures-hash-table-heap-complexi","sec":"database-with-python-s094","q":"In Python, why can the order of items stored by <code>hash(key) % n</code> change between program runs for string keys?","a":"<p>String hashes are randomised per process (hash randomisation, on by default since Python 3.3, controlled by <code>PYTHONHASHSEED</code>) to prevent hash-flooding attacks. Never depend on hash values or set ordering. Use a dict when you need a stable order.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0615","card":"q-database-with-python-093-data-structures-hash-table-heap-complexi","sec":"database-with-python-s094","q":"In Python, how does <code>heapq</code> work, and how do you get a max-heap?","a":"<p><code>heapq</code> stores a binary min-heap in a normal list: the smallest item is always <code>h[0]</code>, <code>heappush</code> and <code>heappop</code> are O(log n) and <code>heapify</code> is O(n). The list is not fully sorted. For a max-heap push negated values (or negated priorities) and negate again on pop.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import heapq\n\nh = [3, 1, 4]\nheapq.heapify(h)\nprint(heapq.heappop(h))  # 1</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0616","card":"q-database-with-python-093-data-structures-hash-table-heap-complexi","sec":"database-with-python-s094","q":"In Python, how do you get the k largest items from a large list efficiently?","a":"<p><code>heapq.nlargest(k, items)</code> keeps a heap of size k, giving O(n log k) time, which is better than sorting everything (O(n log n)) when k is small. For k = 1 use <code>max</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0617","card":"q-database-with-python-093-data-structures-hash-table-heap-complexi","sec":"database-with-python-s094","q":"In Python, what are the complexities of <code>x in list</code>, <code>x in set</code>, <code>list.insert(0, x)</code> and <code>dict[key]</code>?","a":"<p><code>x in list</code> is O(n). <code>x in set</code> and <code>dict[key]</code> are O(1) on average. <code>list.insert(0, x)</code> is O(n) because elements shift. <code>list.append</code> is amortised O(1). Sorting is O(n log n).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0618","card":"q-database-with-python-093-data-structures-hash-table-heap-complexi","sec":"database-with-python-s094","q":"In Python, what does \"amortised O(1)\" mean for <code>list.append</code>?","a":"<p>Most appends are O(1), but occasionally the list is full and must reallocate and copy to a larger array. Over many appends the average cost per append is constant because the capacity grows geometrically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0619","card":"q-database-with-python-093-data-structures-hash-table-heap-complexi","sec":"database-with-python-s094","q":"When should you choose BFS over DFS, and which data structure does each use?","a":"<p>Use BFS (queue) to find the shortest path in an unweighted graph or to process level by level. Use DFS (stack or recursion) for exhaustive search, cycle detection, topological sort or when the graph is deep and narrow. Both are O(V + E). BFS memory grows with the widest level, DFS with the depth, and recursive DFS can hit Python's recursion limit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0620","card":"q-database-with-python-094-file-handling-read-and-write","sec":"database-with-python-s095","q":"How do you read a file line by line?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>with open(&quot;file.txt&quot;) as f:\n    [print(line.strip()) for line in f]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0621","card":"q-database-with-python-094-file-handling-read-and-write","sec":"database-with-python-s095","q":"<code>read()</code> vs <code>readline()</code> vs <code>readlines()</code>?","a":"<p><code>read()</code> returns all content, <code>readline()</code> one line, <code>readlines()</code> all lines as a list.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>with open(&quot;file.txt&quot;) as f:\n    print(f.read())</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0622","card":"q-database-with-python-094-file-handling-read-and-write","sec":"database-with-python-s095","q":"How do you write and append to a file?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>with open(&quot;file.txt&quot;, &quot;w&quot;) as f:\n    f.write(&quot;Hi&quot;)\n\nwith open(&quot;file.txt&quot;, &quot;a&quot;) as f:\n    f.write(&quot;More&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0623","card":"q-database-with-python-094-file-handling-read-and-write","sec":"database-with-python-s095","q":"How do you handle file exceptions?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>try:\n    open(&quot;file.txt&quot;)\nexcept FileNotFoundError:\n    print(&quot;Not found&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0624","card":"q-database-with-python-094-file-handling-read-and-write","sec":"database-with-python-s095","q":"In Python, why should you open files with a <code>with</code> statement?","a":"<p><code>with</code> closes the file when the block ends, even if an exception occurs, so buffered data is flushed and file descriptors are not leaked. Without it you must call <code>close()</code> in a <code>finally</code> block.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>with open(&quot;file.txt&quot;) as f:\n    [print(line.strip()) for line in f]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0625","card":"q-database-with-python-094-file-handling-read-and-write","sec":"database-with-python-s095","q":"In Python, what is the problem with using a list comprehension to print lines, as in <code>[print(line.strip()) for line in f]</code>?","a":"<p>It builds a list of <code>None</code> values only to throw it away, and it uses a comprehension for side effects. A plain <code>for line in f: print(line.strip())</code> is clearer and does not build a list.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0626","card":"q-database-with-python-094-file-handling-read-and-write","sec":"database-with-python-s095","q":"In Python, why can <code>f.read()</code> be a problem for a large file, and what do you do?","a":"<p>It loads the entire file into memory. Iterate over the file object (<code>for line in f</code>), which reads line by line from a buffer, or read fixed-size chunks with <code>f.read(size)</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0627","card":"q-database-with-python-094-file-handling-read-and-write","sec":"database-with-python-s095","q":"In Python, what is the difference between the file modes <code>\"w\"</code>, <code>\"a\"</code>, <code>\"x\"</code> and <code>\"r+\"</code>?","a":"<p><code>\"w\"</code> creates the file or truncates an existing one. <code>\"a\"</code> appends at the end and creates if missing. <code>\"x\"</code> creates and fails with <code>FileExistsError</code> if it exists. <code>\"r+\"</code> reads and writes without truncating and requires the file to exist.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0628","card":"q-database-with-python-094-file-handling-read-and-write","sec":"database-with-python-s095","q":"In Python, why pass <code>encoding=\"utf-8\"</code> to <code>open()</code> for text files?","a":"<p>Without it, the default encoding depends on the platform and locale, so the same code can read or write different bytes on different machines or raise <code>UnicodeDecodeError</code>. Setting the encoding explicitly makes behavior portable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0629","card":"q-database-with-python-094-file-handling-read-and-write","sec":"database-with-python-s095","q":"In Python, does <code>f.write(\"Hi\")</code> add a newline?","a":"<p>No. <code>write</code> writes exactly the string and returns the number of characters written. Add <code>\"\\n\"</code> yourself. <code>print(..., file=f)</code> adds a newline automatically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0630","card":"q-database-with-python-094-file-handling-read-and-write","sec":"database-with-python-s095","q":"In Python, is checking <code>os.path.exists</code> before opening a file a good way to avoid <code>FileNotFoundError</code>?","a":"<p>Not reliably. The file can be deleted or created between the check and the open (a race, TOCTOU), so you still need the exception handling. The usual Python style (EAFP) is to try to open and catch <code>FileNotFoundError</code> or <code>PermissionError</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0631","card":"q-database-with-python-094-file-handling-read-and-write","sec":"database-with-python-s095","q":"In Python, why is <code>pathlib.Path</code> often preferred over <code>os.path</code> strings?","a":"<p><code>Path</code> objects offer readable methods (<code>p / \"sub\"</code>, <code>p.read_text()</code>, <code>p.exists()</code>, <code>p.suffix</code>), handle OS-specific separators, and avoid string concatenation bugs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0632","card":"q-database-with-python-095-file-handling-csv-json-binary-delete","sec":"database-with-python-s096","q":"How do you read and write CSV?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import csv\n\nwith open(&quot;data.csv&quot;, &quot;w&quot;, newline=&quot;&quot;) as f:\n    csv.writer(f).writerow([&quot;a&quot;, 1])</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0633","card":"q-database-with-python-095-file-handling-csv-json-binary-delete","sec":"database-with-python-s096","q":"How do you work with JSON files?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import json\n\nwith open(&quot;data.json&quot;, &quot;w&quot;) as f:\n    json.dump({&quot;a&quot;: 1}, f)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0634","card":"q-database-with-python-095-file-handling-csv-json-binary-delete","sec":"database-with-python-s096","q":"Binary vs text modes?","a":"<p>Text modes <code>\"r\"</code> and <code>\"w\"</code> use strings. Binary modes <code>\"rb\"</code> and <code>\"wb\"</code> use bytes.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>with open(&quot;file.bin&quot;, &quot;wb&quot;) as f:\n    f.write(b&quot;Hi&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0635","card":"q-database-with-python-095-file-handling-csv-json-binary-delete","sec":"database-with-python-s096","q":"How do you delete a file?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import os\nos.remove(&quot;file.txt&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0636","card":"q-database-with-python-095-file-handling-csv-json-binary-delete","sec":"database-with-python-s096","q":"In Python, why do you open a CSV file with <code>newline=\"\"</code> when using the <code>csv</code> module?","a":"<p>The <code>csv</code> module handles line endings itself. Without <code>newline=\"\"</code>, Python translates line endings too, which can produce blank lines on Windows and breaks fields that contain embedded newlines.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import csv\n\nwith open(&quot;data.csv&quot;, &quot;w&quot;, newline=&quot;&quot;) as f:\n    csv.writer(f).writerow([&quot;a&quot;, 1])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0637","card":"q-database-with-python-095-file-handling-csv-json-binary-delete","sec":"database-with-python-s096","q":"In Python, why use the <code>csv</code> module instead of <code>line.split(\",\")</code>?","a":"<p>Real CSV has quoted fields that contain commas, quotes and newlines. <code>csv</code> handles quoting and escaping correctly, while <code>split</code> breaks on those fields. <code>csv.DictReader</code> and <code>DictWriter</code> also give access by column name.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0638","card":"q-database-with-python-095-file-handling-csv-json-binary-delete","sec":"database-with-python-s096","q":"In Python, when would you use <code>pandas.read_csv</code> instead of the <code>csv</code> module?","a":"<p>Use pandas for analysis: it infers types, handles missing values and offers vectorised operations. <code>chunksize</code> lets it stream big files. Use <code>csv</code> for simple row-by-row processing without a heavy dependency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0639","card":"q-database-with-python-095-file-handling-csv-json-binary-delete","sec":"database-with-python-s096","q":"In Python, what is the difference between <code>json.dump</code> and <code>json.dumps</code>, and between <code>json.load</code> and <code>json.loads</code>?","a":"<p><code>dump</code> and <code>load</code> work with file objects. <code>dumps</code> and <code>loads</code> work with strings (the trailing <code>s</code> means string). Use the string forms for HTTP bodies and messages.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import json\n\nwith open(&quot;data.json&quot;, &quot;w&quot;) as f:\n    json.dump({&quot;a&quot;: 1}, f)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0640","card":"q-database-with-python-095-file-handling-csv-json-binary-delete","sec":"database-with-python-s096","q":"In Python, what happens when you <code>json.dumps</code> a <code>datetime</code>, a <code>set</code>, or a dict with integer keys?","a":"<p>A <code>datetime</code> or a <code>set</code> raises <code>TypeError: Object of type ... is not JSON serializable</code>. Supply <code>default=</code> to convert them (for example <code>str</code> or <code>isoformat</code>). Dict keys are always converted to strings, so <code>{1: \"a\"}</code> comes back as <code>{\"1\": \"a\"}</code>, and tuples become lists.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0641","card":"q-database-with-python-095-file-handling-csv-json-binary-delete","sec":"database-with-python-s096","q":"In Python, when should you use <code>json</code> instead of <code>pickle</code> to store data?","a":"<p>Use <code>json</code> for data shared across systems and languages, and for anything from outside. <code>pickle</code> can store arbitrary Python objects but loading untrusted pickle data can execute arbitrary code, and it is Python-specific and version-sensitive.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0642","card":"q-database-with-python-095-file-handling-csv-json-binary-delete","sec":"database-with-python-s096","q":"In Python, when do you open a file in binary mode, as with <code>\"rb\"</code> and <code>\"wb\"</code>?","a":"<p>Use binary mode for non-text data such as images, archives and network payloads. You read and write <code>bytes</code>, and no encoding or newline translation happens. Writing a <code>str</code> in binary mode raises <code>TypeError</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>with open(&quot;file.bin&quot;, &quot;wb&quot;) as f:\n    f.write(b&quot;Hi&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0643","card":"q-database-with-python-095-file-handling-csv-json-binary-delete","sec":"database-with-python-s096","q":"In Python, how do you delete a file or a directory safely?","a":"<p><code>os.remove(path)</code> or <code>Path.unlink()</code> deletes a file and raises <code>FileNotFoundError</code> if missing (<code>unlink(missing_ok=True)</code> in Python 3.8+). <code>os.rmdir</code> removes an empty directory. <code>shutil.rmtree</code> removes a whole tree and cannot be undone, so check the path first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0644","card":"q-database-with-python-096-modules-and-libraries","sec":"database-with-python-s097","q":"<code>import</code> vs <code>from module import *</code>?","a":"<p><code>import</code> keeps names in a namespace. <code>from ... import *</code> gives direct access but risks name conflicts.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import math\nprint(math.pi)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0645","card":"q-database-with-python-096-modules-and-libraries","sec":"database-with-python-s097","q":"How do you install a library?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pip install requests</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0646","card":"q-database-with-python-096-modules-and-libraries","sec":"database-with-python-s097","q":"What is the <code>sys</code> module for?","a":"<p>System-level access such as <code>sys.argv</code> and <code>sys.exit()</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import sys\nprint(sys.argv)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0647","card":"q-database-with-python-096-modules-and-libraries","sec":"database-with-python-s097","q":"How do you generate random numbers, use dates and the OS?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import random\nprint(random.randint(1, 10))  # 1-10\n\nfrom datetime import datetime\nprint(datetime.now())\n\nimport os\nprint(os.getcwd())</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0648","card":"q-database-with-python-096-modules-and-libraries","sec":"database-with-python-s097","q":"In Python, why is <code>from module import *</code> discouraged?","a":"<p>It copies every public name into the current namespace, which can silently overwrite your own names, hides where a name came from, and breaks linters. Import the module or just the names you need. A module can limit what <code>*</code> exports with <code>__all__</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0649","card":"q-database-with-python-096-modules-and-libraries","sec":"database-with-python-s097","q":"In Python, what is the difference between a module and a package?","a":"<p>A module is a single <code>.py</code> file. A package is a directory of modules, traditionally with an <code>__init__.py</code> file, imported with dotted names like <code>package.module</code>. Namespace packages work without <code>__init__.py</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0650","card":"q-database-with-python-096-modules-and-libraries","sec":"database-with-python-s097","q":"In Python, how does the interpreter find a module when you <code>import</code> it?","a":"<p>It checks <code>sys.modules</code> first (already imported), then built-in modules, then searches the directories in <code>sys.path</code>: the script's directory, <code>PYTHONPATH</code> entries, the standard library and <code>site-packages</code>. A local file named like a standard module (such as <code>random.py</code>) shadows it and causes confusing errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0651","card":"q-database-with-python-096-modules-and-libraries","sec":"database-with-python-s097","q":"In Python, why run <code>python -m pip install</code> instead of plain <code>pip install</code>?","a":"<p><code>python -m pip</code> guarantees the package goes into the same interpreter you run. A bare <code>pip</code> can belong to a different Python or environment, which leads to \"installed but cannot import\" problems.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pip install requests</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0652","card":"q-database-with-python-096-modules-and-libraries","sec":"database-with-python-s097","q":"In Python, how do you make installs reproducible across machines?","a":"<p>Pin versions in <code>requirements.txt</code> (for example from <code>pip freeze</code>) or use a lock file from <code>pip-tools</code>, Poetry or <code>uv</code>, install in a virtual environment, and install from that file in CI and production. Pin exact versions (<code>pkg==1.2.3</code>, ideally with hashes) for applications, and use compatible ranges (<code>&gt;=3.0,&lt;4</code>) for libraries. Update pins deliberately and run tests after each change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0653","card":"q-database-with-python-096-modules-and-libraries","sec":"database-with-python-s097","q":"In Python, what is <code>sys.argv</code>, and when do you use <code>argparse</code> instead?","a":"<p><code>sys.argv</code> is the list of command-line arguments with the script name first, all as strings. <code>argparse</code> parses flags, types and defaults, and builds <code>--help</code> output, so use it for any script with options.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import sys\nprint(sys.argv)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0654","card":"q-database-with-python-096-modules-and-libraries","sec":"database-with-python-s097","q":"In Python, why is <code>random</code> not suitable for passwords or tokens, and what do you use?","a":"<p><code>random</code> is a predictable pseudo-random generator that is fine for simulations. Use the <code>secrets</code> module (or <code>os.urandom</code>) for security-sensitive values such as tokens and password reset codes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0655","card":"q-database-with-python-096-modules-and-libraries","sec":"database-with-python-s097","q":"In Python, what is the pitfall of <code>datetime.now()</code>?","a":"<p>It returns a naive local time with no timezone, which is ambiguous across servers and daylight-saving changes. Use timezone-aware values, such as <code>datetime.now(timezone.utc)</code>, and store times in UTC.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from datetime import datetime\nprint(datetime.now())</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0656","card":"q-database-with-python-097-modules-numpy-venv-collections-regex","sec":"database-with-python-s098","q":"NumPy vs lists?","a":"<p>NumPy arrays are fast and fixed-type. Lists are flexible but slower.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import numpy as np\nprint(np.array([1, 2]) + 1)  # [2, 3]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0657","card":"q-database-with-python-097-modules-numpy-venv-collections-regex","sec":"database-with-python-s098","q":"How do you create a virtual environment?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>python -m venv env\nsource env/bin/activate</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0658","card":"q-database-with-python-097-modules-numpy-venv-collections-regex","sec":"database-with-python-s098","q":"What is <code>collections</code> for?","a":"<p>Specialized containers such as <code>Counter</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from collections import Counter\nprint(Counter(&quot;aba&quot;))  # {&#x27;a&#x27;: 2, &#x27;b&#x27;: 1}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0659","card":"q-database-with-python-097-modules-numpy-venv-collections-regex","sec":"database-with-python-s098","q":"How do you use regex?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import re\nprint(re.findall(r&quot;\\d+&quot;, &quot;12ab34&quot;))  # [&#x27;12&#x27;, &#x27;34&#x27;]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0660","card":"q-database-with-python-097-modules-numpy-venv-collections-regex","sec":"database-with-python-s098","q":"In Python, why is a NumPy array faster than a list for numeric work?","a":"<p>NumPy stores one fixed type in a contiguous block of memory and runs operations in compiled C loops (vectorisation), instead of looping over Python objects. It also supports broadcasting, so <code>array + 1</code> adds to every element. Lists are better for mixed or changing data.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import numpy as np\nprint(np.array([1, 2]) + 1)  # [2, 3]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0661","card":"q-database-with-python-097-modules-numpy-venv-collections-regex","sec":"database-with-python-s098","q":"In Python, how does <code>[1, 2] * 2</code> differ from <code>np.array([1, 2]) * 2</code>?","a":"<p>The list gives <code>[1, 2, 1, 2]</code> (repetition). The NumPy array gives <code>[2, 4]</code> (element-wise multiplication). Likewise <code>[1, 2] + [3]</code> joins the lists, but NumPy adds element-wise.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0662","card":"q-database-with-python-097-modules-numpy-venv-collections-regex","sec":"database-with-python-s098","q":"In Python, why create a virtual environment for each project?","a":"<p>Each project gets its own packages and versions, so one project's upgrade does not break another, and the global Python stays clean. Record dependencies in <code>requirements.txt</code> and do not commit the environment folder itself.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>python -m venv env\nsource env/bin/activate</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0663","card":"q-database-with-python-097-modules-numpy-venv-collections-regex","sec":"database-with-python-s098","q":"In Python, how do <code>venv</code>, <code>virtualenv</code>, <code>conda</code> and Poetry differ?","a":"<p><code>venv</code> is built in and makes isolated environments of Python packages. <code>virtualenv</code> is the older, faster third-party equivalent. <code>conda</code> also manages non-Python binaries and the Python version itself, popular in data science. Poetry (and <code>uv</code>) add dependency resolution, lock files and packaging.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0664","card":"q-database-with-python-097-modules-numpy-venv-collections-regex","sec":"database-with-python-s098","q":"In Python, what do <code>Counter</code>, <code>defaultdict</code>, <code>deque</code> and <code>namedtuple</code> from <code>collections</code> give you?","a":"<p><code>Counter</code> counts items. <code>defaultdict</code> creates missing values automatically, for grouping. <code>deque</code> is a double-ended queue with O(1) operations at both ends. <code>namedtuple</code> is a lightweight immutable record with named fields.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0665","card":"q-database-with-python-097-modules-numpy-venv-collections-regex","sec":"database-with-python-s098","q":"In Python, what does <code>Counter(\"aba\")</code> look like, and how do you get the most frequent item?","a":"<p>It is a dict subclass that prints as <code>Counter({'a': 2, 'b': 1})</code>. <code>Counter(\"aba\").most_common(1)</code> returns <code>[('a', 2)]</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0666","card":"q-database-with-python-097-modules-numpy-venv-collections-regex","sec":"database-with-python-s098","q":"In Python, why write regex patterns as raw strings like <code>r\"\\d+\"</code>?","a":"<p>A normal string interprets backslashes before <code>re</code> sees them, so <code>\"\\d\"</code> triggers escape warnings and <code>\"\\b\"</code> becomes a backspace. A raw string passes the backslashes to the regex engine unchanged.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import re\nprint(re.findall(r&quot;\\d+&quot;, &quot;12ab34&quot;))  # [&#x27;12&#x27;, &#x27;34&#x27;]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0667","card":"q-database-with-python-097-modules-numpy-venv-collections-regex","sec":"database-with-python-s098","q":"In Python, what is the difference between <code>re.match</code>, <code>re.search</code> and <code>re.fullmatch</code>?","a":"<p><code>match</code> matches only at the start of the string. <code>search</code> finds the first match anywhere. <code>fullmatch</code> requires the entire string to match. Using <code>match</code> when you meant <code>search</code> is a common bug.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0668","card":"q-database-with-python-097-modules-numpy-venv-collections-regex","sec":"database-with-python-s098","q":"In Python regex, what is the difference between greedy <code>.+</code> and lazy <code>.+?</code>?","a":"<p>Greedy matching takes as much as possible, so the first call returns <code>['&lt;a&gt;&lt;b&gt;']</code>. Lazy matching takes as little as possible, so the second returns <code>['&lt;a&gt;', '&lt;b&gt;']</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import re\nprint(re.findall(r&quot;&lt;.+&gt;&quot;, &quot;&lt;a&gt;&lt;b&gt;&quot;))\nprint(re.findall(r&quot;&lt;.+?&gt;&quot;, &quot;&lt;a&gt;&lt;b&gt;&quot;))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0669","card":"q-database-with-python-097-modules-numpy-venv-collections-regex","sec":"database-with-python-s098","q":"In Python, when should you not use a regex?","a":"<p>Do not parse nested formats such as HTML or JSON with a regex. Use a real parser (<code>json</code>, <code>BeautifulSoup</code>). Simple checks are clearer with <code>str.startswith</code> or <code>in</code>. Badly written patterns can also suffer catastrophic backtracking on hostile input.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0670","card":"q-database-with-python-098-exception-handling","sec":"database-with-python-s099","q":"What is exception handling?","a":"<p>It manages runtime errors so the program does not crash, which makes it robust.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0671","card":"q-database-with-python-098-exception-handling","sec":"database-with-python-s099","q":"How do <code>try</code>, <code>except</code>, <code>else</code> and <code>finally</code> work?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>try:\n    x = 1 / 0\nexcept ZeroDivisionError:\n    print(&quot;Error&quot;)\nelse:\n    print(&quot;OK&quot;)\nfinally:\n    print(&quot;Done&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0672","card":"q-database-with-python-098-exception-handling","sec":"database-with-python-s099","q":"How do you raise a custom exception?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class MyError(Exception):\n    pass\n\nraise MyError(&quot;Oops&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0673","card":"q-database-with-python-098-exception-handling","sec":"database-with-python-s099","q":"Error vs exception?","a":"<p>An error is any issue (such as a syntax error). An exception is a catchable runtime error.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0674","card":"q-database-with-python-098-exception-handling","sec":"database-with-python-s099","q":"How do you catch several exceptions?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>try:\n    int(&quot;a&quot;)\nexcept (ValueError, TypeError):\n    print(&quot;Error&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0675","card":"q-database-with-python-098-exception-handling","sec":"database-with-python-s099","q":"In Python, what is the purpose of the <code>else</code> block in <code>try/except/else/finally</code>?","a":"<p><code>else</code> runs only when the <code>try</code> block raised no exception. It keeps code that should not be protected by the <code>except</code> out of the <code>try</code>, so errors in that code are not caught by accident. <code>finally</code> always runs, so this code prints <code>Error</code> then <code>Done</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>try:\n    x = 1 / 0\nexcept ZeroDivisionError:\n    print(&quot;Error&quot;)\nelse:\n    print(&quot;OK&quot;)\nfinally:\n    print(&quot;Done&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0676","card":"q-database-with-python-098-exception-handling","sec":"database-with-python-s099","q":"In Python, does <code>finally</code> run when the <code>try</code> block executes <code>return</code>, and what if <code>finally</code> also returns?","a":"<p>Yes, <code>finally</code> runs before the function actually returns. A <code>return</code> inside <code>finally</code> overrides the earlier return and also discards any exception in flight, which hides errors, so avoid it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0677","card":"q-database-with-python-098-exception-handling","sec":"database-with-python-s099","q":"In Python, why is a bare <code>except:</code> a bad idea?","a":"<p>It catches everything, including <code>KeyboardInterrupt</code> and <code>SystemExit</code>, so Ctrl+C or <code>sys.exit()</code> can be swallowed, and it hides real bugs. Catch specific exceptions, or at most <code>except Exception</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0678","card":"q-database-with-python-098-exception-handling","sec":"database-with-python-s099","q":"In Python, in what order should multiple <code>except</code> clauses be written?","a":"<p>Python checks them top to bottom and uses the first match. Put specific exceptions before general ones. A general class listed first (such as <code>Exception</code>) would make the specific clauses unreachable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0679","card":"q-database-with-python-098-exception-handling","sec":"database-with-python-s099","q":"In Python, why should a custom exception inherit from <code>Exception</code> and not <code>BaseException</code>?","a":"<p><code>BaseException</code> is the root for system-exiting exceptions (<code>KeyboardInterrupt</code>, <code>SystemExit</code>), so a class derived from it escapes normal <code>except Exception</code> handling. Derive from <code>Exception</code>, or from a project base error so callers can catch the whole family.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class MyError(Exception):\n    pass\n\nraise MyError(&quot;Oops&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0680","card":"q-database-with-python-098-exception-handling","sec":"database-with-python-s099","q":"In Python, what does <code>raise NewError(...) from e</code> do, and how does a bare <code>raise</code> differ?","a":"<p><code>raise ... from e</code> raises a new exception and records <code>e</code> as its cause, so the traceback shows both. A bare <code>raise</code> inside <code>except</code> re-raises the current exception with its original traceback. Use <code>from</code> when translating low-level errors into domain errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0681","card":"q-database-with-python-098-exception-handling","sec":"database-with-python-s099","q":"In Python, what are EAFP and LBYL?","a":"<p>EAFP (\"easier to ask forgiveness than permission\") tries the operation and catches the exception. LBYL (\"look before you leap\") checks conditions first. Python style prefers EAFP, because the check can be racy (for example for files) and the try block is often simpler. LBYL fits when failure is common and expensive.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0682","card":"q-database-with-python-098-exception-handling","sec":"database-with-python-s099","q":"In Python, is a <code>SyntaxError</code> an exception you can catch with <code>try/except</code> in the same file?","a":"<p>No. It is raised while compiling the file, before any code runs, so the <code>try</code> never starts. It can be caught only when code is compiled at run time, as with <code>eval</code> or <code>import</code>. Runtime errors such as <code>ZeroDivisionError</code> are the ones you handle.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0683","card":"q-database-with-python-099-exceptions-logging-assert-keyerror-exit","sec":"database-with-python-s100","q":"How do you log exceptions?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import logging\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.ERROR)\n\ntry:\n    1 / 0\nexcept:\n    logging.error(&quot;Fail&quot;, exc_info=True)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0684","card":"q-database-with-python-099-exceptions-logging-assert-keyerror-exit","sec":"database-with-python-s100","q":"What does <code>assert</code> do?","a":"<p>It raises <code>AssertionError</code> if the condition fails.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>assert 1 == 2, &quot;Wrong&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0685","card":"q-database-with-python-099-exceptions-logging-assert-keyerror-exit","sec":"database-with-python-s100","q":"How do you handle <code>KeyError</code>?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>d = {&quot;a&quot;: 1}\nprint(d.get(&quot;b&quot;, 0))  # 0</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0686","card":"q-database-with-python-099-exceptions-logging-assert-keyerror-exit","sec":"database-with-python-s100","q":"<code>sys.exit()</code> vs raising an exception?","a":"<p><code>sys.exit()</code> stops the program. An exception is a catchable error.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import sys\nsys.exit(1)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0687","card":"q-database-with-python-099-exceptions-logging-assert-keyerror-exit","sec":"database-with-python-s100","q":"In Python, why use <code>logging</code> instead of <code>print</code> in applications?","a":"<p>Logging has levels (DEBUG to CRITICAL), timestamps, destinations (files, syslog, handlers) and configuration, so you can change verbosity without editing code. Use <code>logging.getLogger(__name__)</code> per module. Never log secrets or personal data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0688","card":"q-database-with-python-099-exceptions-logging-assert-keyerror-exit","sec":"database-with-python-s100","q":"In Python, what does <code>exc_info=True</code> or <code>logger.exception()</code> add to a log message?","a":"<p>They include the full traceback in the log record, which is essential for debugging. <code>logger.exception(\"msg\")</code> does the same at ERROR level and is meant to be called inside an <code>except</code> block. The bare <code>except:</code> here should be <code>except Exception:</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import logging\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.ERROR)\n\ntry:\n    1 / 0\nexcept:\n    logging.error(&quot;Fail&quot;, exc_info=True)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0689","card":"q-database-with-python-099-exceptions-logging-assert-keyerror-exit","sec":"database-with-python-s100","q":"In Python, why pass values as <code>logger.info(\"user %s\", name)</code> instead of building the string with an f-string?","a":"<p>The logging module formats the message only if the record is actually emitted, so disabled levels cost nothing. It also lets log tools group messages by template.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0690","card":"q-database-with-python-099-exceptions-logging-assert-keyerror-exit","sec":"database-with-python-s100","q":"In Python, why should <code>assert</code> not be used to validate user input?","a":"<p>Assertions are removed when Python runs with the <code>-O</code> flag, so the check disappears in optimised runs. Use <code>assert</code> for internal invariants and tests, and raise <code>ValueError</code> or a custom exception for input checks.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>assert 1 == 2, &quot;Wrong&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0691","card":"q-database-with-python-099-exceptions-logging-assert-keyerror-exit","sec":"database-with-python-s100","q":"In Python, what are the ways to avoid a <code>KeyError</code> when reading a dict, and when does each fit?","a":"<p><code>d.get(k, default)</code> for an optional key. <code>k in d</code> to test first. <code>try/except KeyError</code> when a missing key is exceptional or the lookup is hot. <code>defaultdict</code> when you always want a default for missing keys. A missing list index raises <code>IndexError</code> instead.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>d = {&quot;a&quot;: 1}\nprint(d.get(&quot;b&quot;, 0))  # 0</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0692","card":"q-database-with-python-099-exceptions-logging-assert-keyerror-exit","sec":"database-with-python-s100","q":"In Python, what does <code>sys.exit(1)</code> actually do, and what does the number mean?","a":"<p>It raises <code>SystemExit</code>, which unwinds the stack, runs <code>finally</code> blocks and exits the interpreter. The argument is the process exit code: 0 means success and non-zero means failure, which shell scripts and CI use. A bare <code>except:</code> or <code>except BaseException</code> can catch it by mistake.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import sys\nsys.exit(1)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0693","card":"q-database-with-python-099-exceptions-logging-assert-keyerror-exit","sec":"database-with-python-s100","q":"In Python, what is the difference between <code>sys.exit()</code> and <code>os._exit()</code>?","a":"<p><code>sys.exit()</code> raises an exception and allows cleanup (<code>finally</code>, <code>atexit</code> handlers, flushing). <code>os._exit()</code> ends the process immediately without cleanup and is used in child processes after <code>fork</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0694","card":"q-database-with-python-100-advanced-python-generators-context-manag","sec":"database-with-python-s101","q":"What are generators?","a":"<p>Lazy iterators that use <code>yield</code>, unlike functions that return everything at once.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def gen():\n    yield 1\n    yield 2\n\nprint(next(gen()))  # 1</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0695","card":"q-database-with-python-100-advanced-python-generators-context-manag","sec":"database-with-python-s101","q":"How do you create a generator with <code>yield</code>?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def gen(n):\n    for i in range(n):\n        yield i\n\nprint(list(gen(3)))  # [0, 1, 2]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0696","card":"q-database-with-python-100-advanced-python-generators-context-manag","sec":"database-with-python-s101","q":"What is a context manager?","a":"<p>An object that manages a resource with <code>__enter__</code> and <code>__exit__</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class CM:\n    def __enter__(self):\n        return self\n\n    def __exit__(self, *args):\n        pass\n\nwith CM():\n    print(&quot;Hi&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0697","card":"q-database-with-python-100-advanced-python-generators-context-manag","sec":"database-with-python-s101","q":"What are metaclasses?","a":"<p>Classes that create classes, used to customize class creation.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class Meta(type):\n    pass\n\nclass C(metaclass=Meta):\n    pass</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0698","card":"q-database-with-python-100-advanced-python-generators-context-manag","sec":"database-with-python-s101","q":"In Python, how is a generator different from a list in memory and in behavior?","a":"<p>A generator yields one value at a time and remembers its state between <code>next()</code> calls, so memory stays constant even for huge or endless sequences. The cost is that it is one-shot (once exhausted it yields nothing), has no <code>len</code> or indexing, and cannot be restarted.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def gen(n):\n    for i in range(n):\n        yield i\n\nprint(list(gen(3)))  # [0, 1, 2]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0699","card":"q-database-with-python-100-advanced-python-generators-context-manag","sec":"database-with-python-s101","q":"In Python, what does <code>next(gen())</code> return here, and why does calling it twice give the same value?","a":"<p>Each call to <code>gen()</code> creates a new generator object that starts from the beginning, so both calls return <code>1</code>. To advance, keep the generator in a variable: <code>g = gen(); next(g); next(g)</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def gen():\n    yield 1\n    yield 2\n\nprint(next(gen()))  # 1</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0700","card":"q-database-with-python-100-advanced-python-generators-context-manag","sec":"database-with-python-s101","q":"In Python, what happens when a generator has no more values?","a":"<p>Calling <code>next()</code> raises <code>StopIteration</code>. A <code>for</code> loop handles this automatically and simply ends.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0701","card":"q-database-with-python-100-advanced-python-generators-context-manag","sec":"database-with-python-s101","q":"In Python, what does <code>yield from</code> do?","a":"<p>It delegates to another iterable or generator, yielding all its values (and passing through <code>send</code> and return values). <code>yield from sub()</code> replaces a loop that yields each item.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0702","card":"q-database-with-python-100-advanced-python-generators-context-manag","sec":"database-with-python-s101","q":"In Python, how does a generator expression differ from a list comprehension in syntax and result?","a":"<p><code>(x*x for x in data)</code> uses parentheses and returns a lazy generator, while <code>[x*x for x in data]</code> builds the whole list immediately. Use the generator form for large inputs consumed once.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0703","card":"q-database-with-python-100-advanced-python-generators-context-manag","sec":"database-with-python-s101","q":"In Python, what does a context manager guarantee, and what do <code>__enter__</code> and <code>__exit__</code> do?","a":"<p><code>__enter__</code> runs on entry and its return value is bound by <code>as</code>. <code>__exit__</code> always runs on exit, even on an exception, and receives the exception type, value and traceback. It is used for files, locks, database transactions and temporary state. If <code>__exit__</code> returns true, the exception is suppressed.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class CM:\n    def __enter__(self):\n        return self\n\n    def __exit__(self, *args):\n        pass\n\nwith CM():\n    print(&quot;Hi&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0704","card":"q-database-with-python-100-advanced-python-generators-context-manag","sec":"database-with-python-s101","q":"In Python, how do you write a context manager with <code>contextlib.contextmanager</code>?","a":"<p>Write a generator with a single <code>yield</code>. Code before <code>yield</code> is the setup, code after it is the cleanup, and wrapping the <code>yield</code> in <code>try/finally</code> makes cleanup run on errors. It is shorter than writing a class with <code>__enter__</code> and <code>__exit__</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0705","card":"q-database-with-python-100-advanced-python-generators-context-manag","sec":"database-with-python-s101","q":"In Python, what is a metaclass, and what is the metaclass of an ordinary class?","a":"<p>A metaclass is the class of a class: it creates and customises class objects. By default the metaclass is <code>type</code>, and <code>type(name, bases, namespace)</code> can create a class dynamically. Frameworks such as Django models and SQLAlchemy's declarative base use this machinery to register fields.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class Meta(type):\n    pass\n\nclass C(metaclass=Meta):\n    pass</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0706","card":"q-database-with-python-100-advanced-python-generators-context-manag","sec":"database-with-python-s101","q":"In Python, when would you use <code>__init_subclass__</code> or a class decorator instead of a metaclass?","a":"<p><code>__init_subclass__</code> (Python 3.6+) runs when a subclass is created and covers most needs such as plugin registries and validation, without metaclass complexity. Metaclasses are justified only when you must change how the class object itself is built, and they conflict when combined.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0707","card":"q-database-with-python-101-advanced-python-threads-processes-asynci","sec":"database-with-python-s102","q":"What are multithreading and the GIL?","a":"<p>The GIL limits CPU-bound parallelism in CPython, so threads are good for I/O.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import threading\nt = threading.Thread(target=lambda: print(&quot;Hi&quot;))\nt.start()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0708","card":"q-database-with-python-101-advanced-python-threads-processes-asynci","sec":"database-with-python-s102","q":"Multiprocessing vs multithreading?","a":"<p>Multithreading shares memory and is GIL-limited. Multiprocessing uses separate memory and has no GIL.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from multiprocessing import Process\nProcess(target=lambda: print(&quot;Hi&quot;)).start()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0709","card":"q-database-with-python-101-advanced-python-threads-processes-asynci","sec":"database-with-python-s102","q":"What is <code>asyncio</code>?","a":"<p>Python's async programming library.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import asyncio\n\nasync def f():\n    await asyncio.sleep(1)\n    print(&quot;Hi&quot;)\n\nasyncio.run(f())</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0710","card":"q-database-with-python-101-advanced-python-threads-processes-asynci","sec":"database-with-python-s102","q":"What are closures?","a":"<p>Nested functions that keep the outer scope.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def outer(x):\n    def inner():\n        return x\n    return inner\n\nf = outer(5)\nprint(f())  # 5</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0711","card":"q-database-with-python-101-advanced-python-threads-processes-asynci","sec":"database-with-python-s102","q":"How do you profile a Python program?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import cProfile\ncProfile.run(&quot;sum(range(1000))&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0712","card":"q-database-with-python-101-advanced-python-threads-processes-asynci","sec":"database-with-python-s102","q":"In CPython, what is the GIL, and what does it mean for threads?","a":"<p>The Global Interpreter Lock lets only one thread run Python bytecode at a time, which protects the interpreter's memory management. So threads do not speed up CPU-bound pure Python code, but they work well for I/O-bound work because the lock is released while waiting. Python 3.13 added an optional experimental free-threaded build (PEP 703) without the GIL.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import threading\nt = threading.Thread(target=lambda: print(&quot;Hi&quot;))\nt.start()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0713","card":"q-database-with-python-101-advanced-python-threads-processes-asynci","sec":"database-with-python-s102","q":"In Python, how do you choose between threads, processes and <code>asyncio</code>?","a":"<p>Use threads for I/O-bound work with blocking libraries. Use <code>multiprocessing</code> (or <code>ProcessPoolExecutor</code>) for CPU-bound work, since each process has its own GIL. Use <code>asyncio</code> for many concurrent I/O tasks on one thread when libraries support <code>await</code>. Processes cost more memory and need pickling for data transfer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0714","card":"q-database-with-python-101-advanced-python-threads-processes-asynci","sec":"database-with-python-s102","q":"In Python, why can this <code>multiprocessing</code> code fail on Windows and macOS?","a":"<p>Those platforms use the <code>spawn</code> start method by default (macOS since Python 3.8). The child re-imports the main module and must pickle the target, and lambdas cannot be pickled. Use a module-level function and put the launch code under <code>if __name__ == \"__main__\":</code>. Linux used <code>fork</code> by default before Python 3.14.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from multiprocessing import Process\nProcess(target=lambda: print(&quot;Hi&quot;)).start()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0715","card":"q-database-with-python-101-advanced-python-threads-processes-asynci","sec":"database-with-python-s102","q":"In Python, what is a race condition in threaded code, and how do you prevent it?","a":"<p>It happens when threads read and update shared data without coordination, so the result depends on timing (for example two threads doing <code>counter += 1</code>, which is not atomic). Use <code>threading.Lock</code>, thread-safe structures such as <code>queue.Queue</code>, or avoid shared state.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0716","card":"q-database-with-python-101-advanced-python-threads-processes-asynci","sec":"database-with-python-s102","q":"In Python, why prefer <code>concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor</code> to creating <code>Thread</code> objects by hand?","a":"<p>It reuses a bounded pool of workers, returns <code>Future</code> objects for results and exceptions, and supports <code>map</code> and <code>with</code> for cleanup. Manual threads need your own joining, result passing and error handling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0717","card":"q-database-with-python-101-advanced-python-threads-processes-asynci","sec":"database-with-python-s102","q":"In Python <code>asyncio</code>, what is the difference between <code>await asyncio.sleep(1)</code> and <code>time.sleep(1)</code> inside an <code>async def</code>?","a":"<p><code>await asyncio.sleep</code> yields control to the event loop so other tasks run during the wait. <code>time.sleep</code> blocks the whole thread and the event loop, so every task freezes. Run blocking calls in an executor (<code>loop.run_in_executor</code> or <code>asyncio.to_thread</code>).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import asyncio\n\nasync def f():\n    await asyncio.sleep(1)\n    print(&quot;Hi&quot;)\n\nasyncio.run(f())</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0718","card":"q-database-with-python-101-advanced-python-threads-processes-asynci","sec":"database-with-python-s102","q":"In Python <code>asyncio</code>, how do you run several coroutines concurrently?","a":"<p>Wrap them with <code>asyncio.gather(*coros)</code> or create tasks with <code>asyncio.create_task</code>. Awaiting coroutines one after another runs them sequentially. <code>gather</code> returns the results in order. In Python 3.11+ <code>asyncio.TaskGroup</code> gives safer error handling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0719","card":"q-database-with-python-101-advanced-python-threads-processes-asynci","sec":"database-with-python-s102","q":"In Python, what is a closure, and what is it used for?","a":"<p>A closure is a nested function that keeps access to variables of its enclosing scope after the outer function has returned. It is used for function factories, callbacks with preset state, and decorators. Rebinding the captured variable needs <code>nonlocal</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def outer(x):\n    def inner():\n        return x\n    return inner\n\nf = outer(5)\nprint(f())  # 5</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0720","card":"q-database-with-python-101-advanced-python-threads-processes-asynci","sec":"database-with-python-s102","q":"In Python, how should you profile a slow script before optimising?","a":"<p>Use <code>cProfile</code> to see time per function and call counts, sort by cumulative time (<code>python -m cProfile -s cumtime script.py</code>), and fix the biggest cost first. Use <code>timeit</code> for small snippets and <code>line_profiler</code> for line-level detail. Measure again after each change. Typical wins are a better algorithm or data structure (a set instead of a list for lookups), caching repeated work, vectorising with NumPy, batching I/O round trips, and parallelism for CPU-bound work.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import cProfile\ncProfile.run(&quot;sum(range(1000))&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0721","card":"q-database-with-python-102-coding-problems-factorial-prime-fibonacc","sec":"database-with-python-s103","q":"Factorial of a number?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def fact(n):\n    return 1 if n &lt;= 0 else n * fact(n-1)\n\nprint(fact(5))  # 120</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0722","card":"q-database-with-python-102-coding-problems-factorial-prime-fibonacc","sec":"database-with-python-s103","q":"Check if a number is prime?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def is_prime(n):\n    if n &lt;= 1: return False\n    for i in range(2, int(n**0.5)+1):\n        if n % i == 0: return False\n    return True\n\nprint(is_prime(7))  # True</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0723","card":"q-database-with-python-102-coding-problems-factorial-prime-fibonacc","sec":"database-with-python-s103","q":"Fibonacci up to <code>n</code> terms?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def fib(n):\n    f = [0, 1]\n    for i in range(2, n):\n        f.append(f[-1] + f[-2])\n    return f[:n]\n\nprint(fib(5))  # [0, 1, 1, 2, 3]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0724","card":"q-database-with-python-102-coding-problems-factorial-prime-fibonacc","sec":"database-with-python-s103","q":"Remove vowels from a string?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def no_vowels(s):\n    return &quot;&quot;.join(c for c in s if c.lower() not in &quot;aeiou&quot;)\n\nprint(no_vowels(&quot;hello&quot;))  # &quot;hll&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0725","card":"q-database-with-python-102-coding-problems-factorial-prime-fibonacc","sec":"database-with-python-s103","q":"In Python, what is wrong with this factorial function for negative input, and how should it behave?","a":"<p>A negative <code>n</code> returns <code>1</code>, which is mathematically wrong. Factorial is defined for non-negative integers, so raise <code>ValueError</code> for <code>n &lt; 0</code>. The recursion also hits <code>RecursionError</code> for large <code>n</code>, so a loop or <code>math.factorial</code> is better in production.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def fact(n):\n    return 1 if n &lt;= 0 else n * fact(n-1)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0726","card":"q-database-with-python-102-coding-problems-factorial-prime-fibonacc","sec":"database-with-python-s103","q":"In Python, why does the prime check only test divisors up to the square root of <code>n</code>?","a":"<p>If <code>n = a * b</code>, one of the factors is at most <code>sqrt(n)</code>, so a larger divisor always has a smaller partner already tested. This makes the check O(sqrt n) instead of O(n).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def is_prime(n):\n    if n &lt;= 1: return False\n    for i in range(2, int(n**0.5)+1):\n        if n % i == 0: return False\n    return True</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0727","card":"q-database-with-python-102-coding-problems-factorial-prime-fibonacc","sec":"database-with-python-s103","q":"In Python, what is the faster way to find all primes up to a limit than calling <code>is_prime</code> for each number?","a":"<p>The Sieve of Eratosthenes. Mark multiples of each prime as composite, starting from <code>p*p</code>. It runs in about O(n log log n) time and O(n) memory.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0728","card":"q-database-with-python-102-coding-problems-factorial-prime-fibonacc","sec":"database-with-python-s103","q":"In Python, what does this Fibonacci function return for <code>fib(1)</code> and <code>fib(0)</code>, and why?","a":"<p><code>fib(1)</code> returns <code>[0]</code> and <code>fib(0)</code> returns <code>[]</code>, because the final slice <code>f[:n]</code> trims the initial <code>[0, 1]</code> list. For <code>n &gt;= 2</code> the loop appends the sum of the last two. Time is O(n) and space O(n).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def fib(n):\n    f = [0, 1]\n    for i in range(2, n):\n        f.append(f[-1] + f[-2])\n    return f[:n]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0729","card":"q-database-with-python-102-coding-problems-factorial-prime-fibonacc","sec":"database-with-python-s103","q":"In Python, how do you produce Fibonacci numbers using constant memory?","a":"<p>Use a generator that keeps two variables: <code>a, b = 0, 1</code>, then <code>yield a</code> and <code>a, b = b, a + b</code>. It yields numbers lazily and uses O(1) space.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0730","card":"q-database-with-python-102-coding-problems-factorial-prime-fibonacc","sec":"database-with-python-s103","q":"In Python, how can you remove vowels from a string efficiently, and what are alternatives?","a":"<p>The generator with <code>join</code> is O(n). A <code>set</code> of vowels makes the membership test explicit. <code>str.translate</code> with a deletion table or <code>re.sub(\"[aeiouAEIOU]\", \"\", s)</code> are alternatives, and <code>translate</code> is usually the fastest for large inputs.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def no_vowels(s):\n    return &quot;&quot;.join(c for c in s if c.lower() not in &quot;aeiou&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0731","card":"q-database-with-python-103-coding-problems-second-largest-merge-rot","sec":"database-with-python-s104","q":"Second largest in a list?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def second_max(lst):\n    if len(lst) &lt; 2: return None\n    m1, m2 = max(lst[0], lst[1]), min(lst[0], lst[1])\n    for x in lst[2:]:\n        m1, m2 = (x, m1) if x &gt; m1 else (m1, x if x &gt; m2 else m2)\n    return m2\n\nprint(second_max([1, 5, 3]))  # 3</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0732","card":"q-database-with-python-103-coding-problems-second-largest-merge-rot","sec":"database-with-python-s104","q":"Merge two sorted lists?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def merge(l1, l2):\n    r, i, j = [], 0, 0\n    while i &lt; len(l1) and j &lt; len(l2):\n        if l1[i] &lt; l2[j]:\n            r.append(l1[i])\n            i += 1\n        else:\n            r.append(l2[j])\n            j += 1\n    return r + l1[i:] + l2[j:]\n\nprint(merge([1, 3], [2, 4]))  # [1, 2, 3, 4]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0733","card":"q-database-with-python-103-coding-problems-second-largest-merge-rot","sec":"database-with-python-s104","q":"Rotate a list by <code>k</code>?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def rotate(lst, k):\n    k %= len(lst)\n    return lst[-k:] + lst[:-k]\n\nprint(rotate([1, 2, 3], 1))  # [3, 1, 2]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0734","card":"q-database-with-python-103-coding-problems-second-largest-merge-rot","sec":"database-with-python-s104","q":"In Python, what does this second-largest function return for <code>[5, 5, 3]</code>, and is it correct?","a":"<p>It returns <code>5</code>. The code treats a duplicate of the maximum as the second largest. If \"second largest\" means second distinct value, the answer should be <code>3</code>, so skip values equal to <code>m1</code> (<code>x &gt; m2 and x != m1</code>) or deduplicate first. In an interview, ask which meaning is intended.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def second_max(lst):\n    if len(lst) &lt; 2: return None\n    m1, m2 = max(lst[0], lst[1]), min(lst[0], lst[1])\n    for x in lst[2:]:\n        m1, m2 = (x, m1) if x &gt; m1 else (m1, x if x &gt; m2 else m2)\n    return m2</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0735","card":"q-database-with-python-103-coding-problems-second-largest-merge-rot","sec":"database-with-python-s104","q":"In Python, what are the ways to find the second largest value, and how do their complexities compare?","a":"<p>A single pass tracking the two largest values is O(n) time and O(1) space. <code>heapq.nlargest(2, lst)</code> is O(n log 2). Sorting is O(n log n) and simplest to write. A set plus sort handles distinct values but costs extra memory.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0736","card":"q-database-with-python-103-coding-problems-second-largest-merge-rot","sec":"database-with-python-s104","q":"In Python, why is merging two sorted lists with two indexes O(n + m), and why is it better than <code>sorted(l1 + l2)</code>?","a":"<p>Each element is appended once, because the sorted order lets you compare only the two current heads. <code>sorted(l1 + l2)</code> ignores that order and is O(n log n), although Timsort detects existing runs and is fast in practice. This merge is also the combine step of merge sort. <code>heapq.merge</code> does it lazily for many lists.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def merge(l1, l2):\n    r, i, j = [], 0, 0\n    while i &lt; len(l1) and j &lt; len(l2):\n        if l1[i] &lt; l2[j]:\n            r.append(l1[i])\n            i += 1\n        else:\n            r.append(l2[j])\n            j += 1\n    return r + l1[i:] + l2[j:]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0737","card":"q-database-with-python-103-coding-problems-second-largest-merge-rot","sec":"database-with-python-s104","q":"In Python, is this merge stable when both lists contain equal values?","a":"<p>No. When <code>l1[i] == l2[j]</code> the <code>else</code> branch takes from <code>l2</code> first, so equal items from <code>l1</code> end up after those from <code>l2</code>. Using <code>&lt;=</code> in the comparison keeps equal items from <code>l1</code> first, which makes it stable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0738","card":"q-database-with-python-103-coding-problems-second-largest-merge-rot","sec":"database-with-python-s104","q":"In Python, how does <code>lst[-k:] + lst[:-k]</code> rotate a list right by <code>k</code>, and why is <code>k %= len(lst)</code> needed?","a":"<p>The last <code>k</code> items move to the front. Reducing <code>k</code> modulo the length handles <code>k</code> larger than the list. It raises <code>ZeroDivisionError</code> for an empty list, so guard for it. For in-place O(1) space use three reversals, or <code>collections.deque.rotate</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def rotate(lst, k):\n    k %= len(lst)\n    return lst[-k:] + lst[:-k]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0739","card":"q-database-with-python-104-coding-problems-bst-anagrams-longest-com","sec":"database-with-python-s105","q":"Binary search tree insert?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class Node:\n    def __init__(self, v):\n        self.v = v\n        self.left = self.right = None\n\nclass BST:\n    def insert(self, root, v):\n        if not root: return Node(v)\n        if v &lt; root.v:\n            root.left = self.insert(root.left, v)\n        else:\n            root.right = self.insert(root.right, v)\n        return root\n\nbst = BST()\nroot = bst.insert(None, 5)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0740","card":"q-database-with-python-104-coding-problems-bst-anagrams-longest-com","sec":"database-with-python-s105","q":"Find anagrams in a list?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def anagrams(lst):\n    d = {}\n    for s in lst:\n        k = &quot;&quot;.join(sorted(s))\n        d.setdefault(k, []).append(s)\n    return [v for v in d.values() if len(v) &gt; 1]\n\nprint(anagrams([&quot;eat&quot;, &quot;tea&quot;, &quot;tan&quot;]))  # [[&#x27;eat&#x27;, &#x27;tea&#x27;]]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0741","card":"q-database-with-python-104-coding-problems-bst-anagrams-longest-com","sec":"database-with-python-s105","q":"Longest common substring?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def lcs(s1, s2):\n    dp = [[0]*(len(s2)+1) for _ in range(len(s1)+1)]\n    m, end = 0, 0\n    for i in range(1, len(s1)+1):\n        for j in range(1, len(s2)+1):\n            if s1[i-1] == s2[j-1]:\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1\n                if dp[i][j] &gt; m:\n                    m = dp[i][j]\n                    end = i\n    return s1[end-m:end]\n\nprint(lcs(&quot;ABCD&quot;, &quot;ABED&quot;))  # &quot;AB&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0742","card":"q-database-with-python-104-coding-problems-bst-anagrams-longest-com","sec":"database-with-python-s105","q":"What property defines a binary search tree, and what is the cost of search and insert?","a":"<p>For every node, values in the left subtree are smaller and values in the right subtree are not smaller (here duplicates go right). Search and insert are O(h), where h is the tree height. That is O(log n) when balanced but O(n) if values arrive sorted and the tree becomes a chain.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class Node:\n    def __init__(self, v):\n        self.v = v\n        self.left = self.right = None\n\nclass BST:\n    def insert(self, root, v):\n        if not root: return Node(v)\n        if v &lt; root.v:\n            root.left = self.insert(root.left, v)\n        else:\n            root.right = self.insert(root.right, v)\n        return root</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0743","card":"q-database-with-python-104-coding-problems-bst-anagrams-longest-com","sec":"database-with-python-s105","q":"What traversal of a binary search tree returns values in sorted order, and how do balanced trees avoid the worst case?","a":"<p>In-order traversal (left, node, right) returns sorted values. Self-balancing trees such as AVL and red-black trees rotate nodes on insert and delete to keep height O(log n). Python has no built-in balanced tree, so people use <code>bisect</code> on a list or <code>sortedcontainers</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0744","card":"q-database-with-python-104-coding-problems-bst-anagrams-longest-com","sec":"database-with-python-s105","q":"In Python, how do you group anagrams, and why does the sorted-letters key work?","a":"<p>Words that are anagrams contain the same letters, so they have the same sorted string, which becomes a dict key. Each group is collected under its key. The time is O(n * k log k) for n words of length k. A letter-count tuple as the key makes it O(n * k).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def anagrams(lst):\n    d = {}\n    for s in lst:\n        k = &quot;&quot;.join(sorted(s))\n        d.setdefault(k, []).append(s)\n    return [v for v in d.values() if len(v) &gt; 1]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0745","card":"q-database-with-python-104-coding-problems-bst-anagrams-longest-com","sec":"database-with-python-s105","q":"In Python, what is the difference between the longest common substring and the longest common subsequence?","a":"<p>A substring must be contiguous. A subsequence keeps order but may skip characters. Both can be solved with dynamic programming in O(n * m) time, but the recurrences differ.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0746","card":"q-database-with-python-104-coding-problems-bst-anagrams-longest-com","sec":"database-with-python-s105","q":"In Python dynamic programming for the longest common substring, why does a mismatch leave <code>dp[i][j]</code> at 0 and a match use <code>dp[i-1][j-1] + 1</code>?","a":"<p><code>dp[i][j]</code> is the length of the common substring that ends exactly at <code>s1[i-1]</code> and <code>s2[j-1]</code>. A match extends the substring ending at the diagonal cell. A mismatch breaks contiguity, so the length restarts at 0. <code>m</code> and <code>end</code> track the best length and where it ends, and the answer is sliced from <code>s1</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def lcs(s1, s2):\n    dp = [[0]*(len(s2)+1) for _ in range(len(s1)+1)]\n    m, end = 0, 0\n    for i in range(1, len(s1)+1):\n        for j in range(1, len(s2)+1):\n            if s1[i-1] == s2[j-1]:\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1\n                if dp[i][j] &gt; m:\n                    m = dp[i][j]\n                    end = i\n    return s1[end-m:end]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0747","card":"q-database-with-python-104-coding-problems-bst-anagrams-longest-com","sec":"database-with-python-s105","q":"In Python, how can you reduce the memory of the longest common substring DP table?","a":"<p>Row <code>i</code> depends only on row <code>i-1</code>, so keep two rows (or one) instead of the whole table, reducing space from O(n * m) to O(min(n, m)). Time stays O(n * m). A suffix automaton or suffix array solves it faster for very long strings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0748","card":"q-database-with-python-105-practical-scenarios-performance-debuggin","sec":"database-with-python-s106","q":"How do you optimize a script for large datasets?","a":"<p>Use generators, NumPy or chunking.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def gen_data(n):\n    yield from range(n)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0749","card":"q-database-with-python-105-practical-scenarios-performance-debuggin","sec":"database-with-python-s106","q":"How do you debug a Python program?","a":"<p>Use <code>print()</code>, <code>pdb</code> or IDE breakpoints.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import pdb\npdb.set_trace()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0750","card":"q-database-with-python-105-practical-scenarios-performance-debuggin","sec":"database-with-python-s106","q":"How do you handle memory leaks?","a":"<p>Use <code>tracemalloc</code> and close resources.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import tracemalloc\ntracemalloc.start()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0751","card":"q-database-with-python-105-practical-scenarios-performance-debuggin","sec":"database-with-python-s106","q":"How do you process a large CSV without loading it all?","a":"<p>Stream it row by row.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import csv\nwith open(&quot;large.csv&quot;) as f:\n    [print(row) for row in csv.reader(f)]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0752","card":"q-database-with-python-105-practical-scenarios-performance-debuggin","sec":"database-with-python-s106","q":"In Python, how would you process a dataset too large to fit in memory?","a":"<p>Stream it. Use generators, read files in chunks or line by line, use <code>pandas.read_csv(chunksize=...)</code>, and keep running aggregates instead of storing everything. For numeric data use NumPy arrays (compact) or memory-mapped files. Move heavy aggregation to a database.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def gen_data(n):\n    yield from range(n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0753","card":"q-database-with-python-105-practical-scenarios-performance-debuggin","sec":"database-with-python-s106","q":"In Python, how do you start a debugger at a specific line, and how do you debug after a crash?","a":"<p>Since Python 3.7 the built-in <code>breakpoint()</code> does the same and can be switched off with the <code>PYTHONBREAKPOINT</code> environment variable. To inspect a crash, run <code>python -m pdb script.py</code> and use post-mortem debugging. For production issues, add structured logging with tracebacks.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import pdb\npdb.set_trace()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0754","card":"q-database-with-python-105-practical-scenarios-performance-debuggin","sec":"database-with-python-s106","q":"In Python, how do you find a memory leak with <code>tracemalloc</code>?","a":"<p>Start tracing, run the suspect workload, then call <code>tracemalloc.take_snapshot()</code> at two points and compare them with <code>snapshot2.compare_to(snapshot1, \"lineno\")</code> to see which lines allocate the growing memory. Common causes are unbounded caches or global lists, lingering references, and unclosed files and connections.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import tracemalloc\ntracemalloc.start()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0755","card":"q-database-with-python-105-practical-scenarios-performance-debuggin","sec":"database-with-python-s106","q":"In Python, why can memory keep growing even though the garbage collector exists?","a":"<p>The collector frees only unreachable objects. Objects still referenced by a global list, a long-lived cache (<code>lru_cache</code> with <code>maxsize=None</code>), a closure or an event-handler registry stay alive. Reference cycles involving <code>__del__</code> or C extensions can also leak. Use bounded caches, <code>weakref</code> and explicit cleanup.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0756","card":"q-database-with-python-105-practical-scenarios-performance-debuggin","sec":"database-with-python-s106","q":"In Python, how do you read a very large CSV without loading it fully, and what is the catch with building lists in the loop?","a":"<p>Iterate over <code>csv.reader(f)</code> row by row, which streams the file. The comprehension in this snippet builds a list of <code>None</code> values for the side effect, so use a plain <code>for</code> loop. Process each row and write results immediately, or use pandas with <code>chunksize</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import csv\nwith open(&quot;large.csv&quot;) as f:\n    [print(row) for row in csv.reader(f)]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0757","card":"q-database-with-python-105-practical-scenarios-performance-debuggin","sec":"database-with-python-s106","q":"In Python, how does <code>__slots__</code> reduce memory for classes with many instances?","a":"<p>It replaces the per-instance <code>__dict__</code> with fixed attribute storage, saving memory when you create millions of small objects. The cost is that you cannot add attributes dynamically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0758","card":"q-database-with-python-106-practical-scenarios-rest-api-tests-cachi","sec":"database-with-python-s107","q":"How do you design a REST API with Flask?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from flask import Flask, jsonify\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n@app.route(&quot;/hi&quot;, methods=[&quot;GET&quot;])\ndef hi():\n    return jsonify({&quot;msg&quot;: &quot;Hello&quot;})\n\napp.run()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0759","card":"q-database-with-python-106-practical-scenarios-rest-api-tests-cachi","sec":"database-with-python-s107","q":"How do you unit test with <code>unittest</code> or <code>pytest</code>?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\n# pytest\ndef test_add():\n    assert add(2, 3) == 5</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0760","card":"q-database-with-python-106-practical-scenarios-rest-api-tests-cachi","sec":"database-with-python-s107","q":"How do you add simple caching?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from functools import lru_cache\n\n@lru_cache\ndef f(x):\n    return x * x\n\nprint(f(5))  # Cached</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0761","card":"q-database-with-python-106-practical-scenarios-rest-api-tests-cachi","sec":"database-with-python-s107","q":"How do you scrape a website?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import requests, bs4\nsoup = bs4.BeautifulSoup(requests.get(&quot;http://example.com&quot;).text, &quot;html.parser&quot;)\nprint(soup.h1.text)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0762","card":"q-database-with-python-106-practical-scenarios-rest-api-tests-cachi","sec":"database-with-python-s107","q":"In Flask, what does the <code>@app.route</code> decorator do, and why not use <code>app.run()</code> in production?","a":"<p><code>@app.route</code> registers the function as the handler for that URL and the listed HTTP methods. <code>app.run()</code> starts Flask's development server, which is single-purpose and not hardened for load or security. Run the app under a WSGI server such as gunicorn or uWSGI behind a reverse proxy.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from flask import Flask, jsonify\napp = Flask(__name__)\n\n@app.route(&quot;/hi&quot;, methods=[&quot;GET&quot;])\ndef hi():\n    return jsonify({&quot;msg&quot;: &quot;Hello&quot;})\n\napp.run()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0763","card":"q-database-with-python-106-practical-scenarios-rest-api-tests-cachi","sec":"database-with-python-s107","q":"In Flask, why return <code>jsonify(...)</code> and not a plain string?","a":"<p><code>jsonify</code> serialises the data to JSON and sets the <code>Content-Type: application/json</code> header, so clients parse it correctly. Newer Flask versions (1.1+) also convert a returned dict to JSON automatically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0764","card":"q-database-with-python-106-practical-scenarios-rest-api-tests-cachi","sec":"database-with-python-s107","q":"When designing a REST API in Python, how do <code>PUT</code>, <code>PATCH</code> and <code>POST</code> differ, and which are idempotent?","a":"<p><code>POST</code> creates a resource or triggers an action and is not idempotent. <code>PUT</code> replaces a whole resource and is idempotent. <code>PATCH</code> changes part of a resource and is not guaranteed idempotent. <code>GET</code>, <code>PUT</code> and <code>DELETE</code> are idempotent, which matters for safe retries. Return proper status codes (201 created, 400 invalid, 404 not found).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0765","card":"q-database-with-python-106-practical-scenarios-rest-api-tests-cachi","sec":"database-with-python-s107","q":"When should you choose FastAPI over Flask or Django for a Python API?","a":"<p>FastAPI is async-first, validates request and response data from type hints (Pydantic) and generates OpenAPI docs automatically, which suits API-only services. Flask is a minimal and flexible micro-framework. Django includes an ORM, admin and auth for full web applications.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0766","card":"q-database-with-python-106-practical-scenarios-rest-api-tests-cachi","sec":"database-with-python-s107","q":"In pytest, how does a plain function with <code>assert</code> become a test, and how does it compare with <code>unittest</code>?","a":"<p>pytest discovers files named <code>test_*.py</code> and functions named <code>test_*</code>, and rewrites <code>assert</code> to show detailed failure output. <code>unittest</code> needs <code>TestCase</code> classes and <code>self.assertEqual</code>. pytest also adds fixtures and <code>@pytest.mark.parametrize</code>, and it runs <code>unittest</code> tests too.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\n# pytest\ndef test_add():\n    assert add(2, 3) == 5</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0767","card":"q-database-with-python-106-practical-scenarios-rest-api-tests-cachi","sec":"database-with-python-s107","q":"In Python tests, how do you test code that calls an external service or database?","a":"<p>Replace the dependency with a fake using <code>unittest.mock.patch</code> or pytest's <code>monkeypatch</code>, or use fixtures that create a temporary or in-memory database. Unit tests should be fast and not depend on the network. Cover the real integration separately with a few integration tests.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0768","card":"q-database-with-python-106-practical-scenarios-rest-api-tests-cachi","sec":"database-with-python-s107","q":"In Python, how does <code>functools.lru_cache</code> work, and what are its limits?","a":"<p>It stores results keyed by the arguments, so repeated calls return the saved value. Arguments must be hashable. The default <code>maxsize</code> is 128 (least recently used entries are evicted), and <code>maxsize=None</code> is unbounded and can grow forever. Use it only on pure functions. Bare <code>@lru_cache</code> without parentheses works in Python 3.8+, and <code>functools.cache</code> (3.9+) is the unbounded version.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from functools import lru_cache\n\n@lru_cache\ndef f(x):\n    return x * x\n\nprint(f(5))  # Cached</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0769","card":"q-database-with-python-106-practical-scenarios-rest-api-tests-cachi","sec":"database-with-python-s107","q":"In Python, when is <code>lru_cache</code> the wrong tool for caching?","a":"<p>When results depend on changing data (database rows, time, files), because stale values are returned until evicted. It is per process, so multiple workers do not share it and cannot invalidate it. Use a shared cache such as Redis with a TTL for that, and design cache invalidation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0770","card":"q-database-with-python-106-practical-scenarios-rest-api-tests-cachi","sec":"database-with-python-s107","q":"In Python, what is missing in this web scraping snippet that can make a script hang or break?","a":"<p><code>requests.get</code> has no timeout by default and can wait forever, so pass <code>timeout=</code>. Also call <code>raise_for_status()</code> to catch HTTP errors. <code>soup.h1</code> is <code>None</code> if the page has no <code>h1</code>, which raises <code>AttributeError</code>. Respect <code>robots.txt</code> and terms of service, send a proper User-Agent, and rate-limit requests.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import requests, bs4\nsoup = bs4.BeautifulSoup(requests.get(&quot;http://example.com&quot;).text, &quot;html.parser&quot;)\nprint(soup.h1.text)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0771","card":"q-database-with-python-106-practical-scenarios-rest-api-tests-cachi","sec":"database-with-python-s107","q":"In Python, how do you scrape a page whose content is rendered by JavaScript?","a":"<p><code>requests</code> plus BeautifulSoup only sees the initial HTML. First look in the browser's network tab for the JSON API the page calls and use that. Otherwise drive a real browser with Playwright or Selenium.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0772","card":"q-database-with-python-107-practical-scenarios-concurrency-and-depl","sec":"database-with-python-s108","q":"How do you handle concurrency in a web app?","a":"<p>Use <code>asyncio</code> or <code>gunicorn</code> workers.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>gunicorn -w 4 app:app</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0773","card":"q-database-with-python-107-practical-scenarios-concurrency-and-depl","sec":"database-with-python-s108","q":"How do you deploy a Python app to production?","a":"<p>Use Docker, gunicorn and a virtualenv.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Dockerfile</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>FROM python:3.9\nCOPY . .\nRUN pip install -r requirements.txt\nCMD [&quot;gunicorn&quot;, &quot;app:app&quot;]</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0774","card":"q-database-with-python-107-practical-scenarios-concurrency-and-depl","sec":"database-with-python-s108","q":"In Python, how does gunicorn handle concurrent requests for a Flask app?","a":"<p><code>-w 4</code> starts four worker processes, each handling requests, so requests run in parallel across CPU cores and a crash affects only one worker. Worker threads (<code>--threads</code>) or async workers add concurrency for I/O-bound apps. The gunicorn docs suggest starting near <code>(2 x cores) + 1</code> workers and tuning by load. Workers do not share memory, so state belongs in a database or cache.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>gunicorn -w 4 app:app</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0775","card":"q-database-with-python-107-practical-scenarios-concurrency-and-depl","sec":"database-with-python-s108","q":"What problems does this Dockerfile have for a production Python app?","a":"<p><code>COPY . .</code> before <code>pip install</code> invalidates the dependency layer on every code change, so copy <code>requirements.txt</code> first, install, then copy the code. It has no <code>WORKDIR</code>, no non-root user, and the full image is large (prefer a <code>-slim</code> variant). The Python version should be a supported release. <code>gunicorn</code> needs a bind address such as <code>--bind 0.0.0.0:8000</code> in a container. Keep secrets out of the image and pass them as environment variables.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Dockerfile</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>FROM python:3.9\nCOPY . .\nRUN pip install -r requirements.txt\nCMD [&quot;gunicorn&quot;, &quot;app:app&quot;]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0776","card":"q-database-with-python-107-practical-scenarios-concurrency-and-depl","sec":"database-with-python-s108","q":"In Python deployment, why use a virtualenv or container instead of installing packages system-wide?","a":"<p>It isolates dependencies per application, makes the runtime reproducible and avoids conflicts with the operating system's own Python packages. A container also packages the OS libraries the app needs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0777","card":"q-database-with-python-107-practical-scenarios-concurrency-and-depl","sec":"database-with-python-s108","q":"In Python web apps, why should the application be stateless when you run several workers?","a":"<p>Requests can land on any worker or machine, and workers do not share memory. State such as sessions, caches and uploads must live in shared stores (database, Redis, object storage), so you can add or restart workers freely.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0778","card":"q-database-with-python-107-practical-scenarios-concurrency-and-depl","sec":"database-with-python-s108","q":"When should you pick async workers or <code>asyncio</code> over multiple gunicorn processes?","a":"<p>Use async (for example FastAPI on uvicorn) when the app spends most time waiting on network I/O and must hold many concurrent connections. Use more processes for CPU-bound request handling. Blocking calls inside async code stall the event loop.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0779","card":"q-database-with-python-108-connection-timeout-error","sec":"database-with-python-s109","q":"A <code>snowflake.connector</code> connection times out. How do you troubleshoot it?","a":"<p>Check network reachability, firewall rules for outbound 443, the Snowflake account configuration and the connection parameters. Set a login timeout and a network timeout, test the socket, then retry with a longer timeout.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nimport logging\nimport socket\n\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.DEBUG)\n\ntry:\n    conn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n        user=&#x27;your_user&#x27;,\n        password=&#x27;your_password&#x27;,\n        account=&#x27;your_account.region.cloud&#x27;,\n        login_timeout=10,\n        network_timeout=30\n    )\n    logging.info(&quot;Connection successful&quot;)\nexcept snowflake.connector.errors.OperationalError as e:\n    logging.error(f&quot;Connection timeout: {e}&quot;)\n    try:\n        socket.create_connection((&quot;snowflake.com&quot;, 443), timeout=5)\n        logging.info(&quot;Network is reachable&quot;)\n    except socket.timeout:\n        logging.error(&quot;Network issue: unable to reach Snowflake&quot;)\n    logging.info(&quot;Check firewall rules for outbound connections to Snowflake&quot;)\n    conn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n        user=&#x27;your_user&#x27;,\n        password=&#x27;your_password&#x27;,\n        account=&#x27;your_account.region.cloud&#x27;,\n        login_timeout=30   # increased timeout\n    )\nfinally:\n    if &#x27;conn&#x27; in locals():\n        conn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0780","card":"q-database-with-python-108-connection-timeout-error","sec":"database-with-python-s109","q":"In the Snowflake Python connector, what is the difference between <code>login_timeout</code> and <code>network_timeout</code>?","a":"<p><code>login_timeout</code> limits how long the connector keeps trying to authenticate and establish the session. <code>network_timeout</code> limits how long it keeps retrying network requests for other operations after login. Set both explicitly so a network problem fails in a bounded time and does not hang the job.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>conn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n    ...\n    login_timeout=10,\n    network_timeout=30\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0781","card":"q-database-with-python-108-connection-timeout-error","sec":"database-with-python-s109","q":"A Python script cannot reach Snowflake and times out. In what order do you check the cause?","a":"<p>Check DNS resolution of the account host, then TCP reachability on port 443, then proxy settings (<code>HTTPS_PROXY</code>, corporate proxy), firewall and egress rules, and any VPN or private link setup. Snowflake offers <code>SYSTEM$ALLOWLIST()</code> to list the hosts to allow and the SnowCD tool to test connectivity. Increase timeouts only after the network path is verified, otherwise you just wait longer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0782","card":"q-database-with-python-108-connection-timeout-error","sec":"database-with-python-s109","q":"How do you tell a DNS failure, a timeout and \"connection refused\" apart when a database connection fails?","a":"<p>A DNS failure means the host name does not resolve (a typo or missing DNS). A timeout means packets are being dropped, usually by a firewall or routing problem, so nothing answers. \"Connection refused\" means the host answered but nothing listens on that port, or the host actively rejected the connection. Each points to a different layer to inspect. For a managed cloud database with a hostname, \"refused\" usually points to the wrong host or port or a local proxy or sidecar, and a timeout to blocked egress.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0783","card":"q-database-with-python-108-connection-timeout-error","sec":"database-with-python-s109","q":"What is wrong with this connectivity check inside an <code>except</code> block for a Snowflake timeout?","a":"<p>It tests the public marketing domain instead of the actual account host, the returned socket is never closed, and only <code>socket.timeout</code> is caught, so DNS errors and refused connections raise uncaught exceptions. Test the real account host name and handle <code>OSError</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>try:\n    socket.create_connection((&quot;snowflake.com&quot;, 443), timeout=5)\n    logging.info(&quot;Network is reachable&quot;)\nexcept socket.timeout:\n    logging.error(&quot;Network issue: unable to reach Snowflake&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0784","card":"q-database-with-python-108-connection-timeout-error","sec":"database-with-python-s109","q":"In Python, why initialise <code>conn = None</code> and close it in <code>finally</code>, or use <code>with</code>, instead of <code>if 'conn' in locals()</code>?","a":"<p><code>conn = None</code> before the <code>try</code> makes the check explicit and avoids <code>NameError</code> tricks with <code>locals()</code>. If <code>connect</code> itself fails, the name would otherwise be unbound, and a <code>finally</code> that closes it would raise <code>NameError</code> and hide the original error. The Snowflake connection also works as a context manager (<code>with snowflake.connector.connect(...) as conn:</code>), which closes it automatically even when an error occurs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0785","card":"q-database-with-python-108-connection-timeout-error","sec":"database-with-python-s109","q":"Why is blindly retrying with a longer timeout a poor fix for a connection timeout?","a":"<p>A longer timeout only delays the failure if the network is blocked, and long hangs tie up workers and mask the cause. Fix the path (firewall, proxy, DNS), and for transient problems use a bounded retry with exponential backoff and a log of each attempt.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0786","card":"q-database-with-python-109-invalid-account-name","sec":"database-with-python-s110","q":"A script fails with \"Invalid account name provided.\" What do you verify?","a":"<p>The account URL format (<code>&lt;account&gt;.&lt;region&gt;.&lt;cloud&gt;</code> or just <code>&lt;account&gt;</code>), the region and the cloud provider (AWS, Azure, GCP). Check the account URL in the Snowflake admin console.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nimport logging\n\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.DEBUG)\n\ndef verify_account_name(account):\n    parts = account.split(&#x27;.&#x27;)\n    if len(parts) == 1:\n        logging.info(&quot;No region/cloud specified, assuming default&quot;)\n    elif len(parts) == 3:\n        logging.info(f&quot;Account: {parts[0]}, Region: {parts[1]}, Cloud: {parts[2]}&quot;)\n    else:\n        logging.error(&quot;Invalid account format&quot;)\n        return False\n    return True\n\ntry:\n    account = &#x27;your_account.region.cloud&#x27;   # e.g. myaccount.us-east-1.aws\n    if verify_account_name(account):\n        conn = snowflake.connector.connect(user=&#x27;your_user&#x27;, password=&#x27;your_password&#x27;, account=account)\n        logging.info(&quot;Connection successful&quot;)\n    else:\n        logging.error(&quot;Fix account name format and retry&quot;)\nexcept snowflake.connector.errors.DatabaseError as e:\n    logging.error(f&quot;Connection failed: {e}&quot;)\n    logging.info(&quot;Verify account name format: &lt;account&gt;.&lt;region&gt;.&lt;cloud&gt;&quot;)\nfinally:\n    if &#x27;conn&#x27; in locals():\n        conn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0787","card":"q-database-with-python-109-invalid-account-name","sec":"database-with-python-s110","q":"In the Snowflake Python connector, what formats can the <code>account</code> parameter take, and what must it not contain?","a":"<p>Use the account identifier: either <code>organization-account</code> (preferred) or the account locator with region and cloud, such as <code>xy12345.us-east-1.aws</code>. Pass only the identifier, not the full URL or the <code>.snowflakecomputing.com</code> suffix. A typo produces an error before any authentication happens.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0788","card":"q-database-with-python-109-invalid-account-name","sec":"database-with-python-s110","q":"In Snowflake, how do you find the correct account identifier from inside the account?","a":"<p>Check the account details in the Snowsight UI, or run <code>SELECT CURRENT_ORGANIZATION_NAME(), CURRENT_ACCOUNT_NAME();</code> to get the organization and account names that form the <code>organization-account</code> identifier.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0789","card":"q-database-with-python-109-invalid-account-name","sec":"database-with-python-s110","q":"In Python, what is the flaw in validating a Snowflake account by splitting it on dots and requiring one or three parts?","a":"<p>The check only validates the shape, not that the account exists, and a hand-written rule goes stale when formats change. It accepts a malformed single part like <code>my account</code>, and the <code>organization-account</code> format has no dots. Treat the real connection attempt as the validator and report the connector's error, instead of reimplementing the rules.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def verify_account_name(account):\n    parts = account.split(&#x27;.&#x27;)\n    if len(parts) == 1:\n        logging.info(&quot;No region/cloud specified, assuming default&quot;)\n    elif len(parts) == 3:\n        logging.info(f&quot;Account: {parts[0]}, Region: {parts[1]}, Cloud: {parts[2]}&quot;)\n    else:\n        logging.error(&quot;Invalid account format&quot;)\n        return False\n    return True</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0790","card":"q-database-with-python-109-invalid-account-name","sec":"database-with-python-s110","q":"How can you tell a wrong account identifier from wrong credentials when a Snowflake connection fails?","a":"<p>A wrong account usually fails before login: the host cannot be resolved or the service does not respond as expected, so you see a connection or \"account not found\" style error. Wrong credentials reach Snowflake and are rejected with an authentication error that names the login or password. Compare the error type and check the host being contacted.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0791","card":"q-database-with-python-110-missing-database-driver","sec":"database-with-python-s111","q":"A MySQL connection fails with \"No suitable driver found.\" What is the cause?","a":"<p>The driver (<code>mysql-connector-python</code> or <code>pymysql</code>) is not installed or not imported. Install it and import it.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import logging\n\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.DEBUG)\n\ntry:\n    import mysql.connector\nexcept ImportError:\n    logging.error(&quot;MySQL driver not found. Installing mysql-connector-python&quot;)\n    import subprocess\n    subprocess.check_call([&#x27;pip&#x27;, &#x27;install&#x27;, &#x27;mysql-connector-python&#x27;])\n    import mysql.connector\n\ntry:\n    conn = mysql.connector.connect(\n        host=&#x27;localhost&#x27;,\n        user=&#x27;your_user&#x27;,\n        password=&#x27;your_password&#x27;,\n        database=&#x27;your_database&#x27;\n    )\n    logging.info(&quot;MySQL connection successful&quot;)\nexcept mysql.connector.Error as e:\n    logging.error(f&quot;Connection failed: {e}&quot;)\nfinally:\n    if &#x27;conn&#x27; in locals():\n        conn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0792","card":"q-database-with-python-110-missing-database-driver","sec":"database-with-python-s111","q":"In Python, what error do you see when a database driver is not installed, and how is it different from \"No suitable driver found\"?","a":"<p>Python raises <code>ModuleNotFoundError</code> (a subclass of <code>ImportError</code>) on <code>import mysql.connector</code>. \"No suitable driver found\" is a Java JDBC message and not a Python one. In Python the fix is to install the correct package into the interpreter that runs the code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0793","card":"q-database-with-python-110-missing-database-driver","sec":"database-with-python-s111","q":"In Python, why is installing a missing driver at run time with <code>subprocess</code> and <code>pip</code> a bad pattern?","a":"<p>Installing at run time makes behavior depend on network access and write permission, produces different versions on each host, and <code>pip</code> may belong to a different Python than the running one. Declare the dependency in <code>requirements.txt</code> or the package metadata and install it during build or deployment. If you must call pip, use <code>[sys.executable, '-m', 'pip', ...]</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>try:\n    import mysql.connector\nexcept ImportError:\n    import subprocess\n    subprocess.check_call([&#x27;pip&#x27;, &#x27;install&#x27;, &#x27;mysql-connector-python&#x27;])\n    import mysql.connector</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0794","card":"q-database-with-python-110-missing-database-driver","sec":"database-with-python-s111","q":"In Python, a driver is installed with pip but the application still reports it missing. What do you check?","a":"<p>The application probably runs under a different interpreter or virtual environment than the one pip installed into. Compare <code>sys.executable</code> and <code>python -m pip list</code> for the running process, check that the virtual environment is activated in the service, cron job or IDE, and look for a local file shadowing the module name.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0795","card":"q-database-with-python-110-missing-database-driver","sec":"database-with-python-s111","q":"In Python, why can installing a database driver fail on a server without compilers, and how do you avoid it?","a":"<p>Some drivers, such as <code>mysqlclient</code> or <code>psycopg2</code> built from source, compile C extensions and need system libraries and headers (MySQL client libraries, <code>libpq</code>). Missing ones cause build errors. Use pure-Python drivers (<code>PyMySQL</code>, <code>mysql-connector-python</code>), prebuilt wheels such as <code>psycopg2-binary</code> or <code>psycopg</code>, or install the system packages in the image.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0796","card":"q-database-with-python-111-works-locally-fails-in-production-connec","sec":"database-with-python-s112","q":"A Snowflake script works locally but fails in production. Which parameters differ between environments?","a":"<p>Account, warehouse, database, schema, role and the environment-specific credentials. Load them per environment from configuration and environment variables.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nimport os\nimport logging\n\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.DEBUG)\n\ndef load_env_config(env=&#x27;prod&#x27;):\n    configs = {\n        &#x27;dev&#x27;: {&#x27;account&#x27;: &#x27;dev_account.region.cloud&#x27;, &#x27;warehouse&#x27;: &#x27;dev_warehouse&#x27;,\n                &#x27;database&#x27;: &#x27;dev_db&#x27;, &#x27;schema&#x27;: &#x27;dev_schema&#x27;, &#x27;role&#x27;: &#x27;dev_role&#x27;},\n        &#x27;prod&#x27;: {&#x27;account&#x27;: &#x27;prod_account.region.cloud&#x27;, &#x27;warehouse&#x27;: &#x27;prod_warehouse&#x27;,\n                 &#x27;database&#x27;: &#x27;prod_db&#x27;, &#x27;schema&#x27;: &#x27;prod_schema&#x27;, &#x27;role&#x27;: &#x27;prod_role&#x27;}\n    }\n    return configs.get(env, configs[&#x27;prod&#x27;])\n\ntry:\n    env = os.getenv(&#x27;ENV&#x27;, &#x27;prod&#x27;)\n    config = load_env_config(env)\n    conn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n        user=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_USER&#x27;),\n        password=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_PASSWORD&#x27;),\n        account=config[&#x27;account&#x27;],\n        warehouse=config[&#x27;warehouse&#x27;],\n        database=config[&#x27;database&#x27;],\n        schema=config[&#x27;schema&#x27;],\n        role=config[&#x27;role&#x27;]\n    )\n    logging.info(f&quot;Connected to Snowflake in {env} environment&quot;)\nexcept snowflake.connector.errors.OperationalError as e:\n    logging.error(f&quot;Connection failed: {e}&quot;)\n    logging.info(f&quot;Current config: {config}&quot;)\nfinally:\n    if &#x27;conn&#x27; in locals():\n        conn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0797","card":"q-database-with-python-111-works-locally-fails-in-production-connec","sec":"database-with-python-s112","q":"A Python database script works on a laptop but fails in production. What differences do you check first?","a":"<p>Configuration (host, account, database, schema, role, warehouse), credentials and permissions, network path (firewall, egress rules, proxy, DNS, private networking, IP allowlists), driver and Python versions, environment variables that are missing in the service, and clock or certificate differences. Test connectivity from the production host itself, not from a laptop.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0798","card":"q-database-with-python-111-works-locally-fails-in-production-connec","sec":"database-with-python-s112","q":"In Python, why is this environment-selection code dangerous?","a":"<p>A missing or misspelled <code>ENV</code> silently falls back to production, so a developer's script can run against the production database. Fail closed: require the variable and raise an error for unknown values, or default to the least harmful environment.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def load_env_config(env=&#x27;prod&#x27;):\n    configs = {\n        ...\n    }\n    return configs.get(env, configs[&#x27;prod&#x27;])\n...\n    env = os.getenv(&#x27;ENV&#x27;, &#x27;prod&#x27;)\n    config = load_env_config(env)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0799","card":"q-database-with-python-111-works-locally-fails-in-production-connec","sec":"database-with-python-s112","q":"In Python, what is the risk of <code>os.getenv('SNOWFLAKE_USER')</code> and <code>os.getenv('SNOWFLAKE_PASSWORD')</code> when the variables are not set?","a":"<p><code>os.getenv</code> returns <code>None</code> instead of failing, so the connector gets <code>None</code> and reports a confusing authentication or parameter error. Use <code>os.environ['NAME']</code> for required settings so a missing variable fails immediately and clearly, and never print secrets in the error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0800","card":"q-database-with-python-111-works-locally-fails-in-production-connec","sec":"database-with-python-s112","q":"In Python, why is logging the full config dictionary on a failed connection risky?","a":"<p>The config can contain account names, roles and sometimes credentials, and logs are widely readable and retained. Log only the non-sensitive identifiers (environment name, host, user) and mask or omit secrets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0801","card":"q-database-with-python-112-ssl-certificate-error","sec":"database-with-python-s113","q":"SSL certificate verification fails when connecting to Snowflake. How do you diagnose and fix it?","a":"<p>Check SSL settings, CA certificates and network proxies. <code>insecure_mode=True</code> bypasses verification and is only for debugging, never production. Point the CA bundle environment variable at the right certificate.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nimport logging\nimport os\n\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.DEBUG)\n\ntry:\n    conn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n        user=&#x27;your_user&#x27;,\n        password=&#x27;your_password&#x27;,\n        account=&#x27;your_account.region.cloud&#x27;,\n        insecure_mode=False   # default SSL verification\n    )\nexcept snowflake.connector.errors.OperationalError as e:\n    logging.error(f&quot;SSL error: {e}&quot;)\n    logging.warning(&quot;Attempting connection with insecure_mode=True (debugging only)&quot;)\n    conn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n        user=&#x27;your_user&#x27;,\n        password=&#x27;your_password&#x27;,\n        account=&#x27;your_account.region.cloud&#x27;,\n        insecure_mode=True\n    )\n    logging.info(&quot;Check CA certificates or proxy settings&quot;)\n    # os.environ[&#x27;REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE&#x27;] = &#x27;/path/to/ca-cert.pem&#x27;\nfinally:\n    if &#x27;conn&#x27; in locals():\n        conn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0802","card":"q-database-with-python-112-ssl-certificate-error","sec":"database-with-python-s113","q":"In the Snowflake Python connector, what does <code>insecure_mode=True</code> do, and why must it never reach production?","a":"<p>It turns off certificate revocation (OCSP) checking for the connection. A revoked or forged certificate is then accepted, which opens the door to man-in-the-middle attacks. Use it at most for a short local test to prove that certificate checking is the cause, then fix the trust problem instead.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0803","card":"q-database-with-python-112-ssl-certificate-error","sec":"database-with-python-s113","q":"A Python script behind a corporate proxy fails TLS certificate verification when it connects to Snowflake. What is the usual cause and the proper fix?","a":"<p>The proxy inspects TLS traffic and re-signs it with the company's own CA, which is not in the trust store Python uses. Fix: add that root CA to a CA bundle file and point the library at it with <code>REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE</code> (or <code>SSL_CERT_FILE</code>), and set <code>HTTPS_PROXY</code> if the proxy is required. Do not disable verification.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0804","card":"q-database-with-python-112-ssl-certificate-error","sec":"database-with-python-s113","q":"In general, what are the common reasons for a \"certificate verify failed\" error in a Python client?","a":"<p>The server certificate chain ends in a CA the client does not trust, the certificate has expired, the host name does not match the certificate, an intermediate certificate is missing, or the client clock is badly wrong. Check the chain with <code>openssl s_client -connect host:443</code>, then fix the trust store or the server, not the verification flag.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0805","card":"q-database-with-python-112-ssl-certificate-error","sec":"database-with-python-s113","q":"In Python with the Snowflake connector, what is wrong with this fallback after an SSL error?","a":"<p>Any failure, including a real attack, silently downgrades the connection to the insecure mode. A debugging switch becomes production behaviour. The code should fail closed: log the error and stop, and allow insecure mode only through an explicit, temporary developer setting.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>except snowflake.connector.errors.OperationalError as e:\n    logging.error(f&quot;SSL error: {e}&quot;)\n    conn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n        ...\n        insecure_mode=True\n    )</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0806","card":"q-database-with-python-113-connection-pooling-for-frequent-connecti","sec":"database-with-python-s114","q":"Frequent connection opens cause delays. How do you pool connections?","a":"<p>Use SQLAlchemy's <code>QueuePool</code> (<code>pool_size</code> is the base number of connections, <code>max_overflow</code> allows extras, <code>pool_timeout</code> is the wait for a free connection). Snowflake has no traditional client-side pooling, so keep connections minimal and reuse them.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from sqlalchemy import create_engine\nfrom sqlalchemy.pool import QueuePool\nimport snowflake.connector\nimport logging\n\nmysql_url = &quot;mysql+mysqlconnector://your_user:your_password@localhost/your_database&quot;\n\ntry:\n    mysql_engine = create_engine(\n        mysql_url,\n        poolclass=QueuePool,\n        pool_size=5,\n        max_overflow=10,\n        pool_timeout=30\n    )\n    with mysql_engine.connect() as conn:\n        conn.execute(&quot;SELECT 1&quot;)\n        logging.info(&quot;MySQL pooled connection successful&quot;)\n\n    snowflake_conn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n        user=&#x27;your_user&#x27;,\n        password=&#x27;your_password&#x27;,\n        account=&#x27;your_account.region.cloud&#x27;,\n        warehouse=&#x27;your_warehouse&#x27;,\n        database=&#x27;your_database&#x27;,\n        schema=&#x27;your_schema&#x27;\n    )\nexcept Exception as e:\n    logging.error(f&quot;Connection error: {e}&quot;)\nfinally:\n    if &#x27;snowflake_conn&#x27; in locals():\n        snowflake_conn.close()\n    if &#x27;mysql_engine&#x27; in locals():\n        mysql_engine.dispose()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0807","card":"q-database-with-python-113-connection-pooling-for-frequent-connecti","sec":"database-with-python-s114","q":"Why is opening a new Snowflake connection for every query expensive, and what should a long-running service do instead?","a":"<p>Each new connection pays for a TLS handshake, authentication and session creation before the first query runs. Open one connection per worker or job, reuse it for many statements (cursors are cheap), and close it when the work ends. A pool is the way to share connections among threads.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0808","card":"q-database-with-python-113-connection-pooling-for-frequent-connecti","sec":"database-with-python-s114","q":"In SQLAlchemy, with <code>create_engine(url, pool_size=5, max_overflow=10, pool_timeout=30)</code>, how many connections can be open at once, and what happens when all are in use?","a":"<p>At most 15 (5 kept in the pool plus 10 overflow). A request for the 16th waits up to <code>pool_timeout</code> seconds and then raises a <code>TimeoutError</code>. Overflow connections are closed when returned instead of being kept.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0809","card":"q-database-with-python-113-connection-pooling-for-frequent-connecti","sec":"database-with-python-s114","q":"In SQLAlchemy, what problems do <code>pool_pre_ping=True</code> and <code>pool_recycle</code> solve?","a":"<p>Idle pooled connections can be closed by the database, a firewall or a load balancer. <code>pool_pre_ping</code> tests a connection when it is checked out and replaces a dead one, avoiding a failed first query. <code>pool_recycle=N</code> closes and reopens connections older than N seconds, so they never outlive a server-side idle timeout such as MySQL <code>wait_timeout</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0810","card":"q-database-with-python-113-connection-pooling-for-frequent-connecti","sec":"database-with-python-s114","q":"In SQLAlchemy, what does <code>engine.dispose()</code> do at application shutdown, and why not only close each connection?","a":"<p><code>conn.close()</code> on a pooled connection only returns it to the pool; the underlying socket stays open. <code>engine.dispose()</code> closes every pooled connection, which matters at application shutdown and in tests.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0811","card":"q-database-with-python-113-connection-pooling-for-frequent-connecti","sec":"database-with-python-s114","q":"When would you choose SQLAlchemy <code>NullPool</code> over the default <code>QueuePool</code>?","a":"<p>When something else does the pooling or when connections must not outlive a call: an external pooler such as PgBouncer, short-lived serverless functions, or forked worker processes. <code>NullPool</code> opens a real connection on every checkout and closes it on release, so it is simple and safe but slower per call.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0812","card":"q-database-with-python-113-connection-pooling-for-frequent-connecti","sec":"database-with-python-s114","q":"A web server forks several worker processes after creating a SQLAlchemy engine at import time. What can go wrong?","a":"<p>Child processes inherit the parent's open sockets, so several processes share the same database connections and corrupt each other's protocol state. Create the engine after the fork (in each worker), or call <code>engine.dispose()</code> in the child so it starts with a fresh pool.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0813","card":"q-database-with-python-114-environment-variable-mismatch","sec":"database-with-python-s115","q":"Credentials in environment variables are wrong. How do you verify and fix it?","a":"<p>Check that every required variable exists with <code>os.getenv()</code>, confirm names, load a <code>.env</code> file with <code>python-dotenv</code>, and report which variable is missing.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nimport os\nimport logging\nfrom dotenv import load_dotenv\n\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.DEBUG)\nload_dotenv()\n\ndef verify_env_vars():\n    required_vars = [&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_USER&#x27;, &#x27;SNOWFLAKE_PASSWORD&#x27;, &#x27;SNOWFLAKE_ACCOUNT&#x27;]\n    for var in required_vars:\n        if not os.getenv(var):\n            logging.error(f&quot;Missing environment variable: {var}&quot;)\n            return False\n    return True\n\ntry:\n    if verify_env_vars():\n        conn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n            user=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_USER&#x27;),\n            password=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_PASSWORD&#x27;),\n            account=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_ACCOUNT&#x27;),\n            warehouse=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_WAREHOUSE&#x27;, &#x27;default_warehouse&#x27;),\n            database=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_DATABASE&#x27;, &#x27;default_db&#x27;),\n            schema=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_SCHEMA&#x27;, &#x27;default_schema&#x27;)\n        )\n        logging.info(&quot;Connection successful&quot;)\n    else:\n        logging.info(&quot;Check .env file or system environment variables&quot;)\nexcept snowflake.connector.errors.DatabaseError as e:\n    logging.error(f&quot;Connection failed: {e}&quot;)\nfinally:\n    if &#x27;conn&#x27; in locals():\n        conn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0814","card":"q-database-with-python-114-environment-variable-mismatch","sec":"database-with-python-s115","q":"A Python app uses <code>load_dotenv()</code> but still connects with the wrong database password. The <code>.env</code> file has the right one. What is a likely cause?","a":"<p><code>load_dotenv()</code> does not override variables that are already set in the process (<code>override=False</code> is the default), so a stale variable exported in the shell, container or CI wins over the file. Print <code>os.getenv</code> for the key, unset the stale variable, or call <code>load_dotenv(override=True)</code> for local development only.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0815","card":"q-database-with-python-114-environment-variable-mismatch","sec":"database-with-python-s115","q":"In Python, what does <code>load_dotenv()</code> from <code>python-dotenv</code> do, and where should the <code>.env</code> file live?","a":"<p>It reads <code>KEY=value</code> lines from a <code>.env</code> file into <code>os.environ</code> for the current process. By default it does not override variables that are already set. Keep the file out of version control (<code>.gitignore</code>) and use it for local development only; in production take values from the platform (container env, secret manager).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0816","card":"q-database-with-python-114-environment-variable-mismatch","sec":"database-with-python-s115","q":"In a Python script that builds Snowflake connection settings from environment variables, what is the risk of defaults like these for required settings?","a":"<p>If the variable is missing in production, the app silently uses a wrong or non-existent warehouse or database and fails later with a confusing error, or worse works against the wrong data. Use defaults only for harmless optional settings. Required ones should be checked at startup and fail fast with the name of the missing variable.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>warehouse=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_WAREHOUSE&#x27;, &#x27;default_warehouse&#x27;),\ndatabase=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_DATABASE&#x27;, &#x27;default_db&#x27;),</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0817","card":"q-database-with-python-114-environment-variable-mismatch","sec":"database-with-python-s115","q":"In Python, how does <code>if not os.getenv(var):</code> treat an unset variable compared with one set to an empty string?","a":"<p>Both are falsy, so both are reported as missing. That is usually what you want for credentials. Use <code>os.getenv(var) is None</code> when you need to tell \"not set\" from \"set but empty\".</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0818","card":"q-database-with-python-114-environment-variable-mismatch","sec":"database-with-python-s115","q":"A script works in your shell, but the same script run by cron or systemd cannot see the database variables. Why?","a":"<p>Environment variables belong to a process and are inherited by its children. Cron and systemd start jobs with a minimal environment, not your login shell's. Define the variables in the unit file or crontab, load them from a file in the job, or inject them through the scheduler or container configuration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0819","card":"q-database-with-python-115-authentication-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s116","q":"A script fails with \"Authentication failed\". What do you check?","a":"<p>Username and password, key pair authentication, MFA and token expiration. For MFA use <code>authenticator='externalbrowser'</code> with a configured browser.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nfrom snowflake.connector.errors import DatabaseError\nimport os\n\ntry:\n    conn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n        user=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_USER&#x27;),\n        password=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_PASSWORD&#x27;),\n        account=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_ACCOUNT&#x27;),\n        authenticator=&#x27;snowflake&#x27;,   # &#x27;externalbrowser&#x27; for MFA/SSO\n        warehouse=&#x27;COMPUTE_WH&#x27;,\n        database=&#x27;TEST_DB&#x27;,\n        schema=&#x27;PUBLIC&#x27;\n    )\n    print(&quot;Connection successful&quot;)\nexcept DatabaseError as e:\n    if &quot;Authentication failed&quot; in str(e):\n        print(&quot;Authentication failed. Verify username, password, or MFA settings.&quot;)\n    raise</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0820","card":"q-database-with-python-115-authentication-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s116","q":"In the Snowflake Python connector, what does the <code>authenticator</code> parameter select, and which values exist?","a":"<p>It chooses how the login is performed. <code>snowflake</code> (default) is user and password; <code>externalbrowser</code> opens a browser for SSO; <code>oauth</code> uses an access token passed in <code>token</code>; <code>snowflake_jwt</code> uses key pair authentication; <code>username_password_mfa</code> supports MFA with token caching; an Okta URL uses native Okta SSO.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0821","card":"q-database-with-python-115-authentication-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s116","q":"Why does <code>authenticator='externalbrowser'</code> not work in a scheduled job or a container?","a":"<p>It needs an interactive browser session to complete the SSO login. Unattended workloads need a non-interactive method: key pair authentication, OAuth with a client credential flow, or a service account without an interactive factor, with the credential in a secret manager.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0822","card":"q-database-with-python-115-authentication-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s116","q":"How can you keep MFA enabled for a human user running scripts but avoid a prompt on every connection?","a":"<p>Use <code>authenticator='username_password_mfa'</code> with MFA token caching (<code>client_request_mfa_token=True</code>) so the connector reuses a cached token for a limited time. For service accounts, do not use MFA at all; use key pair authentication.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0823","card":"q-database-with-python-115-authentication-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s116","q":"How can you find out why a Snowflake login failed, apart from the exception text?","a":"<p>Query the <code>LOGIN_HISTORY</code> view (<code>SNOWFLAKE.ACCOUNT_USAGE</code> or the <code>INFORMATION_SCHEMA</code> table function). It records the user, client IP, authentication method, success flag and error message for each attempt. That separates a wrong password, a disabled or locked user, and a network policy block.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0824","card":"q-database-with-python-115-authentication-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s116","q":"In the Snowflake Python connector, what is fragile about this error check, and what is better?","a":"<p>Matching on message text breaks when the wording changes or is localized. Connector exceptions carry a numeric <code>errno</code> and <code>sqlstate</code>, so compare <code>e.errno</code> with the documented error codes instead.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>...\nexcept DatabaseError as e:\n    if &quot;Authentication failed&quot; in str(e):\n        print(&quot;Authentication failed. Verify username, password, or MFA settings.&quot;)\n    raise</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0825","card":"q-database-with-python-116-hardcoded-credentials","sec":"database-with-python-s117","q":"You find hardcoded Snowflake credentials in code review. How do you refactor?","a":"<p>Move them to environment variables, a secret manager such as AWS Secrets Manager, or the <code>keyring</code> library.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nimport os\nimport boto3\nfrom dotenv import load_dotenv\n\nload_dotenv()\n\ndef get_snowflake_credentials(secret_name, region_name):\n    client = boto3.client(&#x27;secretsmanager&#x27;, region_name=region_name)\n    response = client.get_secret_value(SecretId=secret_name)\n    return response[&#x27;SecretString&#x27;].split(&#x27;:&#x27;)\n\nsecret_name = &quot;snowflake/credentials&quot;\nregion_name = &quot;us-west-2&quot;\nuser, password = (get_snowflake_credentials(secret_name, region_name)\n                  if os.getenv(&#x27;USE_SECRETS&#x27;)\n                  else (os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_USER&#x27;), os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_PASSWORD&#x27;)))\n\nconn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n    user=user,\n    password=password,\n    account=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_ACCOUNT&#x27;),\n    warehouse=&#x27;COMPUTE_WH&#x27;,\n    database=&#x27;TEST_DB&#x27;,\n    schema=&#x27;PUBLIC&#x27;\n)\nconn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0826","card":"q-database-with-python-116-hardcoded-credentials","sec":"database-with-python-s117","q":"What does the <code>keyring</code> library do, and where is it a good fit?","a":"<p>It stores and reads secrets through the operating system's credential store (macOS Keychain, Windows Credential Locker, Linux Secret Service). It suits developer laptops and desktop tools. It is not a fit for servers and containers, where a secret manager or the platform's secret injection is used.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0827","card":"q-database-with-python-116-hardcoded-credentials","sec":"database-with-python-s117","q":"Compare environment variables, a <code>.env</code> file and AWS Secrets Manager for database credentials.","a":"<p>Environment variables are simple but visible in process listings, crash dumps and <code>docker inspect</code>, and they need a restart to change. A <code>.env</code> file is a developer convenience that must never be committed. Secrets Manager adds encryption with KMS, IAM-controlled access, audit through CloudTrail and built-in rotation, at the cost of an API call and a small fee.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0828","card":"q-database-with-python-116-hardcoded-credentials","sec":"database-with-python-s117","q":"In Python with boto3 and AWS Secrets Manager, what is fragile about reading a database login from the secret like this?","a":"<p>Splitting on <code>:</code> breaks when the password contains a colon and returns a positional pair that is easy to mix up. Store the secret as JSON with named keys (<code>{\"user\": ..., \"password\": ...}</code>) and read it with <code>json.loads</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>response = client.get_secret_value(SecretId=secret_name)\nreturn response[&#x27;SecretString&#x27;].split(&#x27;:&#x27;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0829","card":"q-database-with-python-116-hardcoded-credentials","sec":"database-with-python-s117","q":"In AWS, how does code calling <code>boto3.client('secretsmanager')</code> authenticate, and what permission does it need?","a":"<p>It uses the default credential chain, which on AWS resolves to the IAM role of the instance, ECS task or Lambda function, so no keys live in code. The role needs <code>secretsmanager:GetSecretValue</code> on that secret, plus <code>kms:Decrypt</code> when the secret uses a customer-managed KMS key.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0830","card":"q-database-with-python-116-hardcoded-credentials","sec":"database-with-python-s117","q":"Why fetch a secret once and cache it, rather than calling the secret manager before every query?","a":"<p>Each call adds latency and cost, and a busy service can hit API throttling. Fetch at startup, cache it in memory with a short time-to-live, and refresh it when authentication fails (which also picks up rotated values).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0831","card":"q-database-with-python-116-hardcoded-credentials","sec":"database-with-python-s117","q":"How do you stop hardcoded secrets from being committed in the first place?","a":"<p>Add a pre-commit hook and a CI check with a secret scanner such as gitleaks, detect-secrets or git-secrets, keep config files with secrets in <code>.gitignore</code>, and give developers a standard way to load secrets. Scanning history is also needed, since a deleted line stays in git history.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0832","card":"q-database-with-python-117-insufficient-privileges","sec":"database-with-python-s118","q":"The script connects but queries fail with \"Insufficient privileges\". How do you diagnose it?","a":"<p>Check which role is active (<code>CURRENT_ROLE()</code>), grant the needed permissions, or connect with (or <code>USE ROLE</code>) the correct role.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nimport os\n\nconn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n    user=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_USER&#x27;),\n    password=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_PASSWORD&#x27;),\n    account=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_ACCOUNT&#x27;),\n    warehouse=&#x27;COMPUTE_WH&#x27;,\n    database=&#x27;TEST_DB&#x27;,\n    schema=&#x27;PUBLIC&#x27;,\n    role=&#x27;REQUIRED_ROLE&#x27;\n)\n\ncursor = conn.cursor()\ntry:\n    cursor.execute(&quot;SELECT CURRENT_ROLE()&quot;)\n    print(f&quot;Current role: {cursor.fetchone()[0]}&quot;)\n    cursor.execute(&quot;SELECT * FROM TEST_TABLE LIMIT 1&quot;)\nexcept snowflake.connector.errors.ProgrammingError as e:\n    if &quot;Insufficient privileges&quot; in str(e):\n        print(&quot;Insufficient privileges. Verify role permissions or use a different role.&quot;)\n        cursor.execute(&quot;USE ROLE REQUIRED_ROLE&quot;)\n    raise\nfinally:\n    conn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0833","card":"q-database-with-python-117-insufficient-privileges","sec":"database-with-python-s118","q":"In Snowflake, why do privileges come from roles and what is the \"active role\" of a session?","a":"<p>Snowflake uses role-based access control: privileges are granted to roles, and roles are granted to users. A session runs with one primary role (set at connect or with <code>USE ROLE</code>) plus optional secondary roles. A query fails if the active role lacks the needed privilege, even when another role of the same user has it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0834","card":"q-database-with-python-117-insufficient-privileges","sec":"database-with-python-s118","q":"What privileges does a Snowflake role need to run <code>SELECT</code> on a table?","a":"<p><code>SELECT</code> on the table, <code>USAGE</code> on its schema and database, and <code>USAGE</code> on a warehouse to execute the query. Missing <code>USAGE</code> on the schema or database is a common cause of \"does not exist or not authorized\" errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0835","card":"q-database-with-python-117-insufficient-privileges","sec":"database-with-python-s118","q":"How do you inspect what a Snowflake role or user is allowed to do?","a":"<p>Use <code>SHOW GRANTS TO ROLE &lt;role&gt;</code>, <code>SHOW GRANTS TO USER &lt;user&gt;</code> and <code>SHOW GRANTS ON TABLE &lt;table&gt;</code>, and <code>SELECT CURRENT_ROLE()</code> to see the active role. Combine them to find which grant is missing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0836","card":"q-database-with-python-117-insufficient-privileges","sec":"database-with-python-s118","q":"What are future grants in Snowflake, and what problem do they solve?","a":"<p><code>GRANT SELECT ON FUTURE TABLES IN SCHEMA s TO ROLE r</code> grants a privilege on tables that do not exist yet. Without it, every new table needs a manual grant before the application role can read it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0837","card":"q-database-with-python-117-insufficient-privileges","sec":"database-with-python-s118","q":"In the Snowflake Python connector, what is the difference between passing <code>role='X'</code> to <code>connect()</code> and running <code>USE ROLE X</code> afterwards, and which role should an application use?","a":"<p><code>role=</code> makes the session start with that role. <code>USE ROLE</code> changes the role mid-session, and a failure that already happened is not undone. Applications should connect with a dedicated least-privilege service role, never <code>ACCOUNTADMIN</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0838","card":"q-database-with-python-118-key-pair-authentication-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s119","q":"Key pair auth fails with \"Invalid private key\". What is wrong?","a":"<p>Check the key format (PEM, PKCS8 <code>.p8</code>), the passphrase, file permissions (<code>chmod 600</code>) and the connector configuration.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nimport os\nfrom cryptography.hazmat.primitives import serialization\nfrom cryptography.hazmat.backends import default_backend\n\nwith open(&quot;snowflake_key.p8&quot;, &quot;rb&quot;) as key_file:\n    private_key = serialization.load_pem_private_key(\n        key_file.read(),\n        password=os.getenv(&#x27;KEY_PASSPHRASE&#x27;).encode() if os.getenv(&#x27;KEY_PASSPHRASE&#x27;) else None,\n        backend=default_backend()\n    )\n\nos.chmod(&quot;snowflake_key.p8&quot;, 0o600)   # secure permissions\n\nconn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n    user=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_USER&#x27;),\n    private_key=private_key,\n    account=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_ACCOUNT&#x27;),\n    warehouse=&#x27;COMPUTE_WH&#x27;,\n    database=&#x27;TEST_DB&#x27;,\n    schema=&#x27;PUBLIC&#x27;\n)\nconn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0839","card":"q-database-with-python-118-key-pair-authentication-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s119","q":"Why is key pair authentication preferred over a password for Snowflake service accounts?","a":"<p>The private key never leaves the client. Each login sends a signed JWT, so no reusable secret travels over the network. It works without an interactive MFA step, and the user can hold two public keys, which allows rotation without downtime.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0840","card":"q-database-with-python-118-key-pair-authentication-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s119","q":"How do you register a public key for key pair authentication in Snowflake?","a":"<p>Generate an RSA key pair (at least 2048 bits, private key in PKCS8 format), then run <code>ALTER USER &lt;user&gt; SET RSA_PUBLIC_KEY='&lt;base64 body without the BEGIN/END lines&gt;'</code>. The client connects with the private key.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0841","card":"q-database-with-python-118-key-pair-authentication-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s119","q":"After loading a key with <code>load_pem_private_key</code>, which format does the Snowflake connector's <code>private_key</code> parameter expect, and how do you convert it?","a":"<p>The documented approach passes the key as DER-encoded PKCS8 bytes:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from cryptography.hazmat.primitives import serialization\n\npkb = private_key.private_bytes(\n    encoding=serialization.Encoding.DER,\n    format=serialization.PrivateFormat.PKCS8,\n    encryption_algorithm=serialization.NoEncryption(),\n)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0842","card":"q-database-with-python-118-key-pair-authentication-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s119","q":"In <code>cryptography</code>, what happens if <code>load_pem_private_key</code> gets <code>password=None</code> for an encrypted key, or a password for an unencrypted key?","a":"<p>Both raise a <code>TypeError</code> (password missing for an encrypted key, or password given for an unencrypted one). Know whether the <code>.p8</code> file is encrypted, and pass the passphrase as bytes only in that case.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0843","card":"q-database-with-python-118-key-pair-authentication-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s119","q":"Why is calling <code>os.chmod(\"snowflake_key.p8\", 0o600)</code> after the key was already read a weak control?","a":"<p><code>0o600</code> (owner read/write only) is right, but the permission must be set when the file is created or deployed. Setting it after use leaves a window where other users could read the key. Better: keep the key in a secret manager and write it to a restricted location only when needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0844","card":"q-database-with-python-118-key-pair-authentication-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s119","q":"What is the trade-off of encrypting the private key file with a passphrase?","a":"<p>Encryption protects the key if the file leaks, but the passphrase must be stored somewhere too, so the secret problem moves rather than disappears. In production, load both from a secret manager and never keep them in the same place.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0845","card":"q-database-with-python-119-sql-injection","sec":"database-with-python-s120","q":"A script builds MySQL queries by string concatenation. How do you prevent SQL injection?","a":"<p>Use parameterized queries (bind variables in the Snowflake connector, bound parameters in SQLAlchemy).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nimport os\nfrom sqlalchemy import create_engine\n\nconn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n    user=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_USER&#x27;),\n    password=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_PASSWORD&#x27;),\n    account=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_ACCOUNT&#x27;),\n    warehouse=&#x27;COMPUTE_WH&#x27;,\n    database=&#x27;TEST_DB&#x27;,\n    schema=&#x27;PUBLIC&#x27;\n)\nuser_input = &quot;user_input&quot;\ntry:\n    cursor = conn.cursor()\n    query = &quot;SELECT * FROM TEST_TABLE WHERE column_name = %s&quot;\n    cursor.execute(query, (user_input,))\n    results = cursor.fetchall()\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(f&quot;Query error: {e}&quot;)\nfinally:\n    conn.close()\n\n# MySQL with SQLAlchemy\nengine = create_engine(f&quot;mysql+pymysql://{os.getenv(&#x27;MYSQL_USER&#x27;)}:{os.getenv(&#x27;MYSQL_PASSWORD&#x27;)}@localhost/TEST_DB&quot;)\nwith engine.connect() as conn:\n    result = conn.execute(&quot;SELECT * FROM TEST_TABLE WHERE column_name = %s&quot;, (user_input,))</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0846","card":"q-database-with-python-119-sql-injection","sec":"database-with-python-s120","q":"Why can you not bind a table or column name as a query parameter, and what do you do instead?","a":"<p>Bind parameters carry values; the database parses the statement first and fills values later, so identifiers and keywords cannot be placeholders. Validate the name against a fixed allowlist (or a strict identifier pattern) and quote it, then build the statement text from the validated name.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0847","card":"q-database-with-python-119-sql-injection","sec":"database-with-python-s120","q":"In the Snowflake Python connector, how is a value bound by default, and how do you get server-side binding?","a":"<p>The default <code>paramstyle</code> is <code>pyformat</code> (<code>%s</code> or <code>%(name)s</code>), where the connector substitutes escaped values on the client before sending. Setting <code>snowflake.connector.paramstyle = 'qmark'</code> (or <code>numeric</code>) before connecting switches to <code>?</code> placeholders that are bound on the server.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0848","card":"q-database-with-python-119-sql-injection","sec":"database-with-python-s120","q":"Why does this SQLAlchemy 2.0 code not work, and how do you write it?","a":"<p>SQLAlchemy 1.4 deprecated and 2.0 removed executing plain strings. Wrap the SQL in <code>text()</code> and use named parameters:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from sqlalchemy import text\n\nwith engine.connect() as conn:\n    result = conn.execute(\n        text(&quot;SELECT * FROM test_table WHERE column_name = :value&quot;),\n        {&quot;value&quot;: user_input},\n    )</code></pre></div>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>with engine.connect() as conn:\n    result = conn.execute(&quot;SELECT * FROM TEST_TABLE WHERE column_name = %s&quot;, (user_input,))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0849","card":"q-database-with-python-119-sql-injection","sec":"database-with-python-s120","q":"Is escaping quotes by hand a safe way to prevent SQL injection?","a":"<p>No. Escaping rules differ by database, character set and context (for example numeric contexts need no quotes), and one missed case is an exploit. Use parameterized queries, which keep data out of the SQL text entirely.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0850","card":"q-database-with-python-119-sql-injection","sec":"database-with-python-s120","q":"Besides parameterized queries, what other defences reduce SQL injection risk?","a":"<p>Give the application account least privilege, validate input by type and length, use allowlists for dynamic identifiers and sort columns, use an ORM or query builder, and keep error details out of user responses.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0851","card":"q-database-with-python-119-sql-injection","sec":"database-with-python-s120","q":"How do you pass a variable-length list to an <code>IN (...)</code> clause without string concatenation?","a":"<p>Build one placeholder per item, then bind the values:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ids = [1, 2, 3]\nplaceholders = &quot;, &quot;.join([&quot;%s&quot;] * len(ids))\nquery = f&quot;SELECT * FROM test_table WHERE id IN ({placeholders})&quot;\ncursor.execute(query, ids)</code></pre></div>\n<p>Only the placeholders are formatted into the text; the values stay bound.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0852","card":"q-database-with-python-120-expired-oauth-token","sec":"database-with-python-s121","q":"OAuth authentication fails with \"Token expired\". How do you refresh the token?","a":"<p>Use the OAuth refresh-token flow, then connect with <code>authenticator='oauth'</code> and the new access token. On a \"Token expired\" error, refresh and reconnect.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nimport os\nimport requests\n\ndef refresh_oauth_token(refresh_token, client_id, client_secret):\n    url = &quot;https://&lt;snowflake_account&gt;.snowflakecomputing.com/oauth/token&quot;\n    data = {\n        &quot;grant_type&quot;: &quot;refresh_token&quot;,\n        &quot;refresh_token&quot;: refresh_token,\n        &quot;client_id&quot;: client_id,\n        &quot;client_secret&quot;: client_secret\n    }\n    response = requests.post(url, data=data)\n    return response.json()[&#x27;access_token&#x27;]\n\ndef connect_with_token(access_token):\n    return snowflake.connector.connect(\n        user=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_USER&#x27;),\n        account=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_ACCOUNT&#x27;),\n        authenticator=&#x27;oauth&#x27;,\n        token=access_token,\n        warehouse=&#x27;COMPUTE_WH&#x27;,\n        database=&#x27;TEST_DB&#x27;,\n        schema=&#x27;PUBLIC&#x27;\n    )\n\ntry:\n    access_token = refresh_oauth_token(\n        refresh_token=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_REFRESH_TOKEN&#x27;),\n        client_id=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_CLIENT_ID&#x27;),\n        client_secret=os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_CLIENT_SECRET&#x27;)\n    )\n    conn = connect_with_token(access_token)\nexcept snowflake.connector.errors.DatabaseError as e:\n    if &quot;Token expired&quot; in str(e):\n        print(&quot;Token expired. Refreshing token...&quot;)\n        access_token = refresh_oauth_token(\n            os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_REFRESH_TOKEN&#x27;),\n            os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_CLIENT_ID&#x27;),\n            os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_CLIENT_SECRET&#x27;)\n        )\n        conn = connect_with_token(access_token)\nfinally:\n    conn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0853","card":"q-database-with-python-120-expired-oauth-token","sec":"database-with-python-s121","q":"What is the difference between an OAuth access token and a refresh token, and why are access tokens short-lived?","a":"<p>The access token authorizes calls and expires quickly (minutes to hours), which limits damage if it leaks. The refresh token is long-lived and is used only to obtain new access tokens. It is more sensitive, so store it in a secret manager and revoke it when compromised.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0854","card":"q-database-with-python-120-expired-oauth-token","sec":"database-with-python-s121","q":"Why refresh an OAuth token before it expires instead of waiting for a \"Token expired\" error?","a":"<p>A request that fails mid-job may leave work half done. Record the <code>expires_in</code> value from the token response and refresh with a safety margin. Keep an error-driven refresh as a fallback, but retry only once so a bad refresh token does not loop forever.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0855","card":"q-database-with-python-120-expired-oauth-token","sec":"database-with-python-s121","q":"What is the difference between Snowflake OAuth and External OAuth?","a":"<p>With Snowflake OAuth, Snowflake itself is the authorization server, configured with a <code>SECURITY INTEGRATION</code> of type OAUTH. With External OAuth, an identity provider such as Okta or Microsoft Entra ID issues the tokens and Snowflake only validates them (integration type EXTERNAL_OAUTH). External OAuth centralizes user management in the identity provider.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0856","card":"q-database-with-python-120-expired-oauth-token","sec":"database-with-python-s121","q":"In Python with the Snowflake connector, what bug does this code have when the first connection attempt fails?","a":"<p>If <code>connect_with_token</code> raised, <code>conn</code> was never assigned, so <code>conn.close()</code> raises <code>NameError</code> and hides the real error. Set <code>conn = None</code> before the <code>try</code> and close only when it is not <code>None</code>, or use a <code>with</code> block.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>try:\n    ...\n    conn = connect_with_token(access_token)\nexcept snowflake.connector.errors.DatabaseError as e:\n    ...\nfinally:\n    conn.close()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0857","card":"q-database-with-python-120-expired-oauth-token","sec":"database-with-python-s121","q":"What should a token refresh call made with <code>requests.post</code> always include?","a":"<p>A <code>timeout</code> (otherwise it can hang forever), a call to <code>response.raise_for_status()</code> so HTTP errors are not parsed as tokens, and handling for a missing <code>access_token</code> key in the JSON body. Never log the response, since it contains tokens.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0858","card":"q-database-with-python-121-slow-query-in-snowflake","sec":"database-with-python-s122","q":"A complex Snowflake query is slow. How do you improve it?","a":"<p>Optimize the query (simplify joins and conditions, filter early), size the warehouse for the workload and use result caching so repeated queries are not recomputed.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nfrom snowflake.connector import DictCursor\n\nconn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n    user=&#x27;your_user&#x27;,\n    password=&#x27;your_password&#x27;,\n    account=&#x27;your_account&#x27;,\n    warehouse=&#x27;OPTIMIZED_WAREHOUSE&#x27;,   # appropriately sized warehouse\n    database=&#x27;your_db&#x27;,\n    schema=&#x27;your_schema&#x27;\n)\n\nquery = &quot;&quot;&quot;\nSELECT c.customer_id, c.name, COUNT(o.order_id) as order_count\nFROM customers c\nJOIN orders o ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id\nWHERE o.order_date &gt;= &#x27;2023-01-01&#x27;\nGROUP BY c.customer_id, c.name\nHAVING order_count &gt; 10\n&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\ntry:\n    cursor = conn.cursor(DictCursor)\n    cursor.execute(&quot;ALTER SESSION SET USE_CACHED_RESULT = TRUE&quot;)   # result caching\n    cursor.execute(query)\n    for row in cursor.fetchall():\n        print(row)\nfinally:\n    cursor.close()\n    conn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0859","card":"q-database-with-python-121-slow-query-in-snowflake","sec":"database-with-python-s122","q":"In Snowflake, what is the Query Profile and what do you look for in it?","a":"<p>It is the visual execution plan with per-operator time and statistics. Look for a join that multiplies rows, a very large share of time in one operator, \"bytes spilled to local or remote storage\" (the warehouse is too small for the data), and the ratio of partitions scanned to partitions total (poor pruning).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0860","card":"q-database-with-python-121-slow-query-in-snowflake","sec":"database-with-python-s122","q":"What is partition pruning in Snowflake, and how do you improve it?","a":"<p>Snowflake stores tables as micro-partitions with min/max metadata per column, and skips partitions whose range cannot match the filter. Filter on columns the data is naturally ordered by (such as a date), select only the columns you need, and for very large tables define a clustering key on the common filter columns.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0861","card":"q-database-with-python-121-slow-query-in-snowflake","sec":"database-with-python-s122","q":"When is a clustering key worth defining in Snowflake, and what does it cost?","a":"<p>For large tables (typically many terabytes) that are filtered on the same columns and have become poorly ordered by inserts. Automatic reclustering uses credits and adds storage, so it is not worth it for small tables or columns with changing filter patterns.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0862","card":"q-database-with-python-121-slow-query-in-snowflake","sec":"database-with-python-s122","q":"Does a larger Snowflake warehouse always make a query faster?","a":"<p>No. A larger size helps scan-heavy and spilling queries because it adds compute for parallel work, but it does not fix a bad join, a single tiny query, or queueing. Each size step doubles the credit rate, so test with a representative query and compare cost per query, not just time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0863","card":"q-database-with-python-121-slow-query-in-snowflake","sec":"database-with-python-s122","q":"What are the main Snowflake caches, and when is each used?","a":"<p>The result cache returns a stored result for an identical query text when the underlying data has not changed (kept 24 hours, needs no running warehouse). The warehouse local disk cache holds table data read earlier by that warehouse and is lost when it suspends. Metadata answers things like <code>COUNT(*)</code> and min/max without scanning.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0864","card":"q-database-with-python-121-slow-query-in-snowflake","sec":"database-with-python-s122","q":"What does <code>ALTER SESSION SET USE_CACHED_RESULT = TRUE</code> do, and is it needed?","a":"<p>It enables the result cache for the session. The parameter is already <code>TRUE</code> by default, so the line changes nothing. Setting it to <code>FALSE</code> is useful when benchmarking a query so a cached result does not hide the real runtime.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0865","card":"q-database-with-python-121-slow-query-in-snowflake","sec":"database-with-python-s122","q":"Why is <code>SELECT *</code> costly in Snowflake?","a":"<p>Snowflake stores data by column, so selecting every column reads and transfers all of them even when you use two. Naming only the columns you need reduces data scanned and speeds up pruning.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0866","card":"q-database-with-python-121-slow-query-in-snowflake","sec":"database-with-python-s122","q":"When would you use search optimization or a materialized view in Snowflake?","a":"<p>Search optimization speeds up selective point lookups and equality or substring filters on very large tables. A materialized view precomputes a repeated aggregation or filter on a single table so queries read the stored result. Both need Enterprise edition or higher and add storage and maintenance cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0867","card":"q-database-with-python-122-query-timeout","sec":"database-with-python-s123","q":"A long query times out on Snowflake and on a generic SQL database. What do you do?","a":"<p>Look at query complexity, raise the timeout (<code>QUERY_TIMEOUT</code> session parameter for Snowflake, <code>connect_timeout</code> for MySQL), add indexes on frequently queried columns in MySQL, and break big queries into pages with <code>LIMIT</code>/<code>OFFSET</code>. Standard Snowflake tables have no user-created indexes, so rely on warehouse size and clustering instead.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nimport mysql.connector\nfrom snowflake.connector.errors import ProgrammingError\n\nsnowflake_conn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n    user=&#x27;your_user&#x27;,\n    password=&#x27;your_password&#x27;,\n    account=&#x27;your_account&#x27;,\n    warehouse=&#x27;your_warehouse&#x27;,\n    database=&#x27;your_db&#x27;,\n    schema=&#x27;your_schema&#x27;,\n    session_parameters={&#x27;QUERY_TIMEOUT&#x27;: 300}   # 5 minutes\n)\n\nmysql_conn = mysql.connector.connect(\n    host=&#x27;localhost&#x27;,\n    user=&#x27;your_user&#x27;,\n    password=&#x27;your_password&#x27;,\n    database=&#x27;your_db&#x27;,\n    connect_timeout=300\n)\n\nsnowflake_query = &quot;&quot;&quot;\nSELECT * FROM large_table\nWHERE date_column BETWEEN &#x27;2023-01-01&#x27; AND &#x27;2023-12-31&#x27;\n&quot;&quot;&quot;\nmysql_query = &quot;&quot;&quot;\nSELECT * FROM large_table\nWHERE date_column BETWEEN &#x27;2023-01-01&#x27; AND &#x27;2023-12-31&#x27;\nLIMIT 1000 OFFSET %s\n&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\ntry:\n    snowflake_cursor = snowflake_conn.cursor()\n    snowflake_cursor.execute(snowflake_query)\n    snowflake_results = snowflake_cursor.fetchall()\n\n    mysql_cursor = mysql_conn.cursor()\n    offset = 0\n    while True:\n        mysql_cursor.execute(mysql_query, (offset,))\n        results = mysql_cursor.fetchall()\n        if not results:\n            break\n        offset += 1000\nexcept ProgrammingError as e:\n    print(f&quot;Snowflake Error: {e}&quot;)\nexcept mysql.connector.Error as e:\n    print(f&quot;MySQL Error: {e}&quot;)\nfinally:\n    snowflake_cursor.close()\n    snowflake_conn.close()\n    mysql_cursor.close()\n    mysql_conn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0868","card":"q-database-with-python-122-query-timeout","sec":"database-with-python-s123","q":"In the Snowflake Python connector, how do you set a timeout for statements in a session, and what is the default?","a":"<p>Set the <code>STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_IN_SECONDS</code> parameter, for example at connect time:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>conn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n    user=&quot;app_user&quot;,\n    password=&quot;...&quot;,\n    account=&quot;myorg-myaccount&quot;,\n    session_parameters={&quot;STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_IN_SECONDS&quot;: 300},\n)</code></pre></div>\n<p>The default is 172800 seconds (2 days). The parameter can also be set on the account, user or warehouse; when set at both warehouse and session, the lower non-zero value applies.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0869","card":"q-database-with-python-122-query-timeout","sec":"database-with-python-s123","q":"In Snowflake, how does <code>STATEMENT_QUEUED_TIMEOUT_IN_SECONDS</code> differ from <code>STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_IN_SECONDS</code>?","a":"<p>The queued timeout limits how long a statement may wait for warehouse capacity before it starts. The statement timeout limits how long it may run once started. A queued-timeout error points to an overloaded warehouse, not a slow query.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0870","card":"q-database-with-python-122-query-timeout","sec":"database-with-python-s123","q":"In <code>mysql-connector-python</code>, does <code>connect_timeout=300</code> stop a slow query after 300 seconds?","a":"<p>No. It limits the time to establish the connection. To cap query time, MySQL has <code>max_execution_time</code> (milliseconds, applies to read-only <code>SELECT</code> statements, from MySQL 5.7.8), and drivers have read timeouts such as <code>read_timeout</code> in PyMySQL.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0871","card":"q-database-with-python-122-query-timeout","sec":"database-with-python-s123","q":"In MySQL queried from Python, what two problems does this paging query have?","a":"<p>Without <code>ORDER BY</code> the page order is not guaranteed, so rows can be skipped or repeated between pages. And a large <code>OFFSET</code> makes MySQL read and discard all earlier rows, so later pages get slower. Use keyset pagination: <code>WHERE id &gt; :last_id ORDER BY id LIMIT 1000</code>, with an index on <code>id</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>mysql_query = &quot;&quot;&quot;\nSELECT * FROM large_table\nWHERE date_column BETWEEN &#x27;2023-01-01&#x27; AND &#x27;2023-12-31&#x27;\nLIMIT 1000 OFFSET %s\n&quot;&quot;&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0872","card":"q-database-with-python-122-query-timeout","sec":"database-with-python-s123","q":"Standard Snowflake tables have no user indexes. What replaces them?","a":"<p>Micro-partition metadata for pruning, clustering keys for ordering, the search optimization service for point lookups, and warehouse sizing. Hybrid tables (an OLTP-oriented table type) are the exception and do support indexes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0873","card":"q-database-with-python-122-query-timeout","sec":"database-with-python-s123","q":"How do you cancel a long-running query in Snowflake?","a":"<p>In Snowflake call <code>SELECT SYSTEM$CANCEL_QUERY('&lt;query_id&gt;')</code> (the id is <code>cursor.sfqid</code>) or abort from the UI.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0874","card":"q-database-with-python-123-large-result-set-and-memory","sec":"database-with-python-s124","q":"A large result set causes memory problems. How do you handle it?","a":"<p>Fetch in chunks with <code>fetchmany()</code> and process each chunk (for example, append to a CSV with pandas). The connector's <code>fetch_pandas_all()</code> is another option for smaller sets.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nimport pandas as pd\n\nconn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n    user=&#x27;your_user&#x27;, password=&#x27;your_password&#x27;, account=&#x27;your_account&#x27;,\n    warehouse=&#x27;your_warehouse&#x27;, database=&#x27;your_db&#x27;, schema=&#x27;your_schema&#x27;\n)\n\ntry:\n    cursor = conn.cursor()\n    cursor.execute(&quot;SELECT * FROM large_table&quot;)\n    chunk_size = 10000\n    while True:\n        rows = cursor.fetchmany(chunk_size)\n        if not rows:\n            break\n        df = pd.DataFrame(rows, columns=[desc[0] for desc in cursor.description])\n        df.to_csv(&#x27;output.csv&#x27;, mode=&#x27;a&#x27;, index=False)\nfinally:\n    cursor.close()\n    conn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0875","card":"q-database-with-python-123-large-result-set-and-memory","sec":"database-with-python-s124","q":"In the Snowflake connector, when do you use <code>fetch_pandas_batches()</code> instead of <code>fetch_pandas_all()</code>?","a":"<p><code>fetch_pandas_all()</code> loads the whole result into one DataFrame, which is fine only for results that fit in memory. <code>fetch_pandas_batches()</code> yields one DataFrame per result chunk, so you process and discard each. Both need the pandas extra (<code>pip install \"snowflake-connector-python[pandas]\"</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0876","card":"q-database-with-python-123-large-result-set-and-memory","sec":"database-with-python-s124","q":"In Python, what is the bug in this export that appends each fetched chunk to a CSV file with pandas?","a":"<p>Each chunk is appended with its own header row, so the header repeats throughout the file. Write the header only for the first chunk (<code>header=first_chunk</code>) and set the flag to <code>False</code> afterwards.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>df = pd.DataFrame(rows, columns=[desc[0] for desc in cursor.description])\ndf.to_csv(&#x27;output.csv&#x27;, mode=&#x27;a&#x27;, index=False)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0877","card":"q-database-with-python-123-large-result-set-and-memory","sec":"database-with-python-s124","q":"In the Python DB-API, what is <code>cursor.description</code> and what is it used for?","a":"<p>After a query it is a sequence with one 7-item tuple per result column; the first item is the column name (followed by type code, sizes, precision, scale and nullability). It is the standard way to build DataFrame column names or dictionaries from raw rows.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0878","card":"q-database-with-python-123-large-result-set-and-memory","sec":"database-with-python-s124","q":"Why do large result sets blow up memory with some drivers even when you call <code>fetchmany()</code>?","a":"<p>Many drivers use client-side cursors that load the full result into memory on <code>execute</code>, so <code>fetchmany</code> only slices it. Use server-side or streaming cursors: PyMySQL <code>SSCursor</code>, a named cursor in psycopg2, or SQLAlchemy <code>execution_options(stream_results=True)</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0879","card":"q-database-with-python-123-large-result-set-and-memory","sec":"database-with-python-s124","q":"What alternative avoids pulling a very large Snowflake result through Python at all?","a":"<p>Do the work in the warehouse (aggregate or filter in SQL), or unload the result to a stage or cloud storage with <code>COPY INTO @stage FROM (SELECT ...)</code> and download or process the files in parallel. Pulling billions of rows through one connection is slow and memory-bound.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0880","card":"q-database-with-python-124-inconsistent-query-results","sec":"database-with-python-s125","q":"A Snowflake query returns different results across runs. What could cause it and how do you investigate?","a":"<p>Data updates between runs, result caching, transaction isolation and query logic errors. Disable the result cache to force fresh data, log the results, and check the query conditions. Snowflake's transaction isolation is READ COMMITTED.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nfrom snowflake.connector import DictCursor\n\nconn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n    user=&#x27;your_user&#x27;, password=&#x27;your_password&#x27;, account=&#x27;your_account&#x27;,\n    warehouse=&#x27;your_warehouse&#x27;, database=&#x27;your_db&#x27;, schema=&#x27;your_schema&#x27;\n)\n\nquery = &quot;&quot;&quot;\nSELECT * FROM orders\nWHERE order_date = CURRENT_DATE\n&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\ntry:\n    cursor = conn.cursor(DictCursor)\n    cursor.execute(&quot;ALTER SESSION SET USE_CACHED_RESULT = FALSE&quot;)   # fresh data\n    cursor.execute(query)\n    results = cursor.fetchall()\n\n    with open(&#x27;query_log.txt&#x27;, &#x27;a&#x27;) as f:\n        f.write(str(results))\n\n    if not results:\n        print(&quot;No results returned. Verify query conditions.&quot;)\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(f&quot;Error: {e}&quot;)\nfinally:\n    cursor.close()\n    conn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0881","card":"q-database-with-python-124-inconsistent-query-results","sec":"database-with-python-s125","q":"In Snowflake, which transaction isolation level is supported, and what does it mean for two reads inside one transaction?","a":"<p>Only READ COMMITTED. Each statement sees the data committed before that statement began, so two identical SELECTs in the same transaction can return different results if another session commits in between.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0882","card":"q-database-with-python-124-inconsistent-query-results","sec":"database-with-python-s125","q":"A query filtered with <code>WHERE order_date = CURRENT_DATE</code> returns different rows for the same job depending on where it runs. Why?","a":"<p><code>CURRENT_DATE</code> is evaluated in the session time zone. Snowflake's <code>TIMEZONE</code> parameter defaults to <code>America/Los_Angeles</code>, so near midnight UTC the date differs from a UTC client. Set the <code>TIMEZONE</code> parameter explicitly or compute the date in the application and pass it as a bound parameter.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0883","card":"q-database-with-python-124-inconsistent-query-results","sec":"database-with-python-s125","q":"When does the Snowflake result cache return an old result, and when is it not used?","a":"<p>It is reused for the same query text from a role with the same privileges when the underlying data has not changed, within 24 hours. Changed data, a different role, or queries with functions evaluated at run time (such as <code>CURRENT_TIMESTAMP</code> or <code>RANDOM</code>) bypass it. Disable it with <code>USE_CACHED_RESULT = FALSE</code> to prove staleness is not the cause.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0884","card":"q-database-with-python-124-inconsistent-query-results","sec":"database-with-python-s125","q":"How would you investigate a Snowflake query that returned different data on two runs?","a":"<p>Compare the two runs by query id in <code>QUERY_HISTORY</code>, check what DML ran on the tables between them, and rerun the query with Time Travel (<code>AT(TIMESTAMP =&gt; ...)</code>) at each moment to see the data as it was. Also check session parameters such as time zone and the active role.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0885","card":"q-database-with-python-125-batch-insert-performance","sec":"database-with-python-s126","q":"Inserting thousands of rows into Snowflake is slow. How do you optimize it?","a":"<p>Use bulk loading: <code>write_pandas</code> for DataFrames, or <code>PUT</code> a file to a stage and <code>COPY INTO</code> the table. Tune the batch size and run loads in parallel.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom snowflake.connector.pandas_tools import write_pandas\n\nconn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n    user=&#x27;your_user&#x27;, password=&#x27;your_password&#x27;, account=&#x27;your_account&#x27;,\n    warehouse=&#x27;your_warehouse&#x27;, database=&#x27;your_db&#x27;, schema=&#x27;your_schema&#x27;\n)\n\ndata = pd.DataFrame({\n    &#x27;id&#x27;: range(10000),\n    &#x27;name&#x27;: [f&#x27;name_{i}&#x27; for i in range(10000)],\n    &#x27;value&#x27;: [i * 1.5 for i in range(10000)]\n})\n\ntry:\n    success, nchunks, nrows, _ = write_pandas(\n        conn=conn, df=data, table_name=&#x27;target_table&#x27;, auto_create_table=True\n    )\n    print(f&quot;Inserted {nrows} rows in {nchunks} chunks&quot;)\n\n    # Alternative: COPY INTO from a stage\n    cursor = conn.cursor()\n    cursor.execute(&quot;PUT file://data.csv @my_stage&quot;)\n    cursor.execute(&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    COPY INTO target_table\n    FROM @my_stage/data.csv\n    FILE_FORMAT = (TYPE = CSV SKIP_HEADER = 1)\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;)\nfinally:\n    cursor.close()\n    conn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0886","card":"q-database-with-python-125-batch-insert-performance","sec":"database-with-python-s126","q":"Why is row-by-row <code>INSERT</code> slow in Snowflake while <code>COPY INTO</code> is fast?","a":"<p>Snowflake is a columnar warehouse built for bulk loads. Each single-row insert is a round trip and creates tiny micro-partitions, while <code>COPY INTO</code> loads many files in parallel into well-sized partitions. Aim for compressed files of roughly 100 to 250 MB each.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0887","card":"q-database-with-python-125-batch-insert-performance","sec":"database-with-python-s126","q":"What does <code>write_pandas</code> do, and what does it return?","a":"<p>It writes the DataFrame to Parquet files, uploads them to a temporary internal stage with <code>PUT</code>, and runs <code>COPY INTO</code> the table. It returns a tuple of success flag, number of chunks, number of rows inserted and the copy output.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>success, nchunks, nrows, _ = write_pandas(\n    conn=conn, df=data, table_name=&#x27;target_table&#x27;, auto_create_table=True\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0888","card":"q-database-with-python-125-batch-insert-performance","sec":"database-with-python-s126","q":"Why does <code>write_pandas</code> often fail with \"table does not exist\" or column errors even though the table exists?","a":"<p>It quotes identifiers, so the case must match. Snowflake stores unquoted names in upper case, while DataFrame columns and the table name are usually lower case. Upper-case the DataFrame columns and the table name (or create the table with the same quoted names).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0889","card":"q-database-with-python-125-batch-insert-performance","sec":"database-with-python-s126","q":"In the Snowflake connector, how does <code>executemany</code> behave for <code>INSERT ... VALUES</code>, and what limit applies?","a":"<p>With client-side binding, the connector rewrites the batch into multi-row <code>INSERT</code> statements, which is much faster than one round trip per row but still slower than staged files. A single <code>VALUES</code> list is limited to 16,384 rows, so large loads should use <code>COPY</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0890","card":"q-database-with-python-125-batch-insert-performance","sec":"database-with-python-s126","q":"When should you load with <code>PUT</code> plus <code>COPY INTO</code> and when with an external stage?","a":"<p>Use <code>PUT</code> when the file is on the machine running the script. When the data already sits in S3, Azure or GCS, create an external stage with a storage integration and <code>COPY INTO</code> directly from it, avoiding a download and upload through the client.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0891","card":"q-database-with-python-125-batch-insert-performance","sec":"database-with-python-s126","q":"What does <code>ON_ERROR</code> control in Snowflake <code>COPY INTO</code>, and what is the default?","a":"<p>It decides what happens when a row fails to load. The default for bulk loading is <code>ABORT_STATEMENT</code> (the whole load fails). <code>CONTINUE</code> skips bad rows, and <code>SKIP_FILE</code> skips any file containing errors. Use <code>VALIDATION_MODE</code> to check files without loading.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0892","card":"q-database-with-python-125-batch-insert-performance","sec":"database-with-python-s126","q":"Why does <code>COPY INTO</code> not load the same file twice, and how do you override it?","a":"<p>Snowflake keeps load metadata per table (for 64 days), so a staged file already loaded is skipped. Add <code>FORCE = TRUE</code> to reload it deliberately, which can create duplicates.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0893","card":"q-database-with-python-125-batch-insert-performance","sec":"database-with-python-s126","q":"Why does a warehouse that is larger not speed up loading of one big file?","a":"<p><code>COPY INTO</code> parallelizes by file, so one large file is processed by a single thread. Split data into many files of moderate size so all cores of the warehouse have work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0894","card":"q-database-with-python-126-warehouse-overload","sec":"database-with-python-s127","q":"Your app overloads a Snowflake warehouse and queries slow down. How do you fix it?","a":"<p>Scale the warehouse, tag queries for prioritization and tracking, split workloads into smaller queries, and monitor usage with built-in functions.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nfrom snowflake.connector import DictCursor\n\nconn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n    user=&#x27;your_user&#x27;, password=&#x27;your_password&#x27;, account=&#x27;your_account&#x27;,\n    warehouse=&#x27;your_warehouse&#x27;, database=&#x27;your_db&#x27;, schema=&#x27;your_schema&#x27;\n)\n\ntry:\n    cursor = conn.cursor(DictCursor)\n    cursor.execute(&quot;ALTER WAREHOUSE your_warehouse SET WAREHOUSE_SIZE = &#x27;LARGE&#x27;&quot;)\n    cursor.execute(&quot;ALTER SESSION SET QUERY_TAG = &#x27;high_priority_job&#x27;&quot;)\n\n    queries = [\n        &quot;SELECT * FROM large_table WHERE region = &#x27;A&#x27;&quot;,\n        &quot;SELECT * FROM large_table WHERE region = &#x27;B&#x27;&quot;\n    ]\n    for query in queries:\n        cursor.execute(query)\n        results = cursor.fetchall()\n        print(f&quot;Processed {len(results)} rows&quot;)\n\n    cursor.execute(&quot;SELECT * FROM TABLE(GET_WAREHOUSE_USAGE_HISTORY())&quot;)\n    print(cursor.fetchall())\nfinally:\n    cursor.close()\n    conn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0895","card":"q-database-with-python-126-warehouse-overload","sec":"database-with-python-s127","q":"In Snowflake, what is the difference between scaling a warehouse up and scaling it out?","a":"<p>Scaling up means a larger size (more compute per query), which helps a single heavy or spilling query. Scaling out means a multi-cluster warehouse that adds clusters when many queries run at once, which helps concurrency and queueing. Slow big queries need up; many users need out.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0896","card":"q-database-with-python-126-warehouse-overload","sec":"database-with-python-s127","q":"How do <code>MIN_CLUSTER_COUNT</code>, <code>MAX_CLUSTER_COUNT</code> and the scaling policy work in a multi-cluster warehouse?","a":"<p>Snowflake runs between the minimum and maximum number of clusters, starting extra ones when queries queue. The Standard policy starts clusters quickly to avoid queueing; Economy waits until there is enough load to keep a new cluster busy, saving credits. Multi-cluster warehouses need Enterprise edition or higher.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0897","card":"q-database-with-python-126-warehouse-overload","sec":"database-with-python-s127","q":"What is risky about running <code>ALTER WAREHOUSE ... SET WAREHOUSE_SIZE = 'LARGE'</code> from application code?","a":"<p>The warehouse is shared, so the change affects every user and job on it and the credit cost, and the code never sets it back. Resizing does not affect queries already running. Better: separate warehouses per workload and a size chosen by an admin or an automated policy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0898","card":"q-database-with-python-126-warehouse-overload","sec":"database-with-python-s127","q":"Why use separate warehouses for ETL, BI dashboards and ad hoc queries?","a":"<p>Isolation: a heavy load job cannot slow dashboards, each workload can be sized and suspended independently, and credit usage can be attributed per team.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0899","card":"q-database-with-python-126-warehouse-overload","sec":"database-with-python-s127","q":"What are auto-suspend and auto-resume in a Snowflake warehouse, and what is the trade-off?","a":"<p>A warehouse suspends after a set idle time and resumes automatically when a query arrives, so you pay only while it runs (billing has a 60-second minimum each time it resumes). The trade-off is a cold local cache after suspend and a short resume delay, so very short idle times can cost more.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0900","card":"q-database-with-python-126-warehouse-overload","sec":"database-with-python-s127","q":"How do you detect that queries are queueing in Snowflake?","a":"<p>In <code>SNOWFLAKE.ACCOUNT_USAGE.QUERY_HISTORY</code>, look at <code>QUEUED_OVERLOAD_TIME</code> (waiting because the warehouse is full). The parameter <code>MAX_CONCURRENCY_LEVEL</code> (default 8) sets how many statements run at once before queueing. Persistent queueing means scale up, scale out or split workloads.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0901","card":"q-database-with-python-126-warehouse-overload","sec":"database-with-python-s127","q":"How do you track and cap warehouse spending in Snowflake?","a":"<p>Query <code>WAREHOUSE_METERING_HISTORY</code> for credits by warehouse, and create a resource monitor with a credit quota and triggers that notify or suspend the warehouse at set percentages.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0902","card":"q-database-with-python-127-connection-dropped-mid-query","sec":"database-with-python-s128","q":"The connection drops during a long query. How do you handle it gracefully?","a":"<p>Retry with exponential backoff (the <code>tenacity</code> library), set connection timeouts and reconnect inside the retried function.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nfrom tenacity import retry, stop_after_attempt, wait_exponential, retry_if_exception_type\nfrom snowflake.connector.errors import OperationalError\n\nconn_params = {\n    &#x27;account&#x27;: &#x27;your_account&#x27;,\n    &#x27;user&#x27;: &#x27;your_user&#x27;,\n    &#x27;password&#x27;: &#x27;your_password&#x27;,\n    &#x27;database&#x27;: &#x27;your_database&#x27;,\n    &#x27;schema&#x27;: &#x27;your_schema&#x27;,\n    &#x27;warehouse&#x27;: &#x27;your_warehouse&#x27;,\n    &#x27;login_timeout&#x27;: 30\n}\n\n@retry(\n    stop=stop_after_attempt(3),\n    wait=wait_exponential(multiplier=1, min=4, max=10),\n    retry=retry_if_exception_type(OperationalError)\n)\ndef execute_query(query):\n    try:\n        conn = snowflake.connector.connect(**conn_params)\n        cursor = conn.cursor()\n        cursor.execute(query)\n        results = cursor.fetchall()\n        cursor.close()\n        conn.close()\n        return results\n    except OperationalError as e:\n        print(f&quot;Connection error: {e}. Retrying...&quot;)\n        raise   # re-raise so the decorator retries\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f&quot;Error: {e}&quot;)\n        raise\n\ntry:\n    print(execute_query(&quot;SELECT * FROM large_table&quot;))\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(f&quot;Failed after retries: {e}&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0903","card":"q-database-with-python-127-connection-dropped-mid-query","sec":"database-with-python-s128","q":"Is it always safe to retry a query after a connection drops?","a":"<p>No. A <code>SELECT</code> is safe, but an <code>INSERT</code> or <code>UPDATE</code> may have been committed before the drop, so a retry can duplicate or double-apply it. Make writes idempotent (a unique key with <code>MERGE</code> or upsert, or a check-then-write using a job id) before retrying automatically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0904","card":"q-database-with-python-127-connection-dropped-mid-query","sec":"database-with-python-s128","q":"What do these tenacity arguments do?","a":"<p><code>stop_after_attempt(3)</code> gives up after three tries. <code>wait_exponential</code> waits longer between attempts (multiplier 1, so roughly 2, 4, 8 seconds, clamped between 4 and 10). <code>retry_if_exception_type</code> retries only for that error class. After the last attempt tenacity raises <code>RetryError</code> unless <code>reraise=True</code> is set.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>...\n@retry(\n    stop=stop_after_attempt(3),\n    wait=wait_exponential(multiplier=1, min=4, max=10),\n    retry=retry_if_exception_type(OperationalError)\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0905","card":"q-database-with-python-127-connection-dropped-mid-query","sec":"database-with-python-s128","q":"Why should backoff include jitter?","a":"<p>If many clients fail together and retry on the same schedule, they all hit the recovering service at the same moments. Adding random jitter spreads the retries and avoids this thundering herd.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0906","card":"q-database-with-python-127-connection-dropped-mid-query","sec":"database-with-python-s128","q":"Why retry only on transient errors such as <code>OperationalError</code> and not on <code>ProgrammingError</code>?","a":"<p>Transient errors (network drop, timeout, brief outage) can succeed on a later attempt. A syntax error, missing table or permission error will fail every time, so retrying only delays the failure and wastes load.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0907","card":"q-database-with-python-127-connection-dropped-mid-query","sec":"database-with-python-s128","q":"What happens to the connection in this code if <code>cursor.execute(query)</code> raises?","a":"<p>The <code>close</code> calls are skipped, so each failed attempt leaks a connection and the retries multiply the leak. Put cleanup in <code>finally</code> or use <code>with snowflake.connector.connect(...) as conn:</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>conn = snowflake.connector.connect(**conn_params)\ncursor = conn.cursor()\ncursor.execute(query)\nresults = cursor.fetchall()\ncursor.close()\nconn.close()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0908","card":"q-database-with-python-127-connection-dropped-mid-query","sec":"database-with-python-s128","q":"How can you avoid losing the result of a very long Snowflake query when the client connection drops?","a":"<p>Submit it with <code>cursor.execute_async(...)</code>, keep the query id (<code>cursor.sfqid</code>), and later reconnect and fetch the result with <code>cursor.get_results_from_sfqid(query_id)</code> after checking the status with <code>conn.get_query_status(query_id)</code>. The query runs on the server independent of the client's network.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0909","card":"q-database-with-python-127-connection-dropped-mid-query","sec":"database-with-python-s128","q":"Why use <code>client_session_keep_alive=True</code> in the Snowflake connector?","a":"<p>Snowflake sessions expire after four hours of inactivity. With keep-alive the connector sends a heartbeat so a long-lived connection (for example in a service) is not closed while idle.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0910","card":"q-database-with-python-128-syntax-error-in-a-dynamic-query","sec":"database-with-python-s129","q":"A dynamically built query fails with a syntax error. How do you debug and prevent it?","a":"<p>Log the final query text with its parameters, use parameterized queries instead of string building, validate the SQL and test it.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nimport logging\n\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.DEBUG, format=&#x27;%(asctime)s - %(levelname)s - %(message)s&#x27;)\n\nconn_params = {\n    &#x27;account&#x27;: &#x27;your_account&#x27;,\n    &#x27;user&#x27;: &#x27;your_user&#x27;,\n    &#x27;password&#x27;: &#x27;your_password&#x27;,\n    &#x27;database&#x27;: &#x27;your_database&#x27;,\n    &#x27;schema&#x27;: &#x27;your_schema&#x27;\n}\n\ndef execute_safe_query(query, params=None):\n    try:\n        conn = snowflake.connector.connect(**conn_params)\n        cursor = conn.cursor()\n        logging.debug(f&quot;Executing query: {query} with params: {params}&quot;)\n        cursor.execute(query, params or ())\n        results = cursor.fetchall()\n        cursor.close()\n        conn.close()\n        return results\n    except snowflake.connector.errors.ProgrammingError as e:\n        logging.error(f&quot;Syntax error in query: {e}&quot;)\n        raise\n    except Exception as e:\n        logging.error(f&quot;Unexpected error: {e}&quot;)\n        raise\n\ntry:\n    query = &quot;SELECT * FROM employees WHERE department = %s AND salary &gt; %s&quot;\n    results = execute_safe_query(query, (&#x27;Engineering&#x27;, 50000))\n    print(results)\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(f&quot;Query failed: {e}&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0911","card":"q-database-with-python-128-syntax-error-in-a-dynamic-query","sec":"database-with-python-s129","q":"How do you build a query with optional filters without concatenating user values into the SQL?","a":"<p>Collect fixed condition fragments and a separate parameter list, then join them:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>conditions = []\nparams = []\nif department:\n    conditions.append(&quot;department = %s&quot;)\n    params.append(department)\nif min_salary is not None:\n    conditions.append(&quot;salary &gt; %s&quot;)\n    params.append(min_salary)\nwhere = &quot; AND &quot;.join(conditions) if conditions else &quot;TRUE&quot;\ncursor.execute(f&quot;SELECT * FROM employees WHERE {where}&quot;, params)</code></pre></div>\n<p>Only code-controlled text goes into the SQL; user values are bound.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0912","card":"q-database-with-python-128-syntax-error-in-a-dynamic-query","sec":"database-with-python-s129","q":"Why log the query text and the parameters separately instead of the final interpolated SQL?","a":"<p>It shows exactly what the application sent and keeps the statement shape reproducible. Parameters can contain personal data or secrets, so mask or omit sensitive values and avoid logging them at INFO level in production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0913","card":"q-database-with-python-128-syntax-error-in-a-dynamic-query","sec":"database-with-python-s129","q":"In a DB-API driver using <code>%s</code> placeholders, what happens to a literal percent sign such as <code>LIKE 'abc%'</code> when parameters are passed?","a":"<p>The driver formats the statement with the parameters, so a literal <code>%</code> must be written <code>%%</code> (or the wildcard passed as a bound value, <code>LIKE %s</code> with <code>'abc%'</code>). An unescaped <code>%</code> causes a formatting or syntax error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0914","card":"q-database-with-python-128-syntax-error-in-a-dynamic-query","sec":"database-with-python-s129","q":"How do you find where a Snowflake syntax error occurs?","a":"<p>The message reads like <code>SQL compilation error: syntax error line 1 at position 28 unexpected 'FROM'</code>. Count to that position in the logged text, and use <code>cursor.sfqid</code> to find the failed statement in query history.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0915","card":"q-database-with-python-128-syntax-error-in-a-dynamic-query","sec":"database-with-python-s129","q":"How can you check a dynamically built Snowflake statement without running it?","a":"<p>Use <code>EXPLAIN USING TEXT &lt;statement&gt;</code>, which compiles the statement and returns the plan without executing it, so syntax and name errors appear. In unit tests, assert on a function that returns the pair <code>(sql, params)</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0916","card":"q-database-with-python-128-syntax-error-in-a-dynamic-query","sec":"database-with-python-s129","q":"Why use a query builder such as SQLAlchemy Core for dynamic queries?","a":"<p>It builds the statement from objects, so syntax, quoting and parameter binding are handled for you and the same code can target several databases. It removes a whole class of string-assembly mistakes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0917","card":"q-database-with-python-129-data-type-mismatch","sec":"database-with-python-s130","q":"An insert fails with a data type mismatch. How do you find and fix it?","a":"<p>Read the table schema (<code>DESCRIBE TABLE</code>), compare it with your values, cast inputs to the right type (Snowflake data type mapping) and read the error logs.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nimport logging\n\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, format=&#x27;%(asctime)s - %(levelname)s - %(message)s&#x27;)\n\nconn_params = {\n    &#x27;account&#x27;: &#x27;your_account&#x27;, &#x27;user&#x27;: &#x27;your_user&#x27;, &#x27;password&#x27;: &#x27;your_password&#x27;,\n    &#x27;database&#x27;: &#x27;your_database&#x27;, &#x27;schema&#x27;: &#x27;your_schema&#x27;\n}\n\ndef get_column_types(table_name):\n    conn = snowflake.connector.connect(**conn_params)\n    cursor = conn.cursor()\n    cursor.execute(f&quot;DESCRIBE TABLE {table_name}&quot;)\n    schema = {row[0]: row[1] for row in cursor.fetchall()}   # name -&gt; type\n    cursor.close()\n    conn.close()\n    return schema\n\ndef insert_data(table_name, data):\n    try:\n        schema = get_column_types(table_name)\n        logging.info(f&quot;Table schema: {schema}&quot;)\n\n        for row in data:\n            for key, value in row.items():\n                col_type = schema[key.upper()]\n                if &#x27;NUMBER&#x27; in col_type and isinstance(value, str):\n                    row[key] = int(float(value)) if value else None\n                elif &#x27;FLOAT&#x27; in col_type and isinstance(value, str):\n                    row[key] = float(value) if value else None\n\n        conn = snowflake.connector.connect(**conn_params)\n        cursor = conn.cursor()\n        columns = &#x27;, &#x27;.join(data[0].keys())\n        placeholders = &#x27;, &#x27;.join([&#x27;%s&#x27;] * len(data[0]))\n        query = f&quot;INSERT INTO {table_name} ({columns}) VALUES ({placeholders})&quot;\n        cursor.executemany(query, [list(row.values()) for row in data])\n        conn.commit()\n        cursor.close()\n        conn.close()\n        logging.info(&quot;Data inserted successfully&quot;)\n    except snowflake.connector.errors.ProgrammingError as e:\n        logging.error(f&quot;Data type mismatch: {e}&quot;)\n        raise\n\ndata = [\n    {&#x27;id&#x27;: &#x27;1&#x27;, &#x27;name&#x27;: &#x27;John&#x27;, &#x27;salary&#x27;: &#x27;50000.0&#x27;},\n    {&#x27;id&#x27;: &#x27;2&#x27;, &#x27;name&#x27;: &#x27;Jane&#x27;, &#x27;salary&#x27;: &#x27;60000.0&#x27;}\n]\ninsert_data(&#x27;employees&#x27;, data)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0918","card":"q-database-with-python-129-data-type-mismatch","sec":"database-with-python-s130","q":"In the Snowflake Python connector, what Python types come back for <code>NUMBER</code>, <code>FLOAT</code> and <code>TIMESTAMP_NTZ</code> columns?","a":"<p><code>NUMBER</code> with scale 0 returns <code>int</code>, <code>NUMBER</code> with a scale returns <code>decimal.Decimal</code>, <code>FLOAT</code> returns <code>float</code>, and <code>TIMESTAMP_NTZ</code> returns a naive <code>datetime</code>. Decimals matter because they do not serialize to JSON and mix badly with floats.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0919","card":"q-database-with-python-129-data-type-mismatch","sec":"database-with-python-s130","q":"In Snowflake, after <code>DESCRIBE TABLE</code>, why should a Python program match column names in upper case?","a":"<p>Snowflake folds unquoted identifiers to upper case, so <code>DESCRIBE TABLE</code> lists the column as <code>SALARY</code>. Looking up a lower-case Python key would miss it. Quoted identifiers keep their case and must be matched exactly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0920","card":"q-database-with-python-129-data-type-mismatch","sec":"database-with-python-s130","q":"In Python loading data into a Snowflake <code>NUMBER</code> column, what is wrong with this cast?","a":"<p>It ignores the column's scale. A <code>NUMBER(10,2)</code> value such as <code>\"12.75\"</code> becomes <code>12</code>. Going through <code>float</code> also loses precision on large values. Parse with <code>decimal.Decimal</code> and respect the scale from the described type.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>if &#x27;NUMBER&#x27; in col_type and isinstance(value, str):\n    row[key] = int(float(value)) if value else None</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0921","card":"q-database-with-python-129-data-type-mismatch","sec":"database-with-python-s130","q":"What do <code>TRY_CAST</code> and <code>TRY_TO_NUMBER</code> do in Snowflake, and when are they useful?","a":"<p>They return <code>NULL</code> instead of raising an error when the value cannot be converted. They are useful when cleaning messy loads: find the bad rows with <code>WHERE TRY_TO_NUMBER(col) IS NULL AND col IS NOT NULL</code>, then decide whether to fix or reject them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0922","card":"q-database-with-python-129-data-type-mismatch","sec":"database-with-python-s130","q":"Why use <code>Decimal</code> rather than <code>float</code> for amounts you insert into a <code>NUMBER(p, s)</code> column?","a":"<p><code>Decimal('0.1')</code> is exact, while the float <code>0.1</code> is a binary approximation, so sums and comparisons drift. The connector binds <code>Decimal</code> without a floating-point conversion.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0923","card":"q-database-with-python-130-transaction-rollback","sec":"database-with-python-s131","q":"A script fails to roll back after an error. How do you manage transactions properly?","a":"<p>Turn off autocommit, run all statements, commit on success and roll back in the exception handler, then close the connection in <code>finally</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nimport logging\n\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, format=&#x27;%(asctime)s - %(levelname)s - %(message)s&#x27;)\n\nconn_params = {\n    &#x27;account&#x27;: &#x27;your_account&#x27;, &#x27;user&#x27;: &#x27;your_user&#x27;, &#x27;password&#x27;: &#x27;your_password&#x27;,\n    &#x27;database&#x27;: &#x27;your_database&#x27;, &#x27;schema&#x27;: &#x27;your_schema&#x27;\n}\n\ndef execute_transaction(queries):\n    conn = None\n    try:\n        conn = snowflake.connector.connect(**conn_params)\n        conn.autocommit(False)   # explicit transaction control\n        cursor = conn.cursor()\n        for query in queries:\n            logging.info(f&quot;Executing: {query}&quot;)\n            cursor.execute(query)\n        conn.commit()\n        logging.info(&quot;Transaction committed successfully&quot;)\n        cursor.close()\n    except Exception as e:\n        logging.error(f&quot;Transaction failed: {e}&quot;)\n        if conn:\n            conn.rollback()\n            logging.info(&quot;Transaction rolled back&quot;)\n        raise\n    finally:\n        if conn:\n            conn.close()\n\nqueries = [\n    &quot;INSERT INTO employees (id, name) VALUES (1, &#x27;John&#x27;)&quot;,\n    &quot;UPDATE employees SET salary = 50000 WHERE id = 1&quot;\n]\nexecute_transaction(queries)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0924","card":"q-database-with-python-130-transaction-rollback","sec":"database-with-python-s131","q":"In Snowflake, which statements commit an open transaction implicitly?","a":"<p>DDL statements (<code>CREATE</code>, <code>ALTER</code>, <code>DROP</code>) commit the current transaction before they run, and run in their own transaction. A rollback after a DDL statement cannot undo the earlier DML, so keep DDL out of transactional batches.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0925","card":"q-database-with-python-130-transaction-rollback","sec":"database-with-python-s131","q":"In the Snowflake connector, what is the default commit behaviour and how do you change it?","a":"<p><code>autocommit</code> is on by default, so every statement commits immediately. Call <code>conn.autocommit(False)</code> (or run <code>BEGIN</code>) to group statements, then <code>conn.commit()</code> or <code>conn.rollback()</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0926","card":"q-database-with-python-130-transaction-rollback","sec":"database-with-python-s131","q":"What happens to an open Snowflake transaction if the connection closes without a commit?","a":"<p>The transaction is rolled back, so uncommitted changes are lost. This is why a crashed script leaves no partial data, and why you must call <code>commit()</code> explicitly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0927","card":"q-database-with-python-130-transaction-rollback","sec":"database-with-python-s131","q":"In the Snowflake connector, how do you run several SQL statements in one call?","a":"<p><code>cursor.execute</code> runs a single statement by default. Use <code>conn.execute_string(\"stmt1; stmt2;\")</code> (which returns a list of cursors) or the <code>num_statements</code> option. Wrap the batch in an explicit transaction if the statements must succeed or fail together.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0928","card":"q-database-with-python-131-deadlock-in-mysql","sec":"database-with-python-s132","q":"Concurrent updates cause a MySQL deadlock. How do you troubleshoot and prevent it?","a":"<p>Retry the transaction, keep a consistent query and lock order, keep transactions short, and use a suitable isolation level such as READ COMMITTED.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import pymysql\nfrom tenacity import retry, stop_after_attempt, wait_exponential, retry_if_exception_type\nimport logging\n\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, format=&#x27;%(asctime)s - %(levelname)s - %(message)s&#x27;)\n\nconn_params = {\n    &#x27;host&#x27;: &#x27;localhost&#x27;, &#x27;user&#x27;: &#x27;your_user&#x27;,\n    &#x27;password&#x27;: &#x27;your_password&#x27;, &#x27;database&#x27;: &#x27;your_database&#x27;\n}\n\n@retry(\n    stop=stop_after_attempt(3),\n    wait=wait_exponential(multiplier=1, min=2, max=5),\n    retry=retry_if_exception_type(pymysql.err.OperationalError)\n)\ndef execute_concurrent_update(query):\n    conn = None\n    try:\n        conn = pymysql.connect(**conn_params, charset=&#x27;utf8mb4&#x27;, cursorclass=pymysql.cursors.DictCursor)\n        conn.autocommit(False)\n        cursor = conn.cursor()\n        cursor.execute(&quot;SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL READ COMMITTED&quot;)\n        logging.info(f&quot;Executing: {query}&quot;)\n        cursor.execute(query)\n        conn.commit()\n        cursor.close()\n        logging.info(&quot;Update successful&quot;)\n    except pymysql.err.OperationalError as e:\n        logging.error(f&quot;Deadlock detected: {e}&quot;)\n        if conn:\n            conn.rollback()\n        raise\n    except Exception as e:\n        logging.error(f&quot;Unexpected error: {e}&quot;)\n        if conn:\n            conn.rollback()\n        raise\n    finally:\n        if conn:\n            conn.close()\n\nexecute_concurrent_update(&quot;UPDATE employees SET salary = salary + 1000 WHERE id = 1&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0929","card":"q-database-with-python-131-deadlock-in-mysql","sec":"database-with-python-s132","q":"What is a deadlock, and how does InnoDB deal with one?","a":"<p>Two transactions each hold a lock the other needs, so neither can proceed. InnoDB detects the cycle, rolls back one transaction (the victim, usually the one that changed fewer rows) and returns error 1213 \"Deadlock found when trying to get lock; try restarting transaction\". The other transaction continues.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0930","card":"q-database-with-python-131-deadlock-in-mysql","sec":"database-with-python-s132","q":"What is the difference between MySQL error 1213 and error 1205?","a":"<p>1213 is a deadlock: InnoDB aborted your transaction immediately. 1205 is a lock wait timeout: your statement waited longer than <code>innodb_lock_wait_timeout</code> (default 50 seconds) for another transaction's lock, and only that statement is rolled back by default. Both can be retried, but 1205 points to long-held locks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0931","card":"q-database-with-python-131-deadlock-in-mysql","sec":"database-with-python-s132","q":"How do you find the cause of a MySQL deadlock?","a":"<p>Run <code>SHOW ENGINE INNODB STATUS</code> and read the <code>LATEST DETECTED DEADLOCK</code> section, which shows both transactions, their statements and the locks. Set <code>innodb_print_all_deadlocks=ON</code> to write every deadlock to the error log.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0932","card":"q-database-with-python-131-deadlock-in-mysql","sec":"database-with-python-s132","q":"Give an example of how two transactions deadlock and how you prevent it.","a":"<p>Transaction 1 updates row A then row B, while transaction 2 updates row B then row A, so each waits for the other. Prevent it by always touching rows in the same order, keeping transactions short, and committing quickly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0933","card":"q-database-with-python-131-deadlock-in-mysql","sec":"database-with-python-s132","q":"What is wrong with this retry condition for deadlocks?","a":"<p><code>OperationalError</code> covers many problems (lost connection, access denied, unknown database), so permanent errors are retried too. Retry only when the error code is 1213 (or 1205), for example with a predicate that checks <code>e.args[0] == 1213</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>...\n@retry(\n    stop=stop_after_attempt(3),\n    wait=wait_exponential(multiplier=1, min=2, max=5),\n    retry=retry_if_exception_type(pymysql.err.OperationalError)\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0934","card":"q-database-with-python-131-deadlock-in-mysql","sec":"database-with-python-s132","q":"Why can an <code>UPDATE</code> with an unindexed <code>WHERE</code> column cause deadlocks in InnoDB?","a":"<p>InnoDB locks the index records it scans. Without an index it scans the whole table and locks many rows, widening the chance that concurrent transactions collide. Add an index on the filtered column so only the matching rows are locked.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0935","card":"q-database-with-python-131-deadlock-in-mysql","sec":"database-with-python-s132","q":"Does READ COMMITTED reduce deadlocks in InnoDB compared with the default REPEATABLE READ?","a":"<p>Often yes. Under REPEATABLE READ InnoDB uses next-key (gap) locks that block inserts into ranges; READ COMMITTED mostly locks only the matching rows, so fewer conflicts occur. The trade-off is that non-repeatable reads are possible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0936","card":"q-database-with-python-131-deadlock-in-mysql","sec":"database-with-python-s132","q":"In MySQL, what is the scope of <code>SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL READ COMMITTED</code> without the <code>SESSION</code> or <code>GLOBAL</code> keyword?","a":"<p>It applies only to the next transaction in that session, and must be issued before the transaction starts. Use <code>SET SESSION TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL ...</code> to keep it for the whole session.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0937","card":"q-database-with-python-132-time-travel-query-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s133","q":"A Time Travel query fails with \"Invalid timestamp\". How do you troubleshoot it?","a":"<p>Check the table's retention period, make sure the timestamp is within it, use the right timestamp format (UTC with <code>::TIMESTAMP_TZ</code>), and verify the <code>AT (TIMESTAMP =&gt; ...)</code> syntax.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nfrom datetime import datetime\nimport pytz\n\nconn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n    user=&#x27;your_user&#x27;, password=&#x27;your_password&#x27;, account=&#x27;your_account&#x27;,\n    warehouse=&#x27;your_warehouse&#x27;, database=&#x27;your_database&#x27;, schema=&#x27;your_schema&#x27;\n)\n\ntimestamp = datetime.now(pytz.UTC).strftime(&#x27;%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S %Z&#x27;)\ntry:\n    cursor = conn.cursor()\n    cursor.execute(&quot;SHOW TABLES LIKE &#x27;your_table&#x27;&quot;)\n    retention_period = cursor.fetchone()[6]   # retention time in days\n    print(f&quot;Table retention period: {retention_period} days&quot;)\n\n    query = f&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    SELECT * FROM your_table\n    AT (TIMESTAMP =&gt; &#x27;{timestamp}&#x27;::TIMESTAMP_TZ)\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    cursor.execute(query)\n    print(cursor.fetchall())\nexcept snowflake.connector.errors.ProgrammingError as e:\n    print(f&quot;Error: {e}&quot;)\n    print(&quot;Verify timestamp is within table&#x27;s retention period and format is correct.&quot;)\nfinally:\n    cursor.close()\n    conn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0938","card":"q-database-with-python-132-time-travel-query-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s133","q":"What is Snowflake Time Travel, and what are its default and maximum retention periods?","a":"<p>It lets you query, clone or restore data as it was at a past point within the retention period, using <code>AT</code> or <code>BEFORE</code>. The default retention (<code>DATA_RETENTION_TIME_IN_DAYS</code>) is 1 day. Standard edition allows only up to 1 day; Enterprise and higher allow up to 90 days for permanent tables.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0939","card":"q-database-with-python-132-time-travel-query-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s133","q":"What are the ways to point Time Travel at a past moment?","a":"<p><code>AT(TIMESTAMP =&gt; '&lt;ts&gt;'::TIMESTAMP_TZ)</code> for an absolute time, <code>AT(OFFSET =&gt; -300)</code> for seconds ago, and <code>BEFORE(STATEMENT =&gt; '&lt;query_id&gt;')</code> for the state before a specific statement ran.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0940","card":"q-database-with-python-132-time-travel-query-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s133","q":"A bad <code>UPDATE</code> changed many rows in Snowflake. How do you recover the old data?","a":"<p>Use the query id of the bad statement and clone the table from just before it:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>CREATE TABLE orders_restored CLONE orders BEFORE (STATEMENT =&gt; &#x27;&lt;query_id&gt;&#x27;);</code></pre></div>\n<p>Then compare or copy the rows back. This only works inside the retention period.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0941","card":"q-database-with-python-132-time-travel-query-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s133","q":"What happens to data after the Time Travel period ends?","a":"<p>Permanent tables move into Fail-safe for 7 days, where only Snowflake support can recover it. Transient and temporary tables have at most 1 day of Time Travel and no Fail-safe. After that the data is gone.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0942","card":"q-database-with-python-132-time-travel-query-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s133","q":"Why does the Time Travel query cast the timestamp as <code>'...'::TIMESTAMP_TZ</code>?","a":"<p>The <code>TIMESTAMP</code> argument must be a timestamp expression, not a plain string, or Snowflake reports an invalid timestamp. Casting to <code>TIMESTAMP_TZ</code> with an explicit offset removes ambiguity about the session time zone.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>query = f&quot;&quot;&quot;\nSELECT * FROM your_table\nAT (TIMESTAMP =&gt; &#x27;{timestamp}&#x27;::TIMESTAMP_TZ)\n&quot;&quot;&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0943","card":"q-database-with-python-132-time-travel-query-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s133","q":"Why is reading the retention period as <code>cursor.fetchone()[6]</code> from <code>SHOW TABLES</code> fragile?","a":"<p>The position of a column in the output can change between releases. Use <code>SHOW PARAMETERS LIKE 'DATA_RETENTION_TIME_IN_DAYS' IN TABLE &lt;table&gt;</code>, or a dictionary cursor and read the column by name.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0944","card":"q-database-with-python-132-time-travel-query-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s133","q":"What does Time Travel cost, and what is the trade-off of a long retention period?","a":"<p>Snowflake keeps the changed and deleted data for the whole period, so storage cost grows with the churn of the table. A long period gives a larger recovery window; set it per table so only important tables pay for it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0945","card":"q-database-with-python-133-moving-data-between-snowflake-and-postgr","sec":"database-with-python-s134","q":"How do you design and troubleshoot a Snowflake to PostgreSQL transfer?","a":"<p>Manage both connections, keep data types compatible (cast where needed), insert in a single PostgreSQL transaction (commit on success, roll back on error) and log type mismatches or connection issues.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nimport psycopg2\nimport pandas as pd\n\nsnow_conn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n    user=&#x27;your_user&#x27;, password=&#x27;your_password&#x27;, account=&#x27;your_account&#x27;,\n    warehouse=&#x27;your_warehouse&#x27;, database=&#x27;your_database&#x27;, schema=&#x27;your_schema&#x27;\n)\npg_conn = psycopg2.connect(\n    dbname=&#x27;your_db&#x27;, user=&#x27;your_user&#x27;, password=&#x27;your_password&#x27;,\n    host=&#x27;localhost&#x27;, port=&#x27;5432&#x27;\n)\n\ntry:\n    snow_cursor = snow_conn.cursor()\n    snow_cursor.execute(&quot;SELECT * FROM your_snowflake_table&quot;)\n    snow_data = pd.DataFrame(snow_cursor.fetchall(), columns=[desc[0] for desc in snow_cursor.description])\n    snow_data = snow_data.astype(str)   # avoid type mismatches\n\n    pg_cursor = pg_conn.cursor()\n    for _, row in snow_data.iterrows():\n        pg_cursor.execute(\n            &quot;INSERT INTO your_pg_table (column1, column2) VALUES (%s, %s)&quot;,\n            (row[&#x27;COLUMN1&#x27;], row[&#x27;COLUMN2&#x27;])\n        )\n    pg_conn.commit()\nexcept Exception as e:\n    pg_conn.rollback()\n    print(f&quot;Error: {e}&quot;)\nfinally:\n    snow_cursor.close()\n    pg_conn.close()\n    snow_conn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0946","card":"q-database-with-python-133-moving-data-between-snowflake-and-postgr","sec":"database-with-python-s134","q":"Why is inserting a DataFrame into PostgreSQL row by row slow, and what are the faster options?","a":"<p>Each <code>execute</code> is a network round trip. Faster: <code>psycopg2.extras.execute_values</code> (multi-row insert), <code>df.to_sql(..., method='multi', chunksize=...)</code>, or best of all PostgreSQL <code>COPY ... FROM STDIN</code> (<code>cursor.copy_expert</code>), which streams data in bulk.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0947","card":"q-database-with-python-133-moving-data-between-snowflake-and-postgr","sec":"database-with-python-s134","q":"In Python moving data from Snowflake to PostgreSQL with pandas, what is the problem with this line?","a":"<p>Converting everything to strings turns <code>None</code> or <code>NaN</code> into the text <code>'None'</code> or <code>'nan'</code> instead of <code>NULL</code>, and loses numeric and date types, so PostgreSQL either rejects values or stores wrong text. Convert specific columns to the right types and map missing values to <code>None</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>snow_data = snow_data.astype(str)   # avoid type mismatches</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0948","card":"q-database-with-python-133-moving-data-between-snowflake-and-postgr","sec":"database-with-python-s134","q":"How would you move a very large table from Snowflake to PostgreSQL?","a":"<p>Unload from Snowflake to files with <code>COPY INTO @stage FROM table</code> (CSV or Parquet, into S3 or an internal stage), copy the files to a place PostgreSQL can read, and bulk load with <code>COPY</code>. For moderate sizes, stream with <code>fetchmany</code> and <code>copy_expert</code> chunks. Avoid holding the full table in a DataFrame.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0949","card":"q-database-with-python-133-moving-data-between-snowflake-and-postgr","sec":"database-with-python-s134","q":"How do common Snowflake types map to PostgreSQL types?","a":"<p><code>NUMBER(p,s)</code> maps to <code>numeric(p,s)</code>, <code>VARCHAR</code> to <code>varchar</code> or <code>text</code>, <code>BOOLEAN</code> to <code>boolean</code>, <code>TIMESTAMP_NTZ</code> to <code>timestamp</code>, <code>TIMESTAMP_TZ</code> to <code>timestamptz</code>, and semi-structured <code>VARIANT</code> to <code>jsonb</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0950","card":"q-database-with-python-133-moving-data-between-snowflake-and-postgr","sec":"database-with-python-s134","q":"Can one commit make a copy between Snowflake and PostgreSQL atomic?","a":"<p>No. There are two separate systems and no shared transaction manager. Make the load re-runnable instead: load into a staging table, then upsert into the target in one PostgreSQL transaction, and record which batch was loaded.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0951","card":"q-database-with-python-133-moving-data-between-snowflake-and-postgr","sec":"database-with-python-s134","q":"How do you load rows into PostgreSQL so that re-running the job does not create duplicates?","a":"<p>Use an upsert: <code>INSERT INTO t (id, val) VALUES (%s, %s) ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET val = EXCLUDED.val</code>. It needs a unique constraint or primary key on the conflict column.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0952","card":"q-database-with-python-134-special-characters-and-emojis","sec":"database-with-python-s135","q":"Inserting emojis or special characters fails. How do you resolve it?","a":"<p>Use UTF-8 end to end: Python stdout and string encoding, Snowflake string handling and the table collation. Always pass values as bind parameters.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nimport sys\n\nsys.stdout.reconfigure(encoding=&#x27;utf-8&#x27;)\n\nconn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n    user=&#x27;your_user&#x27;, password=&#x27;your_password&#x27;, account=&#x27;your_account&#x27;,\n    warehouse=&#x27;your_warehouse&#x27;, database=&#x27;your_database&#x27;, schema=&#x27;your_schema&#x27;\n)\n\ntry:\n    cursor = conn.cursor()\n    cursor.execute(&quot;CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE test_table (data VARCHAR(100)) COLLATE &#x27;utf8&#x27;&quot;)\n    special_data = &quot;Hello 😊 World&quot;\n    cursor.execute(\n        &quot;INSERT INTO test_table (data) VALUES (%s)&quot;,\n        (special_data.encode(&#x27;utf-8&#x27;).decode(&#x27;utf-8&#x27;),)\n    )\n    conn.commit()\n    print(&quot;Data inserted successfully&quot;)\nexcept snowflake.connector.errors.ProgrammingError as e:\n    print(f&quot;Error: {e}&quot;)   # check table collation and Python encoding are UTF-8\nfinally:\n    cursor.close()\n    conn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0953","card":"q-database-with-python-134-special-characters-and-emojis","sec":"database-with-python-s135","q":"In Snowflake, does a <code>COLLATE</code> clause control the character encoding of a <code>VARCHAR</code> column?","a":"<p>No. Snowflake stores all text as UTF-8. Collation only changes how strings are compared and sorted (for example case-insensitive comparison). <code>VARCHAR(100)</code> limits the number of characters, so an emoji counts as one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0954","card":"q-database-with-python-134-special-characters-and-emojis","sec":"database-with-python-s135","q":"In Python, what does <code>text.encode('utf-8').decode('utf-8')</code> achieve when <code>text</code> is a <code>str</code>, and does it fix emoji problems with a database?","a":"<p>Nothing. It encodes a <code>str</code> to bytes and decodes the bytes back to the same <code>str</code>. The connector already takes Python <code>str</code>; the real fixes are in the console and connection encodings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0955","card":"q-database-with-python-134-special-characters-and-emojis","sec":"database-with-python-s135","q":"In Python, what does <code>sys.stdout.reconfigure(encoding='utf-8')</code> fix, and what does it leave unchanged?","a":"<p>It changes how the script writes text to the console, so emojis and accented letters print instead of raising <code>UnicodeEncodeError</code> on a terminal with another code page (for example cp1252 on Windows). It does not change what is stored in the database; that depends on the connection character set and the column or database encoding.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0956","card":"q-database-with-python-134-special-characters-and-emojis","sec":"database-with-python-s135","q":"Where can text encoding go wrong between a Python script and a database?","a":"<p>In the source file and input (read files with <code>encoding='utf-8'</code>), in the terminal or output stream (<code>sys.stdout.reconfigure(encoding='utf-8')</code> on consoles with another code page, which only affects printing), in the connection character set (<code>charset='utf8mb4'</code> for MySQL, <code>client_encoding</code> for PostgreSQL), and in the database, table and column definitions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0957","card":"q-database-with-python-134-special-characters-and-emojis","sec":"database-with-python-s135","q":"In PostgreSQL, where is the character encoding decided and can you change it later?","a":"<p>The encoding is chosen when the database is created (<code>CREATE DATABASE ... ENCODING 'UTF8'</code>) and cannot be changed afterwards; you must dump and restore into a new database. The <code>client_encoding</code> setting converts between the client and the database.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0958","card":"q-database-with-python-135-dynamic-schema-switching","sec":"database-with-python-s136","q":"How do you switch Snowflake schemas at runtime and troubleshoot it?","a":"<p>Run <code>USE SCHEMA</code>, confirm with <code>CURRENT_SCHEMA()</code>, handle errors, and check that the schema exists and you have permission (<code>SHOW SCHEMAS</code>).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\n\nconn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n    user=&#x27;your_user&#x27;, password=&#x27;your_password&#x27;, account=&#x27;your_account&#x27;,\n    warehouse=&#x27;your_warehouse&#x27;, database=&#x27;your_database&#x27;\n)\n\nschemas = [&#x27;schema1&#x27;, &#x27;schema2&#x27;]\ntry:\n    cursor = conn.cursor()\n    for schema in schemas:\n        cursor.execute(f&quot;USE SCHEMA {schema}&quot;)\n        cursor.execute(&quot;SELECT CURRENT_SCHEMA()&quot;)\n        print(f&quot;Switched to schema: {cursor.fetchone()[0]}&quot;)\n        cursor.execute(&quot;SELECT * FROM your_table LIMIT 1&quot;)\n        print(cursor.fetchall())\nexcept snowflake.connector.errors.ProgrammingError as e:\n    print(f&quot;Error: {e}&quot;)\n    cursor.execute(&quot;SHOW SCHEMAS&quot;)\n    print([row[1] for row in cursor.fetchall()])\nfinally:\n    cursor.close()\n    conn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0959","card":"q-database-with-python-135-dynamic-schema-switching","sec":"database-with-python-s136","q":"What is the scope of <code>USE SCHEMA</code> in Snowflake, and what is the risk when connections are pooled?","a":"<p>It changes the current schema of that session only. In a connection pool the next code that borrows the connection inherits the changed schema, so a query can run against the wrong schema. Reset the context on checkout, or use fully qualified names.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0960","card":"q-database-with-python-135-dynamic-schema-switching","sec":"database-with-python-s136","q":"What is the risk of <code>cursor.execute(f\"USE SCHEMA {schema}\")</code>, and how do you reduce it?","a":"<p>A schema name cannot be bound as a parameter, so a value from input could inject SQL. Validate it against an allowlist of known schema names or a strict identifier pattern, and quote the identifier. Remember that unquoted names fold to upper case and quoted names are case-sensitive.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0961","card":"q-database-with-python-135-dynamic-schema-switching","sec":"database-with-python-s136","q":"When do you use fully qualified names (<code>db.schema.table</code>) rather than <code>USE SCHEMA</code>?","a":"<p>Fully qualified names make each statement independent of session state, so they are safer with pools, threads and reordered code. <code>USE</code> is convenient for scripts that run many statements in one place but depends on hidden state.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0962","card":"q-database-with-python-135-dynamic-schema-switching","sec":"database-with-python-s136","q":"How do the words database and schema differ between MySQL, PostgreSQL and Snowflake?","a":"<p>In MySQL, schema is a synonym for database. In PostgreSQL a database contains schemas and one connection reaches only one database. In Snowflake a database contains schemas, and a session can query across databases using qualified names.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0963","card":"q-database-with-python-135-dynamic-schema-switching","sec":"database-with-python-s136","q":"In SQLAlchemy, how can the same code run against a different schema for each tenant?","a":"<p>Use the <code>schema_translate_map</code> execution option, for example <code>conn.execution_options(schema_translate_map={None: \"tenant_a\"})</code>, which maps the schema of the models to another name at run time (SQLAlchemy 1.1 and later).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0964","card":"q-database-with-python-136-stage-file-upload-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s137","q":"Uploading a file to a Snowflake stage fails. What do you check?","a":"<p>Stage configuration and permissions, the file format, that the file exists locally, and the upload method (<code>PUT</code>). Verify with <code>LIST @stage</code> and <code>SHOW STAGES</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nimport os\n\nconn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n    user=&#x27;your_user&#x27;, password=&#x27;your_password&#x27;, account=&#x27;your_account&#x27;,\n    warehouse=&#x27;your_warehouse&#x27;, database=&#x27;your_database&#x27;, schema=&#x27;your_schema&#x27;\n)\n\ntry:\n    cursor = conn.cursor()\n    cursor.execute(&quot;CREATE OR REPLACE STAGE my_stage FILE_FORMAT = (TYPE = CSV)&quot;)\n\n    file_path = &quot;data.csv&quot;\n    if not os.path.exists(file_path):\n        raise FileNotFoundError(&quot;File not found&quot;)\n\n    cursor.execute(f&quot;PUT file://{file_path} @my_stage AUTO_COMPRESS = TRUE&quot;)\n    print(&quot;File uploaded successfully&quot;)\n    cursor.execute(&quot;LIST @my_stage&quot;)\n    print(cursor.fetchall())\nexcept snowflake.connector.errors.ProgrammingError as e:\n    print(f&quot;Error: {e}&quot;)\n    cursor.execute(&quot;SHOW STAGES&quot;)\n    print(cursor.fetchall())\nfinally:\n    cursor.close()\n    conn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0965","card":"q-database-with-python-136-stage-file-upload-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s137","q":"What is a stage in Snowflake, and which kinds are there?","a":"<p>A stage is a location for data files used for loading and unloading. Internal stages live inside Snowflake: the user stage <code>@~</code>, a table stage <code>@%table</code>, and named stages. External stages point to cloud storage such as S3, Azure Blob or GCS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0966","card":"q-database-with-python-136-stage-file-upload-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s137","q":"What are the roles of <code>PUT</code> and <code>COPY INTO</code> in loading a local file, and why can <code>PUT</code> not run in a Snowsight worksheet?","a":"<p><code>PUT file://... @stage</code> uploads a local file to an internal stage from the client machine, and <code>COPY INTO table FROM @stage</code> loads it into the table. <code>PUT</code> needs access to the local filesystem, so it only works from a client such as the Python connector, SnowSQL or a driver.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0967","card":"q-database-with-python-136-stage-file-upload-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s137","q":"What does <code>AUTO_COMPRESS = TRUE</code> in <code>PUT</code> do, and what does it mean for the staged file name?","a":"<p>It compresses the file with gzip while uploading, so <code>data.csv</code> appears as <code>data.csv.gz</code>. <code>COPY INTO</code> detects the compression automatically. <code>PUT</code> also skips a file that already exists in the stage with the same name unless <code>OVERWRITE = TRUE</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0968","card":"q-database-with-python-136-stage-file-upload-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s137","q":"In Snowflake, what is the pitfall in running this statement from a Python script on every execution?","a":"<p><code>CREATE OR REPLACE</code> drops the existing stage and all files in it. Run on every execution it silently deletes data that was staged earlier. Use <code>CREATE STAGE IF NOT EXISTS</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>cursor.execute(&quot;CREATE OR REPLACE STAGE my_stage FILE_FORMAT = (TYPE = CSV)&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0969","card":"q-database-with-python-136-stage-file-upload-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s137","q":"Which privileges are needed to <code>PUT</code> to and <code>COPY</code> from an internal named stage?","a":"<p><code>WRITE</code> on the stage to <code>PUT</code> files and <code>READ</code> to load from it or <code>GET</code> files, plus <code>USAGE</code> on its database and schema, <code>USAGE</code> on a named file format, and <code>INSERT</code> on the target table for <code>COPY INTO</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0970","card":"q-database-with-python-136-stage-file-upload-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s137","q":"Why use a storage integration for an external stage on S3 instead of putting AWS keys in the stage definition?","a":"<p>A storage integration lets Snowflake assume an IAM role that you control, so no long-lived keys are stored in Snowflake or code, and access can be revoked from AWS. It is the recommended way to secure external stages.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0971","card":"q-database-with-python-136-stage-file-upload-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s137","q":"How do you find out why a <code>COPY INTO</code> load failed?","a":"<p>Query <code>INFORMATION_SCHEMA.COPY_HISTORY</code> (or <code>SNOWFLAKE.ACCOUNT_USAGE.COPY_HISTORY</code>) for the file status and error, use <code>VALIDATE</code> for a past load, or run <code>COPY ... VALIDATION_MODE = RETURN_ERRORS</code> to list row-level errors without loading.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0972","card":"q-database-with-python-136-stage-file-upload-failure","sec":"database-with-python-s137","q":"How do you clean up staged files after a load?","a":"<p>Run <code>COPY INTO ... PURGE = TRUE</code> to delete files after a successful load, or <code>REMOVE @stage/path</code> manually. Staged files cost storage until removed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0973","card":"q-database-with-python-137-concurrent-connections-overload","sec":"database-with-python-s138","q":"Many scripts exceed the Snowflake connection limit. How do you mitigate it?","a":"<p>Pool connections (SQLAlchemy <code>pool_size</code> and <code>max_overflow</code>), limit concurrency, review the account settings and balance load across scripts. Call <code>engine.dispose()</code> to clean up.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from sqlalchemy import create_engine\nfrom urllib.parse import quote_plus\n\nsnowflake_url = (\n    f&quot;snowflake://your_user:{quote_plus(&#x27;your_password&#x27;)}@your_account/&quot;\n    f&quot;your_database/your_schema?warehouse=your_warehouse&quot;\n)\nengine = create_engine(snowflake_url, pool_size=5, max_overflow=10)\n\ntry:\n    with engine.connect() as conn:\n        result = conn.execute(&quot;SELECT CURRENT_WAREHOUSE(), CURRENT_DATABASE()&quot;)\n        print(result.fetchall())\n        conn.execute(&quot;SELECT * FROM your_table LIMIT 1&quot;)\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(f&quot;Error: {e}&quot;)   # check Snowflake account connection limits\nfinally:\n    engine.dispose()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0974","card":"q-database-with-python-137-concurrent-connections-overload","sec":"database-with-python-s138","q":"What actually limits concurrent work against Snowflake from many scripts?","a":"<p>The warehouse, not the number of connections. Each warehouse runs a limited number of statements at once (<code>MAX_CONCURRENCY_LEVEL</code>, default 8) and queues the rest. Fix overload with multi-cluster warehouses, separate warehouses for workloads, or fewer parallel jobs; more connections do not add capacity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0975","card":"q-database-with-python-137-concurrent-connections-overload","sec":"database-with-python-s138","q":"Four web worker processes each use <code>create_engine(..., pool_size=5, max_overflow=10)</code>. How many connections can the database see?","a":"<p>Up to 60 (4 processes times 15), because each process has its own pool. Size per-process pools so that the total stays under the database's connection limit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0976","card":"q-database-with-python-137-concurrent-connections-overload","sec":"database-with-python-s138","q":"Why should the number of worker threads match the size of the connection pool?","a":"<p>With more threads than pool slots, the extra threads block on checkout until <code>pool_timeout</code> and then fail. Set <code>ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=...)</code> (or a semaphore) at most <code>pool_size + max_overflow</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0977","card":"q-database-with-python-137-concurrent-connections-overload","sec":"database-with-python-s138","q":"Why must each thread use its own cursor?","a":"<p>A cursor holds the state of one statement and its result set. Sharing it across threads mixes results and parameters. Create a cursor per thread or per task from a connection obtained from the pool.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0978","card":"q-database-with-python-138-cross-region-connection","sec":"database-with-python-s139","q":"A script in one cloud region cannot reach a Snowflake instance in another region. How do you resolve it?","a":"<p>Use the region-specific account URL, and check cross-region replication, network latency and DNS configuration. <code>CURRENT_REGION()</code> confirms where you connected.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\n\nconn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n    user=&#x27;your_user&#x27;,\n    password=&#x27;your_password&#x27;,\n    account=&#x27;your_account.us-west-2&#x27;,   # specify region\n    warehouse=&#x27;your_warehouse&#x27;,\n    database=&#x27;your_database&#x27;,\n    schema=&#x27;your_schema&#x27;\n)\n\ntry:\n    cursor = conn.cursor()\n    cursor.execute(&quot;SELECT CURRENT_REGION()&quot;)\n    print(f&quot;Connected to region: {cursor.fetchone()[0]}&quot;)\n    cursor.execute(&quot;SELECT * FROM your_table LIMIT 1&quot;)\n    print(cursor.fetchall())\nexcept snowflake.connector.errors.OperationalError as e:\n    print(f&quot;Error: {e}&quot;)\n    print(&quot;Verify account URL and network connectivity&quot;)\nfinally:\n    cursor.close()\n    conn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0979","card":"q-database-with-python-138-cross-region-connection","sec":"database-with-python-s139","q":"Why should the Python application run in the same cloud region as the Snowflake account?","a":"<p>Network latency is added to every round trip, and data transferred out of a region or cloud is billed as egress. Large result sets across regions are slow and costly. Run compute close to the data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0980","card":"q-database-with-python-138-cross-region-connection","sec":"database-with-python-s139","q":"What is Snowflake replication and failover, and what does it need?","a":"<p>Replication copies databases to an account in another region or cloud so you have a disaster recovery copy and local read access; failover groups also move the primary role there. Failover and replication of account objects need Business Critical edition or higher.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0981","card":"q-database-with-python-138-cross-region-connection","sec":"database-with-python-s139","q":"What is PrivateLink for Snowflake, and when do you use it?","a":"<p>AWS PrivateLink (and the Azure and GCP equivalents) lets clients reach Snowflake over private IP addresses inside a VPC, without traffic crossing the public internet. It is used when security policy forbids public access. It needs Business Critical edition and a <code>privatelink</code> account URL.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0982","card":"q-database-with-python-138-cross-region-connection","sec":"database-with-python-s139","q":"A script works from one network but is blocked from another region. What do you check besides the account URL?","a":"<p>The Snowflake network policy (IP allowlist), because a different region usually has a different egress IP; firewall rules for the Snowflake hostnames; and DNS resolution. <code>SELECT SYSTEM$ALLOWLIST()</code> lists the hosts and ports that must be allowed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0983","card":"q-database-with-python-138-cross-region-connection","sec":"database-with-python-s139","q":"How do you confirm which region a Snowflake session is connected to?","a":"<p>Run <code>SELECT CURRENT_REGION()</code> (it returns a value like <code>AWS_US_WEST_2</code>) and <code>CURRENT_ACCOUNT()</code>. This proves the session reached the account you expect.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0984","card":"q-database-with-python-139-null-handling","sec":"database-with-python-s140","q":"A script misreads NULL values from Snowflake. How do you handle them?","a":"<p>NULL arrives as Python <code>None</code>. Check for <code>None</code>, use default values (<code>fillna</code> in pandas) and review the query logic.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nimport pandas as pd\n\nconn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n    user=&#x27;your_user&#x27;, password=&#x27;your_password&#x27;, account=&#x27;your_account&#x27;,\n    warehouse=&#x27;your_warehouse&#x27;, database=&#x27;your_database&#x27;, schema=&#x27;your_schema&#x27;\n)\n\ntry:\n    cursor = conn.cursor()\n    cursor.execute(&quot;SELECT column1, column2 FROM your_table&quot;)\n    df = pd.DataFrame(cursor.fetchall(), columns=[desc[0] for desc in cursor.description])\n\n    df.fillna({&#x27;column1&#x27;: &#x27;default_value&#x27;, &#x27;column2&#x27;: 0}, inplace=True)\n    for index, row in df.iterrows():\n        column1 = row[&#x27;column1&#x27;] if row[&#x27;column1&#x27;] is not None else &#x27;default_value&#x27;\n        column2 = row[&#x27;column2&#x27;] if row[&#x27;column2&#x27;] is not None else 0\n        print(f&quot;Row {index}: {column1}, {column2}&quot;)\nexcept snowflake.connector.errors.ProgrammingError as e:\n    print(f&quot;Error: {e}&quot;)\nfinally:\n    cursor.close()\n    conn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0985","card":"q-database-with-python-139-null-handling","sec":"database-with-python-s140","q":"How does a SQL <code>NULL</code> appear in Python, and what happens in pandas when an integer column has NULLs?","a":"<p>In the DB-API a <code>NULL</code> becomes <code>None</code>. In pandas a column of integers with missing values is converted to <code>float64</code> with <code>NaN</code>, so ids become <code>1.0</code>. Use the nullable <code>Int64</code> dtype (<code>astype('Int64')</code>) to keep integers with <code>&lt;NA&gt;</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0986","card":"q-database-with-python-139-null-handling","sec":"database-with-python-s140","q":"Where should you replace NULLs with defaults: in SQL or in pandas?","a":"<p>Prefer SQL when the default is part of the business result: <code>COALESCE(col, 0)</code>, <code>NVL</code> or <code>IFNULL</code> runs in the database and moves less data. Use pandas <code>fillna</code> for processing steps that exist only in Python. Do not hide NULLs that mean \"unknown\" if the downstream logic needs to tell them apart from zero.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0987","card":"q-database-with-python-139-null-handling","sec":"database-with-python-s140","q":"In pandas, what is redundant in this code that fills NULLs and then loops over rows?","a":"<p><code>fillna</code> already replaced the missing values, so the later <code>is not None</code> checks never find any. Also, pandas often stores missing values as <code>NaN</code>, which is not <code>None</code>, so such a check would miss them; use <code>pd.isna(value)</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>df.fillna({&#x27;column1&#x27;: &#x27;default_value&#x27;, &#x27;column2&#x27;: 0}, inplace=True)\nfor index, row in df.iterrows():\n    column1 = row[&#x27;column1&#x27;] if row[&#x27;column1&#x27;] is not None else &#x27;default_value&#x27;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0988","card":"q-database-with-python-139-null-handling","sec":"database-with-python-s140","q":"What is wrong with <code>if value:</code> as a NULL check in Python?","a":"<p>It treats <code>0</code>, <code>0.0</code>, <code>''</code> and <code>False</code> as missing too. Check <code>value is None</code> (or <code>pd.isna</code>) when zero or empty string are valid data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0989","card":"q-database-with-python-139-null-handling","sec":"database-with-python-s140","q":"How do you load empty CSV fields as NULL in Snowflake <code>COPY INTO</code>?","a":"<p>In the file format, set <code>EMPTY_FIELD_AS_NULL = TRUE</code> (the default for CSV) and use <code>NULL_IF = ('NULL', '')</code> to list text values that mean NULL. Otherwise empty strings and the text \"NULL\" are stored as strings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0990","card":"q-database-with-python-139-null-handling","sec":"database-with-python-s140","q":"How do you compare two columns for equality when either can be NULL?","a":"<p><code>a = b</code> returns NULL if either is NULL. Use a null-safe comparison: <code>a IS NOT DISTINCT FROM b</code> (Snowflake, PostgreSQL) or <code>a &lt;=&gt; b</code> in MySQL.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0991","card":"q-database-with-python-140-query-tags-not-showing-in-history","sec":"database-with-python-s141","q":"Query tags set for cost tracking do not appear in query history. How do you debug it?","a":"<p>Set the tag with <code>ALTER SESSION SET QUERY_TAG</code>, run the query, check the tag in <code>INFORMATION_SCHEMA.QUERY_HISTORY()</code>, and review session parameters (<code>SHOW PARAMETERS LIKE 'QUERY_TAG'</code>), tag visibility and permissions.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\n\nconn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n    user=&#x27;your_user&#x27;, password=&#x27;your_password&#x27;, account=&#x27;your_account&#x27;,\n    warehouse=&#x27;your_warehouse&#x27;, database=&#x27;your_database&#x27;, schema=&#x27;your_schema&#x27;\n)\n\ntry:\n    cursor = conn.cursor()\n    cursor.execute(&quot;ALTER SESSION SET QUERY_TAG = &#x27;cost_tracking_tag&#x27;&quot;)\n    cursor.execute(&quot;SELECT * FROM your_table LIMIT 1&quot;)\n    print(cursor.fetchall())\n\n    cursor.execute(&quot;SELECT * FROM TABLE(INFORMATION_SCHEMA.QUERY_HISTORY()) WHERE QUERY_TAG = &#x27;cost_tracking_tag&#x27;&quot;)\n    print(cursor.fetchall())\nexcept snowflake.connector.errors.ProgrammingError as e:\n    print(f&quot;Error: {e}&quot;)\n    cursor.execute(&quot;SHOW PARAMETERS LIKE &#x27;QUERY_TAG&#x27;&quot;)\n    print(cursor.fetchall())\nfinally:\n    cursor.close()\n    conn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0992","card":"q-database-with-python-140-query-tags-not-showing-in-history","sec":"database-with-python-s141","q":"What is <code>QUERY_TAG</code> in Snowflake and what is it used for?","a":"<p>A free-text session parameter that is attached to every query run in the session. It lets you attribute queries and cost to a job, team or feature and find them later in query history. A JSON string works well because <code>PARSE_JSON(query_tag)</code> can read it in reports.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0993","card":"q-database-with-python-140-query-tags-not-showing-in-history","sec":"database-with-python-s141","q":"How do you set a query tag in the Python connector?","a":"<p>Either run <code>ALTER SESSION SET QUERY_TAG = 'job_name'</code> after connecting, or pass <code>session_parameters={'QUERY_TAG': 'job_name'}</code> to <code>connect()</code>. Clear it with <code>ALTER SESSION UNSET QUERY_TAG</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0994","card":"q-database-with-python-140-query-tags-not-showing-in-history","sec":"database-with-python-s141","q":"What is the pitfall of the <code>INFORMATION_SCHEMA.QUERY_HISTORY()</code> call in this code?","a":"<p>The table function returns only 100 queries by default (<code>RESULT_LIMIT</code>), and the filter is applied afterwards, so your tagged query may not be in the returned rows. Pass <code>RESULT_LIMIT =&gt; 10000</code> and a time range, and it covers only the last 7 days.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>cursor.execute(&quot;SELECT * FROM TABLE(INFORMATION_SCHEMA.QUERY_HISTORY()) WHERE QUERY_TAG = &#x27;cost_tracking_tag&#x27;&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f0995","card":"q-database-with-python-140-query-tags-not-showing-in-history","sec":"database-with-python-s141","q":"When do you use <code>INFORMATION_SCHEMA.QUERY_HISTORY</code> and when <code>SNOWFLAKE.ACCOUNT_USAGE.QUERY_HISTORY</code>?","a":"<p>The <code>INFORMATION_SCHEMA</code> table function is near real time but covers 7 days and what the current role can see. The <code>ACCOUNT_USAGE</code> view keeps 365 days and covers the account, but can lag by up to 45 minutes, so a tag set a minute ago will not be there yet.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0996","card":"q-database-with-python-140-query-tags-not-showing-in-history","sec":"database-with-python-s141","q":"What goes wrong with query tags when connections are pooled?","a":"<p>A tag set on a session stays until changed, so the next job that borrows the connection inherits the old tag and its cost is attributed to the wrong owner. Set (or unset) the tag at the start of each unit of work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0997","card":"q-database-with-python-141-legacy-driver-conflict","sec":"database-with-python-s142","q":"An old MySQL driver conflicts with the newer Snowflake connector. How do you resolve it?","a":"<p>Isolate the app in a virtual environment, pin compatible driver versions with <code>pip</code>, and verify with <code>pip list</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>python -m venv snowflake_env\nsource snowflake_env/bin/activate        # Windows: snowflake_env\\Scripts\\activate\npip install snowflake-connector-python==2.9.0 mysql-connector-python==8.0.33\npip list</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nimport mysql.connector\n\nsnow_conn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n    user=&#x27;your_user&#x27;, password=&#x27;your_password&#x27;, account=&#x27;your_account&#x27;,\n    warehouse=&#x27;your_warehouse&#x27;, database=&#x27;your_database&#x27;, schema=&#x27;your_schema&#x27;\n)\nmysql_conn = mysql.connector.connect(\n    host=&#x27;localhost&#x27;, user=&#x27;your_user&#x27;, password=&#x27;your_password&#x27;, database=&#x27;your_db&#x27;\n)\n\ntry:\n    snow_cursor = snow_conn.cursor()\n    snow_cursor.execute(&quot;SELECT * FROM your_table LIMIT 1&quot;)\n    print(snow_cursor.fetchall())\n\n    mysql_cursor = mysql_conn.cursor()\n    mysql_cursor.execute(&quot;SELECT * FROM your_mysql_table LIMIT 1&quot;)\n    print(mysql_cursor.fetchall())\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(f&quot;Error: {e}&quot;)\nfinally:\n    snow_cursor.close()\n    mysql_cursor.close()\n    snow_conn.close()\n    mysql_conn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f0998","card":"q-database-with-python-141-legacy-driver-conflict","sec":"database-with-python-s142","q":"How do you diagnose a dependency conflict between two Python packages?","a":"<p>Run <code>pip check</code> to report broken requirements, <code>pip show &lt;package&gt;</code> and <code>pip list</code> to see installed versions, and <code>pipdeptree</code> to see which package requires which. Conflicts often come from shared dependencies such as <code>cryptography</code>, <code>pyOpenSSL</code> or <code>urllib3</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f0999","card":"q-database-with-python-141-legacy-driver-conflict","sec":"database-with-python-s142","q":"Why is it a poor long-term choice to pin an old connector version, such as an old <code>snowflake-connector-python</code>?","a":"<p>Old versions miss security and bug fixes and may stop working when the service changes its API or retires old protocol versions. Pin to resolve a conflict short term, then plan an upgrade and test it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1000","card":"q-database-with-python-142-snowflake-stream-not-detecting-changes","sec":"database-with-python-s143","q":"A script monitoring a Snowflake stream for change data capture sees no new data. What could be wrong?","a":"<p>Stream configuration, change tracking on the table, the polling logic and the stream retention period. Check with <code>SHOW STREAMS</code> and <code>SHOW TABLES</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nimport time\n\nconn = snowflake.connector.connect(\n    user=&#x27;your_user&#x27;, password=&#x27;your_password&#x27;, account=&#x27;your_account&#x27;,\n    warehouse=&#x27;your_warehouse&#x27;, database=&#x27;your_database&#x27;, schema=&#x27;your_schema&#x27;\n)\n\ntry:\n    cursor = conn.cursor()\n    cursor.execute(&quot;CREATE OR REPLACE STREAM my_stream ON TABLE your_table&quot;)\n\n    while True:\n        cursor.execute(&quot;SELECT * FROM my_stream&quot;)\n        changes = cursor.fetchall()\n        if changes:\n            print(f&quot;New changes detected: {changes}&quot;)\n        else:\n            print(&quot;No new changes&quot;)\n        time.sleep(60)   # poll every minute\n\n        cursor.execute(&quot;SHOW STREAMS LIKE &#x27;my_stream&#x27;&quot;)\n        print(f&quot;Stream retention: {cursor.fetchone()}&quot;)\nexcept snowflake.connector.errors.ProgrammingError as e:\n    print(f&quot;Error: {e}&quot;)\n    cursor.execute(&quot;SHOW TABLES LIKE &#x27;your_table&#x27;&quot;)\n    print(cursor.fetchall())\nfinally:\n    cursor.close()\n    conn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f1001","card":"q-database-with-python-142-snowflake-stream-not-detecting-changes","sec":"database-with-python-s143","q":"What is a Snowflake stream, and what does it store?","a":"<p>A stream records change data capture (CDC) information for a table: which rows were inserted, updated or deleted since the stream's offset. It stores an offset, not a copy of the data, and adds metadata columns such as <code>METADATA$ACTION</code>, <code>METADATA$ISUPDATE</code> and <code>METADATA$ROW_ID</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1002","card":"q-database-with-python-142-snowflake-stream-not-detecting-changes","sec":"database-with-python-s143","q":"In Snowflake, when does a stream's offset move forward?","a":"<p>Only when the stream is consumed by a DML statement in a transaction that commits, such as <code>INSERT INTO target SELECT ... FROM my_stream</code>. A plain <code>SELECT * FROM my_stream</code> does not advance it, so a polling loop that only selects sees the same changes every time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1003","card":"q-database-with-python-142-snowflake-stream-not-detecting-changes","sec":"database-with-python-s143","q":"What is wrong with creating the stream inside the polling loop's setup using <code>CREATE OR REPLACE STREAM</code>?","a":"<p>Replacing a stream resets its offset to the current table version, so any changes not yet consumed are lost. Create it once with <code>CREATE STREAM IF NOT EXISTS</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>cursor.execute(&quot;CREATE OR REPLACE STREAM my_stream ON TABLE your_table&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f1004","card":"q-database-with-python-142-snowflake-stream-not-detecting-changes","sec":"database-with-python-s143","q":"What is the difference between standard, append-only and insert-only streams?","a":"<p>A standard stream tracks inserts, updates and deletes. An append-only stream tracks only inserts on a table. An insert-only stream is for external tables and tracks new files or rows only.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1005","card":"q-database-with-python-142-snowflake-stream-not-detecting-changes","sec":"database-with-python-s143","q":"When does a Snowflake stream become stale and how do you avoid it?","a":"<p>If its offset falls outside the table's change history (data retention, which Snowflake can extend up to 14 days by default), the stream is stale and cannot be read until recreated. Consume the stream regularly and check the <code>STALE</code> column in <code>SHOW STREAMS</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1006","card":"q-database-with-python-142-snowflake-stream-not-detecting-changes","sec":"database-with-python-s143","q":"Why use a Snowflake task with a stream instead of a Python polling loop?","a":"<p>A task runs on a schedule inside Snowflake and can be defined with <code>WHEN SYSTEM$STREAM_HAS_DATA('my_stream')</code>, so it starts only when there is new data and consumes it in the same transaction. This removes the always-running client process.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1007","card":"q-database-with-python-143-connection-leak-in-a-long-running-app","sec":"database-with-python-s144","q":"A long-running app leaks Snowflake connections. How do you detect and prevent it?","a":"<p>Wrap connections in a context manager that always closes them, monitor open resources (for example with <code>psutil</code>) and stop when a threshold is exceeded.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nfrom contextlib import contextmanager\nimport logging\nimport psutil\nimport os\n\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO)\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\n@contextmanager\ndef snowflake_connection(config):\n    conn = None\n    try:\n        conn = snowflake.connector.connect(**config)\n        yield conn\n    except Exception as e:\n        logger.error(f&quot;Connection error: {e}&quot;)\n        raise\n    finally:\n        if conn:\n            conn.close()\n            logger.info(&quot;Connection closed&quot;)\n\ndef monitor_connections():\n    process = psutil.Process(os.getpid())\n    open_files = process.open_files()\n    logger.info(f&quot;Open file descriptors: {len(open_files)}&quot;)\n    return len(open_files)\n\nconfig = {\n    &#x27;user&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_USER&#x27;),\n    &#x27;password&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_PASSWORD&#x27;),\n    &#x27;account&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_ACCOUNT&#x27;),\n    &#x27;warehouse&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_WAREHOUSE&#x27;),\n    &#x27;database&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_DATABASE&#x27;),\n    &#x27;schema&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_SCHEMA&#x27;)\n}\n\nif monitor_connections() &gt; 50:   # threshold\n    logger.warning(&quot;Too many open connections, aborting&quot;)\n    raise ResourceWarning(&quot;Connection limit exceeded&quot;)\n\nwith snowflake_connection(config) as conn:\n    cursor = conn.cursor()\n    cursor.execute(&quot;SELECT CURRENT_VERSION()&quot;)\n    logger.info(f&quot;Snowflake version: {cursor.fetchone()[0]}&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f1008","card":"q-database-with-python-143-connection-leak-in-a-long-running-app","sec":"database-with-python-s144","q":"Does the Snowflake connection object support the <code>with</code> statement?","a":"<p>Yes. <code>with snowflake.connector.connect(...) as conn:</code> closes the connection on exit, and cursors also work as context managers. A custom wrapper is only needed to add logging or settings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1009","card":"q-database-with-python-143-connection-leak-in-a-long-running-app","sec":"database-with-python-s144","q":"Why does <code>psutil.Process().open_files()</code> not detect leaked database connections?","a":"<p>It lists regular open files, not network sockets. Count connections with <code>Process.net_connections()</code> (psutil 6.0 and later; older versions call it <code>connections()</code>), or watch the database side (for example sessions in <code>SNOWFLAKE.ACCOUNT_USAGE.SESSIONS</code> or <code>SHOW PROCESSLIST</code> in MySQL).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1010","card":"q-database-with-python-143-connection-leak-in-a-long-running-app","sec":"database-with-python-s144","q":"Which coding patterns commonly leak database connections in a web service?","a":"<p>Connections opened per request and not closed on the error path, a generator or background task that keeps one open, and sessions kept in global state. Fix with <code>with</code> blocks, a pool, and framework hooks such as dependencies that <code>yield</code> and close in <code>finally</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1011","card":"q-database-with-python-143-connection-leak-in-a-long-running-app","sec":"database-with-python-s144","q":"In SQLAlchemy, what does the error \"QueuePool limit of size 5 overflow 10 reached, connection timed out\" mean?","a":"<p>Every pooled and overflow connection is checked out and none was returned within <code>pool_timeout</code>. The usual cause is a leak (connections not closed) or too many threads for the pool. Fix the leak first; raising the pool size only delays the error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1012","card":"q-database-with-python-143-connection-leak-in-a-long-running-app","sec":"database-with-python-s144","q":"How do idle connection timeouts differ between MySQL and Snowflake, and what error do you see?","a":"<p>MySQL closes an idle connection after <code>wait_timeout</code> (default 28800 seconds, 8 hours), and the next query fails with \"MySQL server has gone away\". Snowflake ends idle sessions after 4 hours unless <code>client_session_keep_alive</code> is on. Use <code>pool_pre_ping</code> or <code>pool_recycle</code> in pools to handle this.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1013","card":"q-database-with-python-144-monitoring-query-performance","sec":"database-with-python-s145","q":"How do you log and monitor Snowflake query performance and errors from Python?","a":"<p>Log to a file and the console, time every query, tag the session, log failures with elapsed time, and send an alert (email or Slack) on error. Snowflake's query history gives server-side metrics.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nimport logging\nimport time\nimport os\n\nlogging.basicConfig(\n    level=logging.INFO,\n    format=&#x27;%(asctime)s - %(levelname)s - %(message)s&#x27;,\n    handlers=[logging.FileHandler(&#x27;query_performance.log&#x27;), logging.StreamHandler()]\n)\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\ndef send_alert(message):\n    logger.warning(f&quot;Alert: {message}&quot;)   # integrate Slack or email here\n\ndef execute_query_with_monitoring(conn, query):\n    start_time = time.time()\n    try:\n        cursor = conn.cursor()\n        cursor.execute(&quot;ALTER SESSION SET QUERY_TAG = &#x27;performance_monitoring&#x27;&quot;)\n        cursor.execute(query)\n        results = cursor.fetchall()\n        logger.info(f&quot;Query executed successfully: {query[:50]}... Time: {time.time() - start_time:.2f}s&quot;)\n        return results\n    except Exception as e:\n        logger.error(f&quot;Query failed: {query[:50]}... Error: {e}, Time: {time.time() - start_time:.2f}s&quot;)\n        send_alert(f&quot;Query error: {e}&quot;)\n        raise\n\nconfig = {\n    &#x27;user&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_USER&#x27;),\n    &#x27;password&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_PASSWORD&#x27;),\n    &#x27;account&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_ACCOUNT&#x27;),\n    &#x27;warehouse&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_WAREHOUSE&#x27;),\n    &#x27;database&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_DATABASE&#x27;),\n    &#x27;schema&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_SCHEMA&#x27;)\n}\nconn = snowflake.connector.connect(**config)\nresults = execute_query_with_monitoring(conn, &quot;SELECT * FROM my_table WHERE date &gt; &#x27;2023-01-01&#x27;&quot;)\nconn.close()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f1014","card":"q-database-with-python-144-monitoring-query-performance","sec":"database-with-python-s145","q":"Which columns of Snowflake's <code>QUERY_HISTORY</code> help to find where time is spent?","a":"<p><code>TOTAL_ELAPSED_TIME</code> for the overall duration, <code>COMPILATION_TIME</code>, <code>EXECUTION_TIME</code>, <code>QUEUED_OVERLOAD_TIME</code> and <code>QUEUED_PROVISIONING_TIME</code> for the split, plus <code>BYTES_SCANNED</code>, <code>PARTITIONS_SCANNED</code> against <code>PARTITIONS_TOTAL</code>, and <code>BYTES_SPILLED_TO_LOCAL_STORAGE</code> and <code>BYTES_SPILLED_TO_REMOTE_STORAGE</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1015","card":"q-database-with-python-144-monitoring-query-performance","sec":"database-with-python-s145","q":"How do you list the slowest queries of the last day in Snowflake?","a":"<p>Query the account usage view:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">SQL</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>SELECT query_id, user_name, warehouse_name, total_elapsed_time / 1000 AS seconds\nFROM snowflake.account_usage.query_history\nWHERE start_time &gt; DATEADD(&#x27;day&#x27;, -1, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP())\nORDER BY total_elapsed_time DESC\nLIMIT 20;</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1016","card":"q-database-with-python-144-monitoring-query-performance","sec":"database-with-python-s145","q":"In Python, why use <code>time.perf_counter()</code> instead of <code>time.time()</code> to measure how long a query took?","a":"<p><code>perf_counter</code> is a monotonic, high-resolution clock for measuring intervals. <code>time.time()</code> is wall-clock time and can jump when the system clock is adjusted, which can give negative or wrong durations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1017","card":"q-database-with-python-144-monitoring-query-performance","sec":"database-with-python-s145","q":"Why does the log configuration need a rotating handler?","a":"<p><code>FileHandler</code> grows without limit and can fill the disk of a long-running service. Use <code>RotatingFileHandler</code> or <code>TimedRotatingFileHandler</code>, or write to stdout and let the platform collect and rotate logs.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>handlers=[logging.FileHandler(&#x27;query_performance.log&#x27;), logging.StreamHandler()]</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f1018","card":"q-database-with-python-144-monitoring-query-performance","sec":"database-with-python-s145","q":"How do you connect an application log line to the matching server-side query?","a":"<p>Log the query id (<code>cursor.sfqid</code> in the Snowflake connector) together with a request or job id. It lets you open the exact statement in query history and its profile.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1019","card":"q-database-with-python-144-monitoring-query-performance","sec":"database-with-python-s145","q":"Which metrics matter when monitoring database access from an application, and when should you alert?","a":"<p>Latency percentiles (p50, p95, p99), error rate, queue time, rows and bytes scanned, and cost. Alert on sustained breaches of a percentile or error rate, not every single failure, and deduplicate or rate-limit alerts so they do not flood the channel.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1020","card":"q-database-with-python-145-scheduled-maintenance-window","sec":"database-with-python-s146","q":"A script fails during a Snowflake maintenance window. How do you handle interruptions?","a":"<p>Retry with exponential backoff, know the maintenance schedule, and fail over by delaying execution or switching to a backup process.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nimport time\nimport logging\nimport os\nfrom retrying import retry\n\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO)\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\n@retry(\n    stop_max_attempt_number=3,\n    wait_exponential_multiplier=1000,\n    wait_exponential_max=10000,\n    retry_on_exception=lambda e: isinstance(e, snowflake.connector.errors.OperationalError)\n)\ndef execute_query_with_retry(conn, query):\n    cursor = conn.cursor()\n    cursor.execute(query)\n    return cursor.fetchall()\n\ndef run_query_with_maintenance_handling(config, query):\n    try:\n        conn = snowflake.connector.connect(**config)\n        results = execute_query_with_retry(conn, query)\n        logger.info(f&quot;Query executed successfully: {query[:50]}...&quot;)\n        return results\n    except snowflake.connector.errors.OperationalError as e:\n        logger.error(f&quot;Maintenance window likely in progress: {e}&quot;)\n        logger.info(&quot;Switching to failover: delaying execution&quot;)\n        time.sleep(3600)   # wait for maintenance to complete\n        conn = snowflake.connector.connect(**config)\n        return execute_query_with_retry(conn, query)\n    finally:\n        if &#x27;conn&#x27; in locals():\n            conn.close()\n\nconfig = {\n    &#x27;user&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_USER&#x27;),\n    &#x27;password&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_PASSWORD&#x27;),\n    &#x27;account&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_ACCOUNT&#x27;),\n    &#x27;warehouse&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_WAREHOUSE&#x27;),\n    &#x27;database&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_DATABASE&#x27;),\n    &#x27;schema&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_SCHEMA&#x27;)\n}\nresults = run_query_with_maintenance_handling(config, &quot;SELECT * FROM my_table&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f1021","card":"q-database-with-python-145-scheduled-maintenance-window","sec":"database-with-python-s146","q":"How does Snowflake handle maintenance, and what does that mean for a script that \"waits for the maintenance window\"?","a":"<p>Snowflake rolls out weekly releases without planned downtime, so there is no fixed window to wait for. Sleeping for an hour is wasted time; handle brief transient errors with short retries and backoff, and fail the job cleanly if they continue.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1022","card":"q-database-with-python-145-scheduled-maintenance-window","sec":"database-with-python-s146","q":"What are Snowflake behaviour change releases?","a":"<p>Changes that could affect existing workloads are packaged in behaviour change bundles, which first run disabled by default, then enabled by default, and finally become permanent. Test your workload while a bundle is optional, before it becomes enforced.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1023","card":"q-database-with-python-145-scheduled-maintenance-window","sec":"database-with-python-s146","q":"Why prefer <code>tenacity</code> to the <code>retrying</code> package?","a":"<p><code>retrying</code> is no longer maintained, and <code>tenacity</code> is its maintained fork with a richer API: async support, <code>stop_after_delay</code>, jitter and callbacks. For new code use <code>tenacity</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1024","card":"q-database-with-python-145-scheduled-maintenance-window","sec":"database-with-python-s146","q":"What is a circuit breaker and when does it beat sleeping and retrying?","a":"<p>A circuit breaker stops calling a dependency after repeated failures, fails fast for a cooling period, then lets a few trial calls through. It protects both the caller (no blocked threads) and the recovering service. It beats a long <code>time.sleep</code>, which holds a worker while doing nothing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1025","card":"q-database-with-python-145-scheduled-maintenance-window","sec":"database-with-python-s146","q":"In Amazon RDS, how do you keep an application working through a maintenance event or failover?","a":"<p>Connect through the cluster or instance endpoint, not an IP address, because the DNS name moves to the new primary. Retry with backoff, re-resolve DNS on reconnect, set sensible pool recycle times, and use Multi-AZ to shorten the outage.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1026","card":"q-database-with-python-145-scheduled-maintenance-window","sec":"database-with-python-s146","q":"How would you design a batch job so that an outage of the database does not lose work?","a":"<p>Make the job idempotent, queue the work (for example in SQS with a dead-letter queue), retry with backoff, and schedule heavy jobs away from known maintenance times. Failed items are then retried later without duplicates.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1027","card":"q-database-with-python-146-compatibility-after-a-version-upgrade","sec":"database-with-python-s147","q":"A script breaks after a Snowflake version upgrade. How do you identify and fix it?","a":"<p>Read the release notes, check <code>CURRENT_VERSION()</code>, upgrade <code>snowflake-connector-python</code>, and test in a staging environment first.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nimport logging\nimport subprocess\nimport os\n\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO)\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\ndef check_snowflake_version(conn):\n    cursor = conn.cursor()\n    cursor.execute(&quot;SELECT CURRENT_VERSION()&quot;)\n    version = cursor.fetchone()[0]\n    logger.info(f&quot;Current Snowflake version: {version}&quot;)\n    return version\n\ndef update_snowflake_connector():\n    try:\n        subprocess.check_call([&#x27;pip&#x27;, &#x27;install&#x27;, &#x27;--upgrade&#x27;, &#x27;snowflake-connector-python&#x27;])\n        logger.info(&quot;Snowflake connector updated successfully&quot;)\n    except subprocess.CalledProcessError as e:\n        logger.error(f&quot;Failed to update snowflake-connector-python: {e}&quot;)\n        raise\n\ndef test_connection(config):\n    try:\n        conn = snowflake.connector.connect(**config)\n        check_snowflake_version(conn)\n        conn.cursor().execute(&quot;SELECT * FROM my_table LIMIT 1&quot;)\n        logger.info(&quot;Test query executed successfully&quot;)\n        conn.close()\n    except Exception as e:\n        logger.error(f&quot;Compatibility issue detected: {e}&quot;)\n        update_snowflake_connector()\n        conn = snowflake.connector.connect(**config)\n        conn.cursor().execute(&quot;SELECT * FROM my_table LIMIT 1&quot;)\n        logger.info(&quot;Test query executed successfully after update&quot;)\n        conn.close()\n\nconfig = {\n    &#x27;user&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_USER&#x27;),\n    &#x27;password&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_PASSWORD&#x27;),\n    &#x27;account&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_ACCOUNT&#x27;),\n    &#x27;warehouse&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_WAREHOUSE&#x27;),\n    &#x27;database&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_DATABASE&#x27;),\n    &#x27;schema&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_SCHEMA&#x27;)\n}\ntest_connection(config)   # run in staging first</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f1028","card":"q-database-with-python-146-compatibility-after-a-version-upgrade","sec":"database-with-python-s147","q":"How do you check which version of a Python package is installed, and which version of the Snowflake server you talk to?","a":"<p>For the package use <code>pip show snowflake-connector-python</code> or <code>importlib.metadata.version('snowflake-connector-python')</code>. For the server run <code>SELECT CURRENT_VERSION()</code>. A mismatch between client and server behaviour can be a cause of breakage.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1029","card":"q-database-with-python-146-compatibility-after-a-version-upgrade","sec":"database-with-python-s147","q":"Why is this runtime upgrade a bad idea?","a":"<p>It changes the environment of a running production process, so it is not reproducible and may need permissions the app should not have. The process already imported the old module, so the new version only takes effect after a restart. Upgrade in the build pipeline, test, and redeploy. Also use <code>[sys.executable, '-m', 'pip', ...]</code> so the same interpreter is targeted.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>subprocess.check_call([&#x27;pip&#x27;, &#x27;install&#x27;, &#x27;--upgrade&#x27;, &#x27;snowflake-connector-python&#x27;])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f1030","card":"q-database-with-python-146-compatibility-after-a-version-upgrade","sec":"database-with-python-s147","q":"How do you protect an application from breaking changes in a library upgrade?","a":"<p>Pin versions or use a bounded range such as <code>&gt;=3.0,&lt;4</code>, read the release notes, upgrade in a branch with tests running against the new version, deploy to staging first, and roll out gradually. Run tests with deprecation warnings treated as errors (<code>python -W error::DeprecationWarning</code>) to see upcoming breakage early.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1031","card":"q-database-with-python-146-compatibility-after-a-version-upgrade","sec":"database-with-python-s147","q":"How can CI test an application against several versions of a database driver?","a":"<p>Use a test matrix (GitHub Actions matrix, <code>tox</code> or <code>nox</code>) that installs each driver version, runs the same tests, and reports which versions fail. This catches incompatible releases before production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1032","card":"q-database-with-python-147-automated-credential-rotation","sec":"database-with-python-s148","q":"Credentials must be rotated periodically. How do you automate it?","a":"<p>Keep secrets in a secret manager (AWS Secrets Manager), fetch the new credentials, test them with a connection, and fall back to the old credentials on failure. Run the job on a schedule (AWS Lambda or cron).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import snowflake.connector\nimport boto3\nimport json\nimport logging\nimport os\n\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO)\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\ndef get_new_credentials(secret_id):\n    client = boto3.client(&#x27;secretsmanager&#x27;)\n    response = client.get_secret_value(SecretId=secret_id)\n    return json.loads(response[&#x27;SecretString&#x27;])\n\ndef rotate_credentials(secret_id, config):\n    try:\n        new_creds = get_new_credentials(secret_id)\n        config[&#x27;user&#x27;] = new_creds[&#x27;user&#x27;]\n        config[&#x27;password&#x27;] = new_creds[&#x27;password&#x27;]\n        logger.info(&quot;Credentials rotated successfully&quot;)\n\n        conn = snowflake.connector.connect(**config)\n        cursor = conn.cursor()\n        cursor.execute(&quot;SELECT CURRENT_VERSION()&quot;)\n        logger.info(&quot;Connection test successful with new credentials&quot;)\n        conn.close()\n        return config\n    except Exception as e:\n        logger.error(f&quot;Credential rotation failed: {e}&quot;)\n        raise   # roll back to previous credentials or fail over\n\nconfig = {\n    &#x27;user&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_USER&#x27;),\n    &#x27;password&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_PASSWORD&#x27;),\n    &#x27;account&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_ACCOUNT&#x27;),\n    &#x27;warehouse&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_WAREHOUSE&#x27;),\n    &#x27;database&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_DATABASE&#x27;),\n    &#x27;schema&#x27;: os.getenv(&#x27;SNOWFLAKE_SCHEMA&#x27;)\n}\nupdated_config = rotate_credentials(&#x27;snowflake/credentials&#x27;, config)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"database-with-python-f1033","card":"q-database-with-python-147-automated-credential-rotation","sec":"database-with-python-s148","q":"How does AWS Secrets Manager rotate a secret?","a":"<p>It invokes a Lambda rotation function on a schedule that runs four steps: <code>createSecret</code> (generate a new version labelled <code>AWSPENDING</code>), <code>setSecret</code> (change the credential in the database), <code>testSecret</code> (verify the new credential), and <code>finishSecret</code> (move the label <code>AWSCURRENT</code> to the new version). The previous version becomes <code>AWSPREVIOUS</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1034","card":"q-database-with-python-147-automated-credential-rotation","sec":"database-with-python-s148","q":"What is the alternating-users rotation strategy and why is it used?","a":"<p>Two database users are kept; each rotation changes the password of the user that is not currently in use and then switches the secret to it. Applications holding the old credentials keep working until the next rotation, so there is no downtime during the switch.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1035","card":"q-database-with-python-147-automated-credential-rotation","sec":"database-with-python-s148","q":"How does an application pick up a rotated secret without a restart?","a":"<p>Do not keep the value forever: cache it with a short time-to-live (the <code>aws-secretsmanager-caching</code> library does this) and refetch when authentication fails, then reconnect. Pooled connections should be recycled (<code>pool_recycle</code>) so they authenticate again.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1036","card":"q-database-with-python-147-automated-credential-rotation","sec":"database-with-python-s148","q":"In Python rotating Snowflake credentials, what is the problem in this code if the connection test fails?","a":"<p>The shared <code>config</code> is changed before the new credentials are verified, so after a failure the application is left with bad credentials. Test the new credentials with a copy of the config and swap the shared value only after success.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>config[&#x27;user&#x27;] = new_creds[&#x27;user&#x27;]\nconfig[&#x27;password&#x27;] = new_creds[&#x27;password&#x27;]\nlogger.info(&quot;Credentials rotated successfully&quot;)\nconn = snowflake.connector.connect(**config)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"database-with-python-f1037","card":"q-database-with-python-147-automated-credential-rotation","sec":"database-with-python-s148","q":"How do you rotate a key pair used for Snowflake key pair authentication without downtime?","a":"<p>Snowflake lets a user hold two public keys: <code>RSA_PUBLIC_KEY</code> and <code>RSA_PUBLIC_KEY_2</code>. Add the new key as the second one, deploy clients with the new private key, then remove the old key with <code>ALTER USER ... UNSET RSA_PUBLIC_KEY</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"database-with-python-f1038","card":"q-database-with-python-147-automated-credential-rotation","sec":"database-with-python-s148","q":"How do short-lived credentials compare with rotating long-lived ones?","a":"<p>Short-lived credentials (for example Amazon RDS IAM authentication tokens that last 15 minutes, or dynamic secrets from HashiCorp Vault) are issued on demand and expire, so a leak has a small window and rotation is not a separate job. The trade-off is that the application must request a new credential regularly and the identity system becomes a dependency.</p>","hand":true}];
